ervedAt":"2026-10-09T00:10:22.923Z","repositoryId":1331654609,"sha":"1860e4d4a7142cbcc77b4f5e19ca2f122619c910"},{"author":{"account":null,"email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"authoredDate":"2026-08-29T05:33:01Z","committedDate":"2026-08-29T05:33:01Z","committer":{"account":{"login":"web-flow","providerType":"User"},"email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"observedAt":"2026-10-09T00:10:22.923Z","repositoryId":1331654609,"sha":"712a3966ce4670df1b780b7980fb584c34c66bf2"},{"author":{"account":null,"email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"authoredDate":"2026-08-29T02:38:49Z","committedDate":"2026-08-29T02:38:49Z","committer":{"account":{"login":"web-flow","providerType":"User"},"email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"observedAt":"2026-10-09T00:10:22.923Z","repositoryId":1331654609,"sha":"7ba00d999cc7e7e950665461910a28fab7422a23"},{"author":{"account":null,"email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"authoredDate":"2026-08-29T01:23:05Z","committedDate":"2026-08-29T01:23:05Z","committer":{"account":{"login":"web-flow","providerType":"User"},"email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"observedAt":"2026-10-09T00:10:22.923Z","repositoryId":1331654609,"sha":"193a35291d7688df3eb4fc939b01dc4bd9e798bc"},{"author":{"account":null,"email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"authoredDate":"2026-08-29T01:16:37Z","committedDate":"2026-08-29T01:16:37Z","committer":{"account":{"login":"web-flow","providerType":"User"},"email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"observedAt":"2026-10-09T00:10:22.923Z","repositoryId":1331654609,"sha":"dd18a8cceffea50cbe6f466a19a49c5e5a373a92"},{"author":{"account":null,"email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"authoredDate":"2026-08-28T19:16:46Z","committedDate":"2026-08-28T19:16:46Z","committer":{"account":{"login":"web-flow","providerType":"User"},"email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"observedAt":"2026-10-09T00:10:22.923Z","repositoryId":1331654609,"sha":"1d90f38455e6056e33d11e24a41bb5c49459bdb9"},{"author":{"account":null,"email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"authoredDate":"2026-08-28T09:52:16Z","committedDate":"2026-08-28T09:52:16Z","committer":{"account":null,"email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"observedAt":"2026-10-09T00:10:22.923Z","repositoryId":1331654609,"sha":"1cc3e865717f9bff4e5d224a2de263aacb0cf58d"},{"author":{"account":null,"email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"authoredDate":"2026-08-28T09:42:36Z","committedDate":"2026-08-28T09:42:36Z","committer":{"account":null,"email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"observedAt":"2026-10-09T00:10:22.923Z","repositoryId":1331654609,"sha":"e30b70eb9c0aa415d8b5182169052be0e3b90218"}],"observedAt":"2026-10-09T00:10:22.923Z","raw":[{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/eed365b93249720cc4163cc3d5b99349e950a688/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-07T18:18:17Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-07T18:18:17Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix(publication): engineering-only fixes from the #112 gap report (G1a, G3, G4, G4a) (#132)\n\n* test(publication): pin cross-document atomicity, interruption and concurrency behaviour\n\nAdds tests for #112 that pin current behaviour rather than change it:\n- a reader applying new tenants before the data sources they name, and a\n  retirement before the unbinding that preceded it (mixed revisions);\n- an interrupted publication leaving the published set and readers at\n  mixed document revisions (filesystem and Kubernetes adapters);\n- overlapping writers publishing a dangling binding (write skew) and a\n  lost update at one revision, plus the resourceVersion positive control;\n- last-known-good lost on reader restart and a forward-incompatible\n  document stopping new readers.\n\nTests named current_behaviour_* pass because the gap exists; none are\nignored. No production code changes.\n\nRefs #112\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DU1CMkx64XgQPMRUBG4oUY\n\n* docs(publication): add M2 publication gap report against #112\n\nAudits fabric-runtime-publication, fabric-publication-kubernetes and the\nfabric-tenant-runtime reader against #112''s acceptance criteria: per-document\natomicity versus cross-document ordering, last-known-good behaviour and a\nreader-compatibility matrix. Each gap is classified as engineering or as\nwaiting on D01-1/D01-2.\n\nRefs #112\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DU1CMkx64XgQPMRUBG4oUY\n\n* docs(publication): apply adversarial review to the #112 gap report\n\n- Correct the publisher.rs single-flight citation (:85-88).\n- Add G8: catalogue and tenants are never cross-checked (UnboundDataSource),\n  a deliberate ADR 0018 part 4 choice, so changing it is D01-1.\n- Narrow G1a: a combined refresher does not help per-volume kubelet skew;\n  deferring unresolved bindings is a runtime-plane change.\n- Reclassify the Lease option (G4) and the pre-publication hold (G5) as\n  D01-1, scope the fail-closed claim to tested states, cite the caller\n  that exits on a failed prime, and mention HeldPayloadLost.\n- Make the G6 test distinguish a refused document from an accepted one.\n\nRefs #112\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DU1CMkx64XgQPMRUBG4oUY\n\n* fix(runtime): refresh data sources before tenants on every pass\n\nGap G1a in docs/roadmap/m2-publication-gap-report.md. The runtime primed\ndata sources before tenants but then refreshed them on two independent\nloops, so a refresh could apply a tenant binding ahead of the DataSource\npublished alongside it and serve MissingDataSource (a 500) until the\nother loop caught up. One loop now reloads data sources and then tenants\non every pass (ResourceRefresher::spawn_in_order); a failed load of one\nstill leaves the other''s last good snapshot and its own reload alone.\n\nRuntimeHandles now wraps the single refresh handle (refresh_now and\nshutdown). No wire shape changes.\n\nTests: the G1 current_behaviour test is split into the fixed behaviour\n(a_reader_applies_new_data_sources_before_the_tenants_that_name_them) and\nthe residual per-volume skew that G1b (D01-1) owns. G2''s test now\nsimulates an unprojected tenants volume. New registration test pins the\norder on every refresh.\n\nRefs #112\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DU1CMkx64XgQPMRUBG4oUY\n\n* fix(publication): re-offer an interrupted publication within the same pass\n\nGap G3 in docs/roadmap/m2-publication-gap-report.md. Unwritable is the\none refusal that can leave earlier documents written and later ones not,\nso readers serve mixed revisions until the snapshot is offered again,\nwhich used to wait for the controller''s next scheduled pass.\npublish_with_retry now re-offers the same snapshot at once on\nUnwritable, up to twice (MAX_INTERRUPTED_RETRIES), with its own budget\nbeside the DivergentPayload one. Re-offering is safe because the plan\nre-reads what is held: documents already written settle as unchanged.\n\nThe pass outcome compares each accepted revision with what was held when\nthe pass began (report_outcome.rs), so a document written by an\ninterrupted offer is still reported as written by the pass.\n\nThe adapter-level current_behaviour tests for G3 stay: the adapters still\nwrite three independent documents, and making an interruption invisible\nto readers is G1b (D01-1). New tests cover the re-offer, an exhausted\nbudget, both budgets in one pass, and the pass outcome.\n\nRefs #112\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DU1CMkx64XgQPMRUBG4oUY\n\n* fix(publication): enforce one filesystem publisher and recheck read-only objects\n\nGap G4 in docs/roadmap/m2-publication-gap-report.md, engineering part.\nADR 0018 answers concurrency with exactly one writer; nothing enforced it.\n\nFilesystem adapter: publish holds an exclusive advisory lock on\n.{tenants file}.lock from reading what is held to the last write\n(filesystem/lock.rs). A second publication is refused at once with\nUnwritable and nothing written. This closes the write skew, the lost\nupdate at one revision and the shared temp-path collision for every\npublisher that goes through the adapter.\n\nKubernetes adapter: before the first write, re-read every object the plan\nrelied on but will not write and refuse with Unwritable if its\nresourceVersion moved (confirm.rs). The controller''s immediate re-offer\n(G3) then re-reads and re-plans. This narrows the cross-object write-skew\nwindow to the moments between the check and the writes; it cannot close\nit without a Lease (RBAC, D01-1) or a cross-document generation (G1b,\nD01-1).\n\nTests: fixed-behaviour tests for both adapters; the current_behaviour\ntests are renamed to the residuals they now pin (writers that bypass the\nlock; writers that overlap after their checks).\n\nRefs #112\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DU1CMkx64XgQPMRUBG4oUY\n\n* docs(publication): record the #112 engineering fixes in the gap report\n\nAdds an Engineering fixes section mapping G1a, G3 and G4''s engineering\nparts to their fixes, fixed-behaviour tests and residual current_behaviour\ntests, and lists what stays deferred to D01-1 and D01-2. Also fixes the\nsimilar-names lint in the Kubernetes concurrency fake.\n\nRefs #112\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DU1CMkx64XgQPMRUBG4oUY\n\n* test(publication): pin overlapping filesystem writers corrupting one document\n\nRiley''s review on #131 found that the gap report''s per-document atomicity\nclaim held only with a single writer. atomic_write stages every write of a\ntarget through one fixed sibling path, so two overlapping writers share one\nstaging inode. They can publish an empty file, or interleaved bytes that\nare not JSON.\n\n- Add two deterministic current_behaviour_* tests in\n  filesystem/atomic_write_tests.rs. Writer A runs write_and_sync and\n  atomic_write one statement at a time and writer B is the real\n  atomic_write, so neither test uses sleeps or threads. Neither is ignored.\n- Add gap G4a to the report, with file:line evidence, classified as\n  engineering: ADR 0018 part 5 requires a sibling temp file, not a\n  fixed name.\n- Make the per-document atomicity claims (summary, criteria table and\n  the atomicity table) state their single-writer condition. Make G3''s\n  recovery claim conditional on the held documents still parsing.\n\nRefs #112\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DU1CMkx64XgQPMRUBG4oUY\n\n* fix(publication): stage each filesystem write exclusively (G4a)\n\nGap G4a in docs/roadmap/m2-publication-gap-report.md. atomic_write staged\nevery write of a document through one fixed sibling path opened with a\ntruncating create, so two overlapping writers could empty or interleave\neach other''s staged bytes and publish an empty or non-JSON document.\n\nThe staging file is now created with create_new: a second writer is\nrefused with AlreadyExists and touches nothing, and atomic_write never\nremoves a staging file it did not create. Because a crashed writer''s\nleftover would otherwise refuse every later write, publish removes stale\nstaging files while it holds the publication lock. The path stays a fixed\nsibling, as ADR 0018 part 5 requires.\n\nThe two current_behaviour_overlapping_writers_* tests now assert the\nfixed behaviour; new tests cover the refused writer leaving the other''s\nstaging alone and stale staging cleanup, directly and through publish.\n\nRefs #112\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DU1CMkx64XgQPMRUBG4oUY\n\n* fix(runtime): bound each reload and address adversarial review\n\nReview findings on the #112 engineering fixes:\n\n- One refresh loop now abandons a reload after one refresh interval\n  (runtime.refresh_timed_out), so a hung data-sources load cannot hold\n  back tenant refreshes, deprovisioning included. A panic in either\n  reload stopping both is documented. New test:\n  a_hung_data_source_load_does_not_hold_back_tenant_refreshes.\n- The refresher rustdoc and gap report name the residual case of a\n  publication landing between the two reads of one pass (G1b, D01-1).\n- The gap report''s G1a row records the runtime-plane edit and the\n  RuntimeHandles API change as needing sign-off against ADR 0018.\n- The re-offer log is neutral (control_plane.publication.reoffered):\n  Unwritable also covers the lock and moved-object refusals that wrote\n  nothing.\n- lock.rs documents that it is keyed on the tenants path alone and is\n  taken before the plan; report_outcome.rs documents the second-writer\n  case; the two test fakes in the 121-150 band state their reason.\n\nRefs #112\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DU1CMkx64XgQPMRUBG4oUY\n\n* docs(publication): scope G4a recovery by document type\n\nA corrupt tenants or data-sources document refuses every later pass as\nUnreadable until an operator repairs it, because the plan parses both\nheld payloads. A corrupt catalogue is only byte-compared, so an ordinary\npublication at a higher revision replaces it (or the controller''s\nDivergentPayload re-offer does). Notes that the G4a fix stops overlapping\nadapter writers from producing such a file.\n\nRefs #112\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DU1CMkx64XgQPMRUBG4oUY\n\n* fix(publication): remove a staging file only when this write created it\n\nReview 5446215896 (P2). atomic_write removed the staging path after any\ncreate error other than AlreadyExists, so a create that failed with, say,\nEMFILE deleted another writer''s staging file. Ownership is now decided by\nwhether the create returned a file: a failed create returns at once and\nremoves nothing; a failure after a successful create (fill, fsync,\nrename) still removes the file this call made.\n\nThe create step is passed into atomic_write_with so tests can inject an\nOS failure. New tests: a_failed_create_never_removes_a_staging_file_it_did_not_make\n(foreign staging file keeps its path and bytes under an injected EMFILE;\nfails against the old cleanup) and\na_failure_after_a_successful_create_removes_the_staging_file.\n\nAudit of the same pattern elsewhere on the branch: the rename-failure\ncleanup only runs after this call''s create succeeded; remove_stale_staging\nruns only under the publication lock; lock.rs and the Kubernetes adapter\nremove nothing.\n\nRefs #112\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DU1CMkx64XgQPMRUBG4oUY\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"c5a228d07c7fca62952e5a3e3ef2774132bfd64f","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/c5a228d07c7fca62952e5a3e3ef2774132bfd64f"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/eed365b93249720cc4163cc3d5b99349e950a688","verification":{"payload":"tree c5a228d07c7fca62952e5a3e3ef2774132bfd64f\nparent 37966aa97a60ccc011ed7beadf4c02429f23e6ab\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1791397097 +1300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1791397097 +1300\n\nfix(publication): engineering-only fixes from the #112 gap report (G1a, G3, G4, G4a) (#132)\n\n* test(publication): pin cross-document atomicity, interruption and concurrency behaviour\n\nAdds tests for #112 that pin current behaviour rather than change it:\n- a reader applying new tenants before the data sources they name, and a\n  retirement before the unbinding that preceded it (mixed revisions);\n- an interrupted publication leaving the published set and readers at\n  mixed document revisions (filesystem and Kubernetes adapters);\n- overlapping writers publishing a dangling binding (write skew) and a\n  lost update at one revision, plus the resourceVersion positive control;\n- last-known-good lost on reader restart and a forward-incompatible\n  document stopping new readers.\n\nTests named current_behaviour_* pass because the gap exists; none are\nignored. No production code changes.\n\nRefs #112\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DU1CMkx64XgQPMRUBG4oUY\n\n* docs(publication): add M2 publication gap report against #112\n\nAudits fabric-runtime-publication, fabric-publication-kubernetes and the\nfabric-tenant-runtime reader against #112''s acceptance criteria: per-document\natomicity versus cross-document ordering, last-known-good behaviour and a\nreader-compatibility matrix. Each gap is classified as engineering or as\nwaiting on D01-1/D01-2.\n\nRefs #112\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DU1CMkx64XgQPMRUBG4oUY\n\n* docs(publication): apply adversarial review to the #112 gap report\n\n- Correct the publisher.rs single-flight citation (:85-88).\n- Add G8: catalogue and tenants are never cross-checked (UnboundDataSource),\n  a deliberate ADR 0018 part 4 choice, so changing it is D01-1.\n- Narrow G1a: a combined refresher does not help per-volume kubelet skew;\n  deferring unresolved bindings is a runtime-plane change.\n- Reclassify the Lease option (G4) and the pre-publication hold (G5) as\n  D01-1, scope the fail-closed claim to tested states, cite the caller\n  that exits on a failed prime, and mention HeldPayloadLost.\n- Make the G6 test distinguish a refused document from an accepted one.\n\nRefs #112\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DU1CMkx64XgQPMRUBG4oUY\n\n* fix(runtime): refresh data sources before tenants on every pass\n\nGap G1a in docs/roadmap/m2-publication-gap-report.md. The runtime primed\ndata sources before tenants but then refreshed them on two independent\nloops, so a refresh could apply a tenant binding ahead of the DataSource\npublished alongside it and serve MissingDataSource (a 500) until the\nother loop caught up. One loop now reloads data sources and then tenants\non every pass (ResourceRefresher::spawn_in_order); a failed load of one\nstill leaves the other''s last good snapshot and its own reload alone.\n\nRuntimeHandles now wraps the single refresh handle (refresh_now and\nshutdown). No wire shape changes.\n\nTests: the G1 current_behaviour test is split into the fixed behaviour\n(a_reader_applies_new_data_sources_before_the_tenants_that_name_them) and\nthe residual per-volume skew that G1b (D01-1) owns. G2''s test now\nsimulates an unprojected tenants volume. New registration test pins the\norder on every refresh.\n\nRefs #112\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DU1CMkx64XgQPMRUBG4oUY\n\n* fix(publication): re-offer an interrupted publication within the same pass\n\nGap G3 in docs/roadmap/m2-publication-gap-report.md. Unwritable is the\none refusal that can leave earlier documents written and later ones not,\nso readers serve mixed revisions until the snapshot is offered again,\nwhich used to wait for the controller''s next scheduled pass.\npublish_with_retry now re-offers the same snapshot at once on\nUnwritable, up to twice (MAX_INTERRUPTED_RETRIES), with its own budget\nbeside the DivergentPayload one. Re-offering is safe because the plan\nre-reads what is held: documents already written settle as unchanged.\n\nThe pass outcome compares each accepted revision with what was held when\nthe pass began (report_outcome.rs), so a document written by an\ninterrupted offer is still reported as written by the pass.\n\nThe adapter-level current_behaviour tests for G3 stay: the adapters still\nwrite three independent documents, and making an interruption invisible\nto readers is G1b (D01-1). New tests cover the re-offer, an exhausted\nbudget, both budgets in one pass, and the pass outcome.\n\nRefs #112\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DU1CMkx64XgQPMRUBG4oUY\n\n* fix(publication): enforce one filesystem publisher and recheck read-only objects\n\nGap G4 in docs/roadmap/m2-publication-gap-report.md, engineering part.\nADR 0018 answers concurrency with exactly one writer; nothing enforced it.\n\nFilesystem adapter: publish holds an exclusive advisory lock on\n.{tenants file}.lock from reading what is held to the last write\n(filesystem/lock.rs). A second publication is refused at once with\nUnwritable and nothing written. This closes the write skew, the lost\nupdate at one revision and the shared temp-path collision for every\npublisher that goes through the adapter.\n\nKubernetes adapter: before the first write, re-read every object the plan\nrelied on but will not write and refuse with Unwritable if its\nresourceVersion moved (confirm.rs). The controller''s immediate re-offer\n(G3) then re-reads and re-plans. This narrows the cross-object write-skew\nwindow to the moments between the check and the writes; it cannot close\nit without a Lease (RBAC, D01-1) or a cross-document generation (G1b,\nD01-1).\n\nTests: fixed-behaviour tests for both adapters; the current_behaviour\ntests are renamed to the residuals they now pin (writers that bypass the\nlock; writers that overlap after their checks).\n\nRefs #112\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DU1CMkx64XgQPMRUBG4oUY\n\n* docs(publication): record the #112 engineering fixes in the gap report\n\nAdds an Engineering fixes section mapping G1a, G3 and G4''s engineering\nparts to their fixes, fixed-behaviour tests and residual current_behaviour\ntests, and lists what stays deferred to D01-1 and D01-2. Also fixes the\nsimilar-names lint in the Kubernetes concurrency fake.\n\nRefs #112\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DU1CMkx64XgQPMRUBG4oUY\n\n* test(publication): pin overlapping filesystem writers corrupting one document\n\nRiley''s review on #131 found that the gap report''s per-document atomicity\nclaim held only with a single writer. atomic_write stages every write of a\ntarget through one fixed sibling path, so two overlapping writers share one\nstaging inode. They can publish an empty file, or interleaved bytes that\nare not JSON.\n\n- Add two deterministic current_behaviour_* tests in\n  filesystem/atomic_write_tests.rs. Writer A runs write_and_sync and\n  atomic_write one statement at a time and writer B is the real\n  atomic_write, so neither test uses sleeps or threads. Neither is ignored.\n- Add gap G4a to the report, with file:line evidence, classified as\n  engineering: ADR 0018 part 5 requires a sibling temp file, not a\n  fixed name.\n- Make the per-document atomicity claims (summary, criteria table and\n  the atomicity table) state their single-writer condition. Make G3''s\n  recovery claim conditional on the held documents still parsing.\n\nRefs #112\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DU1CMkx64XgQPMRUBG4oUY\n\n* fix(publication): stage each filesystem write exclusively (G4a)\n\nGap G4a in docs/roadmap/m2-publication-gap-report.md. atomic_write staged\nevery write of a document through one fixed sibling path opened with a\ntruncating create, so two overlapping writers could empty or interleave\neach other''s staged bytes and publish an empty or non-JSON document.\n\nThe staging file is now created with create_new: a second writer is\nrefused with AlreadyExists and touches nothing, and atomic_write never\nremoves a staging file it did not create. Because a crashed writer''s\nleftover would otherwise refuse every later write, publish removes stale\nstaging files while it holds the publication lock. The path stays a fixed\nsibling, as ADR 0018 part 5 requires.\n\nThe two current_behaviour_overlapping_writers_* tests now assert the\nfixed behaviour; new tests cover the refused writer leaving the other''s\nstaging alone and stale staging cleanup, directly and through publish.\n\nRefs #112\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DU1CMkx64XgQPMRUBG4oUY\n\n* fix(runtime): bound each reload and address adversarial review\n\nReview findings on the #112 engineering fixes:\n\n- One refresh loop now abandons a reload after one refresh interval\n  (runtime.refresh_timed_out), so a hung data-sources load cannot hold\n  back tenant refreshes, deprovisioning included. A panic in either\n  reload stopping both is documented. New test:\n  a_hung_data_source_load_does_not_hold_back_tenant_refreshes.\n- The refresher rustdoc and gap report name the residual case of a\n  publication landing between the two reads of one pass (G1b, D01-1).\n- The gap report''s G1a row records the runtime-plane edit and the\n  RuntimeHandles API change as needing sign-off against ADR 0018.\n- The re-offer log is neutral (control_plane.publication.reoffered):\n  Unwritable also covers the lock and moved-object refusals that wrote\n  nothing.\n- lock.rs documents that it is keyed on the tenants path alone and is\n  taken before the plan; report_outcome.rs documents the second-writer\n  case; the two test fakes in the 121-150 band state their reason.\n\nRefs #112\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DU1CMkx64XgQPMRUBG4oUY\n\n* docs(publication): scope G4a recovery by document type\n\nA corrupt tenants or data-sources document refuses every later pass as\nUnreadable until an operator repairs it, because the plan parses both\nheld payloads. A corrupt catalogue is only byte-compared, so an ordinary\npublication at a higher revision replaces it (or the controller''s\nDivergentPayload re-offer does). Notes that the G4a fix stops overlapping\nadapter writers from producing such a file.\n\nRefs #112\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DU1CMkx64XgQPMRUBG4oUY\n\n* fix(publication): remove a staging file only when this write created it\n\nReview 5446215896 (P2). atomic_write removed the staging path after any\ncreate error other than AlreadyExists, so a create that failed with, say,\nEMFILE deleted another writer''s staging file. Ownership is now decided by\nwhether the create returned a file: a failed create returns at once and\nremoves nothing; a failure after a successful create (fill, fsync,\nrename) still removes the file this call made.\n\nThe create step is passed into atomic_write_with so tests can inject an\nOS failure. New tests: a_failed_create_never_removes_a_staging_file_it_did_not_make\n(foreign staging file keeps its path and bytes under an injected EMFILE;\nfails against the old cleanup) and\na_failure_after_a_successful_create_removes_the_staging_file.\n\nAudit of the same pattern elsewhere on the branch: the rename-failure\ncleanup only runs after this call''s create succeeded; remove_stale_staging\nruns only under the publication lock; lock.rs and the Kubernetes adapter\nremove nothing.\n\nRefs #112\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DU1CMkx64XgQPMRUBG4oUY\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqxozpCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAFuQQAB30Z0eTxjN4gED+t4kxg393\nMGTxev+UMMRZ8oFiV+T/MbBPZVI8O7A4gV6pD5B/wpW9i9wctA0bJDxbB/HDnSQP\nvS0RS8qhYE+ejvNsi1JQGzlsmZqQFjgJVwk5f4jtMi97dJBH+2qp9GZPt5a1Xqzt\nRUAfPQvIQ4/DY2ki+Dp5XHnz8BkKDRknn/0vBzC7movxCIXqCJVhOLvaH2ZHhMfG\nyyaFOChFcAZ0F3AJp4i/kRB0razaYySVhNEsGyKG4nHRHCcK3gdbXRUUSYTUi35Q\n0Kp8d81x9jUDmx9MdisYMM/igkq6WKHiRMaQuWDRC/zSxACQdjUG0dit5v1xmFXK\nC7Dzoub848n9ceWurpauLmDPIU6CUPngUZaugrgW6+76ZA0q6Il/xhCFhNZIExOb\nuKR/EMkz9oG3kIpdVM9hag2MD7tuFEbmaMRg5ikt7sINjrfanZSW6ONGrzPJXznP\nqJJ2wkO2mp2PB5dBU+Bqbz5e+Gm67o1nWn4d4LK398W1Nr8hUVbySiFoiIK1wQr8\npeutlP9il+jP2BS9V1U4IQ0WpTWvG6QDen+3oGBqBdFPF29c5NRShI++N0cBQycU\nfXgUMEQ1fsvsMKeHZBvtkv1FT7+CutZ3r8pZkxyA8eFNbIlGBYITTtrcU1a2TMsW\n/Xf6Q1KhQTtaQyevhf3x\n=GDdA\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-10-07T18:18:17Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/eed365b93249720cc4163cc3d5b99349e950a688","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKGVlZDM2NWI5MzI0OTcyMGNjNDE2M2NjM2Q1Yjk5MzQ5ZTk1MGE2ODg","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/37966aa97a60ccc011ed7beadf4c02429f23e6ab","sha":"37966aa97a60ccc011ed7beadf4c02429f23e6ab","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/37966aa97a60ccc011ed7beadf4c02429f23e6ab"}],"sha":"eed365b93249720cc4163cc3d5b99349e950a688","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/eed365b93249720cc4163cc3d5b99349e950a688"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/336672273?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/IngeniousRiley/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/IngeniousRiley/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/IngeniousRiley/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/IngeniousRiley/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/IngeniousRiley","id":336672273,"login":"IngeniousRiley","node_id":"U_kgDOFBE2EQ","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/IngeniousRiley/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/IngeniousRiley/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/IngeniousRiley/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/IngeniousRiley/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/IngeniousRiley/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/IngeniousRiley","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/37966aa97a60ccc011ed7beadf4c02429f23e6ab/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-07T18:17:38Z","email":"riley@pukekos.co.nz","name":"IngeniousRiley"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-07T18:17:38Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Prepare isolated disposable dogfood profile and gated verification (#101)\n\n* Prepare isolated disposable dogfood profile and gated runtime smoke\n\n* Read disposable operator password privately from verified terminal\n\n* chore(dogfood): pin the disposable profile to main bb864f4\n\nMoves the pinned source commit from 1f46788 to bb864f4 (main after #105,\n#106 and #129) in profile.toml, dogfood.py, the README and the workflow.\nBetween the two commits only console sources and roadmap documents\nchanged; the control-plane configuration schema, the console nginx.conf\nand both Dockerfiles are byte-identical, so the TOML schema mirror and the\nsecurity-header comparison stand unchanged.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DU1CMkx64XgQPMRUBG4oUY\n\n* docs(dogfood): runbook prerequisites, protected services and owned-resource table\n\nAdds to examples/disposable-dogfood/README.md, for #111:\n- an explicit scope statement: this profile is not WorkSpec, runtime or\n  tenant acceptance; off-host backup is not a disposable-trial gate;\n  activation needs separate authorization\n- the restricted SSH forwarding prerequisite (restrict, port-forwarding,\n  permitopen for 127.0.0.1:18780 and 127.0.0.1:18781, a refusing forced\n  command, identical local ports so the issuer 127.0.0.1:18781 matches) and\n  the sshd Match block that keeps remote forwarding off\n- the shared services the trial is protected from (Keycloak, OpenBao, Git,\n  the host daemon and host ports) and how each is protected\n- one table of resources, ceilings, port and digest ownership and the exact\n  owned cleanup for each\n\ndogfood.py gains RESOURCE_LIMITS (the compose ceilings, now validated as\nexact values rather than presence) and ssh_forward_options /\nssh_forward_command, which generate the two SSH lines the runbook quotes.\nscripts/tests/test_dogfood_runbook.py fails if the runbook''s sections or any\nvalue in its tables drift from profile.toml or dogfood.py. The canaries\ncommand the runbook names lands in the next commit.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DU1CMkx64XgQPMRUBG4oUY\n\n* feat(dogfood): fail-closed pre and post canaries with redacted receipts\n\nAdds `dogfood.py canaries --phase pre|post` for #111.\n\npre (needs --yes): read-only preconditions first -- daemon identity, a\nreal lock and a valid ownership receipt, the three recorded containers\nowned and running, the recorded network owned and internal with a gateway\ninside the profile subnet, the gate as the first rules of INPUT and\nDOCKER-USER in `iptables -S`, every host TCP listener from /proc/net/tcp\nand tcp6, and both loopback ports answering from the host. Only then one\nthrowaway probe (the pinned node builder base by digest, --rm, --pull\nnever, read-only, no capabilities, non-root, 64m/0.25 CPU/32 pids, logs\noff, project and canary labels) on the internal network: two positive\ncontrols must answer 200, and the bridge gateway on every observed and\ndeclared host port and both published ports, every specifically bound\nhost address, 1.1.1.1:53 and a public HTTPS URL must time out or have no\nroute or name. A refusal, a connect, a TLS error, or a missing, extra or\nunparseable result fails. A foreign container holding the probe name is\nrefused and never removed.\n\npost (read-only): no container, network or volume with the project\nlabel or this profile''s names, no fabric-dogfood0 interface, no iptables\nrule naming the bridge or subnet, no .out/.\n\nBoth phases write .canaries/canary-<phase>-<UTC>.json (0600, exclusive)\nwith the verdict, findings, full `iptables -S` and a reduced network\ninspect; the bootstrap admin credential is redacted and a receipt that\nstill contains it is refused. profile.toml gains protected_host_ports (a\nvalidated floor of shared-service ports); `build` pulls the probe image by\ndigest after checking the pinned console Dockerfile builds from it.\n\nscripts/tests/test_dogfood_canaries.py drives both phases through the CLI\nwith scripted Docker, iptables, /proc, /sys and loopback fakes: pass,\nfail (refused, connected, public reached, TLS reached, failed positive\ncontrols, gate absent or displaced, loopback down, stopped containers,\nforeign probe, leftovers after cleanup) and missing evidence (iptables or\n/proc unreadable, no gateway, probe image absent, empty, malformed,\npartial or extra probe output, probe exit 125). Nothing was executed\nagainst a real host.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DU1CMkx64XgQPMRUBG4oUY\n\n* feat(dogfood): offline activation packet bound to activate\n\nAdds `dogfood.py packet` for #111: one deterministic, offline document for\nthe authorizer -- source commit, the three locked image IDs, the Keycloak\nbase digest, the probe image digest, the daemon identity, the SHA-256 of\nevery rendered artefact, published and internal ports, the SSH key options,\nforward command and sshd restrictions, the five gate rules and their exact\ninverse, every container''s ceilings including the probe, the pre and post\ncanary plan, the activation sequence, cleanup and rollback, and the scope\nstatement -- ending in `packet-sha256:` over all of it. It makes no Docker\ncall, and prints nothing on stdout while `check` fails, while the lock is\nsynthetic, or once an activation exists.\n\n`activate` now requires `--packet-sha256` and refuses before any Docker\ncall unless it equals the digest of the packet the artefacts on disk\nproduce, so an approval cannot be carried to a rebuilt image or a\nre-rendered file.\n\nscripts/tests/test_dogfood_packet.py compares the packet for the shipped\nprofile and a synthetic lock against golden/dogfood_packet.md, checks the\nfingerprint, determinism and every approved value, and the refusals.\nExisting activate tests pass the digest; two new tests cover a missing,\nmalformed, wrong or stale digest.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DU1CMkx64XgQPMRUBG4oUY\n\n* test(dogfood): use a neutral sentinel for the canary redaction fixture\n\nCodeQL flagged clear-text storage of a password at\nscripts/tests/test_dogfood_canaries.py:136 (alert 8). The flow is a\nsynthetic test fixture only: the test wrote a password-named constant into\na temporary .ephemeral/keycloak.env so it could assert that canary receipts\nredact it. In dogfood.py no credential reaches a receipt:\nephemeral_secret_values reads every value in keycloak.env,\nwrite_canary_receipt redacts each one from the serialized receipt and\nrefuses to write if any survives.\n\nThe fixture is now a neutral sentinel written as the env file''s value; the\ntwo tests still assert it never appears in a written receipt, including\nwhen it is injected into the iptables evidence. No suppression and no\nCodeQL configuration change.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DU1CMkx64XgQPMRUBG4oUY\n\n* fix(dogfood): address adversarial review of canaries, packet and SSH guidance\n\nFrom the independent review of d7f6bf2:\n- The public URL canary now passes only on a failed name resolution. A\n  name that resolved and then timed out means Docker''s embedded resolver\n  forwarded the lookup out of the internal network, which is a leak, so it\n  fails. New failing case in the tests.\n- The sshd Match block (AllowTcpForwarding local, PermitListen none, ...)\n  is now required rather than advised. `port-forwarding` in authorized_keys\n  re-enables remote forwarding, which would let the key holder listen on\n  the execution host. The runbook and packet state the minimum OpenSSH\n  versions (7.2 for `restrict`, 7.8 for `PermitListen`); on an older\n  server the operator stops instead of loosening the setup.\n- The runbook and the code say why IPv6 host listeners are probed by port\n  on the IPv4 gateway: the sandbox network has IPv6 disabled, so the probe\n  has no IPv6 route to try.\n- The probe argv test now asserts the absence of every widening flag\n  (cap-add, device, sysctl, mounts, publish, namespaces, dns, entrypoint).\n\nDeclined, with reasons, in the PR thread: secret_store is already\nvalidated; gate ordering already refuses any rule above the gate (tested);\nthe probe image is addressed by digest, so its content cannot change.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DU1CMkx64XgQPMRUBG4oUY\n\n* fix(dogfood): fail closed on unverified cleanup and keep the gate until it is verified\n\nFixes both blockers Riley raised on a8ea954 (PR comment 6042335792), and\nthe same patterns wherever else they occurred.\n\nP2, errors treated as absence:\n- docker_inspect now returns None only when a successful container or\n  network listing lacks the reference (new docker_present). A listing that\n  fails raises. So does something that is listed but cannot be inspected or\n  parsed. Neither is ever read as \"already gone\".\n- remove_recorded lists the recorded IDs again after removing them and\n  refuses if any remains. reset therefore fails closed on inspection,\n  listing or removal failures, and keeps the ownership receipt and local\n  state until removal or absence is verified.\n- canaries: a keycloak.env that exists but cannot be read now refuses\n  instead of being treated as nothing to redact.\n\nP1, gate removal advised before cleanup was verified:\n- Every activation failure after the credential exists goes through one\n  rollback. That covers compose up, ownership verification, the port check,\n  the Keycloak readiness timeout, bootstrap and inspection errors,\n  readiness findings after bootstrap (no longer reported as \"activated\"),\n  and an interrupt. The rollback removes by recorded ID, or by provable\n  ownership when there is no receipt, then requires a listing to show\n  nothing with this profile''s names or recorded IDs.\n- Only then are the credential and receipt deleted, and gate removal\n  advised after `canaries --phase post`. Otherwise the output says\n  ROLLBACK NOT VERIFIED and KEEP the host gate, keeps the receipt and\n  credential, and names the recovery.\n- The post canary accepts the gate complete or absent, never partial, and\n  records which. The cleanup order in reset output, `enforcement`, the\n  packet and the README is now: reset (verified), post canary with the gate\n  in place, REMOVE lines, post canary again.\n- ci_smoke: the runner''s gate cleanup is kept and counted as failed when an\n  earlier cleanup failed.\n\nNew regression tests:\n- readiness timeout with a verified rollback, and with an unverified one\n- interrupt; readiness findings\n- foreign same-named container; listing failure during rollback\n- reset with container inspection failure, malformed network inspect,\n  listing failure, and unverified removal\n- canary network inspect failure and malformed output; unreadable\n  credential file\n- complete, absent and partial gate states\n- ci_smoke gate kept after a failed cleanup\n\nThe Docker fake lists, inspects and removes statefully, and refuses an\ninspect that no listing justified.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DU1CMkx64XgQPMRUBG4oUY\n\n* fix(dogfood): roll back on any exception after activation starts resources\n\nAn exception outside the expected set (for example a KeyError from a\nmalformed inspect document) now also goes through the verified rollback\nbefore it propagates. A rollback that itself hits any exception is\nreported as unverified, which keeps the gate and the evidence, and is\nnever read as success. Adds a regression test.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DU1CMkx64XgQPMRUBG4oUY\n\n* fix(dogfood): refuse a malformed Docker listing instead of reading it as absence\n\nFrom the re-review of 9a390d8: a listing line without a tab, without a\nfull 64-hex ID or without a name now refuses. Before, it could leave a\nnamed resource unmatched and so look absent. Adds tests for malformed,\nempty and multi-name listings.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DU1CMkx64XgQPMRUBG4oUY\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: IngeniousRiley <336672273+IngeniousRiley@users.noreply.github.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"acc09fc916001ca22f26c08ebdf100bca817164e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/acc09fc916001ca22f26c08ebdf100bca817164e"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/37966aa97a60ccc011ed7beadf4c02429f23e6ab","verification":{"payload":"tree acc09fc916001ca22f26c08ebdf100bca817164e\nparent 2429836aa1331acd603a5a5485254e75940de540\nauthor IngeniousRiley <riley@pukekos.co.nz> 1791397058 +1300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1791397058 +1300\n\nPrepare isolated disposable dogfood profile and gated verification (#101)\n\n* Prepare isolated disposable dogfood profile and gated runtime smoke\n\n* Read disposable operator password privately from verified terminal\n\n* chore(dogfood): pin the disposable profile to main bb864f4\n\nMoves the pinned source commit from 1f46788 to bb864f4 (main after #105,\n#106 and #129) in profile.toml, dogfood.py, the README and the workflow.\nBetween the two commits only console sources and roadmap documents\nchanged; the control-plane configuration schema, the console nginx.conf\nand both Dockerfiles are byte-identical, so the TOML schema mirror and the\nsecurity-header comparison stand unchanged.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DU1CMkx64XgQPMRUBG4oUY\n\n* docs(dogfood): runbook prerequisites, protected services and owned-resource table\n\nAdds to examples/disposable-dogfood/README.md, for #111:\n- an explicit scope statement: this profile is not WorkSpec, runtime or\n  tenant acceptance; off-host backup is not a disposable-trial gate;\n  activation needs separate authorization\n- the restricted SSH forwarding prerequisite (restrict, port-forwarding,\n  permitopen for 127.0.0.1:18780 and 127.0.0.1:18781, a refusing forced\n  command, identical local ports so the issuer 127.0.0.1:18781 matches) and\n  the sshd Match block that keeps remote forwarding off\n- the shared services the trial is protected from (Keycloak, OpenBao, Git,\n  the host daemon and host ports) and how each is protected\n- one table of resources, ceilings, port and digest ownership and the exact\n  owned cleanup for each\n\ndogfood.py gains RESOURCE_LIMITS (the compose ceilings, now validated as\nexact values rather than presence) and ssh_forward_options /\nssh_forward_command, which generate the two SSH lines the runbook quotes.\nscripts/tests/test_dogfood_runbook.py fails if the runbook''s sections or any\nvalue in its tables drift from profile.toml or dogfood.py. The canaries\ncommand the runbook names lands in the next commit.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DU1CMkx64XgQPMRUBG4oUY\n\n* feat(dogfood): fail-closed pre and post canaries with redacted receipts\n\nAdds `dogfood.py canaries --phase pre|post` for #111.\n\npre (needs --yes): read-only preconditions first -- daemon identity, a\nreal lock and a valid ownership receipt, the three recorded containers\nowned and running, the recorded network owned and internal with a gateway\ninside the profile subnet, the gate as the first rules of INPUT and\nDOCKER-USER in `iptables -S`, every host TCP listener from /proc/net/tcp\nand tcp6, and both loopback ports answering from the host. Only then one\nthrowaway probe (the pinned node builder base by digest, --rm, --pull\nnever, read-only, no capabilities, non-root, 64m/0.25 CPU/32 pids, logs\noff, project and canary labels) on the internal network: two positive\ncontrols must answer 200, and the bridge gateway on every observed and\ndeclared host port and both published ports, every specifically bound\nhost address, 1.1.1.1:53 and a public HTTPS URL must time out or have no\nroute or name. A refusal, a connect, a TLS error, or a missing, extra or\nunparseable result fails. A foreign container holding the probe name is\nrefused and never removed.\n\npost (read-only): no container, network or volume with the project\nlabel or this profile''s names, no fabric-dogfood0 interface, no iptables\nrule naming the bridge or subnet, no .out/.\n\nBoth phases write .canaries/canary-<phase>-<UTC>.json (0600, exclusive)\nwith the verdict, findings, full `iptables -S` and a reduced network\ninspect; the bootstrap admin credential is redacted and a receipt that\nstill contains it is refused. profile.toml gains protected_host_ports (a\nvalidated floor of shared-service ports); `build` pulls the probe image by\ndigest after checking the pinned console Dockerfile builds from it.\n\nscripts/tests/test_dogfood_canaries.py drives both phases through the CLI\nwith scripted Docker, iptables, /proc, /sys and loopback fakes: pass,\nfail (refused, connected, public reached, TLS reached, failed positive\ncontrols, gate absent or displaced, loopback down, stopped containers,\nforeign probe, leftovers after cleanup) and missing evidence (iptables or\n/proc unreadable, no gateway, probe image absent, empty, malformed,\npartial or extra probe output, probe exit 125). Nothing was executed\nagainst a real host.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DU1CMkx64XgQPMRUBG4oUY\n\n* feat(dogfood): offline activation packet bound to activate\n\nAdds `dogfood.py packet` for #111: one deterministic, offline document for\nthe authorizer -- source commit, the three locked image IDs, the Keycloak\nbase digest, the probe image digest, the daemon identity, the SHA-256 of\nevery rendered artefact, published and internal ports, the SSH key options,\nforward command and sshd restrictions, the five gate rules and their exact\ninverse, every container''s ceilings including the probe, the pre and post\ncanary plan, the activation sequence, cleanup and rollback, and the scope\nstatement -- ending in `packet-sha256:` over all of it. It makes no Docker\ncall, and prints nothing on stdout while `check` fails, while the lock is\nsynthetic, or once an activation exists.\n\n`activate` now requires `--packet-sha256` and refuses before any Docker\ncall unless it equals the digest of the packet the artefacts on disk\nproduce, so an approval cannot be carried to a rebuilt image or a\nre-rendered file.\n\nscripts/tests/test_dogfood_packet.py compares the packet for the shipped\nprofile and a synthetic lock against golden/dogfood_packet.md, checks the\nfingerprint, determinism and every approved value, and the refusals.\nExisting activate tests pass the digest; two new tests cover a missing,\nmalformed, wrong or stale digest.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DU1CMkx64XgQPMRUBG4oUY\n\n* test(dogfood): use a neutral sentinel for the canary redaction fixture\n\nCodeQL flagged clear-text storage of a password at\nscripts/tests/test_dogfood_canaries.py:136 (alert 8). The flow is a\nsynthetic test fixture only: the test wrote a password-named constant into\na temporary .ephemeral/keycloak.env so it could assert that canary receipts\nredact it. In dogfood.py no credential reaches a receipt:\nephemeral_secret_values reads every value in keycloak.env,\nwrite_canary_receipt redacts each one from the serialized receipt and\nrefuses to write if any survives.\n\nThe fixture is now a neutral sentinel written as the env file''s value; the\ntwo tests still assert it never appears in a written receipt, including\nwhen it is injected into the iptables evidence. No suppression and no\nCodeQL configuration change.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DU1CMkx64XgQPMRUBG4oUY\n\n* fix(dogfood): address adversarial review of canaries, packet and SSH guidance\n\nFrom the independent review of d7f6bf2:\n- The public URL canary now passes only on a failed name resolution. A\n  name that resolved and then timed out means Docker''s embedded resolver\n  forwarded the lookup out of the internal network, which is a leak, so it\n  fails. New failing case in the tests.\n- The sshd Match block (AllowTcpForwarding local, PermitListen none, ...)\n  is now required rather than advised. `port-forwarding` in authorized_keys\n  re-enables remote forwarding, which would let the key holder listen on\n  the execution host. The runbook and packet state the minimum OpenSSH\n  versions (7.2 for `restrict`, 7.8 for `PermitListen`); on an older\n  server the operator stops instead of loosening the setup.\n- The runbook and the code say why IPv6 host listeners are probed by port\n  on the IPv4 gateway: the sandbox network has IPv6 disabled, so the probe\n  has no IPv6 route to try.\n- The probe argv test now asserts the absence of every widening flag\n  (cap-add, device, sysctl, mounts, publish, namespaces, dns, entrypoint).\n\nDeclined, with reasons, in the PR thread: secret_store is already\nvalidated; gate ordering already refuses any rule above the gate (tested);\nthe probe image is addressed by digest, so its content cannot change.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DU1CMkx64XgQPMRUBG4oUY\n\n* fix(dogfood): fail closed on unverified cleanup and keep the gate until it is verified\n\nFixes both blockers Riley raised on a8ea954 (PR comment 6042335792), and\nthe same patterns wherever else they occurred.\n\nP2, errors treated as absence:\n- docker_inspect now returns None only when a successful container or\n  network listing lacks the reference (new docker_present). A listing that\n  fails raises. So does something that is listed but cannot be inspected or\n  parsed. Neither is ever read as \"already gone\".\n- remove_recorded lists the recorded IDs again after removing them and\n  refuses if any remains. reset therefore fails closed on inspection,\n  listing or removal failures, and keeps the ownership receipt and local\n  state until removal or absence is verified.\n- canaries: a keycloak.env that exists but cannot be read now refuses\n  instead of being treated as nothing to redact.\n\nP1, gate removal advised before cleanup was verified:\n- Every activation failure after the credential exists goes through one\n  rollback. That covers compose up, ownership verification, the port check,\n  the Keycloak readiness timeout, bootstrap and inspection errors,\n  readiness findings after bootstrap (no longer reported as \"activated\"),\n  and an interrupt. The rollback removes by recorded ID, or by provable\n  ownership when there is no receipt, then requires a listing to show\n  nothing with this profile''s names or recorded IDs.\n- Only then are the credential and receipt deleted, and gate removal\n  advised after `canaries --phase post`. Otherwise the output says\n  ROLLBACK NOT VERIFIED and KEEP the host gate, keeps the receipt and\n  credential, and names the recovery.\n- The post canary accepts the gate complete or absent, never partial, and\n  records which. The cleanup order in reset output, `enforcement`, the\n  packet and the README is now: reset (verified), post canary with the gate\n  in place, REMOVE lines, post canary again.\n- ci_smoke: the runner''s gate cleanup is kept and counted as failed when an\n  earlier cleanup failed.\n\nNew regression tests:\n- readiness timeout with a verified rollback, and with an unverified one\n- interrupt; readiness findings\n- foreign same-named container; listing failure during rollback\n- reset with container inspection failure, malformed network inspect,\n  listing failure, and unverified removal\n- canary network inspect failure and malformed output; unreadable\n  credential file\n- complete, absent and partial gate states\n- ci_smoke gate kept after a failed cleanup\n\nThe Docker fake lists, inspects and removes statefully, and refuses an\ninspect that no listing justified.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DU1CMkx64XgQPMRUBG4oUY\n\n* fix(dogfood): roll back on any exception after activation starts resources\n\nAn exception outside the expected set (for example a KeyError from a\nmalformed inspect document) now also goes through the verified rollback\nbefore it propagates. A rollback that itself hits any exception is\nreported as unverified, which keeps the gate and the evidence, and is\nnever read as success. Adds a regression test.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DU1CMkx64XgQPMRUBG4oUY\n\n* fix(dogfood): refuse a malformed Docker listing instead of reading it as absence\n\nFrom the re-review of 9a390d8: a listing line without a tab, without a\nfull 64-hex ID or without a name now refuses. Before, it could leave a\nnamed resource unmatched and so look absent. Adds tests for malformed,\nempty and multi-name listings.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DU1CMkx64XgQPMRUBG4oUY\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: IngeniousRiley <336672273+IngeniousRiley@users.noreply.github.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqxozCCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA1X0QAAWer6RXQjIquO+cUoVwRPLM\n53gnL9SamzdA58cn7+JaXy6yPBh0u4887v5M+6wMkZZ7TGGNoN2ptUE4WBXUuLCh\neFE/eX1i8IXZi4vjtbRvX+nTwMx8nMwqRm6xCEBwcRXV3U8ZqLZKKXM/qxpMyHuX\nikRS22HhCVDrUXEPwriZUyIuOKtqR34aEXvtZaidD/xPqOSkSF8xfNqP549FUh6d\n+9GqyW5MnULAgyxIAD5hOcbmuVc17Idab8bGM4MT8dqoNXNV2QpWPpaKLUGHbmjw\n4Kwcg7dXn8U/XH48MTaOFA6Ou/+g5YF1sJJx8pp4DS+tV8oNxAoL0/32gqmQ1x42\nNka7civFzlVAEC7gnFDyyzDL/KnOZyiuvgYgwGo/KzDOuw0ik/cgCwAI6ImQrwpi\nZtca16t3PK5cGh/vkviN239ySAJG7wgYAv9CGdfCt33CJpYZrdzJ85bgJvdflWNc\nQGk3Y7FqUXGSucRuHoQEJ/3uOo5g6fL7iaEMEuTuhmCQ9fYqpIzYn1Y+qHPnPR7E\ns9Kp6cJDcXgNtL6FeWY6DEBQhX0C3PPvCbVMrzPlv1CANJl0seSal8vDdPnDwgiU\ndKw9HWo4WxqKGur4YsT7L/ewE2qctMGIHy7b3fx7C8b1rzHpR9rjLUVjI9DAnI4s\nhQ0xP6400KMvzmKX65rN\n=Ix/Q\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-10-07T18:17:38Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/37966aa97a60ccc011ed7beadf4c02429f23e6ab","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKDM3OTY2YWE5N2E2MGNjYzAxMWVkN2JlYWRmNGMwMjQyOWYyM2U2YWI","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/2429836aa1331acd603a5a5485254e75940de540","sha":"2429836aa1331acd603a5a5485254e75940de540","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/2429836aa1331acd603a5a5485254e75940de540"}],"sha":"37966aa97a60ccc011ed7beadf4c02429f23e6ab","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/37966aa97a60ccc011ed7beadf4c02429f23e6ab"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/2429836aa1331acd603a5a5485254e75940de540/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-07T17:53:44Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-07T17:53:44Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"docs(publication): M2 publication gap report and tests pinning current atomicity behaviour (#112) (#131)\n\n* test(publication): pin cross-document atomicity, interruption and concurrency behaviour\n\nAdds tests for #112 that pin current behaviour rather than change it:\n- a reader applying new tenants before the data sources they name, and a\n  retirement before the unbinding that preceded it (mixed revisions);\n- an interrupted publication leaving the published set and readers at\n  mixed document revisions (filesystem and Kubernetes adapters);\n- overlapping writers publishing a dangling binding (write skew) and a\n  lost update at one revision, plus the resourceVersion positive control;\n- last-known-good lost on reader restart and a forward-incompatible\n  document stopping new readers.\n\nTests named current_behaviour_* pass because the gap exists; none are\nignored. No production code changes.\n\nRefs #112\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DU1CMkx64XgQPMRUBG4oUY\n\n* docs(publication): add M2 publication gap report against #112\n\nAudits fabric-runtime-publication, fabric-publication-kubernetes and the\nfabric-tenant-runtime reader against #112''s acceptance criteria: per-document\natomicity versus cross-document ordering, last-known-good behaviour and a\nreader-compatibility matrix. Each gap is classified as engineering or as\nwaiting on D01-1/D01-2.\n\nRefs #112\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DU1CMkx64XgQPMRUBG4oUY\n\n* docs(publication): apply adversarial review to the #112 gap report\n\n- Correct the publisher.rs single-flight citation (:85-88).\n- Add G8: catalogue and tenants are never cross-checked (UnboundDataSource),\n  a deliberate ADR 0018 part 4 choice, so changing it is D01-1.\n- Narrow G1a: a combined refresher does not help per-volume kubelet skew;\n  deferring unresolved bindings is a runtime-plane change.\n- Reclassify the Lease option (G4) and the pre-publication hold (G5) as\n  D01-1, scope the fail-closed claim to tested states, cite the caller\n  that exits on a failed prime, and mention HeldPayloadLost.\n- Make the G6 test distinguish a refused document from an accepted one.\n\nRefs #112\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DU1CMkx64XgQPMRUBG4oUY\n\n* test(publication): pin overlapping filesystem writers corrupting one document\n\nRiley''s review on #131 found that the gap report''s per-document atomicity\nclaim held only with a single writer. atomic_write stages every write of a\ntarget through one fixed sibling path, so two overlapping writers share one\nstaging inode. They can publish an empty file, or interleaved bytes that\nare not JSON.\n\n- Add two deterministic current_behaviour_* tests in\n  filesystem/atomic_write_tests.rs. Writer A runs write_and_sync and\n  atomic_write one statement at a time and writer B is the real\n  atomic_write, so neither test uses sleeps or threads. Neither is ignored.\n- Add gap G4a to the report, with file:line evidence, classified as\n  engineering: ADR 0018 part 5 requires a sibling temp file, not a\n  fixed name.\n- Make the per-document atomicity claims (summary, criteria table and\n  the atomicity table) state their single-writer condition. Make G3''s\n  recovery claim conditional on the held documents still parsing.\n\nRefs #112\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DU1CMkx64XgQPMRUBG4oUY\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"dd3e9b42e46027109d63df617f07408a9b3b6ca2","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/dd3e9b42e46027109d63df617f07408a9b3b6ca2"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/2429836aa1331acd603a5a5485254e75940de540","verification":{"payload":"tree dd3e9b42e46027109d63df617f07408a9b3b6ca2\nparent e478d7def533d3a1bc73dff79e1a48afdc9ed0e0\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1791395624 +1300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1791395624 +1300\n\ndocs(publication): M2 publication gap report and tests pinning current atomicity behaviour (#112) (#131)\n\n* test(publication): pin cross-document atomicity, interruption and concurrency behaviour\n\nAdds tests for #112 that pin current behaviour rather than change it:\n- a reader applying new tenants before the data sources they name, and a\n  retirement before the unbinding that preceded it (mixed revisions);\n- an interrupted publication leaving the published set and readers at\n  mixed document revisions (filesystem and Kubernetes adapters);\n- overlapping writers publishing a dangling binding (write skew) and a\n  lost update at one revision, plus the resourceVersion positive control;\n- last-known-good lost on reader restart and a forward-incompatible\n  document stopping new readers.\n\nTests named current_behaviour_* pass because the gap exists; none are\nignored. No production code changes.\n\nRefs #112\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DU1CMkx64XgQPMRUBG4oUY\n\n* docs(publication): add M2 publication gap report against #112\n\nAudits fabric-runtime-publication, fabric-publication-kubernetes and the\nfabric-tenant-runtime reader against #112''s acceptance criteria: per-document\natomicity versus cross-document ordering, last-known-good behaviour and a\nreader-compatibility matrix. Each gap is classified as engineering or as\nwaiting on D01-1/D01-2.\n\nRefs #112\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DU1CMkx64XgQPMRUBG4oUY\n\n* docs(publication): apply adversarial review to the #112 gap report\n\n- Correct the publisher.rs single-flight citation (:85-88).\n- Add G8: catalogue and tenants are never cross-checked (UnboundDataSource),\n  a deliberate ADR 0018 part 4 choice, so changing it is D01-1.\n- Narrow G1a: a combined refresher does not help per-volume kubelet skew;\n  deferring unresolved bindings is a runtime-plane change.\n- Reclassify the Lease option (G4) and the pre-publication hold (G5) as\n  D01-1, scope the fail-closed claim to tested states, cite the caller\n  that exits on a failed prime, and mention HeldPayloadLost.\n- Make the G6 test distinguish a refused document from an accepted one.\n\nRefs #112\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DU1CMkx64XgQPMRUBG4oUY\n\n* test(publication): pin overlapping filesystem writers corrupting one document\n\nRiley''s review on #131 found that the gap report''s per-document atomicity\nclaim held only with a single writer. atomic_write stages every write of a\ntarget through one fixed sibling path, so two overlapping writers share one\nstaging inode. They can publish an empty file, or interleaved bytes that\nare not JSON.\n\n- Add two deterministic current_behaviour_* tests in\n  filesystem/atomic_write_tests.rs. Writer A runs write_and_sync and\n  atomic_write one statement at a time and writer B is the real\n  atomic_write, so neither test uses sleeps or threads. Neither is ignored.\n- Add gap G4a to the report, with file:line evidence, classified as\n  engineering: ADR 0018 part 5 requires a sibling temp file, not a\n  fixed name.\n- Make the per-document atomicity claims (summary, criteria table and\n  the atomicity table) state their single-writer condition. Make G3''s\n  recovery claim conditional on the held documents still parsing.\n\nRefs #112\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DU1CMkx64XgQPMRUBG4oUY\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqxocoCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAucMQAHTWk1vPoJwdDcr8INMAfeDi\nINBFRJf/VKzgIGVImDhKdDJ0Lpj0kaAIpkQWbAjVVMyZBxBlM8XoLhbUEuYB2Z3v\nZrXzDs/dyc2aohxpbQKagTxn2jw428fQtknMfNqQES3/qGfPnsXvO4qngNeHd4/B\nLoh8ZWpg4OZZ/3arWfdLUc6bDngrYkNhXfc/tLsgPUe22swF06+9A/YxLFoRFvh4\nmp8WTJ4YixW/tJ5LkieMbcnnWsFhXJtL+WOzVm3jEX3ArCQa0JikO7ImZc0lOSL8\nAKSexsGtY4k3BDCKrZZK0810JBSo9XoP/K1aGH5PMbcbykpdE8x7nFphnEmdN635\nXRcEWM+u8CGOZjWe7qyaMc0C2Ujldpp8uth5GCsbvxXTZ3hVsxPlsWq9S5GiJEIE\nSvAc/lL6pibO+sVSFO6Eqwk/zcDUaxJSTyBvW8QAQ9/T0sLloHAt/L3UXFXz+fz3\nyVbKIfMoeLPh/IlOmKSQppYM2kMlPn8Mapzz8QoeXs8q7JME13gtaozDEJZToC/6\nj+92wTYJ7nS6sVftUMT/cWMI54VJDJclMOtUDViCNB/t06XN8FNEDMg927YJyvpf\nLEdkZU8MB42yfb2uM0bCatfBtlg3ajAwzR7xLw+A5tmoBpC3s2cE6ct7vIq9olA7\n+9DlAn/aoWscuhtbm2ad\n=YCnh\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-10-07T17:53:44Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_ur