nent descriptor''s digest. fabric-platform-git reads\nand writes components.yaml schema 2 and 3, refuses described under schema 2\nby name, and plan::apply is now an exhaustive match. The console gives each\ndiagnostic its own wording, invalid with its reason.\n\nAn evaluator example, the one the release will run, reads\nghcr.io/fieldstatenz/saas-fabric 0.3.0-preview.15 today and answers\nundescribed, as it should.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Registries become integrations an operator registers, read only at public addresses and with a credential only where it was registered\n\nSlice 3 of ADR 0026.\n\nfabric-registry builds a registry from settings: an HTTPS endpoint, the host\nit names repositories by, a realm rule for its kind (GHCR''s and Docker Hub''s\nrealms are fixed; a distribution registry''s is recorded when it is\nregistered; the deployment''s own follows its first challenge and then holds\nit), an optional credential presented only for the repositories registered\nunder it, and an address policy. Authentication follows WWW-Authenticate,\nsends a credential only to a realm the kind allows and never across origins,\nand a realm refusing it marks it refused so a sweep does not retry it into a\nlocked account. An operator''s registry is read only at public addresses,\nchecked after resolution on every connection and before any IP-literal hop.\nRegistries routes the Registry port by host and never falls through.\n\nThe control plane gains registries as integration state beside the Git\napplications'': records in the instance partition, credentials in the secret\nstore under a server-minted id, proven before recorded, audited, with their\nown errors and routes under /api/integrations/registries. The same router\nserves Platform Management. The console''s Integrations page gains an Image\nregistries section that shows what was proven, never \"connected\".\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* ci: trig