 F4 corrected; F3 (keyed update/delete) deferred to #67. The connector-acceptance CI job runs the suite in required mode against images pinned by digest and is the recorded proof.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)","tree":{"sha":"048a60f4109d841ce2d29814f1b3084894297192","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/048a60f4109d841ce2d29814f1b3084894297192"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/e47d13a42db54f3aa873b5359bdf9b70b652eaa2","verification":{"payload":"tree 048a60f4109d841ce2d29814f1b3084894297192\nparent bc1f58ccd4f00957dcdadc41d7ad2c176a3e1ef7\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1788673729 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788673729 +1200\n\nProve the NDC adapter and tenant isolation against a real running PostgreSQL connector (#66)\n\nCloses #62. Five slices plus four review rounds; the composed acceptance test drives the real Data API through the real NDC adapter to a real ndc-postgres v3.1.0 and postgres 16, two tenants on one physical table under discriminator isolation, with five mutations proved against the real database. Falsified assumptions F1, F2 and F4 corrected; F3 (keyed update/delete) deferred to #67. The connector-acceptance CI job runs the suite in required mode against images pinned by digest and is the recorded proof.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqnP7BCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAANvsQAHdeHHKAE8CcegKbhUUUBDrV\nS0lDV8kcPQz8Y+8XJ8cFZ/lMTwv5Rh7htHL3D4wnsScL27H0NHPSA/Nzc37prIrT\nu0kbI1ChOvlLyb1D9bdMARrC4/BjKveEl/2UZBu7HdbXN82HzjgbUl3qHhmRpOpo\nvS6aqTf8STX645yjnIX75gtRAhSNsLVHMN+y7HDEu0Vy55sKEDMrbAeSMVUpyITo\ng1GGJK65cn/IVfLOaI376f94n+cFBj5rMvTEHiiuFI3qAVDbPSCPsCOzFOUBh3Ye\nNYm+9BaoAyWXWc+aCqNhp1Qjs1U/XwdxE/XJIoR0etwVxkL2qhHXgSZw7qj3KqM5\nAJCR4jHNI2naPY7zKa+TmdQ3velg1AGEX6j27LY1LJqpJXdY0b73mO6QS9iZofJg\nSbnzA7zKIHTikgwsKMx8q4oF0cUnBN9+UKZm/vyWdwVK8Yldkn5nqJWi3RDcI4CN\nk9Nm5T5rDgKRy7cENNf9jHoX9CRat4EL3jk0/gLq1uUmsSaupBYHu6PIdhc3VvZf\nCLY9X/mDNo43JRZUutDcAYD2CtNXQN6Efy4SkasSHGQqmrilGRP6oWJiTfk2iZPF\n/KVzlBGyOgpDvj0Uze4DML70u6xW+P+JBP0tjCqWXmbjnG8MXnmzyqXz+jVKHIEN\nfL/j/5crOftgQKUMVI1J\n=+mtP\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-06T05:48:49Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/e47d13a42db54f3aa873b5359bdf9b70b652eaa2","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKGU0N2QxM2E0MmRiNTRmM2FhODczYjUzNTliZGY5YjcwYjY1MmVhYTI","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/bc1f58ccd4f00957dcdadc41d7ad2c176a3e1ef7","sha":"bc1f58ccd4f00957dcdadc41d7ad2c176a3e1ef7","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/bc1f58ccd4f00957dcdadc41d7ad2c176a3e1ef7"}],"sha":"e47d13a42db54f3aa873b5359bdf9b70b652eaa2","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/e47d13a42db54f3aa873b5359bdf9b70b652eaa2"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/bc1f58ccd4f00957dcdadc41d7ad2c176a3e1ef7/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-05T23:47:41Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-05T23:47:41Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Publish runtime state as three versioned documents, and prove two tenants through the real consumer (#65)\n\n* Publish runtime state as three independently versioned documents\n\nAdd ADR 0018 for issue #60 (M1). The runtime plane''s tenant, DataSource\nand catalogue registries are complete and tested but sit unfed: nothing\nwrites tenants.json, data-sources.json or catalog.json. This decision\nanswers how a producer publishes them without sharing a Rust type with\nthe runtime (the two planes must not touch) and without inventing\nphysical placement from client intent, which ADR 0007 forbids.\n\nA new neither-plane crate, fabric-runtime-publication, owns its own\nwire-contract types, validated through the same identifier rules the\nruntime uses and kept honest by deny_unknown_fields plus a composed\nacceptance test. Each of the three documents is a full snapshot with\nits own DocumentRevision, written atomically (payload before manifest)\nand refused outright on a stale, divergent, or unintended-emptying\npublication. DataSource retirement is enforced against the currently\nheld tenants document, closing the ordering window rather than just\ndocumenting it. The missing input — per-tenant provisioner output — is\nnamed (ProvisionedPlacement) rather than faked.\n\nThis is an ADR only: no code changes, and the four downstream workers\n(the platform repo, the Kubernetes adapter, control-plane adoption, and\nthe Synthesis Cloud isolation seam) get an explicit list of what they\nmay rely on without re-deriving it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Declare the runtime''s publication contract before anything writes to it\n\nfabric-tenant-runtime and fabric-data-api already know how to consume\ntenants.json, data-sources.json, and catalog.json -- the reconciled-resource\nlifecycle, the divergent-payload guard, the last-good snapshot on a failed\nload. Nothing writes those files yet, and the producer cannot reuse the\nconsumer''s own types without putting a control-plane crate in the runtime\nplane''s graph.\n\nAdd fabric-runtime-publication, in neither plane, depending on nothing but\nfabric-core. It declares its own copy of every wire shape -- the three\ndocument types, DocumentManifest, and the identifiers that live in the\nruntime plane and had to be re-declared over the same parse functions -- and\nrenders them as deterministic, two-space-indented JSON so publishing the same\nsnapshot twice produces identical bytes. Fidelity with the consumer is pinned\nby round-trip tests, not a shared type: this crate''s own canonical JSON\ndeserialises as the real TenantRuntimeBinding, DataSource, and\nResourceCatalog, and the shipped examples parse as this crate''s own types\nwithout being regenerated.\n\nNo port and no filesystem adapter yet -- this is the contract, not the\nwriter.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Make plane separation transitive, and fence the publisher off\n\ncheck_dependency_direction already pins every crate''s declared edges, but\nthat table is hand-maintained: a crate in neither plane could depend on one\nplane while something in the other plane depended on it, and no single edge\nin `expected` would ever cross the line for a reviewer to notice. ADR 0018\nnames this gap and closes it with two checks computed straight from the\nplane sets.\n\nGraph now keeps each internal dependency''s kind (normal, dev, build) instead\nof collapsing them, so a new `non_dev_dependencies` sits beside the existing\ndev-inclusive `direct_dependencies` without changing it -- every check that\nrelied on the old behaviour, `check_the_planes_do_not_meet` included, is\nuntouched.\n\nTwo additive invariants:\n\n- `check_plane_reachability_is_transitive`: every crate''s non-dev dependency\n  closure touches at most one plane. Catches a production edge that bridges\n  the planes through a crate in neither one, which the direct-edge check\n  cannot see two hops away.\n- `check_runtime_plane_cannot_reach_the_publisher`: no crate in RUNTIME_PLANE\n  may reach fabric-runtime-publication over any table, dev included. \"Exactly\n  one writer\" is an RBAC guarantee today; this makes it structural, so a\n  runtime binary can never even name the crate that would let it become a\n  second one.\n\nVerified both fire: a non-dev bridge from fabric-runtime-publication into\nfabric-client-model trips the first, and a dev-dependency from\nfabric-tenant-runtime onto fabric-runtime-publication trips the second\n(transitively, for every runtime-plane crate that depends on it too). Both\nproof edits were reverted; the tree carries only the two checks and the doc\nupdate.\n\n11 architectural invariants now hold across 21 crates.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Give the runtime publication contract a port, a verdict, and a filesystem writer\n\nfabric-runtime-publication gains what ADR 0018 parts 2-6 specify but did not\nyet build: the RuntimePublication port (current/publish/describe), a\nRuntimeSnapshot that offers all three documents with a per-document Emptying\nintent, and a pure verdict() over the presence and revision tables so every\nrefusal row is a unit test rather than a claim in a doc comment. The\nwhole-snapshot guards -- referential integrity, retirement against the held\ntenants document, unintended emptying, the unconditional empty-catalogue\nrefusal -- run before any verdict is computed, and every verdict is resolved\nbefore FilesystemRuntimePublication writes a single byte.\n\nThe adapter writes data sources, then the catalogue, then tenants, each\npayload before its manifest, via a sibling temp file that is fsync''d and\nrenamed over the target -- so the target path is only ever produced by that\nrename, and the temp file never survives a call, success or failure. std::fs\nrather than tokio::fs, because this crate''s tokio dependency carries no `fs`\nfeature and the adapter runs at most on a poll interval, not a request path.\n\nSixteen tests drive the adapter over a real temporary directory, including\none that proves JsonFileSource reads the files this adapter writes as the\nruntime''s own TenantRuntimeBinding and DataSource. Nine more pin the verdict\nand guard functions without touching a filesystem at all.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Validate collection and schema names, and refuse a stale revision when the payload is gone\n\nTwo blocking gaps in the runtime publication contract: `collection` and\n`schema` were plain `String`, unlike every other identifier this crate\nre-declares, even though ADR 0018''s Decision part 1 names `CollectionName`\nalongside `ConnectorId`, `ConnectionName`, and `FieldName` as one the producer\nmust validate itself. A value the producer accepted unchecked could fail the\nconsumer''s own parse at startup -- for the catalogue, stopping the process\nfrom starting. Add `CollectionName` and `SchemaName` to `src/ids/`, in the\nsame shape as the three newtypes already there, and use them at both sites.\n\nThe crate''s own docs had drifted from what they described: `docs/CONTEXT.md`\nclaimed the ADR never named `CollectionName` for re-declaration (it does);\n`docs/README.md` claimed every identifier goes through\n`fabric_core::naming::{parse_dns_label, parse_identifier}` (this crate never\ncalls the former, and `secrets`/`credentials`/`Secret::reference` go through\nneither). `scripts/check_architecture.py`''s `expected` table pre-authorised\ntwo production edges -- to `fabric-connector` and `fabric-identity` -- that\nnothing in this crate uses; reduced to the three real edges, with the\njustification for the extras removed from both the script and\n`docs/CONTEXT.md`. `docs/architecture/crate-dependencies.md` claimed\n`fabric-core` was, until this crate, the only one answering to neither plane;\nit was never the only one, and `fabric-git-host` proves \"neither plane\" was\nnever the same claim as \"does no I/O\" -- corrected, keeping the ADR''s\ndistinction intact.\n\nNon-blocking findings, taken together: the round-trip tests now loop every\nisolation and connection variant, not just one; the shipped example corpus is\nnow carried through the whole fidelity path -- re-rendered through the\ncanonical serialiser and re-parsed as the consumer''s own types, not just read\nonce by the producer''s. `CatalogDocument` gains `resources()` and `get()`.\n`DocumentManifest::new` stamps `CONTRACT_VERSION` in one place, and\n`DocumentKind::{payload_file, manifest_file}` are what the filesystem adapter\nnow builds every path from, instead of the literals it used to close over.\n`TenantBindingDocument.data` becomes `TenantDataBindings`, which refuses an\nempty map at construction while still deserialising one the way the consumer\ndoes.\n\nFrom the S2 report: `verdict()`''s \"manifest held, payload missing\" row wrote\nunconditionally, without checking the incoming revision against the held\none -- so a stale republication with no payload to compare against would\nhave been accepted. The revision check now runs before the missing-payload\nbranch, so an older revision is still refused. `atomic_write` now `fsync`s\nthe containing directory after every rename, so the rename itself survives a\ncrash rather than only the bytes it points at -- the temp-file-gone-on-every-\npath property is unchanged.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Serve two tenants from the files the publisher actually wrote\n\nThe composed acceptance test ADR 0018 exists for: publish a fixture\nthrough the real FilesystemRuntimePublication, then build the real\nbuild_runtime (over the real JsonFileSource) and the real\nbuild_data_api (over the real ResourceCatalog, deserialised straight\nfrom the published catalog.json since fabric-api''s own\nstartup::catalog::load is pub(super)) and drive both with bearer\ntokens for two tenants sharing one discriminator-isolated DataSource.\n\nThe recording connector applies the predicate it receives to a shared\ncorpus instead of dispatching on tenant identity, so a missing or\nwrong predicate breaks the isolation tests instead of passing by\ncoincidence. Producer-side refusals (stale revision, divergent\npayload, a dangling DataSource, an unintended emptying) and the\nconsumer''s own survival of a malformed published document are covered\nalongside the happy path; every test that depends on a background\nrefresh polls a bounded, real window rather than trusting a single\nsleep, since refresh_now only notifies.\n\nAdds fabric-identity and fabric-connector as dev-dependencies of\nfabric-runtime-publication for token minting and the recording\nconnector, and records both edges in check_architecture.py''s expected\ntable -- the publisher fence (no runtime-plane crate may reach\nfabric-runtime-publication) still holds.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Fail closed when a held manifest outlives its payload\n\nTwo review findings on the publication port and its filesystem adapter were\nblocking. filesystem/held.rs mapped an absent payload to Vec::new()\nregardless of whether a manifest was held beside it, so the tenants\ndocument''s emptying guard and the retirement guard -- both of which read the\nheld tenants document -- silently disarmed whenever that payload was lost:\na snapshot with zero tenants at the held revision would write [] with no\nEmptying::Intended, and a data source still bound to a real tenant could be\nretired out from under it. A held manifest proves something was published;\nan absent payload means the content is lost, not that nothing was. The\ntenants document is now parsed through parse_held_tenants, which\ndistinguishes all three states -- no manifest (never published, no\nconstraint), manifest and payload (parse and check), manifest without\npayload (refuse the whole publication as the new PublicationError::\nHeldPayloadLost, whose message tells the operator to restore the payload or\nremove the manifest). Data sources'' and the catalogue''s own held payloads\nare left on the existing lenient parse_documents, now with a rustdoc\nexplaining why that asymmetry is safe: their held state gates only their\nown verdict, and a manifest-without-payload there is already an\nunconditional Write (ADR 0018 part 6) that any republish recovers from --\nproved for both by\na_held_manifest_without_its_payload_is_republishable_at_the_same_revision\nand its new catalogue-side twin. Two new integration tests drive the\nemptying and retirement guards through a lost tenants payload and confirm\nboth now refuse with HeldPayloadLost.\n\nSecond, errors.rs and report.rs both claimed a publication is either\nrefused with nothing written or fully applied -- false for Unwritable.\npublish() writes data sources, then the catalogue, then tenants as\nsequential file operations; an I/O failure between two of them leaves\nearlier documents written and the rest untouched. That guarantee is now\nscoped correctly to every other variant (StaleRevision, DivergentPayload,\nDanglingDataSource, RetiredDataSourceStillBound, EmptyingNotIntended,\nEmptyCatalogue, EmptyTenantData, HeldPayloadLost, and Unreadable, which can\nonly fire before a write is planned), Unwritable is documented as the one\nexception, and docs/README.md gains a \"Partial writes across documents\"\nsection explaining why retrying the same call at the same revisions always\nfinishes the job. a_publication_that_failed_between_documents_is_completed_\nby_the_next_one proves it: obstruct the catalogue''s own write so data\nsources land and the catalogue fails before tenants is attempted, then\nrepublish unobstructed and see Unchanged, Written, Written.\n\nTaken together with the blocking pair, twelve non-blocking findings from\nthe same review:\n\n- An incoming tenant binding with an empty data map -- reachable only\n  through Deserialize -- is now refused by refuse_empty_tenant_data,\n  symmetric with refuse_empty_catalogue, instead of being silently dropped\n  by the consumer on arrival.\n- PlacementClassDocument''s round-trip test now loops all six variants\n  through fabric_tenant_runtime::PlacementClass instead of asserting one\n  variant against itself.\n- current() gains a test covering all three held-manifest presence states.\n- verdict_tests.rs''s duplicate \"orphaned payload\" test is gone; a comment on\n  its surviving neighbour points at the integration test that actually\n  proves that row against a file.\n- atomic_write::sync_directory is #[cfg(unix)], with a documented no-op\n  elsewhere -- opening a directory to fsync it is not portable, and\n  docs/README.md now says the extra rename-durability is Unix-only.\n- Stale \"future slice\" rustdoc in canonical.rs, document_revision.rs, and\n  docs/README.md is corrected now that the guard has shipped; Verdict::\n  Write''s doc no longer claims the manifest is written \"when it changes\"\n  when write_if_needed writes both unconditionally.\n- filesystem/held.rs''s parsing logic moved into its own file, filesystem/\n  parse.rs -- turning held bytes into typed documents is a different concern\n  from reading them off disk, and it is also where parse_held_tenants above\n  belongs. held.rs still needed a stated reason for the 121-150 line band\n  it remained in after the split.\n- The five hand-copied identifier newtypes (ConnectorId, ConnectionName,\n  FieldName, CollectionName, SchemaName) now come from one\n  identifier_newtype! macro declared in ids.rs, mirroring\n  fabric_connector::ids::identifier_newtype, which this crate cannot depend\n  on across the plane boundary. Same public API, serde attributes, and KIND\n  strings.\n- A held manifest''s own document field is now checked against the kind of\n  file it was read from and refused as Unreadable on a mismatch, instead of\n  being read and ignored.\n- DocumentManifest''s fields are private with accessors, so a wrong\n  contract_version can no longer be built by literal; serde still reads and\n  writes the fields directly. DocumentKind::payload_file() is removed: it\n  had no production caller -- DocumentPaths derives only the manifest name\n  from the kind, deliberately leaving the payload path exactly as the\n  caller supplied it, so a caller naming it something other than\n  tenants.json still works. This corrects 686f2fb''s own commit message,\n  which claimed the adapter built every path from payload_file and\n  manifest_file; it never did.\n- The rename-and-sibling integration test is renamed to what it actually\n  proves -- temp-file cleanup on both the success and failure path -- and\n  the three overlapping no-op tests are collapsed into the one that already\n  covered all three properties.\n- lib.rs''s \"What this crate owns\" now says the composed acceptance test\n  through the real runtime and Data API lives under tests/, without writing\n  it -- another slice is adding it.\n\nThe JSON wire shape of every document is unchanged. docs/CONTEXT.md is\nuntouched: a concurrent slice is appending to it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Prove the runtime publication guards by mutation, and catch the docs up\n\nTen mutations run against fabric-runtime-publication in this worktree,\nfollowing docs/delivery.md''s standard: mutate, watch the named test fail,\nrevert, keep the test. Nine caught what they were built to catch; one\n(zeroing a discriminator value through the constant the fixture''s own\ncorpus also reads) left everything green, because that constant feeds both\nsides of the isolation check it was meant to break -- the same class of\nblind spot delivery.md already names. The other near-miss, decision 22''s\nprediction that removing atomic rename might leave nothing failing, did not\nhold: three tests still caught it. Both are recorded as findings rather than\nsilently fixed.\n\nAlso corrects what the crate''s own docs had fallen behind on: CONTEXT.md\nstill named a removed `payload_file()`, undercounted validate.rs''s guards\nand PublicationError''s variants, and placed `parse_documents` in the wrong\nmodule; the control-plane architecture''s \"Runtime publication boundary\"\nsection still said the seam \"belongs in fabric-reconciliation or a\nsibling\", which ADR 0018 supersedes; crate-dependencies.md described the\ncomposed test''s two extra dev-dependencies as a later slice''s future work\nwhen they are already in the graph; and verification.md''s Gates table and\narchitecture-invariant count were stale from before this crate existed.\nThe Gates table now carries real numbers from a run in this worktree at\n9e7c83b, noted as such.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Give the recording connector a corpus that does not follow the fixture\n\nMutation 1a in the verification record zeroed a discriminator constant and\nleft all thirteen composed tests green, because the same constant fed both\nthe published binding and the connector''s rows: the mutation moved both\nsides together. The corpus is now the database''s own truth, written as\nliterals, so a change to what is published is a change to one side only --\nand the same mutation now fails seven of thirteen.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Read a payload as held content even when its manifest is missing\n\nBLOCKING: filesystem/parse.rs''s parse_held_tenants mapped a missing manifest\nto Vec::new() regardless of whether a payload sat beside it -- exactly the\nshipped examples/*.json shape. With a populated tenants.json and no\ntenants.manifest.json, the emptying guard read the held set as empty, so an\noffered tenants: [] at Emptying::NotIntended was accepted and written\nstraight over a populated file. Renamed to parse_held_documents and made\ngeneric: the no-manifest arm now parses whatever payload is present\n(parse_documents(payload, document)) instead of discarding it, while a\nmanifest without its payload still refuses as HeldPayloadLost. The module\ndoc''s three-state table is corrected, four new unit tests pin the four\n(manifest, payload) combinations -- including one with T = u64 proving the\nbytes are actually parsed, not merely defaulted -- and a new integration\ntest, a_populated_tenants_payload_with_no_manifest_still_guards_against_\nemptying, seeds a directory the shipped-examples way and checks every one\nof the six canonical files is byte- and inode-identical after the refusal.\n\nData sources get the same protection: their own emptying guard reads held\ndata-sources content the identical way, so a lost data-sources payload with\na held manifest disarmed it too. adapter.rs''s publish() now parses\nheld_data_sources through parse_held_documents as well, passing its\nmanifest. HeldPayloadLost''s rustdoc in errors.rs now names both documents\nand says why the catalogue is exempt: it is only ever byte-compared inside\nverdict, never parsed for a guard, so it can never be lost this way. The\nexisting data-sources \"manifest without payload\" test, which asserted the\nold republishable behaviour, is renamed to\na_held_data_sources_manifest_without_its_payload_is_refused_as_held_payload_\nlost and now expects the refusal; a new twin test,\nan_emptying_publication_is_refused_when_the_held_data_sources_payload_is_\nlost, proves Emptying::Intended never even gets considered once the payload\nis gone. The catalogue''s own lost-payload test keeps its old assertion, with\nits comment now explaining why it alone is unaffected.\n\nNon-blocking findings from the same review:\n\n- a_failed_refresh_leaves_the_runtime_serving_the_last_good_snapshot and\n  a_malformed_published_document_does_not_deprovision_the_tenants_already_\n  serving held assertions across a fixed 500ms window without ever proving\n  a refresh ran. tests/support/counting_source.rs adds CountingSource<T>, a\n  ResourceSource decorator that counts every load() call (the Err path\n  included) by delegating to the real JsonFileSource; build_stack now\n  builds the runtime over it and Stack exposes the two counters. Both tests\n  now record the count, write the malformed bytes, call refresh_now, poll\n  (bounded 2s) for the count to advance, and only then assert -- replacing\n  hold_across, which is now dead and removed.\n- an_emptying_publication_is_refused_unless_it_is_intended now captures the\n  six canonical files'' inode+mtime before the refused call and asserts them\n  unchanged after, with the comment pointing at that assertion instead of\n  only the runtime''s continued 200s.\n- the_same_logical_article_key_reaches_a_different_predicate_for_each_tenant\n  now also asserts each tenant''s clauses do not contain the other tenant''s\n  predicate, matching the absence proof\n  each_call_reaches_the_connector_carrying_only_its_own_tenant_predicate\n  already gives by equality.\n- port.rs''s publish rustdoc now lists EmptyTenantData and the (now\n  dual-document) HeldPayloadLost among what is refused.\n- validate.rs''s module doc corrected from \"six\" to \"five\" guard functions.\n- errors.rs''s band-reason comment now says the file is one variant from the\n  150-line ceiling, and names the two ways out -- trim rustdoc to what a\n  caller acts on, or find a real second concept -- rather than an\n  exemption; rewrapped elsewhere in the same file so the line count does\n  not move.\n- filesystem_runtime_publication.rs and tests/support/mod.rs gain\n  #![cfg(unix)]: the atomic-write guarantee they prove is itself\n  Unix-only, and so is std::os::unix::fs::MetadataExt, which both already\n  used. published_state_serves_two_tenants.rs gets the same gate as a\n  consequence -- its support module now vanishes on non-Unix, so the whole\n  suite that depends on it must too.\n\nThe JSON wire shape of every document is unchanged.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Say what is held by the payload, not the manifest, and close the two mutation findings\n\nThe README and CONTEXT described the lost-payload rule as tenants-only and\ncalled a payload with no manifest \"nothing published\"; the code now reads a\npresent payload as held content whichever way the manifest is, for tenants\nand data sources alike, and only byte-compares the catalogue. The\nverification record''s two findings are marked closed: the connector''s corpus\nno longer follows the fixture constant, so the zeroed-discriminator mutation\nfails seven of thirteen, and the adapter suite has a direct emptying test.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Count a load once it has finished, and record the mutations as rerun\n\nThe counting source counted a load at entry, so a poll could return while\nthe failed load was still in flight; it counts on completion now. The\nconnector''s globex row and its id are literals for the same reason its acme\nrow is. The parse of held content has one body instead of two. The README\nsays which checks are manifest-gated -- staleness and divergence both -- and\nwarns that a first publication over a seeded directory arms the retirement\nguard. The verification record carries the observed failure lines for the\ntwo mutations that were findings, both rerun.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"1dba2fa34b479d7802d8f3d70d18fa558abd3839","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/1dba2fa34b479d7802d8f3d70d18fa558abd3839"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/bc1f58ccd4f00957dcdadc41d7ad2c176a3e1ef7","verification":{"payload":"tree 1dba2fa34b479d7802d8f3d70d18fa558abd3839\nparent f9c5abf1c54da903bc3b2aa054fbfc393ef0f516\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1788652061 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788652061 +1200\n\nPublish runtime state as three versioned documents, and prove two tenants through the real consumer (#65)\n\n* Publish runtime state as three independently versioned documents\n\nAdd ADR 0018 for issue #60 (M1). The runtime plane''s tenant, DataSource\nand catalogue registries are complete and tested but sit unfed: nothing\nwrites tenants.json, data-sources.json or catalog.json. This decision\nanswers how a producer publishes them without sharing a Rust type with\nthe runtime (the two planes must not touch) and without inventing\nphysical placement from client intent, which ADR 0007 forbids.\n\nA new neither-plane crate, fabric-runtime-publication, owns its own\nwire-contract types, validated through the same identifier rules the\nruntime uses and kept honest by deny_unknown_fields plus a composed\nacceptance test. Each of the three documents is a full snapshot with\nits own DocumentRevision, written atomically (payload before manifest)\nand refused outright on a stale, divergent, or unintended-emptying\npublication. DataSource retirement is enforced against the currently\nheld tenants document, closing the ordering window rather than just\ndocumenting it. The missing input — per-tenant provisioner output — is\nnamed (ProvisionedPlacement) rather than faked.\n\nThis is an ADR only: no code changes, and the four downstream workers\n(the platform repo, the Kubernetes adapter, control-plane adoption, and\nthe Synthesis Cloud isolation seam) get an explicit list of what they\nmay rely on without re-deriving it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Declare the runtime''s publication contract before anything writes to it\n\nfabric-tenant-runtime and fabric-data-api already know how to consume\ntenants.json, data-sources.json, and catalog.json -- the reconciled-resource\nlifecycle, the divergent-payload guard, the last-good snapshot on a failed\nload. Nothing writes those files yet, and the producer cannot reuse the\nconsumer''s own types without putting a control-plane crate in the runtime\nplane''s graph.\n\nAdd fabric-runtime-publication, in neither plane, depending on nothing but\nfabric-core. It declares its own copy of every wire shape -- the three\ndocument types, DocumentManifest, and the identifiers that live in the\nruntime plane and had to be re-declared over the same parse functions -- and\nrenders them as deterministic, two-space-indented JSON so publishing the same\nsnapshot twice produces identical bytes. Fidelity with the consumer is pinned\nby round-trip tests, not a shared type: this crate''s own canonical JSON\ndeserialises as the real TenantRuntimeBinding, DataSource, and\nResourceCatalog, and the shipped examples parse as this crate''s own types\nwithout being regenerated.\n\nNo port and no filesystem adapter yet -- this is the contract, not the\nwriter.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Make plane separation transitive, and fence the publisher off\n\ncheck_dependency_direction already pins every crate''s declared edges, but\nthat table is hand-maintained: a crate in neither plane could depend on one\nplane while something in the other plane depended on it, and no single edge\nin `expected` would ever cross the line for a reviewer to notice. ADR 0018\nnames this gap and closes it with two checks computed straight from the\nplane sets.\n\nGraph now keeps each internal dependency''s kind (normal, dev, build) instead\nof collapsing them, so a new `non_dev_dependencies` sits beside the existing\ndev-inclusive `direct_dependencies` without changing it -- every check that\nrelied on the old behaviour, `check_the_planes_do_not_meet` included, is\nuntouched.\n\nTwo additive invariants:\n\n- `check_plane_reachability_is_transitive`: every crate''s non-dev dependency\n  closure touches at most one plane. Catches a production edge that bridges\n  the planes through a crate in neither one, which the direct-edge check\n  cannot see two hops away.\n- `check_runtime_plane_cannot_reach_the_publisher`: no crate in RUNTIME_PLANE\n  may reach fabric-runtime-publication over any table, dev included. \"Exactly\n  one writer\" is an RBAC guarantee today; this makes it structural, so a\n  runtime binary can never even name the crate that would let it become a\n  second one.\n\nVerified both fire: a non-dev bridge from fabric-runtime-publication into\nfabric-client-model trips the first, and a dev-dependency from\nfabric-tenant-runtime onto fabric-runtime-publication trips the second\n(transitively, for every runtime-plane crate that depends on it too). Both\nproof edits were reverted; the tree carries only the two checks and the doc\nupdate.\n\n11 architectural invariants now hold across 21 crates.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Give the runtime publication contract a port, a verdict, and a filesystem writer\n\nfabric-runtime-publication gains what ADR 0018 parts 2-6 specify but did not\nyet build: the RuntimePublication port (current/publish/describe), a\nRuntimeSnapshot that offers all three documents with a per-document Emptying\nintent, and a pure verdict() over the presence and revision tables so every\nrefusal row is a unit test rather than a claim in a doc comment. The\nwhole-snapshot guards -- referential integrity, retirement against the held\ntenants document, unintended emptying, the unconditional empty-catalogue\nrefusal -- run before any verdict is computed, and every verdict is resolved\nbefore FilesystemRuntimePublication writes a single byte.\n\nThe adapter writes data sources, then the catalogue, then tenants, each\npayload before its manifest, via a sibling temp file that is fsync''d and\nrenamed over the target -- so the target path is only ever produced by that\nrename, and the temp file never survives a call, success or failure. std::fs\nrather than tokio::fs, because this crate''s tokio dependency carries no `fs`\nfeature and the adapter runs at most on a poll interval, not a request path.\n\nSixteen tests drive the adapter over a real temporary directory, including\none that proves JsonFileSource reads the files this adapter writes as the\nruntime''s own TenantRuntimeBinding and DataSource. Nine more pin the verdict\nand guard functions without touching a filesystem at all.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Validate collection and schema names, and refuse a stale revision when the payload is gone\n\nTwo blocking gaps in the runtime publication contract: `collection` and\n`schema` were plain `String`, unlike every other identifier this crate\nre-declares, even though ADR 0018''s Decision part 1 names `CollectionName`\nalongside `ConnectorId`, `ConnectionName`, and `FieldName` as one the producer\nmust validate itself. A value the producer accepted unchecked could fail the\nconsumer''s own parse at startup -- for the catalogue, stopping the process\nfrom starting. Add `CollectionName` and `SchemaName` to `src/ids/`, in the\nsame shape as the three newtypes already there, and use them at both sites.\n\nThe crate''s own docs had drifted from what they described: `docs/CONTEXT.md`\nclaimed the ADR never named `CollectionName` for re-declaration (it does);\n`docs/README.md` claimed every identifier goes through\n`fabric_core::naming::{parse_dns_label, parse_identifier}` (this crate never\ncalls the former, and `secrets`/`credentials`/`Secret::reference` go through\nneither). `scripts/check_architecture.py`''s `expected` table pre-authorised\ntwo production edges -- to `fabric-connector` and `fabric-identity` -- that\nnothing in this crate uses; reduced to the three real edges, with the\njustification for the extras removed from both the script and\n`docs/CONTEXT.md`. `docs/architecture/crate-dependencies.md` claimed\n`fabric-core` was, until this crate, the only one answering to neither plane;\nit was never the only one, and `fabric-git-host` proves \"neither plane\" was\nnever the same claim as \"does no I/O\" -- corrected, keeping the ADR''s\ndistinction intact.\n\nNon-blocking findings, taken together: the round-trip tests now loop every\nisolation and connection variant, not just one; the shipped example corpus is\nnow carried through the whole fidelity path -- re-rendered through the\ncanonical serialiser and re-parsed as the consumer''s own types, not just read\nonce by the producer''s. `CatalogDocument` gains `resources()` and `get()`.\n`DocumentManifest::new` stamps `CONTRACT_VERSION` in one place, and\n`DocumentKind::{payload_file, manifest_file}` are what the filesystem adapter\nnow builds every path from, instead of the literals it used to close over.\n`TenantBindingDocument.data` becomes `TenantDataBindings`, which refuses an\nempty map at construction while still deserialising one the way the consumer\ndoes.\n\nFrom the S2 report: `verdict()`''s \"manifest held, payload missing\" row wrote\nunconditionally, without checking the incoming revision against the held\none -- so a stale republication with no payload to compare against would\nhave been accepted. The revision check now runs before the missing-payload\nbranch, so an older revision is still refused. `atomic_write` now `fsync`s\nthe containing directory after every rename, so the rename itself survives a\ncrash rather than only the bytes it points at -- the temp-file-gone-on-every-\npath property is unchanged.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Serve two tenants from the files the publisher actually wrote\n\nThe composed acceptance test ADR 0018 exists for: publish a fixture\nthrough the real FilesystemRuntimePublication, then build the real\nbuild_runtime (over the real JsonFileSource) and the real\nbuild_data_api (over the real ResourceCatalog, deserialised straight\nfrom the published catalog.json since fabric-api''s own\nstartup::catalog::load is pub(super)) and drive both with bearer\ntokens for two tenants sharing one discriminator-isolated DataSource.\n\nThe recording connector applies the predicate it receives to a shared\ncorpus instead of dispatching on tenant identity, so a missing or\nwrong predicate breaks the isolation tests instead of passing by\ncoincidence. Producer-side refusals (stale revision, divergent\npayload, a dangling DataSource, an unintended emptying) and the\nconsumer''s own survival of a malformed published document are covered\nalongside the happy path; every test that depends on a background\nrefresh polls a bounded, real window rather than trusting a single\nsleep, since refresh_now only notifies.\n\nAdds fabric-identity and fabric-connector as dev-dependencies of\nfabric-runtime-publication for token minting and the recording\nconnector, and records both edges in check_architecture.py''s expected\ntable -- the publisher fence (no runtime-plane crate may reach\nfabric-runtime-publication) still holds.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Fail closed when a held manifest outlives its payload\n\nTwo review findings on the publication port and its filesystem adapter were\nblocking. filesystem/held.rs mapped an absent payload to Vec::new()\nregardless of whether a manifest was held beside it, so the tenants\ndocument''s emptying guard and the retirement guard -- both of which read the\nheld tenants document -- silently disarmed whenever that payload was lost:\na snapshot with zero tenants at the held revision would write [] with no\nEmptying::Intended, and a data source still bound to a real tenant could be\nretired out from under it. A held manifest proves something was published;\nan absent payload means the content is lost, not that nothing was. The\ntenants document is now parsed through parse_held_tenants, which\ndistinguishes all three states -- no manifest (never published, no\nconstraint), manifest and payload (parse and check), manifest without\npayload (refuse the whole publication as the new PublicationError::\nHeldPayloadLost, whose message tells the operator to restore the payload or\nremove the manifest). Data sources'' and the catalogue''s own held payloads\nare left on the existing lenient parse_documents, now with a rustdoc\nexplaining why that asymmetry is safe: their held state gates only their\nown verdict, and a manifest-without-payload there is already an\nunconditional Write (ADR 0018 part 6) that any republish recovers from --\nproved for both by\na_held_manifest_without_its_payload_is_republishable_at_the_same_revision\nand its new catalogue-side twin. Two new integration tests drive the\nemptying and retirement guards through a lost tenants payload and confirm\nboth now refuse with HeldPayloadLost.\n\nSecond, errors.rs and report.rs both claimed a publication is either\nrefused with nothing written or fully applied -- false for Unwritable.\npublish() writes data sources, then the catalogue, then tenants as\nsequential file operations; an I/O failure between two of them leaves\nearlier documents written and the rest untouched. That guarantee is now\nscoped correctly to every other variant (StaleRevision, DivergentPayload,\nDanglingDataSource, RetiredDataSourceStillBound, EmptyingNotIntended,\nEmptyCatalogue, EmptyTenantData, HeldPayloadLost, and Unreadable, which can\nonly fire before a write is planned), Unwritable is documented as the one\nexception, and docs/README.md gains a \"Partial writes across documents\"\nsection explaining why retrying the same call at the same revisions always\nfinishes the job. a_publication_that_failed_between_documents_is_completed_\nby_the_next_one proves it: obstruct the catalogue''s own write so data\nsources land and the catalogue fails before tenants is attempted, then\nrepublish unobstructed and see Unchanged, Written, Written.\n\nTaken together with the blocking pair, twelve non-blocking findings from\nthe same review:\n\n- An incoming tenant binding with an empty data map -- reachable only\n  through Deserialize -- is now refused by refuse_empty_tenant_data,\n  symmetric with refuse_empty_catalogue, instead of being silently dropped\n  by the consumer on arrival.\n- PlacementClassDocument''s round-trip test now loops all six variants\n  through fabric_tenant_runtime::PlacementClass instead of asserting one\n  variant against itself.\n- current() gains a test covering all three held-manifest presence states.\n- verdict_tests.rs''s duplicate \"orphaned payload\" test is gone; a comment on\n  its surviving neighbour points at the integration test that actually\n  proves that row against a file.\n- atomic_write::sync_directory is #[cfg(unix)], with a documented no-op\n  elsewhere -- opening a directory to fsync it is not portable, and\n  docs/README.md now says the extra rename-durability is Unix-only.\n- Stale \"future slice\" rustdoc in canonical.rs, document_revision.rs, and\n  docs/README.md is corrected now that the guard has shipped; Verdict::\n  Write''s doc no longer claims the manifest is written \"when it changes\"\n  when write_if_needed writes both unconditionally.\n- filesystem/held.rs''s parsing logic moved into its own file, filesystem/\n  parse.rs -- turning held bytes into typed documents is a different concern\n  from reading them off disk, and it is also where parse_held_tenants above\n  belongs. held.rs still needed a stated reason for the 121-150 line band\n  it remained in after the split.\n- The five hand-copied identifier newtypes (ConnectorId, ConnectionName,\n  FieldName, CollectionName, SchemaName) now come from one\n  identifier_newtype! macro declared in ids.rs, mirroring\n  fabric_connector::ids::identifier_newtype, which this crate cannot depend\n  on across the plane boundary. Same public API, serde attributes, and KIND\n  strings.\n- A held manifest''s own document field is now checked against the kind of\n  file it was read from and refused as Unreadable on a mismatch, instead of\n  being read and ignored.\n- DocumentManifest''s fields are private with accessors, so a wrong\n  contract_version can no longer be built by literal; serde still reads and\n  writes the fields directly. DocumentKind::payload_file() is removed: it\n  had no production caller -- DocumentPaths derives only the manifest name\n  from the kind, deliberately leaving the payload path exactly as the\n  caller supplied it, so a caller naming it something other than\n  tenants.json still works. This corrects 686f2fb''s own commit message,\n  which claimed the adapter built every path from payload_file and\n  manifest_file; it never did.\n- The rename-and-sibling integration test is renamed to what it actually\n  proves -- temp-file cleanup on both the success and failure path -- and\n  the three overlapping no-op tests are collapsed into the one that already\n  covered all three properties.\n- lib.rs''s \"What this crate owns\" now says the composed acceptance test\n  through the real runtime and Data API lives under tests/, without writing\n  it -- another slice is adding it.\n\nThe JSON wire shape of every document is unchanged. docs/CONTEXT.md is\nuntouched: a concurrent slice is appending to it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Prove the runtime publication guards by mutation, and catch the docs up\n\nTen mutations run against fabric-runtime-publication in this worktree,\nfollowing docs/delivery.md''s standard: mutate, watch the named test fail,\nrevert, keep the test. Nine caught what they were built to catch; one\n(zeroing a discriminator value through the constant the fixture''s own\ncorpus also reads) left everything green, because that constant feeds both\nsides of the isolation check it was meant to break -- the same class of\nblind spot delivery.md already names. The other near-miss, decision 22''s\nprediction that removing atomic rename might leave nothing failing, did not\nhold: three tests still caught it. Both are recorded as findings rather than\nsilently fixed.\n\nAlso corrects what the crate''s own docs had fallen behind on: CONTEXT.md\nstill named a removed `payload_file()`, undercounted validate.rs''s guards\nand PublicationError''s variants, and placed `parse_documents` in the wrong\nmodule; the control-plane architecture''s \"Runtime publication boundary\"\nsection still said the seam \"belongs in fabric-reconciliation or a\nsibling\", which ADR 0018 supersedes; crate-dependencies.md described the\ncomposed test''s two extra dev-dependencies as a later slice''s future work\nwhen they are already in the graph; and verification.md''s Gates table and\narchitecture-invariant count were stale from before this crate existed.\nThe Gates table now carries real numbers from a run in this worktree at\n9e7c83b, noted as such.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Give the recording connector a corpus that does not follow the fixture\n\nMutation 1a in the verification record zeroed a discriminator constant and\nleft all thirteen composed tests green, because the same constant fed both\nthe published binding and the connector''s rows: the mutation moved both\nsides together. The corpus is now the database''s own truth, written as\nliterals, so a change to what is published is a change to one side only --\nand the same mutation now fails seven of thirteen.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Read a payload as held content even when its manifest is missing\n\nBLOCKING: filesystem/parse.rs''s parse_held_tenants mapped a missing manifest\nto Vec::new() regardless of whether a payload sat beside it -- exactly the\nshipped examples/*.json shape. With a populated tenants.json and no\ntenants.manifest.json, the emptying guard read the held set as empty, so an\noffered tenants: [] at Emptying::NotIntended was accepted and written\nstraight over a populated file. Renamed to parse_held_documents and made\ngeneric: the no-manifest arm now parses whatever payload is present\n(parse_documents(payload, document)) instead of discarding it, while a\nmanifest without its payload still refuses as HeldPayloadLost. The module\ndoc''s three-state table is corrected, four new unit tests pin the four\n(manifest, payload) combinations -- including one with T = u64 proving the\nbytes are actually parsed, not merely defaulted -- and a new integration\ntest, a_populated_tenants_payload_with_no_manifest_still_guards_against_\nemptying, seeds a directory the shipped-examples way and checks every one\nof the six canonical files is byte- and inode-identical after the refusal.\n\nData sources get the same protection: their own emptying guard reads held\ndata-sources content the identical way, so a lost data-sources payload with\na held manifest disarmed it too. adapter.rs''s publish() now parses\nheld_data_sources through parse_held_documents as well, passing its\nmanifest. HeldPayloadLost''s rustdoc in errors.rs now names both documents\nand says why the catalogue is exempt: it is only ever byte-compared inside\nverdict, never parsed for a guard, so it can never be lost this way. The\nexisting data-sources \"manifest without payload\" test, which asserted the\nold republishable behaviour, is renamed to\na_held_data_sources_manifest_without_its_payload_is_refused_as_held_payload_\nlost and now expects the refusal; a new twin test,\nan_emptying_publication_is_refused_when_the_held_data_sources_payload_is_\nlost, proves Emptying::Intended never even gets considered once the payload\nis gone. The catalogue''s own lost-payload test keeps its old assertion, with\nits comment now explaining why it alone is unaffected.\n\nNon-blocking findings from the same review:\n\n- a_failed_refresh_leaves_the_runtime_serving_the_last_good_snapshot and\n  a_malformed_published_document_does_not_deprovision_the_tenants_already_\n  serving held assertions across a fixed 500ms window without ever proving\n  a refresh ran. tests/support/counting_source.rs adds CountingSource<T>, a\n  ResourceSource decorator that counts every load() call (the Err path\n  included) by delegating to the real JsonFileSource; build_stack now\n  builds the runtime over it and Stack exposes the two counters. Both tests\n  now record the count, write the malformed bytes, call refresh_now, poll\n  (bounded 2s) for the count to advance, and only then assert -- replacing\n  hold_across, which is now dead and removed.\n- an_emptying_publication_is_refused_unless_it_is_intended now captures the\n  six canonical files'' inode+mtime before the refused call and asserts them\n  unchanged after, with the comment pointing at that assertion instead of\n  only the runtime''s continued 200s.\n- the_same_logical_article_key_reaches_a_different_predicate_for_each_tenant\n  now also asserts each tenant''s clauses do not contain the other tenant''s\n  predicate, matching the absence proof\n  each_call_reaches_the_connector_carrying_only_its_own_tenant_predicate\n  already gives by equality.\n- port.rs''s publish rustdoc now lists EmptyTenantData and the (now\n  dual-document) HeldPayloadLost among what is refused.\n- validate.rs''s module doc corrected from \"six\" to \"five\" guard functions.\n- errors.rs''s band-reason comment now says the file is one variant from the\n  150-line ceiling, and names the two ways out -- trim rustdoc to what a\n  caller acts on, or find a real second concept -- rather than an\n  exemption; rewrapped elsewhere in the same file so the line count does\n  not move.\n- filesystem_runtime_publication.rs and tests/support/mod.rs gain\n  #![cfg(unix)]: the atomic-write guarantee they prove is itself\n  Unix-only, and so is std::os::unix::fs::MetadataExt, which both already\n  used. published_state_serves_two_tenants.rs gets the same gate as a\n  consequence -- its support module now vanishes on non-Unix, so the whole\n  suite that depends on it must too.\n\nThe JSON wire shape of every document is unchanged.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Say what is held by the payload, not the manifest, and close the two mutation findings\n\nThe README and CONTEXT described the lost-payload rule as tenants-only and\ncalled a payload with no manifest \"nothing published\"; the code now reads a\npresent payload as held content whichever way the manifest is, for tenants\nand data sources alike, and only byte-compares the catalogue. The\nverification record''s two findings are marked closed: the connector''s corpus\nno longer follows the fixture constant, so the zeroed-discriminator mutation\nfails seven of thirteen, and the adapter suite has a direct emptying test.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Count a load once it has finished, and record the mutations as rerun\n\nThe counting source counted a load at entry, so a poll could return while\nthe failed load was still in flight; it counts on completion now. The\nconnector''s globex row and its id are literals for the same reason its acme\nrow is. The parse of held content has one body instead of two. The README\nsays which checks are manifest-gated -- staleness and divergence both -- and\nwarns that a first publication over a seeded directory arms the retirement\nguard. The verification record carries the observed failure lines for the\ntwo mutations that were findings, both rerun.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqnKodCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAvTYQAC5gaUjFq7in2WMC12xdNjVR\n+fWBZvUS5R1ROpkDPclrnS4tHFf5ToV4by0VOdDK8KsGp9MNgbWg2f/HrCzyESM/\nlDlHQF8meVA+uk7WDFqQcz18upduDFUbm6XtR4XX4D7d2pWMdt4+Ty/TXh4nNQwI\noXztynHBVzfGXRlLgoroz8Sbex9X1RAYFUMF/NmsRsPWebkfLvtJK0/wnvCsAALm\nTvDlkYr19USwsQVOdiRi78Co8emGSimYfIPEPa1G4BXf5F8OnfyugtOcY5ZCyC+p\ndBpXD8cBQR2JbhImqjmV0OwDvTHGI0JHM7O6t/cI3OQyLhdhZLHdw6+86sNUvt0G\n76s79rrFpea7RE/sd2ktMBd8oJuLnau1+gr9n3URQYfMM/N1F44Tisob2uCoEBve\nIzuX44A0cnW2gioEEI5MIIU70hoYsHXwsEwSxgHo3jUaVWC4kNzFc0uPXQJ2zEya\n382Ujr1VE97zmEDKtlaqY24mz89OfN8FQ8x5s7nmjiyyyBQSkzb7GdRD6kZpUlbc\nLB0tPxq/csAcV39zI+tiFS9cWs4gMUMf2nwwJL0UKzi5Xij7TXZnDYfjIsTYv/GG\nhalBKGZx91dEDiaqQWgwLtorcEvfR+FBiiy8Xj2U25kO4BUZ8uN960Rrhy+NjTE7\nB16YXAPOrLeZp7Inhl91\n=pszw\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-05T23:47:42Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/bc1f58ccd4f00957dcdadc41d7ad2c176a3e1ef7","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKGJjMWY1OGNjZDRmMDA5NTdkY2RhZGM0MWQ3YWQyYzE3NmEzZTFlZjc","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/f9c5abf1c54da903bc3b2aa054fbfc393ef0f516","sha":"f9c5abf1c54da903bc3b2aa054fbfc393ef0f516","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/f9c5abf1c54da903bc3b2aa054fbfc393ef0f516"}],"sha":"bc1f58ccd4f00957dcdadc41d7ad2c176a3e1ef7","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/bc1f58ccd4f00957dcdadc41d7ad2c176a3e1ef7"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/f9c5abf1c54da903bc3b2aa054fbfc393ef0f516/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-05T19:38:13Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-05T19:38:13Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Stop writing to a repository the operator has stopped targeting (#64)\n\n* Stop writing to a repository the operator has stopped targeting\n\nEvery delegated operation cloned the platform repository out of the lock and\nreleased it before awaiting. So a sweep that started against repository A and\nan operator who disconnected or rebound half a second later produced a commit\nlanding in A *after* the platform had been told to stop targeting it. The\nmanifest revision could not see it: it proves A''s manifest did not move, and\nA''s manifest had not moved.\n\nTwo guarantees, and both are needed.\n\nAn unbind drains. The binding''s state moves behind a `tokio::sync::RwLock`,\nand every operation holds the read guard across the await of the call it\ndelegates, so `connect`, `disconnect` and `unusable` complete only once\neverything that began against the old repository has finished. Nothing this\nplatform reports as done was done to a repository it no longer targets, and\nnothing starts against one after it is replaced. Those three, and\n`is_connected`, become async — a non-blocking read would report \"not\nconnected\" mid-rebind, which is a lie about a platform connected on both\nsides of it — and `IntegrationTarget` becomes async so a caller can wait.\n\nAnd a decision is bound to the binding it was read from, because draining\nsettles what is in flight and says nothing about a decision read a minute ago\nand written now. A generation counter sits beside the bound repository under\nthe same lock; a read''s revision carries it, and a write presenting another\ngeneration''s is a conflict — the same answer as a manifest that moved, and\nfor the same reason. `DesiredRevision` is opaque by contract, so the tag is\ninvisible to the service, the console and the adapter alike.\n\n`DesiredStateBinding` is left alone. It has the same race and it fails\ndifferently, and untangling it is its own change.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Bound the drain, so a disconnect cannot quietly not happen\n\nThe drain the last commit added waits for whatever is running against the old\nrepository, and nothing bounded how long that could be. One `advance` is around\nthirty calls to the Git host, each bounded only by `http_timeout_seconds`, so a\nhost that accepts every connection and answers each one just inside the limit\nholds the binding for minutes. The lock is write-preferring, so the operator''s\ndisconnect queues behind it — and the disconnect is cut off at\n`request_timeout_seconds`, which drops the handler future and removes the\nqueued writer. The operator is told `504`, the unbind never happened, the key\nwas never deleted, and the platform is still pointed at the repository they\nasked it to forget.\n\nSo the adapter now bounds the operation rather than the request.\n`PlatformRepositoryConfig` gains `operation_timeout_seconds`, every\n`DesiredState` method is wrapped in it, and a spent budget is `Unavailable`\nnaming the budget and nothing else. The trait states the contract, because it\nis the binding''s lock that makes it load-bearing rather than a nicety. The\ndeployment sets `platform_management.operation_timeout_seconds`, default 20,\nand startup refuses to run unless it is at least 1 and strictly less than\n`request_timeout_seconds` — so a disconnect can always finish inside one\nrequest.\n\nFive smaller corrections, all from the same review:\n\n`DesiredStateError::Conflict` had no arm and fell to the catch-all, so a stale\ndecision — which the generation check made reachable from an operator''s own\npause or rollback click — answered `503 platform_unavailable` with a\n`Retry-After` and a server-error log line. It is a `409 platform_state_moved`,\nretried by reading again rather than by asking again.\n\nThe tokio comments claimed more isolation than a workspace-inherited feature\nlist gives; they now say what is actually enabled and that nothing enforces the\nrule. `is_connected`''s rustdoc claimed a non-blocking read would lie during a\nswap, which was never true — it is async because the lock is. `disconnect`\nclaimed an in-flight commit could be interrupted by the credential vanishing,\nwhich it cannot: the App holds its key by value. And the drain claims were\nabsolute where cancellation makes them not: a future dropped mid-write releases\nthe lock with its last request possibly already sent, so the caveat is stated\nin `swap.rs` and in the architecture doc.\n\nTwo files that were already at 149 and 150 lines are split rather than exempted:\nthe desired-state trait into `desired_state/port.rs`, and the platform arms of\nthe status match into `status_mapping/platform.rs`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Say what the drain bound covers, and what can still cancel a write\n\nThe operation budget is a third thing that can drop a write mid-flight,\nand the only one that reaches an unattended sweep; the guarantee startup\nchecks is about the unbind, not the rest of a disconnect; and the trait''s\nwrites answer more than a conflict. Each is now said where it is relied on.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Bound an operation by not starting work, not by dropping work already sent\n\nThe drain was still open at both ends, and each end was a cancellation. The\nbudget was a `tokio::time::timeout` around the operation, so a host that was\nmerely slow rather than silent had its ref update dropped at one second with the\nrequest already on the wire -- the guard released, a queued disconnect free to\nreturn, and the commit landing in a repository the operator had just been told\nthe platform had stopped writing to. `swap.rs` said so in as many words. And the\nsame thing happened without the budget: an operator''s browser closing, or the\nrequest timeout firing, drops the handler future, and the guard lived in that\nfuture.\n\nTwo changes, and both are needed.\n\nThe budget no longer abandons anything. It records when the operation began in a\ntask-local, and the single site that sends -- `host/sending.rs::attempt` -- asks\nbefore each call whether the budget is spent. Twice, because minting an\ninstallation token is itself a request, and checking only at the top would let a\nmint that consumed the last of the budget be followed by the call anyway. A\nrequest already sent runs to its own outcome under `http_timeout_seconds`; the\none after it is refused, `Unavailable`, naming the budget and nothing else. So\nan operation ends within the budget plus one request timeout, and the platform\nnever has a call on the wire it has stopped waiting for.\n\nThat changes the arithmetic startup checks. It is now\n`operation_timeout_seconds + git_host.http_timeout_seconds <\nrequest_timeout_seconds`, with all three named in the refusal, and the default\nbudget moves from twenty to fifteen so the shipped values still leave five\nseconds for the rest of a disconnect. A budget that fits on its own and not with\nthe one call it cannot cut short is exactly the case the old check let through,\nand it has a test of its own.\n\nAnd a delegated operation survives its caller. `PlatformDesiredState::current`\nbecomes an `Arc<RwLock<Live>>` so the read guard can be *owned*, `Live` hands\nback the `Arc` rather than a `&dyn`, and every operation runs in a task that\nowns the guard, the repository and its arguments while the delegate waits on the\njoin. A caller that goes away cancels nothing: the work finishes, the guard is\nheld until it does, and the drain waits. `fabric-platform-management` now\ndeclares `rt` on tokio deliberately, for this and for nothing else, and the\nmanifest comment says so.\n\nThe invariant the docs may now state without qualification: a disconnect or a\nrebind returns only after every request the platform started against the old\nrepository has an outcome, and the platform never starts one against it\nafterwards. `swap.rs`, `binding.rs`, the `DesiredState` trait, `budget.rs`,\n`disconnect.rs` and the architecture doc all say that, and each also states the\none residual no lock can close -- a call the platform gave up on after\n`http_timeout_seconds` is not a call the host gave up applying, so a ref update\nreported as failed may be committed a moment later. Nothing withdraws it and\nnothing reports it as done.\n\nTwo tests where the finding was: one delays the ref update past the budget but\ninside the request timeout and proves the write completes and the host recorded\nit completing; one delays an early call and proves nothing started after the\ndeadline. The support server grew a per-request delay and a record of what it\nfinished answering, which is the difference the first test turns on. The\n`never answers` tests now assert the bound is the budget plus one request\ntimeout rather than the budget alone, and the one asserting what the operator is\ntold moved to a host that answers slowly, because a host that says nothing at\nall now fails as a request timeout and names that instead. In the binding,\n`an_operation_outlives_a_caller_that_stopped_waiting` aborts the calling task\nmid-write and proves the disconnect still waits, the write still lands, and the\nunbind then takes effect.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Bound the wait for a bearer, which the budget could not see\n\nThe bound the last commit established -- an operation ends within its budget\nplus one request timeout -- was false under the App posture, and false in the\ndirection that matters. `host/sending.rs::attempt` checks the budget before\nobtaining a bearer and again before the call it was building, but what sits\nbetween the two is `BearerSource::bearer`, which holds one mutex across the\ntoken exchange so that concurrent sweeps share a token rather than each minting\ntheir own. Nothing bounded the wait for that mutex. With an expired token and a\ntoken endpoint that stalls, N concurrent operations each burn a whole\n`http_timeout_seconds` failing to mint before the next is let in, so one\n`attempt` spans N of them with the binding''s read guard held -- the first check\nalready passed, the second not yet reached. On the shipped defaults three\nconcurrent operations make the drain thirty seconds against a twenty-five\nsecond guarantee, and an operator''s disconnect is cut off by the request\ntimeout having released nothing. That is the failure the last two commits were\nwritten to prevent, and a regression on the `timeout` implementation, which\nbounded this case at fifteen.\n\nSo the acquisition as a whole is bounded rather than gated, at whatever is left\nof the budget plus one request. `port/budget/bearer.rs` reads the task-local and\nworks out the allowance; unbudgeted -- a test driving a host method directly --\nthat is one request. A third check would not have closed it, because it bounds\nthe mint and leaves the queue in front of it, which is where the time goes.\nExpiry is `Unavailable` naming the budget and nothing else, from the one\nconstructor both refusals now share.\n\nThis is the single thing the budget may cut short, and it does not weaken the\nrule beside it. That rule protects the write path: a ref update dropped\nmid-flight releases the binding while the host may still be applying it. A token\nexchange has no desired-state side effect at all, so giving up on one costs a\nwasted mint and leaves the cache holding what it held. `budget.rs`, `sending.rs`\nand `port.rs` say so, and the places that claimed the budget never abandons \"a\ncall already sent\" now say \"a write\".\n\nThe test is two operations on one repository over a token endpoint that sits for\nten seconds, against a three-second request timeout and a one-second budget.\nBoth must answer `Unavailable` inside five seconds; unbounded the second gives\nup at six, and bounded it gives up at four. It drives the App credential so the\nmint path is real -- the throwaway RSA key the clients integration''s tests use,\ncopied rather than reached for across a crate boundary, and the fake host\nalready answers `/access_tokens`.\n\nSeven smaller findings, from the same review.\n\nThe budget tests mostly passed under the implementation this replaced, so three\nare strengthened. `no_request_starts_once_the_budget_is_spent` now asserts the\nhost finished answering the delayed branch-head read and that the operation\nwaited it out, which is what separates \"refused the next call\" from \"dropped\nthis one\". Both `never answers` tests assert a floor as well as a ceiling. And\nthe in-flight test had six requests to start inside a one-second budget, which a\nloaded runner could lose: the budget is three seconds now, the delayed ref update\nfour, against an eight-second request.\n\nThe residual list was short by two. A panic inside an operation drops the guard\nand the in-flight request together, and a detached operation does not survive the\nruntime being dropped once graceful shutdown has returned. Both land where the\nnetwork residual does -- the platform gave up on a call the host may still apply\n-- and neither is a swap returning early. `holding.rs`, `swap.rs` and the\narchitecture doc now state all three.\n\n`within_budget` nested inside another would have started the clock again, giving\na composed operation two budgets and quietly doubling the bound checked at\nstartup; it keeps the outer start. `PlatformDesiredState` requires a Tokio\nruntime now that every operation is spawned, and says so on the type and on\n`outliving`. `git_host.http_timeout_seconds` and `request_timeout_seconds` each\nname the startup sum they participate in, and the example configuration grows a\ncommented `[platform_management]` section stating the rule beside the value.\n`fabric-platform-git` declares tokio''s `time` feature in both dependency lists,\nsince the manifest comment is about being honest on exactly that.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Bound the wait to forget a bearer too, since it queues on the same mutex\n\nThe acquisition was bounded and the invalidation after a 401 was not, and\nboth wait on the lock a stalled mint holds. The four places that still said\nthe budget never abandons a call already sent now say a write, which is the\nrule; and the stalled-mint test gained the floor that proves the second\noperation queued at all.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Anchor the bearer allowance to the operation''s start, not to now\n\nMeasured from now, the allowance handed out after the budget was spent was\na fresh request timeout, so a 401 landing late could hold the binding for\nthe budget plus two requests. Anchored to the bound, it is what remains of\nthe budget plus one request at any call site, gated or not -- and three\nunit tests say so in seconds rather than by argument.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Keep the trait file inside the size gate after rebasing onto chart rollback\n\nThe rollback commit''s fuller `roll_back` doc and this branch''s bounded-operation\nsection met in one file four lines over the limit; the same words, tighter.\nAlso the blank line the removed 501 arm left behind.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Settle the record and the binding together, whoever stops waiting\n\nThe detached-operation protection covered delegated `DesiredState` calls and\nstopped there. The integration workflow that changes *what is bound* still ran\ninside the operator''s request, and it writes two places. `store_and_bind` saves\nthe record and then rebinds, and the rebind blocks on the platform binding''s\nwrite guard while the old operations drain. A request timeout or a closed\nbrowser drops that future between the two halves, and the platform is left with\na persistent record naming repository B and a live `PlatformDesiredState` still\nreading A -- until a restart or some later rebind happens to notice. Nothing\nreports it, because both halves succeeded as far as anything here can see.\n\n`disconnect` had the mirror. It unbinds, deletes the key, clears the record; a\ncancellation landing after the drain and before the deletions leaves the binding\nreleased with the key and the record still there. Its own rustdoc said the\nopposite in a section header -- \"A disconnect that was cut off has done nothing\n... nothing has been released\" -- which was false for exactly that window. The\nsection is gone and replaced with what is true.\n\nTwo changes, and both are needed.\n\nA transition runs to completion in a task the caller only awaits. `settling.rs`\ntakes the whole of one -- save then rebind, or unbind then delete then clear --\nmoves it into a `tokio::spawn`, and joins it. Dropping the caller''s future\ndetaches rather than cancels, so an operator who is told `504` still gets the\nchange; asking again is safe rather than the only repair. The collaborators are\nalready `Arc`s so the task clones refcounts, and the record and the key are\ncopied outright. Same shape and same reasoning as `outliving` in the platform\nbinding''s `holding.rs`, including the two residuals it states: a panic, and a\ndetached task not surviving the runtime being dropped after graceful shutdown.\nBoth are answered as `Unavailable` and logged as a transition nobody observed to\nfinish, never as one that failed.\n\nAnd transitions serialise. Two `choose_repository` calls, or a disconnect during\na rebind, could otherwise interleave into save(B) / save(C) / rebind(C) /\nrebind(B) and land stored C with live B -- the same split by another route. A\n`tokio::Mutex<()>` on the service is held across each whole transition, taken\n*inside* the spawned task so that queueing for a turn is as uncancellable as the\nturn itself. Which of two overlapping requests ends up last is whichever reached\nthe lock second; what cannot happen is neither.\n\n`restore` goes through the transition too. It stores nothing, so it has no split\nof its own to protect, but it is the third place the live binding is ever\nsettled and one outside the order is one that could land after a transition\nasked for later. Startup still awaits it: the transition is detached from a\n*caller*, not from whoever waits for it.\n\nThree tests, and each fails without the change. Two park a fake target on a gate\nand abort the spawned request task once the target reports the call was entered\n-- which is what a request timeout or a browser going away does -- then open the\ngate: the rebind must still settle the binding on the repository it stored, and\nthe disconnect must still delete the key and clear the record. Both time out\nagainst the old implementation, having settled nothing. The third overlaps two\n`choose_repository` calls against a target that holds only the first, and pins\nthe binds landing in the order they were asked for; unordered it produces\n`[a, c, b]`, which is the stored-C-live-B split written out. The gate holds only\nthe calls a test asks for, deliberately: a gate that held every call would\nserialise the transitions on the service''s behalf and prove nothing.\n\nThe wording finding from the same review, everywhere it appears. \"The budget\nplus the host call fits inside one request, so an operator''s disconnect always\nfits inside one API request\" bounds only the drain once it starts -- a disconnect\nspends time before it and deletes a key and a record after it, and a rebind\nstores and builds before it waits. So the claim is now \"the maximum drain time is\nbounded below the API request timeout, leaving explicit headroom for the rest of\nthe integration operation\" in `disconnect.rs`, `config/app_config.rs`,\n`config/git_host.rs`, `config/platform_management.rs`, `startup/platform.rs`\n(the section heading and the refusal message), `startup/platform_target.rs`, the\nexample configuration and the architecture document. The startup check''s own\ntests said the cut-off disconnect \"released nothing\"; with the transition it\nreleases everything and loses only the answer, and the comments say that.\n\nFour files rather than two, because `binding.rs` outgrew the size gate and the\nseams were already there: `settling.rs` is the mechanism, `restore.rs` is\nstartup picking a stored integration back up, `stored.rs` is the record and the\nkey read back, and `binding.rs` keeps the transition that settles a repository\nand the pointing both it and `restore` share.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Account for a change that lands after its request stopped waiting\n\nThe disconnect wrote its operator''s line inside the transition; the two\nrebinds still wrote theirs in the caller, so the one change this branch\nnewly guarantees will land was the one that could land with nobody named.\nThe line now goes in last, inside. The application''s key and its record\nare written as one transition for the same reason, the overlap test says\nwhat the order actually promises -- whichever reached it second -- and\nproves the order was held, and the residual a rebind already in hand\nleaves behind a disconnect is stated rather than discovered.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Refuse a transition prepared against an integration that has since moved\n\nAn already-prepared rebind could resurrect a disconnected integration.\n`choose_repository` obtains the record and the private key, asks the host what\nthe installation reaches, and only then calls `store_and_bind` -- which\nserialised the `save record; bind target` and nothing in front of it. So: the\nrebind reads record A and key K and prepares B; a disconnect takes the\ntransition lock, unbinds, deletes K, clears the record and releases; the rebind\ntakes the lock, saves B and binds B using the K it captured. Record B, binding\nB, key gone -- and the next restart finds a record with no key and reports the\nintegration connected and failing. The disconnect had been undone by authority\ncaptured before the serialisation point. Ordering the *writes* was never enough,\nbecause what gives a transition the authority to write is what it read.\n\nSo the serialised transition includes the state acquisition. The reads have to\nhappen first, so the state is stamped instead: `service/order.rs` replaces the\nbare `Arc<Mutex<()>>` with an `Order` owning the turn and a generation counter\nbeside it. `Order::observed` reads the generation lock-free, so a request\npreparing itself never queues behind a drain merely to read a number, and a read\nthat is stale is stale one way only -- too old, never too new, and too old is a\nrefusal rather than a resurrection. `Order::settle` takes the turn and, *under\nit*, compares the generation the caller was prepared against; unequal, and the\ntransition is not run at all. The compare has to be under the turn, or another\ncould land between the compare and the turn, which is the same race one level\ndown. The bump follows the transition however it ended, because a transition\nthat ran may have moved the state whatever it returned -- a disconnect whose key\ndeletion succeeded and whose record clear did not has left an integration\nnothing can use, and a rebind prepared before it must not put the record back.\nSo the bump is a drop guard, not a line after the await: a panic, or a task\ndropped with its runtime, moves the generation too. A transition refused for\nbeing stale did not run, and moves nothing. The generation itself is a token\nonly the order can mint, and `store_and_bind` takes it only inside a `Prepared`,\nwhich only `prepared()` can build -- so a transition cannot be built on one read\nlate or typed in.\n\n`service/stored.rs` grows `prepared()`: the generation, then the record, then\nthe key, handed back as one value that `store_and_bind` takes whole. The order\nis the point -- a generation read *after* the record would miss a transition\nthat landed between the two reads and would compare equal, letting through\nexactly what this refuses. Both callers that settle a repository go through it,\nso neither can get that order wrong.\n\nWhich transitions check, and why the others do not, is stated at each call site.\n`store_and_bind` checks: both its callers hold a record and a key obtained while\nthey were away talking to the Git host. `disconnect` does not -- \"forget it all\"\nis what the operator wants whatever landed in between, and it is the thing that\nmoves the generation rather than something to be refused by it. `restore` does\nnot, because startup runs before anything else has a turn to take. And\n`complete_creation` does not, because what it writes came from the host moments\nago and is the first thing this platform knows about the application: a creation\nracing a disconnect is a creation, and refusing it strands a key nobody can\nre-mint.\n\nThe refusal is its own answer. `IntegrationError::Moved` is neither `Refused` --\nthe request was well-formed and would have been applied a moment earlier -- nor\n`Unavailable`, since nothing was unreachable. It becomes\n`ControlPlaneError::IntegrationMoved`, `409 Conflict`, code `integration_moved`,\nbeside `revision_conflict` and `platform_state_moved` for the reason those two\nare apart: all three mean \"read again and redo it\", and a console that could only\nsee `409` would not know which page to reload. It carries no `Retry-After`.\n`logging/integration.rs` warns it, beside `operator_refused` -- nothing failed\nand nothing was written, but somebody''s click did not take effect and this is the\nonly record of why. The attribution line stays inside the transition and last, so\na refusal names nobody, because nothing landed.\n\nThree tests, and the two adversarial ones fail without the compare.\n`a_rebind_prepared_before_a_disconnect_cannot_resurrect_it` is the review''s own.\nThe fake host gains a gate, so the rebind is parked *after* its reads and\n*before* it queues; a door on the target could not reproduce that, because by\nthe time the target is reached the turn has been taken and the compare made. The\ndisconnect then runs to completion, and the rebind is let go. The record, the key\nand the binding must all still be gone, and the rebind must be told the\nintegration moved -- the state is asserted first, so with the order alone the\nfailure is \"the integration must still be disconnected\" rather than a missing\nerror code. `a_choice_prepared_against_state_that_has_since_moved_is_refused`\nreplaces `two_overlapping_transitions_each_apply_in_full_and_in_order`, whose\nsecond choice used to be allowed to land: two choices prepared against one\ngeneration, the first lands, the second is refused, stored and live agree on the\nfirst. It keeps that test''s proof that the order is held across a parked\ntransition and that the door is not doing the order''s job.\n`a_disconnect_always_wins_whatever_landed_before_it` is the other side -- a\nrebind lands and moves the generation, and the disconnect queued behind it still\nclears everything. It passes with or without the compare, which is the point.\n\nTwo new files because two existing ones would have outgrown the size gate:\n`order.rs` holds the turn and the generation, and `choose.rs` takes\n`choose_repository` out of an `install.rs` that was already holding two\noperations. `settling.rs` keeps the whole argument, including why a bare order is\nnot enough, and the architecture document''s \"A transition outlives the request\nthat asked for it\" gains the generation rule. `disconnect.rs` no longer states\nthe resurrection as a residual it cannot close, because it is closed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"39c6785d276db38aea9d79638c0e39a34af7dcc2","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/39c6785d276db38aea9d79638c0e39a34af7dcc2"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/f9c5abf1c54da903bc3b2aa054fbfc393ef0f516","verification":{"payload":"tree 39c6785d276db38aea9d79638c0e39a34af7dcc2\nparent 0f6eabe736af80b8060103a0c49135c88b8a272d\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1788637093 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788637093 +1200\n\nStop writing to a repository the operator has stopped targeting (#64)\n\n* Stop writing to a repository the operator has stopped targeting\n\nEvery delegated operation cloned the platform repository out of the lock and\nreleased it before awaiting. So a sweep that started against repository A and\nan operator who disconnected or rebound half a second later produced a commit\nlanding in A *after* the platform had been told to stop targeting it. The\nmanifest revision could not see it: it proves A''s manifest did not move, and\nA''s manifest had not moved.\n\nTwo guarantees, and both are needed.\n\nAn unbind drains. The binding''s state moves behind a `tokio::sync::RwLock`,\nand every operation holds the read guard across the await of the call it\ndelegates, so `connect`, `disconnect` and `unusable` complete only once\neverything that began against the old repository has finished. Nothing this\nplatform reports as done was done to a repository it no longer targets, and\nnothing starts against one after it is replaced. Those three, and\n`is_connected`, become async — a non-blocking read would report \"not\nconnected\" mid-rebind, which is a lie about a platform connected on both\nsides of it — and `IntegrationTarget` becomes async so a caller can wait.\n\nAnd a decision is bound to the binding it was read from, because draining\nsettles what is in flight and says nothing about a decision read a minute ago\nand written now. A generation counter sits beside the bound repository under\nthe same lock; a read''s revision carries it, and a write presenting another\ngeneration''s is a conflict — the same answer as a manifest that moved, and\nfor the same reason. `DesiredRevision` is opaque by contract, so the tag is\ninvisible to the service, the console and the adapter alike.\n\n`DesiredStateBinding` is left alone. It has the same race and it fails\ndifferently, and untangling it is its own change.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Bound the drain, so a disconnect cannot quietly not happen\n\nThe drain the last commit added waits for whatever is running against the old\nrepository, and nothing bounded how long that could be. One `advance` is around\nthirty calls to the Git host, each bounded only by `http_timeout_seconds`, so a\nhost that accepts every connection and answers each one just inside the limit\nholds the binding for minutes. The lock is write-preferring, so the operator''s\ndisconnect queues behind it — and the disconnect is cut off at\n`request_timeout_seconds`, which drops the handler future and removes the\nqueued writer. The operator is told `504`, the unbind never happened, the key\nwas never deleted, and the platform is still pointed at the repository they\nasked it to forget.\n\nSo the adapter now bounds the operation rather than the request.\n`PlatformRepositoryConfig` gains `operation_timeout_seconds`, every\n`DesiredState` method is wrapped in it, and a spent budget is `Unavailable`\nnaming the budget and nothing else. The trait states the contract, because it\nis the binding''s lock that makes it load-bearing rather than a nicety. The\ndeployment sets `platform_management.operation_timeout_seconds`, default 20,\nand startup refuses to run unless it is at least 1 and strictly less than\n`request_timeout_seconds` — so a disconnect can always finish inside one\nrequest.\n\nFive smaller corrections, all from the same review:\n\n`DesiredStateError::Conflict` had no arm and fell to the catch-all, so a stale\ndecision — which the generation check made reachable from an operator''s own\npause or rollback click — answered `503 platform_unavailable` with a\n`Retry-After` and a server-error log line. It is a `409 platform_state_moved`,\nretried by reading again rather than by asking again.\n\nThe tokio comments claimed more isolation than a workspace-inherited feature\nlist gives; they now say what is actually enabled and that nothing enforces the\nrule. `is_connected`''s rustdoc claimed a non-blocking read would lie during a\nswap, which was never true — it is async because the lock is. `disconnect`\nclaimed an in-flight commit could be interrupted by the credential vanishing,\nwhich it cannot: the App holds its key by value. And the drain claims were\nabsolute where cancellation makes them not: a future dropped mid-write releases\nthe lock with its last request possibly already sent, so the caveat is stated\nin `swap.rs` and in the architecture doc.\n\nTwo files that were already at 149 and 150 lines are split rather than exempted:\nthe desired-state trait into `desired_state/port.rs`, and the platform arms of\nthe status match into `status_mapping/platform.rs`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Say what the drain bound covers, and what can still cancel a write\n\nThe operation budget is a third thing that can drop a write mid-flight,\nand the only one that reaches an unattended sweep; the guarantee startup\nchecks is about the unbind, not the rest of a disconnect; and the trait''s\nwrites answer more than a conflict. Each is now said where it is relied on.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Bound an operation by not starting work, not by dropping work already sent\n\nThe drain was still open at both ends, and each end was a cancellation. The\nbudget was a `tokio::time::timeout` around the operation, so a host that was\nmerely slow rather than silent had its ref update dropped at one second with the\nrequest already on the wire -- the guard released, a queued disconnect free to\nreturn, and the commit landing in a repository the operator had just been told\nthe platform had stopped writing to. `swap.rs` said so in as many words. And the\nsame thing happened without the budget: an operator''s browser closing, or the\nrequest timeout firing, drops the handler future, and the guard lived in that\nfuture.\n\nTwo changes, and both are needed.\n\nThe budget no longer abandons anything. It records when the operation began in a\ntask-local, and the single site that sends -- `host/sending.rs::attempt` -- asks\nbefore each call whether the budget is spent. Twice, because minting an\ninstallation token is itself a request, and checking only at the top would let a\nmint that consumed the last of the budget be followed by the call anyway. A\nrequest already sent runs to its own outcome under `http_timeout_seconds`; the\none after it is refused, `Unavailable`, naming the budget and nothing else. So\nan operation ends within the budget plus one request timeout, and the platform\nnever has a call on the wire it has stopped waiting for.\n\nThat changes the arithmetic startup checks. It is now\n`operation_timeout_seconds + git_host.http_timeout_seconds <\nrequest_timeout_seconds`, with all three named in the refusal, and the default\nbudget moves from twenty to fifteen so the shipped values still leave five\nseconds for the rest of a disconnect. A budget that fits on its own and not with\nthe one call it cannot cut short is exactly the case the old check let through,\nand it has a test of its own.\n\nAnd a delegated operation survives its caller. `PlatformDesiredState::current`\nbecomes an `Arc<RwLock<Live>>` so the read guard can be *owned*, `Live` hands\nback the `Arc` rather than a `&dyn`, and every operation runs in a task that\nowns the guard, the repository and its arguments while the delegate waits on the\njoin. A caller that goes away cancels nothing: the work finishes, the guard is\nheld until it does, and the drain waits. `fabric-platform-management` now\ndeclares `rt` on tokio deliberately, for this and for nothing else, and the\nmanifest comment says so.\n\nThe invariant the docs may now state without qualification: a disconnect or a\nrebind returns only after every request the platform started against the old\nrepository has an outcome, and the platform never starts one against it\nafterwards. `swap.rs`, `binding.rs`, the `DesiredState` trait, `budget.rs`,\n`disconnect.rs` and the architecture doc all say that, and each also states the\none residual no lock can close -- a call the platform gave up on after\n`http_timeout_seconds` is not a call the host gave up applying, so a ref update\nreported as failed may be committed a moment later. Nothing withdraws it and\nnothing reports it as done.\n\nTwo tests where the finding was: one delays the ref update past the budget but\ninside the request timeout and proves the write completes and the host recorded\nit completing; one delays an early call and proves nothing started after the\ndeadline. The support server grew a per-request delay and a record of what it\nfinished answering, which is the difference the first test turns on. The\n`never answers` tests now assert the bound is the budget plus one request\ntimeout rather than the budget alone, and the one asserting what the operator is\ntold moved to a host that answers slowly, because a host that says nothing at\nall now fails as a request timeout and names that instead. In the binding,\n`an_operation_outlives_a_caller_that_stopped_waiting` aborts the calling task\nmid-write and proves the disconnect still waits, the write still lands, and the\nunbind then takes effect.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Bound the wait for a bearer, which the budget could not see\n\nThe bound the last commit established -- an operation ends within its budget\nplus one request timeout -- was false under the App posture, and false in the\ndirection that matters. `host/sending.rs::attempt` checks the budget before\nobtaining a bearer and again before the call it was building, but what sits\nbetween the two is `BearerSource::bearer`, which holds one mutex across the\ntoken exchange so that concurrent sweeps share a token rather than each minting\ntheir own. Nothing bounded the wait for that mutex. With an expired token and a\ntoken endpoint that stalls, N concurrent operations each burn a whole\n`http_timeout_seconds` failing to mint before the next is let in, so one\n`attempt` spans N of them with the binding''s read guard held -- the first check\nalready passed, the second not yet reached. On the shipped defaults three\nconcurrent operations make the drain thirty seconds against a twenty-five\nsecond guarantee, and an operator''s disconnect is cut off by the request\ntimeout having released nothing. That is the failure the last two commits were\nwritten to prevent, and a regression on the `timeout` implementation, which\nbounded this case at fifteen.\n\nSo the acquisition as a whole is bounded rather than gated, at whatever is left\nof the budget plus one request. `port/budget/bearer.rs` reads the task-local and\nworks out the allowance; unbudgeted -- a test driving a host method directly --\nthat is one request. A third check would not have closed it, because it bounds\nthe mint and leaves the queue in front of it, which is where the time goes.\nExpiry is `Unavailable` naming the budget and nothing else, from the one\nconstructor both refusals now share.\n\nThis is the single thing the budget may cut short, and it does not weaken the\nrule beside it. That rule protects the write path: a ref update dropped\nmid-flight releases the binding while the host may still be applying it. A token\nexchange has no desired-state side effect at all, so giving up on one costs a\nwasted mint and leaves the cache holding what it held. `budget.rs`, `sending.rs`\nand `port.rs` say so, and the places that claimed the budget never abandons \"a\ncall already sent\" now say \"a write\".\n\nThe test is two operations on one repository over a token endpoint that sits for\nten seconds, against a three-second request timeout and a one-second budget.\nBoth must answer `Unavailable` inside five seconds; unbounded the second gives\nup at six, and bounded it gives up at four. It drives the App credential so the\nmint path is real -- the throwaway RSA key the clients integration''s tests use,\ncopied rather than reached for across a crate boundary, and the fake host\nalready answers `/access_tokens`.\n\nSeven smaller findings, from the same review.\n\nThe budget tests mostly passed under the implementation this replaced, so three\nare strengthened. `no_request_starts_once_the_budget_is_spent` now asserts the\nhost finished answering the delayed branch-head read and that the operation\nwaited it out, which is what separates \"refused the next call\" from \"dropped\nthis one\". Both `never answers` tests assert a floor as well as a ceiling. And\nthe in-flight test had six requests to start inside a one-second budget, which a\nloaded runner could lose: the budget is three seconds now, the delayed ref update\nfour, against an eight-second request.\n\nThe residual list was short by two. A panic inside an operation drops the guard\nand the in-flight request together, and a detached operation does not survive the\nruntime being dropped once graceful shutdown has returned. Both land where the\nnetwork residual does -- the platform gave up on a call the host may still apply\n-- and neither is a swap returning early. `holding.rs`, `swap.rs` and the\narchitecture doc now state all three.\n\n`within_budget` nested inside another would have started the clock again, giving\na composed operation two budgets and quietly doubling the bound checked at\nstartup; it keeps the outer start. `PlatformDesiredState` requires a Tokio\nruntime now that every operation is spawned, and says so on the type and on\n`outliving`. `git_host.http_timeout_seconds` and `request_timeout_seconds` each\nname the startup sum they participate in, and the example configuration grows a\ncommented `[platform_management]` section stating the rule beside the value.\n`fabric-platform-git` declares tokio''s `time` feature in both dependency lists,\nsince the manifest comment is about being honest on exactly that.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Bound the wait to forget a bearer too, since it queues on the same mutex\n\nThe acquisition was bounded and the invalidation after a 401 was not, and\nboth wait on the lock a stalled mint holds. The four places that still said\nthe budget never abandons a call already sent now say a write, which is the\nrule; and the stalled-mint test gained the floor that proves the second\noperation queued at all.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Anchor the bearer allowance to the operation''s start, not to now\n\nMeasured from now, the allowance handed out after the budget was spent was\na fresh request timeout, so a 401 landing late could hold the binding for\nthe budget plus two requests. Anchored to the bound, it is what remains of\nthe budget plus one request at any call site, gated or not -- and three\nunit tests say so in seconds rather than by argument.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Keep the trait file inside the size gate after rebasing onto chart rollback\n\nThe rollback commit''s fuller `roll_back` doc and this branch''s bounded-operation\nsection met in one file four lines over the limit; the same words, tighter.\nAlso the blank line the removed 501 arm left behind.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Settle the record and the binding together, whoever stops waiting\n\nThe detached-operation protection covered delegated `DesiredState` calls and\nstopped there. The integration workflow that changes *what is bound* still ran\ninside the operator''s request, and it writes two places. `store_and_bind` saves\nthe record and then rebinds, and the rebind blocks on the platform binding''s\nwrite guard while the old operations drain. A request timeout or a closed\nbrowser drops that future between the two halves, and the platform is left with\na persistent record naming repository B and a live `PlatformDesiredState` still\nreading A -- until a restart or some later rebind happens to notice. Nothing\nreports it, because both halves succeeded as far as anything here can see.\n\n`disconnect` had the mirror. It unbinds, deletes the key, clears the record; a\ncancellation landing after the drain and before the deletions leaves the binding\nreleased with the key and the record still there. Its own rustdoc said the\nopposite in a section header -- \"A disconnect that was cut off has done nothing\n... nothing has been released\" -- which was false for exactly that window. The\nsection is gone and replaced with what is true.\n\nTwo changes, and both are needed.\n\nA transition runs to completion in a task the caller only awaits. `settling.rs`\ntakes the whole of one -- save then rebind, or unbind then delete then clear --\nmoves it into a `tokio::spawn`, and joins it. Dropping the caller''s future\ndetaches rather than cancels, so an operator who is told `504` still gets the\nchange; asking again is safe rather than the only repair. The collaborators are\nalready `Arc`s so the task clones refcounts, and the record and the key are\ncopied outright. Same shape and same reasoning as `outliving` in the platform\nbinding''s `holding.rs`, including the two residuals it states: a panic, and a\ndetached task not surviving the runtime being dropped after graceful shutdown.\nBoth are answered as `Unavailable` and logged as a transition nobody observed to\nfinish, never as one that failed.\n\nAnd transitions serialise. Two `choose_repository` calls, or a disconnect during\na rebind, could otherwise interleave into save(B) / save(C) / rebind(C) /\nrebind(B) and land stored C with live B -- the same split by another route. A\n`tokio::Mutex<()>` on the service is held across each whole transition, taken\n*inside* the spawned task so that queueing for a turn is as uncancellable as the\nturn itself. Which of two overlapping requests ends up last is whichever reached\nthe lock second; what cannot happen is neither.\n\n`restore` goes through the transition too. It stores nothing, so it has no split\nof its own to protect, but it is the third place the live binding is ever\nsettled and one outside the order is one that could land after a transition\nasked for later. Startup still awaits it: the transition is detached from a\n*caller*, not from whoever waits for it.\n\nThree tests, and each fails without the change. Two park a fake target on a gate\nand abort the spawned request task once the target reports the call was entered\n-- which is what a request timeout or a browser going away does -- then open the\ngate: the rebind must still settle the binding on the repository it stored, and\nthe disconnect must still delete the key and clear the record. Both time out\nagainst the old implementation, having settled nothing. The third overlaps two\n`choose_repository` calls against a target that holds only the first, and pins\nthe binds landing in the order they were asked for; unordered it produces\n`[a, c, b]`, which is the stored-C-live-B split written out. The gate holds only\nthe calls a test asks for, deliberately: a gate that held every call would\nserialise the transitions on the service''s behalf and prove nothing.\n\nThe wording finding from the same review, everywhere it appears. \"The budget\nplus the host call fits inside one request, so an operator''s disconnect always\nfits inside one API request\" bounds only the drain once it starts -- a disconnect\nspends time before it and deletes a key and a record after it, and a rebind\nstores and builds before it waits. So the claim is now \"the maximum drain time is\nbounded below the API request timeout, leaving explicit headroom for the rest of\nthe integration operation\" in `disconnect.rs`, `config/app_config.rs`,\n`config/git_host.rs`, `config/platform_management.rs`, `startup/platform.rs`\n(the section heading and the refusal message), `startup/platform_target.rs`, the\nexample configuration and the architecture document. The startup check''s own\ntests said the cut-off disconnect \"released nothing\"; with the transition it\nreleases everything and loses only the answer, and the comments say that.\n\nFour files rather than two, because `binding.rs` outgrew the size gate and the\nseams were already there: `settling.rs` is the mechanism, `restore.rs` is\nstartup picking a stored integration back up, `stored.rs` is the record and the\nkey read back, and `binding.rs` keeps the transition that settles a repository\nand the pointing both it and `restore` share.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Account for a change that lands after its request stopped waiting\n\nThe disconnect wrote its operator''s line inside the transition; the two\nrebinds still wrote theirs in the caller, so the one change this branch\nnewly guarantees will land was the one that could land with nobody named.\nThe line now goes in last, inside. The application''s key and its record\nare written as one transition for the same reason, the overlap test says\nwhat the order actually promises -- whichever reached it second -- and\nproves the order was held, and the residual a rebind already in hand\nleaves behind a disconnect is stated rather than discovered.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Refuse a transition prepared against an integration that has since moved\n\nAn already-prepared rebind could resurrect a disconnected integration.\n`choose_repository` obtains the record and the private key, asks the host what\nthe installation reaches, and only then calls `store_and_bind` -- which\nserialised the `save record; bind target` and nothing in front of it. So: the\nrebind reads record A and key K and prepares B; a disconnect takes the\ntransition lock, unbinds, deletes K, clears the record and releases; the rebind\ntakes the lock, saves B and binds B using the K it captured. Record B, binding\nB, key gone -- and the next restart finds a record with no key and reports the\nintegration connected and failing. The disconnect had been undone by authority\ncaptured before the serialisation point. Ordering the *writes* was never enough,\nbecause what gives a transition the authority to write is what it read.\n\nSo the serialised transition includes the state acquisition. The reads have to\nhappen first, so the state is stamped instead: `service/order.rs` replaces the\nbare `Arc<Mutex<()>>` with an `Order` owning the turn and a generation counter\nbeside it. `Order::observed` reads the generation lock-free, so a request\npreparing itself never queues behind a drain merely to read a number, and a read\nthat is stale is stale one way only -- too old, never too new, and too old is a\nrefusal rather than a resurrection. `Order::settle` takes the turn and, *under\nit*, compares the generation the caller was prepared against; unequal, and the\ntransition is not run at all. The compare has to be under the turn, or another\ncould land between the compare and the turn, which is the same race one level\ndown. The bump follows the transition however it ended, because a transition\nthat ran may have moved the state whatever it returned -- a disconnect whose key\ndeletion succeeded and whose record clear did not has left an integration\nnothing can use, and a rebind prepared before it must not put the record back.\nSo the bump is a drop guard, not a line after the await: a panic, or a task\ndropped with its runtime, moves the generation too. A transition refused for\nbeing stale did not run, and moves nothing. The generation itself is a token\nonly the order can mint, and `store_and_bind` takes it only inside a `Prepared`,\nwhich only `prepared()` can build -- so a transition cannot be built on one read\nlate or typed in.\n\n`service/stored.rs` grows `prepared()`: the generation, then the record, then\nthe key, handed back as one value that `store_and_bind` takes whole. The order\nis the point -- a generation read *after* the record would miss a transition\nthat landed between the two reads and would compare equal, letting through\nexactly what this refuses. Both callers that settle a repository go through it,\nso neither can get that order wrong.\n\nWhich transitions check, and why the others do not, is stated at each call site.\n`store_and_bind` checks: both its callers hold a record and a key obtained while\nthey were away talking to the Git host. `disconnect` does not -- \"forget it all\"\nis what the operator wants whatever landed in between, and it is the thing that\nmoves the generation rather than something to be refused by it. `restore` does\nnot, because startup runs before anything else has a turn to take. And\n`complete_creation` does not, because what it writes came from the host moments\nago and is the first thing this platform knows about the application: a creation\nracing a disconnect is a creation, and refusing it strands a key nobody can\nre-mint.\n\nThe refusal is its own answer. `IntegrationError::Moved` is neither `Refused` --\nthe request was well-formed and would have been applied a moment earlier -- nor\n`Unavailable`, since nothing was unreachable. It becomes\n`ControlPlaneError::IntegrationMoved`, `409 Conflict`, code `integration_moved`,\nbeside `revision_conflict` and `platform_state_moved` for the reason those two\nare apart: all three mean \"read again and redo it\", and a console that could only\nsee `409` would not know which page to reload. It carries no `Retry-After`.\n`logging/integration.rs` warns it, beside `operator_refused` -- nothing failed\nand nothing was written, but somebody''s click did not take effect and this is the\nonly record of why. The attribution line stays inside the transition and last, so\na refusal names nobody, because nothing landed.\n\nThree tests, and the two adversarial ones fail without the compare.\n`a_rebind_prepared_before_a_disconnect_cannot_resurrect_it` is the review''s own.\nThe fake host gains a gate, so the rebind is parked *after* its reads and\n*before* it queues; a door on the target could not reproduce that, because by\nthe time the target is reached the turn has been taken and the compare made. The\ndisconnect then runs to completion, and the rebind is let go. The record, the key\nand the binding must all still be gone, and the rebind must be told the\nintegration moved -- the state is asserted first, so with the order alone the\nfailure is \"the integration must still be disconnected\" rather than a missing\nerror code. `a_choice_prepared_against_state_that_has_since_moved_is_refused`\nreplaces `two_overlapping_transitions_each_apply_in_full_and_in_order`, whose\nsecond choice used to be allowed to land: two choices prepared against one\ngeneration, the first lands, the second is refused, stored and live agree on the\nfirst. It keeps that test''s proof that the order is held across a parked\ntransition and that the door is not doing the order''s job.\n`a_disconnect_always_wins_whatever_landed_before_it` is the other side -- a\nrebind lands and moves the generation, and the disconnect queued behind it still\nclears everything. It passes with or without the compare, which is the point.\n\nTwo new files because two existing ones would have outgrown the size gate:\n`order.rs` holds the turn and the generation, and `choose.rs` takes\n`choose_repository` out of an `install.rs` that was already holding two\noperations. `settling.rs` keeps the whole argument, including why a bare order is\nnot enough, and the architecture document''s \"A transition outlives the request\nthat asked for it\" gains the generation rule. `disconnect.rs` no longer states\nthe resurrection as a residual it cannot close, because it is closed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqnG+lCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAyj8QAKfITOPSCHFyQGE5NaJeps9T\nIAe1GQMh2X+poptwtI4z9jg/Q+2VEXECCmRgyVrr66FTz+199+HJsJQSnYbPV00N\nzBBRcJIPtyqxaZYHeapb5d39VGrgH3agWw44LCt3vpUz/HpkkFWeCDvxbNt9TGnn\nBj0t6fcmYVri8zgnmozRneBSq9mCT8u8xLXh044afqQZPJlxaFVPuezWYBvbewi/\nWOHyM+qKFOAAYZNMN19v5LOKnF/wywNsfG58nGV9wcZpN6mzlPchponb+TBr8orJ\nIU0I1IlViHGgNF5I2Ipb09LOniz9vVFC7Z8tM8PBM/Gzqrc9WGoDL1EPXxNzLj8g\nOLMXcih0tHrjFOWRI0+22hsG0vJkWsOcp8So0ew7s1OZ2s/OAs1VFTpmBqS06ZEo\nsj8b/f1+7h+hHLgjNUvWJsFx3ZW2NBVvXy/kGq/zEcYr1vkkdIvROPeC5Mv5Hxhr\nwBH1O4EKsUYJfSCB6JLpBrB8iZQkAFLK3VqoEuatFRCsuXpA9rW58V92mcz+g9xP\nfDJxC5evO3HJ4YH0S2gcudH5aB2ABbf/FXImeLk/ACLqYVOZMSXTZ/Nzgh/LH5Mo\nga/TgvY1boU+418iv5Ed9YAB9S3yAPK33XYGVMrtFSrlVlwEoqdMOYazARkuk9DE\n49Nr23qWHw/zAc+XuU0R\n=2VTk\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-05T19:38:13Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/f9c5abf1c54da903bc3b2aa054fbfc393ef0f516","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKGY5YzVhYmYxYzU0ZGE5MDNiYzNiMmFhMDU0ZmJmYzM5M2VmMGY1MTY","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/0f6eabe736af80b8060103a0c49135c88b8a272d","sha":"0f6eabe736af80b8060103a0c49135c88b8a272d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/0f6eabe736af80b8060103a0c49135c88b8a272d"}],"sha":"f9c5abf1c54da903bc3b2aa054fbfc393ef0f516","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/f9c5abf1c54da903bc3b2aa054fbfc393ef0f516"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/0f6eabe736af80b8060103a0c49135c88b8a272d/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-04T19:37:11Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-04T19:37:11Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Read a chart without pretending it is three images (#59)\n\n* Read a chart without pretending it is three images\n\nPlatform Management can now manage a component published as a Helm chart. The\ncapability is deliberately not symmetrical with the image one, because the two\nartifacts do not guarantee the same things.\n\n# Two ports, and the second is not the first widened\n\nA chart repository answers a different question with a weaker answer: an index\nlists versions, and that is all it lists. `ChartIndex::versions` sits beside\n`Registry` rather than inside it, because reusing the registry port would have\nmeant a `resolve` returning a digest nobody has and a provenance nobody\nchecked.\n\nThe first draft of chart discovery did exactly what this avoids -- it built a\n`ReleaseUnit` with an empty source revision and no images, and a comment\nexcusing it. `Discovery` now carries a `Release`, which is either a release\nunit or a chart version, so a chart never reports provenance it never had.\n\n# Rollback is refused, in the signatures\n\nRolling back an image pins a digest: the bytes an environment returns to are\nthe bytes it ran. A classic chart repository pins a version, and the bytes\nbehind `7.3.0` can be republished -- so \"put me back on what I was running\" is\na promise it cannot keep.\n\n`advance` takes a `Release`; `roll_back` takes a `ReleaseUnit`, which only\nimages produce. There is nothing to forget to check. Above that, one gate\nserves both the candidate listing and the write, so they refuse for the same\nreason: `501`, because retrying with a different version answers the wrong\nquestion. The console omits the control rather than offering one whose only\noutcome is a refusal.\n\nPause and resume are offered for both. Stopping an environment advancing needs\nno immutability.\n\n# The Argo renderer matches both halves of the identity\n\nAn Application deploying a chart normally has two sources -- the chart, and the\nrepository holding its values, which carries a `targetRevision` of its own that\nmust never move. Matching on the chart name alone is enough for the files this\nplatform has today and is not enough as a rule: two sources could name charts\nof the same name from different repositories, and the difference is which\nsoftware gets deployed. Zero matches or several both refuse.\n\n# A latent defect this found\n\nThe series rule -- an automatic policy walks forward within the desired\nversion''s own line -- is `core == core`, applied unconditionally. Every stable\nadvance changes the core, so a stable component could never advance and would\nreport \"nothing newer\" however much its repository published. Nothing had\nnoticed because the only managed component was a preview.\n\nThe series now constrains prereleases only. What should bound a *stable*\nadvance is left unsettled rather than invented: patch and minor are ordinary,\na major upgrade is not something to take on a sweep, and until that is decided\na stable component should be `manual`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Close five gaps the CI gates could not see\n\nEvery check was green and none of them covered these.\n\n# A decision is applied to the state it was taken against\n\nThe selector''s docs said \"concurrent changes are enforced by the write, whose\nprecondition includes the manifest this decision was read from\". They described\nthe intended design. What was implemented used the revision the *adapter* had\njust read for itself, which proves only that nothing changed during the write —\nso an operator who added a hold between a sweep''s read and its write watched it\nbe ignored.\n\nThe read now hands back a revision and every write demands it. Two tests: a\nhold added in between is refused, and a fresh revision still applies, so the\ncheck is rejecting staleness rather than rejecting everything.\n\n# A chart release is compared against the chart it claims to be\n\n`Release::Chart` carries the repository and chart it was discovered from, and\nthe write compares three statements of that identity: what the component is\npublished as, what discovery found, and what the file pins. A version is only a\nnumber, and a number is plausible against the wrong chart.\n\n# The Argo renderer knows one location, not one shape\n\nIt walks into `spec.sources` and treats entries only there as sources. A list\nelsewhere carrying `chart:` and `targetRevision:` -- an annotation, a nested\n`helm.parameters` -- is somebody else''s data, and editing it because it looked\nsimilar is the arbitrary-edit engine this design refuses.\n\n# Stable plus automatic fails closed\n\nA prerelease advances within its line and the line stops it crossing to\nanother. A stable version has no line, so nothing stopped 7.3.0 becoming 8.0.0\non a sweep. Which upgrades an automatic stable policy may take is undecided,\nand the safe answer to an undecided rule is to do nothing and say so. What is\nnewer is still reported, so the decision that is owed stays visible.\n\n# The chart index is read defensively\n\nBounded at eight megabytes, applied as the body arrives rather than after it is\nall held. Build metadata accepted, preserved for pinning and ignored for\nprecedence as SemVer requires. A version this cannot read is refused rather\nthan skipped -- skipping means answering \"the newest is X\" while holding\nsomething that might have been newer. Two releases of equal precedence are\nrefused, because there is no newest of them. Eight tests over a real socket.\n\nOne fix had to come first: `PartialEq` on `Version` was derived over the text\nwhile `Ord` used the precedence key, so two versions differing only in build\nmetadata were unequal and yet compared equal. A `BTreeSet` would have kept both\nor one depending on insertion order.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Refuse a numeric prerelease identifier with a leading zero\n\nSemVer forbids `01` where `1` is meant, and the parser accepted it. The\nrule now lives in one place, shared with the version core. The ordering\ncompares a canonical numeric identifier by its length and then its\ndigits, which the leading-zero rule makes exact -- and which removes the\n`u64` parse that silently fell back to string order past its size, so\nthere is no longer a number at which precedence quietly changes its mind.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Read back a Helm version with its build metadata, and refuse a release that is not this component''s\n\nA chart''s desired version was read with the image grammar, which rejects\nthe `+` a chart repository publishes -- so a version this build had just\nadvanced to was one it could not read. The artifact now decides the\ngrammar, and an advance-then-read round trip pins it.\n\nA release''s shape and identity were compared with the artifact''s only\ninside the renderer, per pin, so a component with no pins accepted an\nimage release for a chart, a chart release for images, or a release of\nanother chart entirely. The check now runs before any file is read, over\nevery combination explicitly, and the renderer keeps the one leg it alone\ncan see: whether the pin names the chart the artifact does.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Edit the one Argo source the pin names, and nothing else in the file\n\nThe revision editor entered any `sources:` below `spec:`, read any key at\nany depth inside an entry as the entry''s own, matched keys by prefix,\ndepended on key order, and wrote every line back with `\\n` -- turning a\nCRLF file into an LF one and adding a final newline its author left off.\n\nIt now reads the whole file before deciding. Only the `sources:` that is a\ndirect child of the top-level `spec:` is the list; only an entry''s own\nkeys are its identity; a duplicate key or a shape it cannot read is\nrefused; a block scalar''s body is text rather than structure, measured\nfrom its key rather than from the dash that opens the source; and the one\nline that changes changes only in its version token. Line endings,\nquoting, the separation after the colon and any trailing comment come\nback byte for byte.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Trust only the chart asked for, and only over HTTPS\n\nA chart repository''s index was read over whatever scheme its URL named,\nand a redirect was followed wherever it pointed, including from HTTPS back\nto plain HTTP. The reader now refuses a repository that is not `https://`,\none carrying a credential, a query or a fragment, and refuses to leave\nHTTPS on any hop. A test serves from a loopback socket through a\nconstructor nothing in production calls.\n\nEvery chart''s entries were also forced through the requested chart''s\nstrict shape, so an unrelated chart''s odd entry made this one\nundiscoverable -- and reading the rest as `Value` instead let an unrelated\nchart''s aliases turn a bounded download into gigabytes. The requested\nchart''s entries are now the only ones read into a shape; everything else\nis walked and kept nowhere. An index saying `entries`, or the requested\nchart, twice is refused rather than read last-wins, and duplicate\ndetection is a set rather than a scan. The architecture doc says so.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Show a chart index URL by where it went, not what it was carrying\n\nA refusal was built straight from a `reqwest::Url`''s own `Display`, or from\nthe raw string a caller configured -- and `Url`''s `Display` keeps userinfo.\n`https://user:secret@host/index.yaml` put the password straight into\n`RegistryError::Refused`, which the control-plane API returns to the\nconsole verbatim and which a sweep failure logs. A redirect target a\nrepository''s own response named carried the same risk: a server, malicious\nor merely misconfigured, controls it.\n\nEvery URL this crate names in a message now goes through one renderer,\n`transport::shown`, that keeps only scheme, host, a non-default port, and\npath -- never userinfo, query, or fragment. It lives in its own file so\n`transport.rs` and `index_url.rs` stay under the line-count guideline. The\n\"does not parse\" message no longer echoes the raw address either -- even\nthough `url::ParseError`''s own text never carried it, the input might have.\n\nA unit test on the renderer proves userinfo, query, and fragment never\nsurvive it. An integration test proves `HelmCharts::new` refuses\n`https://user:secret@example.test/charts` without `secret` anywhere in the\nrefusal''s detail.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Validate build metadata as identifiers, not one character run\n\nBuild metadata was checked as a single run of `[0-9A-Za-z.-]`, which reads\n`1.2.4+foo.`, `1.2.4+.foo`, and `1.2.4+foo..bar` as legal -- a `.` in any\nposition, not the dot-separated, non-empty identifiers `SemVer` §10\nactually describes. Such a version could be selected as an upgrade and\nwritten into Argo''s `targetRevision`.\n\nThe suffix is now split on `.` and each piece checked against the\nidentifier shape `legal_prerelease_identifier` already enforces, minus its\nstricter numeric case -- §10 permits a leading zero where §9 does not,\nsince build metadata is never compared. That shared shape is one function,\n`is_identifier_shape`, rather than a predicate written twice and free to\ndrift apart.\n\nFive negative cases and five positive ones in `version_tests.rs`, plus one\nproving a prerelease and its build metadata are validated together.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Show even a URL with no structure without leaking what it opaquely carries\n\n`shown` rendered scheme, host, and path unconditionally, on the assumption\nthat userinfo, query, and fragment were the only places a credential could\nhide. That held for a hierarchical URL, but `oci:user:secret@registry.example/charts`\nand any other cannot-be-a-base address -- no `/` right after the scheme --\nparse with no host, no userinfo, and no query at all; `url` treats the whole\nremainder as one opaque blob it calls the path. `shown` printed that blob\nverbatim, so a realistic OCI-style paste put the same credential straight\nback into the message meant to keep it out, reaching the console and a sweep\nlog exactly as before this fix ever landed.\n\n`shown` now checks `Url::cannot_be_a_base` before looking at anything else\nand renders such an address as only its scheme. `validated_index_url` also\nrefuses one outright, with its own rustdoc section explaining why that check\nalone is not the fix: `reqwest` runs this crate''s redirect policy before its\nown scheme checks, so a redirected-to cannot-be-a-base URL still reaches\n`decide` and `shown` without ever passing back through here. `shown`''s own\nunconditional check is what actually closes it.\n\nWhile in there: a redirect target''s path is server-controlled and unbounded,\nso `shown` now caps it at 200 characters with a `…` marker, matching\n`SafeDiagnostic::MAX`''s reasoning -- the path is still not treated as secret,\nonly its length is bounded. And the build-metadata test added for the\nprevious finding asserted only its happy path; it now also asserts that a\nbad prerelease is refused despite a legal build, and a bad build is refused\ndespite a legal prerelease.\n\nA `shown` unit table covers the hierarchical case, two cannot-be-a-base\nschemes, `file:` with an empty host, an IPv6 host, and a default port beside\na non-default one. Two integration tests, beside the existing userinfo one,\nassert `oci:user:secret@registry.example/charts` is refused without `secret`\nin the detail -- one through `validated_index_url`''s own unit tests, one\nthrough `HelmCharts::new(10).versions(..)` end to end.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Offer rollback for a chart, and name the guarantee it cannot give\n\nRollback means restoring a previously selected desired version. That is the\ndefinition, and under it both artifact kinds have one. For images it restores\nthe exact bytes as well, because a release unit carries every digest. For a\nchart it restores the version, and a chart repository can republish the bytes\nbehind a version -- so the environment comes back to what it was asked to run\nrather than provably to what it ran.\n\nThat difference is now stated rather than enforced. The console says it in one\nline beside the candidates and the architecture doc says it in the table; no\npart of the platform refuses on the strength of it. The alternative reading --\nrollback requires immutable artifact identity, so a chart has none -- is\ncoherent, and it left an operator whose chart upgrade broke login with no route\nback but a hand edit of the platform repository. That is the break-glass path,\nnot an operator experience. `501 rollback_unsupported` goes with the refusal.\n\nSo `DesiredState::roll_back` takes a whole `Release` rather than a\n`ReleaseUnit`, which is where the old rule lived; `roll_back_component` takes a\n`WantedVersion`, and both writes now go through one mapping, so neither can\naccept a shape the other would refuse. The adapter still refuses a release\nshaped for the other kind before reading a file, which is what stops a chart\ncomponent being written an image release''s version string.\n\n`History` holds whole releases, and the chart searches mirror the image ones:\nthe same three tests, the same bound of five, the same `more`, so the console\nmeets one shape. A chart candidate carries no source revision, because an index\nlists versions and no provenance -- the API omits the field and the console\nlays the row out without the line, rather than rendering \"built from\" about\nsomething nothing observed. `ComponentStatus` reports the artifact kind in\nplace of `rollable`, because a boolean had nowhere to say which half of the\nguarantee applies.\n\nOne defect fixed on the way. Looking backwards passed the desired version as\nthe series unconditionally, which is the same latent bug advancement had until\nrecently: every stable release changes the `major.minor.patch` core, so 7.3.0\ncounted as a different line from 7.3.1 and a stable component was offered\nnowhere to go, whatever its registry held. Both directions now read that rule\nfrom one function.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Offer rollback to a component that does not advance, and pin the series rule going backwards\n\nThe brake hid every control behind `automatic`, so a stable chart on\n`manual` -- the setting the platform recommends for it -- had no way back\nin the console, and neither did a paused component whose operator paused\nbecause a release broke. Pause stays behind the policy; rollback does not.\n\nGoing backwards, a preview now provably stays in its line, and the chart\nfake publishes build metadata so the index''s spelling is tested in both\ndirections. The chart port states the uniqueness its searches count on.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Define rollback as restoring an older published version, not one previously selected\n\nThe platform keeps no history of what an environment selected or ran; it\noffers what the registry or chart repository publishes now, below the\ndesired version. The definition said more than that, in the one section\nfuture work will be judged against, so it now says exactly that -- in the\narchitecture doc, the rustdoc, the console''s comments and the tests.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"5d8fd2233f84e96db9cef8e4c51fed015dca26ee","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/5d8fd2233f84e96db9cef8e4c51fed015dca26ee"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/0f6eabe736af80b8060103a0c49135c88b8a272d","verification":{"payload":"tree 5d8fd2233f84e96db9cef8e4c51fed015dca26ee\nparent 7c13f461af895ea3c70f3dede9f531fdfdc571fb\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1788550631 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788550631 +1200\n\nRead a chart without pretending it is three images (#59)\n\n* Read a chart without pretending it is three images\n\nPlatform Management can now manage a component published as a Helm chart. The\ncapability is deliberately not symmetrical with the image one, because the two\nartifacts do not guarantee the same things.\n\n# Two ports, and the second is not the first widened\n\nA chart repository answers a different question with a weaker answer: an index\nlists versions, and that is all it lists. `ChartIndex::versions` sits beside\n`Registry` rather than inside it, because reusing the registry port would have\nmeant a `resolve` returning a digest nobody has and a provenance nobody\nchecked.\n\nThe first draft of chart discovery did exactly what this avoids -- it built a\n`ReleaseUnit` with an empty source revision and no images, and a comment\nexcusing it. `Discovery` now carries a `Release`, which is either a release\nunit or a chart version, so a chart never reports provenance it never had.\n\n# Rollback is refused, in the signatures\n\nRolling back an image pins a digest: the bytes an environment returns to are\nthe bytes it ran. A classic chart repository pins a version, and the bytes\nbehind `7.3.0` can be republished -- so \"put me back on what I was running\" is\na promise it cannot keep.\n\n`advance` takes a `Release`; `roll_back` takes a `ReleaseUnit`, which only\nimages produce. There is nothing to forget to check. Above that, one gate\nserves both the candidate listing and the write, so they refuse for the same\nreason: `501`, because retrying with a different version answers the wrong\nquestion. The console omits the control rather than offering one whose only\noutcome is a refusal.\n\nPause and resume are offered for both. Stopping an environment advancing needs\nno immutability.\n\n# The Argo renderer matches both halves of the identity\n\nAn Application deploying a chart normally has two sources -- the chart, and the\nrepository holding its values, which carries a `targetRevision` of its own that\nmust never move. Matching on the chart name alone is enough for the files this\nplatform has today and is not enough as a rule: two sources could name charts\nof the same name from different repositories, and the difference is which\nsoftware gets deployed. Zero matches or several both refuse.\n\n# A latent defect this found\n\nThe series rule -- an automatic policy walks forward within the desired\nversion''s own line -- is `core == core`, applied unconditionally. Every stable\nadvance changes the core, so a stable component could never advance and would\nreport \"nothing newer\" however much its repository published. Nothing had\nnoticed because the only managed component was a preview.\n\nThe series now constrains prereleases only. What should bound a *stable*\nadvance is left unsettled rather than invented: patch and minor are ordinary,\na major upgrade is not something to take on a sweep, and until that is decided\na stable component should be `manual`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Close five gaps the CI gates could not see\n\nEvery check was green and none of them covered these.\n\n# A decision is applied to the state it was taken against\n\nThe selector''s docs said \"concurrent changes are enforced by the write, whose\nprecondition includes the manifest this decision was read from\". They described\nthe intended design. What was implemented used the revision the *adapter* had\njust read for itself, which proves only that nothing changed during the write —\nso an operator who added a hold between a sweep''s read and its write watched it\nbe ignored.\n\nThe read now hands back a revision and every write demands it. Two tests: a\nhold added in between is refused, and a fresh revision still applies, so the\ncheck is rejecting staleness rather than rejecting everything.\n\n# A chart release is compared against the chart it claims to be\n\n`Release::Chart` carries the repository and chart it was discovered from, and\nthe write compares three statements of that identity: what the component is\npublished as, what discovery found, and what the file pins. A version is only a\nnumber, and a number is plausible against the wrong chart.\n\n# The Argo renderer knows one location, not one shape\n\nIt walks into `spec.sources` and treats entries only there as sources. A list\nelsewhere carrying `chart:` and `targetRevision:` -- an annotation, a nested\n`helm.parameters` -- is somebody else''s data, and editing it because it looked\nsimilar is the arbitrary-edit engine this design refuses.\n\n# Stable plus automatic fails closed\n\nA prerelease advances within its line and the line stops it crossing to\nanother. A stable version has no line, so nothing stopped 7.3.0 becoming 8.0.0\non a sweep. Which upgrades an automatic stable policy may take is undecided,\nand the safe answer to an undecided rule is to do nothing and say so. What is\nnewer is still reported, so the decision that is owed stays visible.\n\n# The chart index is read defensively\n\nBounded at eight megabytes, applied as the body arrives rather than after it is\nall held. Build metadata accepted, preserved for pinning and ignored for\nprecedence as SemVer requires. A version this cannot read is refused rather\nthan skipped -- skipping means answering \"the newest is X\" while holding\nsomething that might have been newer. Two releases of equal precedence are\nrefused, because there is no newest of them. Eight tests over a real socket.\n\nOne fix had to come first: `PartialEq` on `Version` was derived over the text\nwhile `Ord` used the precedence key, so two versions differing only in build\nmetadata were unequal and yet compared equal. A `BTreeSet` would have kept both\nor one depending on insertion order.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Refuse a numeric prerelease identifier with a leading zero\n\nSemVer forbids `01` where `1` is meant, and the parser accepted it. The\nrule now lives in one place, shared with the version core. The ordering\ncompares a canonical numeric identifier by its length and then its\ndigits, which the leading-zero rule makes exact -- and which removes the\n`u64` parse that silently fell back to string order past its size, so\nthere is no longer a number at which precedence quietly changes its mind.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Read back a Helm version with its build metadata, and refuse a release that is not this component''s\n\nA chart''s desired version was read with the image grammar, which rejects\nthe `+` a chart repository publishes -- so a version this build had just\nadvanced to was one it could not read. The artifact now decides the\ngrammar, and an advance-then-read round trip pins it.\n\nA release''s shape and identity were compared with the artifact''s only\ninside the renderer, per pin, so a component with no pins accepted an\nimage release for a chart, a chart release for images, or a release of\nanother chart entirely. The check now runs before any file is read, over\nevery combination explicitly, and the renderer keeps the one leg it alone\ncan see: whether the pin names the chart the artifact does.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Edit the one Argo source the pin names, and nothing else in the file\n\nThe revision editor entered any `sources:` below `spec:`, read any key at\nany depth inside an entry as the entry''s own, matched keys by prefix,\ndepended on key order, and wrote every line back with `\\n` -- turning a\nCRLF file into an LF one and adding a final newline its author left off.\n\nIt now reads the whole file before deciding. Only the `sources:` that is a\ndirect child of the top-level `spec:` is the list; only an entry''s own\nkeys are its identity; a duplicate key or a shape it cannot read is\nrefused; a block scalar''s body is text rather than structure, measured\nfrom its key rather than from the dash that opens the source; and the one\nline that changes changes only in its version token. Line endings,\nquoting, the separation after the colon and any trailing comment come\nback byte for byte.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Trust only the chart asked for, and only over HTTPS\n\nA chart repository''s index was read over whatever scheme its URL named,\nand a redirect was followed wherever it pointed, including from HTTPS back\nto plain HTTP. The reader now refuses a repository that is not `https://`,\none carrying a credential, a query or a fragment, and refuses to leave\nHTTPS on any hop. A test serves from a loopback socket through a\nconstructor nothing in production calls.\n\nEvery chart''s entries were also forced through the requested chart''s\nstrict shape, so an unrelated chart''s odd entry made this one\nundiscoverable -- and reading the rest as `Value` instead let an unrelated\nchart''s aliases turn a bounded download into gigabytes. The requested\nchart''s entries are now the only ones read into a shape; everything else\nis walked and kept nowhere. An index saying `entries`, or the requested\nchart, twice is refused rather than read last-wins, and duplicate\ndetection is a set rather than a scan. The architecture doc says so.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Show a chart index URL by where it went, not what it was carrying\n\nA refusal was built straight from a `reqwest::Url`''s own `Display`, or from\nthe raw string a caller configured -- and `Url`''s `Display` keeps userinfo.\n`https://user:secret@host/index.yaml` put the password straight into\n`RegistryError::Refused`, which the control-plane API returns to the\nconsole verbatim and which a sweep failure logs. A redirect target a\nrepository''s own response named carried the same risk: a server, malicious\nor merely misconfigured, controls it.\n\nEvery URL this crate names in a message now goes through one renderer,\n`transport::shown`, that keeps only scheme, host, a non-default port, and\npath -- never userinfo, query, or fragment. It lives in its own file so\n`transport.rs` and `index_url.rs` stay under the line-count guideline. The\n\"does not parse\" message no longer echoes the raw address either -- even\nthough `url::ParseError`''s own text never carried it, the input might have.\n\nA unit test on the renderer proves userinfo, query, and fragment never\nsurvive it. An integration test proves `HelmCharts::new` refuses\n`https://user:secret@example.test/charts` without `secret` anywhere in the\nrefusal''s detail.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Validate build metadata as identifiers, not one character run\n\nBuild metadata was checked as a single run of `[0-9A-Za-z.-]`, which reads\n`1.2.4+foo.`, `1.2.4+.foo`, and `1.2.4+foo..bar` as legal -- a `.` in any\nposition, not the dot-separated, non-empty identifiers `SemVer` §10\nactually describes. Such a version could be selected as an upgrade and\nwritten into Argo''s `targetRevision`.\n\nThe suffix is now split on `.` and each piece checked against the\nidentifier shape `legal_prerelease_identifier` already enforces, minus its\nstricter numeric case -- §10 permits a leading zero where §9 does not,\nsince build metadata is never compared. That shared shape is one function,\n`is_identifier_shape`, rather than a predicate written twice and free to\ndrift apart.\n\nFive negative cases and five positive ones in `version_tests.rs`, plus one\nproving a prerelease and its build metadata are validated together.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Show even a URL with no structure without leaking what it opaquely carries\n\n`shown` rendered scheme, host, and path unconditionally, on the assumption\nthat userinfo, query, and fragment were the only places a credential could\nhide. That held for a hierarchical URL, but `oci:user:secret@registry.example/charts`\nand any other cannot-be-a-base address -- no `/` right after the scheme --\nparse with no host, no userinfo, and no query at all; `url` treats the whole\nremainder as one opaque blob it calls the path. `shown` printed that blob\nverbatim, so a realistic OCI-style paste put the same credential straight\nback into the message meant to keep it out, reaching the console and a sweep\nlog exactly as before this fix ever landed.\n\n`shown` now checks `Url::cannot_be_a_base` before looking at anything else\nand renders such an address as only its scheme. `validated_index_url` also\nrefuses one outright, with its own rustdoc section explaining why that check\nalone is not the fix: `reqwest` runs this crate''s redirect policy before its\nown scheme checks, so a redirected-to cannot-be-a-base URL still reaches\n`decide` and `shown` without ever passing back through here. `shown`''s own\nunconditional check is what actually closes it.\n\nWhile in there: a redirect target''s path is server-controlled and unbounded,\nso `shown` now caps it at 200 characters with a `…` marker, matching\n`SafeDiagnostic::MAX`''s reasoning -- the path is still not treated as secret,\nonly its length is bounded. And the build-metadata test added for the\nprevious finding asserted only its happy path; it now also asserts that a\nbad prerelease is refused despite a legal build, and a bad build is refused\ndespite a legal prerelease.\n\nA `shown` unit table covers the hierarchical case, two cannot-be-a-base\nschemes, `file:` with an empty host, an IPv6 host, and a default port beside\na non-default one. Two integration tests, beside the existing userinfo one,\nassert `oci:user:secret@registry.example/charts` is refused without `secret`\nin the detail -- one through `validated_index_url`''s own unit tests, one\nthrough `HelmCharts::new(10).versions(..)` end to end.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Offer rollback for a chart, and name the guarantee it cannot give\n\nRollback means restoring a previously selected desired version. That is the\ndefinition, and under it both artifact kinds have one. For images it restores\nthe exact bytes as well, because a release unit carries every digest. For a\nchart it restores the version, and a chart repository can republish the bytes\nbehind a version -- so the environment comes back to what it was asked to run\nrather than provably to what it ran.\n\nThat difference is now stated rather than enforced. The console says it in one\nline beside the candidates and the architecture doc says it in the table; no\npart of the platform refuses on the strength of it. The alternative reading --\nrollback requires immutable artifact identity, so a chart has none -- is\ncoherent, and it left an operator whose chart upgrade broke login with no route\nback but a hand edit of the platform repository. That is the break-glass path,\nnot an operator experience. `501 rollback_unsupported` goes with the refusal.\n\nSo `DesiredState::roll_back` takes a whole `Release` rather than a\n`ReleaseUnit`, which is where the old rule lived; `roll_back_component` takes a\n`WantedVersion`, and both writes now go through one mapping, so neither can\naccept a shape the other would refuse. The adapter still refuses a release\nshaped for the other kind before reading a file, which is what stops a chart\ncomponent being written an image release''s version string.\n\n`History` holds whole releases, and the chart searches mirror the image ones:\nthe same three tests, the same bound of five, the same `more`, so the console\nmeets one shape. A chart candidate carries no source revision, because an index\nlists versions and no provenance -- the API omits the field and the console\nlays the row out without the line, rather than rendering \"built from\" about\nsomething nothing observed. `ComponentStatus` reports the artifact kind in\nplace of `rollable`, because a boolean had nowhere to say which half of the\nguarantee applies.\n\nOne defect fixed on the way. Looking backwards passed the desired version as\nthe series unconditionally, which is the same latent bug advancement had until\nrecently: every stable release changes the `major.minor.patch` core, so 7.3.0\ncounted as a different line from 7.3.1 and a stable component was offered\nnowhere to go, whatever its registry held. Both directions now read that rule\nfrom one function.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Offer rollback to a component that does not advance, and pin the series rule going backwards\n\nThe brake hid every control behind `automatic`, so a stable chart on\n`manual` -- the setting the platform recommends for it -- had no way back\nin the console, and neither did a paused component whose operator paused\nbecause a release broke. Pause stays behind the policy; rollback does not.\n\nGoing backwards, a preview now provably stays in its line, and the chart\nfake publishes build metadata so the index''s spelling is tested in both\ndirections. The chart port states the uniqueness its searches count on.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Define rollback as restoring an older published version, not one previously selected\n\nThe platform keeps no history of what an environment selected or ran; it\noffers what the registry or chart repository publishes now, below the\ndesired version. The definition said more than that, in the one section\nfuture work will be judged against, so it now says exactly that -- in the\narchitecture doc, the rustdoc, the console''s comments and the tests.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqmx3nCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAZ7EQAHD+ukiqjMMI7zN+0glCTA7A\n41vsPNA+1M6/cK2BrM50YiHa/JgpD/ZD10ty5tm8C+ccXvfR3QPniqml77xNJa9/\nh1HVhv4yObQjzEON5rOntZQkCpHmvEMdHnSxuc4mXQKPfpmGeq+8kOhizIlu+MGn\nWl84cOhkQ2Yd7DYoIU24Xxe76ovZN1rbCyoE9J+n9nEl/NHD11hyxt3TJ1o9CxnP\nFUc91NvFnwqK9UzoY62UwGFN9Ann9O53DlF+QVm3hCXJtmC60AKiA7fycEazog38\n2PGrdZf4ZzCIaM/OXfw7/Iyb0qMnudFXDa/4yHhNrboYsopitnC/oHh7mQ6gTcoI\nNzi4pRLHv5A6j8mlzscZojjyrCGL5Ud/r2FccyLMTVgLdQ/AcjZrztY5nRnqKYvc\nKtwMCr5LOVgBSTyJDZ6NiR4nF1d4mnjo7cavnrf9cF6S9SV4RDdDp6+oTqz1/jU7\nJQ8Ffz2bszy4NMV9WPN4yP6sY9mA04kulWZ+sNTGBN6UwH2jm4sq3Fz9knj0mj6J\nyPhRFiDfdAKsvJyP3gQ/JAyMx8Abc815TK//eVSbxH+ErLSfqIspF50pYcJXxqfj\nqbfpHP6fVq9VuAieu0TSkNoq0c/RldvQdawOGPodrEFGt3I158NVdQljhrhZfnlk\nUjS3EL8M4D5NIq75CZuO\n=IKht\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-04T19:37:11Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/0f6eabe736af80b8060103a0c49135c88b8a272d","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKDBmNmVhYmU3MzZhZjgwYjgwNjAxMDNhMGM0OTEzNWM4OGI4YTI3MmQ","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/7c13f461af895ea3c70f3dede9f531fdfdc571fb","sha":"7c13f461af895ea3c70f3dede9f531fdfdc571fb","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/7c13f461af895ea3c70f3dede9f531fdfdc571fb"}],"sha":"0f6eabe736af80b8060103a0c49135c88b8a272d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/0f6eabe736af80b8060103a0c49135c88b8a272d"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/7c13f461af895ea3c70f3dede9f531fdfdc571fb/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-02T23:13:58Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-02T23:13:58Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Separate what a component is published as from how it is written (#58)\n\n* Separate what a component is published as from how it is written\n\nPlatform Management manages one of sixteen applications, and the next fifteen\nare not more rows. Seven are Helm charts pinned by `targetRevision` in an Argo\nApplication, which is a different artifact *and* a different way of writing a\nversion into Git — two axes, not one longer list.\n\nSchema 2 separates them. `artifact` says where versions come from; `pinnedIn`\nsays where they are written, and each entry names its renderer. Both are closed\nenums with one variant today, which is the shape that makes a second additive.\n\nThree consequences worth naming.\n\n`pinnedIn` moves from per-image to per-component. A chart has one version and\nno images to tell apart, so the two kinds have to answer \"where is this\nwritten\" in the same place — and a reader asking what moving a component\ntouches now finds one list instead of assembling it.\n\n`sourceRevision` becomes optional. An image carries the commit it was built\nfrom and Fabric checks that every image agrees; a chart published by somebody\nelse carries no such thing, and filling the field in would record something\nnobody observed.\n\n`renderer` is the field that would ruin this design if it grew a general\nescape. A `jsonPath`, a regex or a YAML pointer here would turn a trusted\nplatform document into an arbitrary repository-edit engine and Fabric into the\ndeputy that runs it — the same mistake as letting a caller name a file, one\nlevel further in. Every renderer knows exactly what it edits; a pin somewhere\nnew is a variant, reviewed like the code it is.\n\n# This one has no product value\n\nIt is the first of two. Helm discovery and the Argo renderer are the capability;\nthis is the shape they plug into, landed separately so that a schema change can\nbe verified against LucentRoot before a second artifact kind is built on it.\nGetting the shape wrong underneath a Helm implementation costs more than the\nextra hand-migration, and LucentRoot has one component, so that migration is a\nfew lines of break-glass edit.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Make the two constraints structural rather than commented\n\nTwo tightenings on schema 2, both turning a rule that was written down into\none that cannot be broken.\n\n# Provenance belongs to the artifact kind\n\n`sourceRevision` was optional on a shared `Desired`, with a comment saying an\nOCI component still needs one. That is a rule you can relax for Helm and\naccidentally relax for both: an OCI component that lost its provenance would\nhave parsed and stayed valid.\n\nIt now lives inside `Artifact::Oci`, non-optional. An `Oci` without it does not\nparse, and — once the variant exists — a `Helm` with it will not either,\nbecause the enum denies unknown fields. It is also where it belongs: the commit\nevery image was built from is what makes three images one release unit rather\nthan three that happen to share a tag.\n\n# The renderer is the variant, not a field beside one\n\n`pinnedIn` carried a `renderer` name and an optional `image`, and the\ncombinations that make no sense were rejected downstream. Now the renderer is\nthe *tag*: `kustomize-image` has a required `image` field, so a pin without one\nis not a pin this schema can describe, and a renderer this build does not\nimplement names no variant at all.\n\nThe difference shows in two of the tests written yesterday. Both still pass,\nand both now pass because the document does not parse rather than because\nsomething further in refused it — which means nothing downstream has to check,\nand the arm that handles a renderer cannot be reached without what it needs.\n\nThis is the property that keeps the design honest as renderers are added. A\n`jsonPath` or a regex here would turn a trusted platform document into an\narbitrary repository-edit engine; making each renderer a variant with exactly\nthe fields it uses is what makes that shape awkward to add rather than easy.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Say \"version 1\" during a migration, not \"unknown field\"\n\nThe version check ran after the whole document was parsed, so a manifest from\nan older schema failed on whichever field had moved. That gets the diagnostic\nexactly backwards: the one moment somebody most needs to be told \"this file is\nversion 1 and I read version 2\" is the moment they are told about an unknown\nkey instead.\n\nIt matters now because there is about to be such a moment. Deploying a build\nthat reads schema 2 into an environment whose manifest is still schema 1 is the\nmigration, and the window between the two is when an operator reads this\nmessage.\n\nThe version is now read on its own first, by a struct that is permissive about\neverything else, because its whole job is to answer the version question for a\ndocument this build may otherwise be unable to read at all.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"c72faf8d8931090a4e5c297c4b0762030642be51","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/c72faf8d8931090a4e5c297c4b0762030642be51"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/7c13f461af895ea3c70f3dede9f531fdfdc571fb","verification":{"payload":"tree c72faf8d8931090a4e5c297c4b0762030642be51\nparent d28eac902d7d1646990e616c231b45c9da9038d9\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1788390838 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788390838 +1200\n\nSeparate what a component is published as from how it is written (#58)\n\n* Separate what a component is published as from how it is written\n\nPlatform Management manages one of sixteen applications, and the next fifteen\nare not more rows. Seven are Helm charts pinned by `targetRevision` in an Argo\nApplication, which is a different artifact *and* a different way of writing a\nversion into Git — two axes, not one longer list.\n\nSchema 2 separates them. `artifact` says where versions come from; `pinnedIn`\nsays where they are written, and each entry names its renderer. Both are closed\nenums with one variant today, which is the shape that makes a second additive.\n\nThree consequences worth naming.\n\n`pinnedIn` moves from per-image to per-component. A chart has one version and\nno images to tell apart, so the two kinds have to answer \"where is this\nwritten\" in the same place — and a reader asking what moving a component\ntouches now finds one list instead of assembling it.\n\n`sourceRevision` becomes optional. An image carries the commit it was built\nfrom and Fabric checks that every image agrees; a chart published by somebody\nelse carries no such thing, and filling the field in would record something\nnobody observed.\n\n`renderer` is the field that would ruin this design if it grew a general\nescape. A `jsonPath`, a regex or a YAML pointer here would turn a trusted\nplatform document into an arbitrary repository-edit engine and Fabric into the\ndeputy that runs it — the same mistake as letting a caller name a file, one\nlevel further in. Every renderer knows exactly what it edits; a pin somewhere\nnew is a variant, reviewed like the code it is.\n\n# This one has no product value\n\nIt is the first of two. Helm discovery and the Argo renderer are the capability;\nthis is the shape they plug into, landed separately so that a schema change can\nbe verified against LucentRoot before a second artifact kind is built on it.\nGetting the shape wrong underneath a Helm implementation costs more than the\nextra hand-migration, and LucentRoot has one component, so that migration is a\nfew lines of break-glass edit.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Make the two constraints structural rather than commented\n\nTwo tightenings on schema 2, both turning a rule that was written down into\none that cannot be broken.\n\n# Provenance belongs to the artifact kind\n\n`sourceRevision` was optional on a shared `Desired`, with a comment saying an\nOCI component still needs one. That is a rule you can relax for Helm and\naccidentally relax for both: an OCI component that lost its provenance would\nhave parsed and stayed valid.\n\nIt now lives inside `Artifact::Oci`, non-optional. An `Oci` without it does not\nparse, and — once the variant exists — a `Helm` with it will not either,\nbecause the enum denies unknown fields. It is also where it belongs: the commit\nevery image was built from is what makes three images one release unit rather\nthan three that happen to share a tag.\n\n# The renderer is the variant, not a field beside one\n\n`pinnedIn` carried a `renderer` name and an optional `image`, and the\ncombinations that make no sense were rejected downstream. Now the renderer is\nthe *tag*: `kustomize-image` has a required `image` field, so a pin without one\nis not a pin this schema can describe, and a renderer this build does not\nimplement names no variant at all.\n\nThe difference shows in two of the tests written yesterday. Both still pass,\nand both now pass because the document does not parse rather than because\nsomething further in refused it — which means nothing downstream has to check,\nand the arm that handles a renderer cannot be reached without what it needs.\n\nThis is the property that keeps the design honest as renderers are added. A\n`jsonPath` or a regex here would turn a trusted platform document into an\narbitrary repository-edit engine; making each renderer a variant with exactly\nthe fields it uses is what makes that shape awkward to add rather than easy.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Say \"version 1\" during a migration, not \"unknown field\"\n\nThe version check ran after the whole document was parsed, so a manifest from\nan older schema failed on whichever field had moved. That gets the diagnostic\nexactly backwards: the one moment somebody most needs to be told \"this file is\nversion 1 and I read version 2\" is the moment they are told about an unknown\nkey instead.\n\nIt matters now because there is about to be such a moment. Deploying a build\nthat reads schema 2 into an environment whose manifest is still schema 1 is the\nmigration, and the window between the two is when an operator reads this\nmessage.\n\nThe version is now read on its own first, by a struct that is permissive about\neverything else, because its whole job is to answer the version question for a\ndocument this build may otherwise be unable to read at all.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqmK22CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAbgIQAIxj+YSOSt3R+DWD1sHPlh1B\n/uOo7G7p6UYNBd9xb1/D2A2r1ho6JpPGU7lMqQuAqdlybixAByNd4foQrkmUac75\nY7o11sfEd8vj3UDD0sCQnox0Yfm5WxPgPGmkWm7R1mvYAJb7OpAnr224FgjRvh7m\nlFjC2t98Li+8PvhDZflecQ+CPBXQxwjZAH4fxcfinMkQRi05wqYA5u/ewx61hIdC\nK7HVvG87sJjopVzHr9NjgCopNF4aR2Q6CnNrSr4n4/XxOEQGXzs0Cn62STWCRKJK\nMa2sEedMKbYFnJHlEpFuTI81ZW6Lrd09e2lG6s6KeXV7gkFlrkqXSNrIfmkLWFja\nxV9b17MfsF0BbE6N+PBSxkMaezxKI7W1IweUJfC8ibX/zLNm2y9f7zkcAQN+BZa1\nGmr9VPZtqwD1vd1KLc8tVwevY4MSD4x2Tg6gBUVz6VGS4gniuyTfx2fhag3j272h\nLsLk4CyyEv0nYlASWxf/sdRTGPxZfvq6YLHALUEku2Jud3EB2HDxXXxgZLLlKMtY\nyOo0Yh1yN0K3wug3T04l8GmWU1Of9OY8xnUHWAs7dYqa2SeMxj88Z5KpV5u88tFe\nyQY7GkxMOgZI45zkKVGiz0NnuFfePz3liGWEn11+k8Mu0/rCpK1Tg638CjY4T67Q\ncG6Oo8sWgL4pEB3wydh/\n=Iet4\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-02T23:13:59Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/7c13f461af895ea3c70f3dede9f531fdfdc571fb","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKDdjMTNmNDYxYWY4OTVlYTNjNzBmM2RlZGU5ZjUzMWZkZmRjNTcxZmI","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/d28eac902d7d1646990e616c231b45c9da9038d9","sha":"d28eac902d7d1646990e616c231b45c9da9038d9","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/d28eac902d7d1646990e616c231b45c9da9038d9"}],"sha":"7c13f461af895ea3c70f3dede9f531fdfdc571fb","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/7c13f461af895ea3c70f3dede9f531fdfdc571fb"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/d28eac902d7d1646990e616c231b45c9da9038d9/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-02T08:26:14Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-02T08:26:14Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Roll back without asking the registry five times (#57)\n\n* Roll back without asking the registry five times\n\nRolling back returned `504` against LucentRoot. Nothing was written — the\ncommit is atomic and it never got that far — but the operator saw a gateway\ntimeout and no explanation.\n\nThe cause was in the shape of the operation rather than anywhere slow.\n`roll_back` re-derived the whole candidate listing to check that the version\nasked for was in it, so a click paid for five versions of registry work, and\nthen the Git write on top. Measured against GHCR, one version is about three\nseconds — three images, a manifest and a config blob each, sequential — so the\nlisting alone is fifteen, and the request budget is thirty.\n\nIt resolves the one version now. Membership in that list was never the property\nthat mattered: what matters is that the version is in this component''s channel\nand series, sits strictly below what is desired, and resolves to a complete\ncoherent release unit. That is checked on the version named, for a fifth of the\nwork.\n\nOne consequence is deliberate. A version older than the listing''s bound is\nstill rollable if a caller names it — the bound limits what is *offered*, and a\nsafety rule that made a real release unrollable because five newer ones existed\nwould be a strange one.\n\nThe listing bound drops from ten to five for the same reason it exists at all.\nTen candidates was over thirty seconds and would have timed out the way the\nrollback did; five is comfortably inside the budget and further back than an\noperator rolls. It is a latency bound, and now says so — raising it needs\nconcurrency first, not a bigger number.\n\nThe fake registry counts resolutions, because how many a request makes is the\ndifference between an answer and a gateway timeout.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Refuse a rollback that brings its own digest\n\nThe version is re-resolved against the registry on the rollback request, not\ncarried over from the listing the console fetched moments ago. That was already\ntrue; it was not enforced, and the performance fix in this branch is exactly\nwhat would tempt somebody to change it — the browser already has the digests,\nso sending them back looks free.\n\nIt is not free. A digest a caller sends is the thing that would actually be\ndeployed, taken on trust. So the request body is `deny_unknown_fields`: a body\ncarrying one is refused rather than silently dropped, because a change that\nsent them should fail loudly instead of appearing to work.\n\nRe-resolving is not redundant with the listing either. It is what makes a\nversion withdrawn between the two requests a refusal rather than a deployment\nfrom a stale candidate object.\n\nTwo tests, and the second is the one that keeps the first honest: a body with a\ndigest is refused, and a body with only a version gets past deserialization to\nthe platform. Without the second, the first would pass if every body were\nrejected.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"23383e6968cc8812de2115f724c39047e3c769b2","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/23383e6968cc8812de2115f724c39047e3c769b2"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/d28eac902d7d1646990e616c231b45c9da9038d9","verification":{"payload":"tree 23383e6968cc8812de2115f724c39047e3c769b2\nparent f51c5f09ec568926516881256c2b7596569651ca\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1788337574 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788337574 +1200\n\nRoll back without asking the registry five times (#57)\n\n* Roll back without asking the registry five times\n\nRolling back returned `504` against LucentRoot. Nothing was written — the\ncommit is atomic and it never got that far — but the operator saw a gateway\ntimeout and no explanation.\n\nThe cause was in the shape of the operation rather than anywhere slow.\n`roll_back` re-derived the whole candidate listing to check that the version\nasked for was in it, so a click paid for five versions of registry work, and\nthen the Git write on top. Measured against GHCR, one version is about three\nseconds — three images, a manifest and a config blob each, sequential — so the\nlisting alone is fifteen, and the request budget is thirty.\n\nIt resolves the one version now. Membership in that list was never the property\nthat mattered: what matters is that the version is in this component''s channel\nand series, sits strictly below what is desired, and resolves to a complete\ncoherent release unit. That is checked on the version named, for a fifth of the\nwork.\n\nOne consequence is deliberate. A version older than the listing''s bound is\nstill rollable if a caller names it — the bound limits what is *offered*, and a\nsafety rule that made a real release unrollable because five newer ones existed\nwould be a strange one.\n\nThe listing bound drops from ten to five for the same reason it exists at all.\nTen candidates was over thirty seconds and would have timed out the way the\nrollback did; five is comfortably inside the budget and further back than an\noperator rolls. It is a latency bound, and now says so — raising it needs\nconcurrency first, not a bigger number.\n\nThe fake registry counts resolutions, because how many a request makes is the\ndifference between an answer and a gateway timeout.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Refuse a rollback that brings its own digest\n\nThe version is re-resolved against the registry on the rollback request, not\ncarried over from the listing the console fetched moments ago. That was already\ntrue; it was not enforced, and the performance fix in this branch is exactly\nwhat would tempt somebody to change it — the browser already has the digests,\nso sending them back looks free.\n\nIt is not free. A digest a caller sends is the thing that would actually be\ndeployed, taken on trust. So the request body is `deny_unknown_fields`: a body\ncarrying one is refused rather than silently dropped, because a change that\nsent them should fail loudly instead of appearing to work.\n\nRe-resolving is not redundant with the listing either. It is what makes a\nversion withdrawn between the two requests a refusal rather than a deployment\nfrom a stale candidate object.\n\nTwo tests, and the second is the one that keeps the first honest: a body with a\ndigest is refused, and a body with only a version gets past deserialization to\nthe platform. Without the second, the first would pass if every body were\nrejected.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJql92mCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA5BgQADhLsUKongwdD3asfeuBnHwe\nQlVm4pLx6kOhtwJuiMPNtZfgEWKblSShK7SLtVNvpQCc731Cri2eRZ3DTVTf3fBa\n0d+xVsyp7TBACl9B61qZrRCCCS4Mc2zg6yv1khe8f469XLoM3NKQqr5lLi6po7HH\nIvjJxtEEpijRpUD7blXGHuwwWTZtltLOoiBeKJ+WaCs6zQC4vc2HAw7t9DVSognj\nqOrT6vw83XEUFW6+f8lVm66UM019++ad7eBGw/sK7SGAzJp76w9aMeJkRjFYikij\nvlEB00gvzfQMPJ7W7nU2834YqkCmqEBvtNZU87D4gJdE0c5u9vD0JTu2I0L9+Frh\nLu/l7FbUPY2I5NmzIjAidmHvRJ8bq+246xaKifFp2Tga8VddYgB9UjVAdNq3M0YG\nZ9hLdr99uTTvrj/8AxjAA227BbzKM42zuZQeEmnZbFOwi2WdtigzI9QPP9w5d49x\nBu2Atd1zeDcDW9FDuTyPlLQd1FBUAa+aVKUE49X/QmQ3/8KVZ1tKuhbS9EBM7P3p\nsz1OmWqytupy8FPNa0GmT5D0A9Q+fdZhfvpDiHf3ujceIuGqsX8KgCDS13O4isic\nuiu46ByLb/31mcD3oaLxFYTl1bHjlPXF2o4bIuvNRnVFRE+f6i2boPEQCdiqrNzu\nTBMrdZQvRgJPy6KpR+Hn\n=s1fd\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-02T08:26:15Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/d28eac902d7d1646990e616c231b45c9da9038d9","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKGQyOGVhYzkwMmQ3ZDE2NDY5OTBlNjE2YzIzMWI0NWM5ZGE5MDM4ZDk","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/f51c5f09ec568926516881256c2b7596569651ca","sha":"f51c5f09ec568926516881256c2b7596569651ca","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/f51c5f09ec568926516881256c2b7596569651ca"}],"sha":"d28eac902d7d1646990e616c231b45c9da9038d9","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/d28eac902d7d1646990e616c231b45c9da9038d9"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/f51c5f09ec568926516881256c2b7596569651ca/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-02T00:32:54Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-02T00:32:54Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Let an operator put an environment back on something it ran (#56)\n\n* Let an operator put an environment back on something it ran\n\nThe brake stops an environment where it is. This puts it somewhere it was.\n\nAn operator names a version and nothing else. Every version they can name is\none Fabric resolved from the registry to a complete, coherent release unit —\nthree images that exist and agree about the commit they were built from — and\nwhat gets written is that unit, resolved again at the moment of the write: the\nversion, the source commit, three digests, and a hold, in one commit.\n\n# What cannot be expressed\n\nThere is no request shape carrying a digest, so a browser cannot name one and\ncannot name a wrong one. There is no version field to type into, so \"roll back\nto whatever Git used to say\" is not sayable. A version whose images were never\nwhole is not offered and is refused if asked for: rolling back to it would\ndeploy a composition nobody ever ran.\n\n`history` is `discover` in the opposite direction — same resolution, same\ncoherence rule, `Direction::Below`. It returns whole release units rather than\nbare versions, which is what leaves nothing for a caller to supply. Incomplete\nand incoherent candidates are dropped rather than reported: their diagnostics\nexist to explain why an environment did not *advance*, and an operator looking\nfor somewhere to retreat to has no use for a list of places they cannot go.\n\n# The hold is not optional\n\nAn environment moved backwards under a live automatic policy would be advanced\nforward again by the next sweep, and the operator would watch their rollback\ndisappear. So the hold travels in the same commit. In the adapter that is a\nprivate `HoldChange`; in the port it is the shape of the trait — `advance` has\nno hold parameter at all, which is what keeps the selector unable to clear one\nin order to succeed.\n\nIt records `reason: rollback` rather than `paused`, so a later reader can tell\nwhich act stopped the environment. The policy is untouched: this is \"put me\nhere and stay until I say otherwise\", not a decision to stop advancing forever.\n\n# The bound is reported\n\nEach candidate costs a registry call per image, so the search stops at ten. It\nsays so — `more: true`, rendered as \"older versions exist and are not listed\".\nA list that stopped quietly would read as \"this is everything there is\", which\nis the same near-truth as the `Available —` this console used to show.\n\nFour files were split to stay under the limits. A fifth split was started and\nbacked out: separating the port into `DesiredState` and `DesiredStateWrites`\nwould have made all seven implementors change for a mechanical reason, so the\nerror type moved instead.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Pin that rollback history is not a queue\n\nAn environment rolled back to preview.1, with preview.3 and preview.4\npublished since, resumes onto preview.4 — not onto preview.2, the version it\ncame from.\n\nThat is already how it behaves, and for a good reason: nothing remembers a\nrollback. The selector reads the floor from desired state and asks the registry\nwhat is above it, so \"what we came from\" is not something it could prefer even\nif somebody wanted it to. The test exists because the property is invisible in\nthe code that provides it, and the obvious wrong implementation — treating the\nversions skipped past as a queue to replay — would pass every other test here.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"0ce3b83addfd4e19048e5ccfbb917f21ca1c71cc","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/0ce3b83addfd4e19048e5ccfbb917f21ca1c71cc"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/f51c5f09ec568926516881256c2b7596569651ca","verification":{"payload":"tree 0ce3b83addfd4e19048e5ccfbb917f21ca1c71cc\nparent b08ba9e91725740591d430ae143f9c4fbc9cc406\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1788309174 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788309174 +1200\n\nLet an operator put an environment back on something it ran (#56)\n\n* Let an operator put an environment back on something it ran\n\nThe brake stops an environment where it is. This puts it somewhere it was.\n\nAn operator names a version and nothing else. Every version they can name is\none Fabric resolved from the registry to a complete, coherent release unit —\nthree images that exist and agree about the commit they were built from — and\nwhat gets written is that unit, resolved again at the moment of the write: the\nversion, the source commit, three digests, and a hold, in one commit.\n\n# What cannot be expressed\n\nThere is no request shape carrying a digest, so a browser cannot name one and\ncannot name a wrong one. There is no version field to type into, so \"roll back\nto whatever Git used to say\" is not sayable. A version whose images were never\nwhole is not offered and is refused if asked for: rolling back to it would\ndeploy a composition nobody ever ran.\n\n`history` is `discover` in the opposite direction — same resolution, same\ncoherence rule, `Direction::Below`. It returns whole release units rather than\nbare versions, which is what leaves nothing for a caller to supply. Incomplete\nand incoherent candidates are dropped rather than reported: their diagnostics\nexist to explain why an environment did not *advance*, and an operator looking\nfor somewhere to retreat to has no use for a list of places they cannot go.\n\n# The hold is not optional\n\nAn environment moved backwards under a live automatic policy would be advanced\nforward again by the next sweep, and the operator would watch their rollback\ndisappear. So the hold travels in the same commit. In the adapter that is a\nprivate `HoldChange`; in the port it is the shape of the trait — `advance` has\nno hold parameter at all, which is what keeps the selector unable to clear one\nin order to succeed.\n\nIt records `reason: rollback` rather than `paused`, so a later reader can tell\nwhich act stopped the environment. The policy is untouched: this is \"put me\nhere and stay until I say otherwise\", not a decision to stop advancing forever.\n\n# The bound is reported\n\nEach candidate costs a registry call per image, so the search stops at ten. It\nsays so — `more: true`, rendered as \"older versions exist and are not listed\".\nA list that stopped quietly would read as \"this is everything there is\", which\nis the same near-truth as the `Available —` this console used to show.\n\nFour files were split to stay under the limits. A fifth split was started and\nbacked out: separating the port into `DesiredState` and `DesiredStateWrites`\nwould have made all seven implementors change for a mechanical reason, so the\nerror type moved instead.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Pin that rollback history is not a queue\n\nAn environment rolled back to preview.1, with preview.3 and preview.4\npublished since, resumes onto preview.4 — not onto preview.2, the version it\ncame from.\n\nThat is already how it behaves, and for a good reason: nothing remembers a\nrollback. The selector reads the floor from desired state and asks the registry\nwhat is above it, so \"what we came from\" is not something it could prefer even\nif somebody wanted it to. The test exists because the property is invisible in\nthe code that provides it, and the obvious wrong implementation — treating the\nversions skipped past as a queue to replay — would pass every other test here.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJql262CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAolMQABZGxFug/WcrHyMI3TNsXXtW\nUzjz6jXa/6RWmSpeDHnMNp6qofMviSSj0tFeFJOv8XjXCHcrugn1R00IFUVh8qoP\nqEGqRhStx3amD+ZhLsMAsSv4RKYilydmDkpy4AOqFKsVrBujLwk25C1NhGUsEa4L\noptaFZtQlEzYYroPXXSV8CMVdVzYsvNm9nPLdxRaeHCxoz+le6bNvRse916MKyBn\nZ9dG5IXeYfQWTWTdQhQHoKAKO76rLEi8hFpkGlUkump1k7dXl/qfXiOlvMIV9arM\n+s6BWANEhGcZRCtcVghRYBhEzRtmrTLoq/Ly1mqrX/aSr1Jil7lE8ij2WHLg3XGp\nvMfMxKnIxXMujd1w9JnjXuS213BSMmE+k2MPtrikpop+b3r/eQ2GVzs1dNcIKziB\neGvkca9H6CKOVmgwvsg5v/9dHCID6VuGvsxaj4K6Mt82Ki2nGkJTfppj+euNc4zY\nhfmPFeRiL04XNfq2SXnnCkOXJDlyaUg7a2O90j0wXgqFKeO0MzREekBf3lIqfgQW\nfOilHp4/QzFYZK34R8HM1g9MinurFedvbIOtfNIsfI3q/QstSkXS81ZtzZFZpyHJ\nKgLIVVB3eMbZP5XcD4RqT0wzlm6uBcOyrCH19BpoO8Ev+TxCyj4TCKc9LKMyKX2v\n5qDZn7yg8aDAMX11cOwZ\n=81r8\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-02T00:32:54Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/f51c5f09ec568926516881256c2b7596569651ca","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKGY1MWM1ZjA5ZWM1Njg5MjY1MTY4ODEyNTZjMmI3NTk2NTY5NjUxY2E","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/b08ba9e91725740591d430ae143f9c4fbc9cc406","sha":"b08ba9e91725740591d430ae143f9c4fbc9cc406","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/b08ba9e91725740591d430ae143f9c4fbc9cc406"}],"sha":"f51c5f09ec568926516881256c2b7596569651ca","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/f51c5f09ec568926516881256c2b7596569651ca"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/b08ba9e91725740591d430ae143f9c4fbc9cc406/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-01T21:28:45Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-01T21:28:45Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Let an operator stop an environment without editing Git (#55)\n\nLucentRoot has been advancing unattended since the bootstrap, and the only way\nto stop it was to hand-edit the platform repository. Git stays the break-glass\npath and is expected to keep working; it should not also be the normal one.\n\nPause writes a hold and leaves the desired version alone. Resume lifts the hold\nand does nothing else — what happens next is the next sweep''s to decide, from\nwhat it observes then rather than from what was true when somebody clicked.\n\nNeither is a policy change. `update` stays `automatic` and the effective state\nreads `Automatic — Paused`: an operator who paused before testing a preview\nsaid \"not for now\", not \"not ever\", and the manifest must not record the\nsecond.\n\nRollback is deliberately not here. It changes the version *and* holds, in one\ncommit, and needs a version list drawn from observed registry history — so it\nis its own slice. Pause needs no registry at all, which is what makes it a\ncomplete capability on its own and worth landing first.\n\n# Two verbs, not a flag\n\n`advance` still cannot express a hold. That is what guarantees an automatic\npass cannot clear one in order to succeed, so pause and resume are separate\nport operations with a different caller rather than an argument to it. The\nsweep''s fakes now panic if either is ever reached, so the selector getting at\nan operator''s control is a test failure and not a surprise in production.\n\nPause also cannot move a version: the write never asks what `pinnedIn`\ndeclares, so it touches one file and no deployment overlay. There is no code\npath, which is better than a comment saying there should not be one.\n\n# Naming the component\n\nA caller may select an identifier the environment''s manifest already carries,\nand Fabric uses it as a lookup key and nothing else — never a repository path,\na file path, a registry location or any other locator. The read that precedes\nevery write is what enforces it, and a name the manifest does not carry selects\nnothing.\n\nA component that does not advance on its own is refused rather than accepted: a\nhold on it would stop nothing and show an operator `Paused` about something\nthat was never moving. Resuming something unheld writes no commit either — the\nrepository''s history is the audit trail, and an empty commit records that\nsomebody clicked rather than that anything happened.\n\n# Found on the way\n\n`components_manifest` skipped the environment check its sibling writes have, so\na manifest describing somewhere else was served under this environment''s name.\nA read, and still a lie. It goes through the same reader now.\n\n`status_mapping` had an arm wrapped in a block for no reason but to keep\n`match_same_arms` quiet. Saying why arms share a status is better than the\ntrick.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"ebe00d66f5ab5839f0cba38c7ef619400fa344df","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/ebe00d66f5ab5839f0cba38c7ef619400fa344df"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/b08ba9e91725740591d430ae143f9c4fbc9cc406","verification":{"payload":"tree ebe00d66f5ab5839f0cba38c7ef619400fa344df\nparent f20d6dc953b9d9a9bbce2cae52a0549da026fc27\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1788298125 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788298125 +1200\n\nLet an operator stop an environment without editing Git (#55)\n\nLucentRoot has been advancing unattended since the bootstrap, and the only way\nto stop it was to hand-edit the platform repository. Git stays the break-glass\npath and is expected to keep working; it should not also be the normal one.\n\nPause writes a hold and leaves the desired version alone. Resume lifts the hold\nand does nothing else — what happens next is the next sweep''s to decide, from\nwhat it observes then rather than from what was true when somebody clicked.\n\nNeither is a policy change. `update` stays `automatic` and the effective state\nreads `Automatic — Paused`: an operator who paused before testing a preview\nsaid \"not for now\", not \"not ever\", and the manifest must not record the\nsecond.\n\nRollback is deliberately not here. It changes the version *and* holds, in one\ncommit, and needs a version list drawn from observed registry history — so it\nis its own slice. Pause needs no registry at all, which is what makes it a\ncomplete capability on its own and worth landing first.\n\n# Two verbs, not a flag\n\n`advance` still cannot express a hold. That is what guarantees an automatic\npass cannot clear one in order to succeed, so pause and resume are separate\nport operations with a different caller rather than an argument to it. The\nsweep''s fakes now panic if either is ever reached, so the selector getting at\nan operator''s control is a test failure and not a surprise in production.\n\nPause also cannot move a version: the write never asks what `pinnedIn`\ndeclares, so it touches one file and no deployment overlay. There is no code\npath, which is better than a comment saying there should not be one.\n\n# Naming the component\n\nA caller may select an identifier the environment''s manifest already carries,\nand Fabric uses it as a lookup key and nothing else — never a repository path,\na file path, a registry location or any other locator. The read that precedes\nevery write is what enforces it, and a name the manifest does not carry selects\nnothing.\n\nA component that does not advance on its own is refused rather than accepted: a\nhold on it would stop nothing and show an operator `Paused` about something\nthat was never moving. Resuming something unheld writes no commit either — the\nrepository''s history is the audit trail, and an empty commit records that\nsomebody clicked rather than that anything happened.\n\n# Found on the way\n\n`components_manifest` skipped the environment check its sibling writes have, so\na manifest describing somewhere else was served under this environment''s name.\nA read, and still a lie. It goes through the same reader now.\n\n`status_mapping` had an arm wrapped in a block for no reason but to keep\n`match_same_arms` quiet. Saying why arms share a status is better than the\ntrick.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJql0ONCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAjdwQABzUvjZDVWC94KjsosF9/Gsx\nFdMaWpYQpxmk0VTwrMxtS+yL/uUm2LcQavXhZg9ZF3gOBrI3prUpUmuOrionv7+x\nd4LLSpRa/y2GWMUYdfGO921bImfSIXgPw3FvZMnjCvZTiV2u7YqruwsqUHRq2oAz\nhJ3T+PbHFrVtwCB8DADoAogfW8kOQeFyByGzqT5tndccuqbu3D2gjKYRL6ySpFfO\naXSFOFJeDXkTa0Td+ybhwluSDV6i3ErDHesrRxqLWWpnuu/imRO+knbRvI4EPm5o\nE16MqBP3mn99EeSPalMJ1HjHpabBcHoxyBwu4Jb6JMqfmuR+gJvofMjxidBrrLL5\nGBBeomCOI3SHW1qX1MJUcPGopOU3PLpRTpu19x8Gef0bDMoSc2UfXj9Uy+LmFN9F\nel4APak02Vfm0ppOcdrzsOAF3fB5aVL/I16MmCc9U53rxF5f9kCMf5CLf7Gyo+2t\nZOSnxfqcHHlD7FAF3VIpxXafcCLvT+3MZwsnsXWTgRo5LtFwZJjEMH4g+lzXdLwl\npL6FFCiChUKCC2sfAP3/kk4654n8P24xSXduGeVbU57jv1xUBIDQIsvDWTE0MZRK\n5QiBg9KBh9FWcJO0vpMOovkt0zKmW3FmzFNGvz4tVtYmtHcCFfSMhuCa6pun+1k0\ntWXYNMa0gDkkwaGJ0V0J\n=JNWT\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-01T21:28:46Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/b08ba9e91725740591d430ae143f9c4fbc9cc406","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKGIwOGJhOWU5MTcyNTc0MDU5MWQ0MzBhZTE0M2Y5YzRmYmM5Y2M0MDY","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/f20d6dc953b9d9a9bbce2cae52a0549da026fc27","sha":"f20d6dc953b9d9a9bbce2cae52a0549da026fc27","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/f20d6dc953b9d9a9bbce2cae52a0549da026fc27"}],"sha":"b08ba9e91725740591d430ae143f9c4fbc9cc406","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/b08ba9e91725740591d430ae143f9c4fbc9cc406"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/f20d6dc953b9d9a9bbce2cae52a0549da026fc27/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-01T20:05:04Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-01T20:05:04Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Give the narrower fact its narrower name (#54)\n\n`Discovery.available` was never the available version. It is the newest\neligible version *newer than desired* — which is the right value for a\nselector and the wrong name for anything. The console rendered it under the\nspecification''s `Available` heading, so an environment running the newest\npreview there was reported:\n\n    Desired    0.3.0-preview.2\n    Available  —\n\nwhich reads as \"nothing is available\" about a version that plainly is.\n\nThe field is now `newer`, from discovery through the API to the panel, and the\nrow reads `Newer version`. Nothing about the computation changed. What changed\nis that the type no longer promises more than it observed: nothing here looks\nat whether the desired version is still published, so no name it carries may\nimply that it did.\n\nTwo fixes were rejected on the way. Inferring \"desired exists, therefore it is\navailable\" is sound until an artifact is deleted or a registry is unreachable,\nand then the console is confidently wrong about the one thing an operator\nopened it to learn. Widening discovery to enumerate what a registry holds is\nthe eventual answer, but it belongs to a versions view — doing it now would be\na broader scan on every sweep for no new operator capability, and candidates\nare deliberately only examined above the floor because that is what stops\nautomatic selection moving an environment backwards.\n\nThe rename turned up one thing worth having found. `reconcile` used to report\n`available: Some(unit.version)` after advancing: correct under the old name,\nand self-contradictory under the new one, since it would claim something newer\nexists and name the version it also reports as desired. It reports `None`,\nwhich is what \"what would Fabric advance to next\" actually answers.\n\nThe panel splits into two groups. The first is the three-state model — desired,\nnewer, running. The second is the decision: policy, and whether desired state\nneeds advancing. `Newer version` and `Desired state` overlap in the steady\nstate and that is fine; they answer different questions.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"7f537aff01a28dcd7847bcef128128ca04c73fd7","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/7f537aff01a28dcd7847bcef128128ca04c73fd7"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/f20d6dc953b9d9a9bbce2cae52a0549da026fc27","verification":{"payload":"tree 7f537aff01a28dcd7847bcef128128ca04c73fd7\nparent 936e77614e931f620d13d5d3691c7f1571942805\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1788293104 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788293104 +1200\n\nGive the narrower fact its narrower name (#54)\n\n`Discovery.available` was never the available version. It is the newest\neligible version *newer than desired* — which is the right value for a\nselector and the wrong name for anything. The console rendered it under the\nspecification''s `Available` heading, so an environment running the newest\npreview there was reported:\n\n    Desired    0.3.0-preview.2\n    Available  —\n\nwhich reads as \"nothing is available\" about a version that plainly is.\n\nThe field is now `newer`, from discovery through the API to the panel, and the\nrow reads `Newer version`. Nothing about the computation changed. What changed\nis that the type no longer promises more than it observed: nothing here looks\nat whether the desired version is still published, so no name it carries may\nimply that it did.\n\nTwo fixes were rejected on the way. Inferring \"desired exists, therefore it is\navailable\" is sound until an artifact is deleted or a registry is unreachable,\nand then the console is confidently wrong about the one thing an operator\nopened it to learn. Widening discovery to enumerate what a registry holds is\nthe eventual answer, but it belongs to a versions view — doing it now would be\na broader scan on every sweep for no new operator capability, and candidates\nare deliberately only examined above the floor because that is what stops\nautomatic selection moving an environment backwards.\n\nThe rename turned up one thing worth having found. `reconcile` used to report\n`available: Some(unit.version)` after advancing: correct under the old name,\nand self-contradictory under the new one, since it would claim something newer\nexists and name the version it also reports as desired. It reports `None`,\nwhich is what \"what would Fabric advance to next\" actually answers.\n\nThe panel splits into two groups. The first is the three-state model — desired,\nnewer, running. The second is the decision: policy, and whether desired state\nneeds advancing. `Newer version` and `Desired state` overlap in the steady\nstate and that is fine; they answer different questions.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqly/wCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAASDQQAFPPet6LrxtEElqALAIwoZ86\nIqEr0ntpwQZ93I9NjrohYCyh6JDRvdqYNxNVMsooNzNUbL8MfFQeASo7Z0v8U8i5\nUw52eyOmdY61uf+S3f5zgPpnoBUaMpcr0kQxavS4810Ok00NdztHak6Jr1CkVXg0\nAfoN0o52Q019QBe+uaNkDdCFMfFGk9LCfqklVAN3pRcDhet9V1YPaDH76zKCo8kD\nZxUmiROzMyfhDFCD2j+C9F0Qt1GZDTP9wgdYT/XmkT1geosqr68oY/MJ8siYqZ3R\nb+VeMMw+AmRY2/GKqm0j/lauz1OZLfPWBn1CsRPp7EFJ9zuc+Wk6QVE+dmnSTGzn\nAp+vQ04diWxgyZVW4ywkqKBdNyPaf8ojrWWuShjzfke5oaDpFvgj6aNC4A1Pdja5\nR5UfqBHMW1Twdga6GsG1k0w7GC0lAK8JAFcki040rYfybdSdXesId27bOKaq5GG6\nXCzWXWQWX4j2HIA46pIDkdlxinXUGmQvqwOaoWy0gAWXo13zaJIw6jAkLTI4SUx/\nk3O42jUe7+kuktbBg+gP1+V8smsi0NN7H0nVxObdCEM48KDw+m0zWf77eaw/sN46\nP+b/XWkg8oujwufFfM+9b/xmcRGwl3bYojWMn5xx0nx0Gp9OujGu40MUzw8BNAYm\naByd1E+PIxXvoGL+UpUq\n=Z6Xi\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-01T20:05:05Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/f20d6dc953b9d9a9bbce2cae52a0549da026fc27","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKGYyMGQ2ZGM5NTNiOWQ5YTliYmNlMmNhZTUyYTA1NDlkYTAyNmZjMjc","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/936e77614e931f620d13d5d3691c7f1571942805","sha":"936e77614e931f620d13d5d3691c7f1571942805","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/936e77614e931f620d13d5d3691c7f1571942805"}],"sha":"f20d6dc953b9d9a9bbce2cae52a0549da026fc27","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/f20d6dc953b9d9a9bbce2cae52a0549da026fc27"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/936e77614e931f620d13d5d3691c7f1571942805/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-01T15:30:24Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-01T15:30:24Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Give platform management its own application to connect (#53)\n\n* Give platform management its own application to connect\n\nThe connection flow was already indifferent to what it connects. What was\nstill client-shaped was everything around it: one manifest with the client''s\ncallbacks baked in, one service in the composition root, one set of routes,\none field in router state. So platform management had a target and no way for\nan operator to ever give it a repository.\n\nThree seams now carry the difference, and nothing else does.\n\n`AppPurpose` supplies the application''s name and its callback segment. Both\nhave to be per-purpose — GitHub rejects the second application to ask for a\nname, and a browser handed the wrong callback finishes one flow into the\nother''s routes. Client configuration keeps `SaaS Fabric` and\n`/api/integrations/git/…` byte for byte; deployments already have an\napplication under that name with those callbacks stored against it.\n\n`Flow` is a type, not a path segment. The handlers are written once and\nmounted twice, and which service a route acts on is decided by the turbofish\nit is mounted with. A caller who could name an integration is one refactor\naway from naming a third that does not exist, which is the shape §15 forbids\nand the one already closed once in `GET /api/platform`.\n\n`IntegrationKind` decides the store paths, as it already did.\n\nBoth services are built over one secret-store client rather than two, which\nalso removes the second login `client_secrets` used to cost.\n\nOne correction while wiring it: a stored integration that could not be bound\nreported as \"nothing is connected\", which would send an operator to connect\nsomething they already have. `PlatformDesiredState` now has three states, and\nanswers `Unavailable` for the one in the middle. `IntegrationTarget` gained\n`unusable`, defaulting to nothing, because client configuration reports its\nhealth from what its sweep observes and should not be made to invent somewhere\nto put this. An unreadable store is deliberately not reported that way — it\nsays nothing about whether a record is in it.\n\nThe console still only shows the client integration. That is the next change.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Show both integrations, and what to do about each\n\nThe API grew a second connection flow and the console still knew about one.\nAn operator could reach `/api/integrations/platform` with curl and nothing\nelse, which is not a feature.\n\nIntegrations is now a place: two cards, one per integration, each saying what\nstate it is in and offering the one action that helps.\n\nThe state vocabulary is shared, and it is the point. `Unavailable` means the\nintegration exists and does not work — distinct from `Not connected`, which\nmeans nobody has made one. An operator shown \"not connected\" about something\nthey connected last week goes and connects it a second time instead of finding\nout why the first one stopped, so that card offers **Reconnect** and\n**Disconnect** and never **Connect**. Client configuration gets the same three\nwords rather than its old four: `invalid` and `error` differ in why the\nplatform cannot read and not in what an operator does next.\n\nPlatform Management''s state needs both halves of the answer — the application''s\nlifecycle from `/api/integrations/platform`, and whether reading through it\nworks from `/api/platform`. An application that does not exist yet is Not\nconnected however the platform route answers; only once there is one does that\nroute decide between Connected and Unavailable.\n\nRepositories are never typed. The picker is fed from what that installation\nreaches, read from the host each time, and the control plane refuses anything\nelse anyway. The one free-text field in either flow is the organisation an\napplication is created in, which is not a repository.\n\n`GitIntegration` became `ConnectApplication` and takes its endpoints as a\nprop, the way the handlers take a `Flow`: which application it creates is\ndecided by whoever renders it, and there is no default to fall back to. Same\nfor `RepositoryPicker`.\n\nEnvironment policy is deliberately not here. Connecting a repository\nestablishes authority; whether an environment advances automatically is a\ndifferent concern with a different blast radius, and it stays beside the\nenvironment it governs.\n\nTwo CodeQL alerts, both mine and both fair: `{outcome:?}` debug-formats an\n`IntegrationError` whose variants carry text from the Git host and the stores.\nHarmless in a test, and not a habit worth setting in the file that reads as\nthe example of how to test this.\n\nNot verified visually: the only operator posture is OIDC, so a local console\nneeds a Keycloak. The states are covered by tests that render the real\ncomponents; the layout gets looked at during the bootstrap.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"6661f9ecf51689f7aeee99413ddbec9d74543427","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/6661f9ecf51689f7aeee99413ddbec9d74543427"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/936e77614e931f620d13d5d3691c7f1571942805","verification":{"payload":"tree 6661f9ecf51689f7aeee99413ddbec9d74543427\nparent 2a7e26a0a4667b151c3db0a98f3a68a003695548\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1788276624 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788276624 +1200\n\nGive platform management its own application to connect (#53)\n\n* Give platform management its own application to connect\n\nThe connection flow was already indifferent to what it connects. What was\nstill client-shaped was everything around it: one manifest with the client''s\ncallbacks baked in, one service in the composition root, one set of routes,\none field in router state. So platform management had a target and no way for\nan operator to ever give it a repository.\n\nThree seams now carry the difference, and nothing else does.\n\n`AppPurpose` supplies the application''s name and its callback segment. Both\nhave to be per-purpose — GitHub rejects the second application to ask for a\nname, and a browser handed the wrong callback finishes one flow into the\nother''s routes. Client configuration keeps `SaaS Fabric` and\n`/api/integrations/git/…` byte for byte; deployments already have an\napplication under that name with those callbacks stored against it.\n\n`Flow` is a type, not a path segment. The handlers are written once and\nmounted twice, and which service a route acts on is decided by the turbofish\nit is mounted with. A caller who could name an integration is one refactor\naway from naming a third that does not exist, which is the shape §15 forbids\nand the one already closed once in `GET /api/platform`.\n\n`IntegrationKind` decides the store paths, as it already did.\n\nBoth services are built over one secret-store client rather than two, which\nalso removes the second login `client_secrets` used to cost.\n\nOne correction while wiring it: a stored integration that could not be bound\nreported as \"nothing is connected\", which would send an operator to connect\nsomething they already have. `PlatformDesiredState` now has three states, and\nanswers `Unavailable` for the one in the middle. `IntegrationTarget` gained\n`unusable`, defaulting to nothing, because client configuration reports its\nhealth from what its sweep observes and should not be made to invent somewhere\nto put this. An unreadable store is deliberately not reported that way — it\nsays nothing about whether a record is in it.\n\nThe console still only shows the client integration. That is the next change.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Show both integrations, and what to do about each\n\nThe API grew a second connection flow and the console still knew about one.\nAn operator could reach `/api/integrations/platform` with curl and nothing\nelse, which is not a feature.\n\nIntegrations is now a place: two cards, one per integration, each saying what\nstate it is in and offering the one action that helps.\n\nThe state vocabulary is shared, and it is the point. `Unavailable` means the\nintegration exists and does not work — distinct from `Not connected`, which\nmeans nobody has made one. An operator shown \"not connected\" about something\nthey connected last week goes and connects it a second time instead of finding\nout why the first one stopped, so that card offers **Reconnect** and\n**Disconnect** and never **Connect**. Client configuration gets the same three\nwords rather than its old four: `invalid` and `error` differ in why the\nplatform cannot read and not in what an operator does next.\n\nPlatform Management''s state needs both halves of the answer — the application''s\nlifecycle from `/api/integrations/platform`, and whether reading through it\nworks from `/api/platform`. An application that does not exist yet is Not\nconnected however the platform route answers; only once there is one does that\nroute decide between Connected and Unavailable.\n\nRepositories are never typed. The picker is fed from what that installation\nreaches, read from the host each time, and the control plane refuses anything\nelse anyway. The one free-text field in either flow is the organisation an\napplication is created in, which is not a repository.\n\n`GitIntegration` became `ConnectApplication` and takes its endpoints as a\nprop, the way the handlers take a `Flow`: which application it creates is\ndecided by whoever renders it, and there is no default to fall back to. Same\nfor `RepositoryPicker`.\n\nEnvironment policy is deliberately not here. Connecting a repository\nestablishes authority; whether an environment advances automatically is a\ndifferent concern with a different blast radius, and it stays beside the\nenvironment it governs.\n\nTwo CodeQL alerts, both mine and both fair: `{outcome:?}` debug-formats an\n`IntegrationError` whose variants carry text from the Git host and the stores.\nHarmless in a test, and not a habit worth setting in the file that reads as\nthe example of how to test this.\n\nNot verified visually: the only operator posture is OIDC, so a local console\nneeds a Keycloak. The states are covered by tests that render the real\ncomponents; the layout gets looked at during the bootstrap.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqlu+QCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAuCAQABeeoz2GMCmP+sNFD9KfvWq7\nZFj1iyISJQKE0o3vA3+p9hDqTXNPbD+MIADb4mZ0u/HCt6dcaCmfIXgAdDsiw6Lp\nckNpU6qvrSnEELkfwPi7mwO9BcT0SOeHLdfz5n3bZ7ah8iMPEqBcz4vWRmuE0Y+p\nddizBfFt0czeVYoHWoDLr+7LD3IcSZ2gEbKtasZ0BwIsX0h5dISQlDtDTA2G71bs\nYbwjSK1mEAt8iUVvjuqHYLauKnYZGaeVcm9lMlScQsMfHOJpsWE+SidWJjgCHlOf\nJQxnAqKgqn5FTv1XbomhZ7eRCjhsTlz9R0YKw2r88R4oDe2ztz0Q8jCaCeWDiL0k\nof0niYQMpHmgWzCNqqd7AyonCRM3mMO1yZDBIXI3WI+lkAJEdqvSU/LYBjlFT3uF\nSzljCx7TtXKMseRXllCSyUSNBQn0RMmtuP386Ma7MWRmVky71YZwJJys0+Qy07DY\ncJqdEAtVQTXafs9m9poeb3xFDM/ENV3k7qxRQd/PMCHRAZhdHpLfhhwkoFEwXHJq\n/JoTLzKahmvdQjMiz/m3OwW/Rs+Cjo1tWt5OFY8paXF3Tf+R20mWZlEMU0b33nKN\nMQGaFUpHvcKdcJuWabv2jazm0fQoHx5rt1S5fMMljeDWAoCrc2Cbs6SmrAdBN8Uw\nef0iPnRx9LLIY3YB015/\n=kfWm\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-01T15:47:00Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/936e77614e931f620d13d5d3691c7f1571942805","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKDkzNmU3NzYxNGU5MzFmNjIwZDEzZDVkMzY5MWM3ZjE1NzE5NDI4MDU","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/2a7e26a0a4667b151c3db0a98f3a68a003695548","sha":"2a7e26a0a4667b151c3db0a98f3a68a003695548","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/2a7e26a0a4667b151c3db0a98f3a68a003695548"}],"sha":"936e77614e931f620d13d5d3691c7f1571942805","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/936e77614e931f620d13d5d3691c7f1571942805"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/2a7e26a0a4667b151c3db0a98f3a68a003695548/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-01T14:47:59Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-01T14:47:59Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Make the connection flow indifferent to what it connects (#52)\n\nThe connection flow -- create an application, install it, choose a repository,\nstore the record, restore it on restart -- is identical for both integrations.\nWhat differs is the last step: client configuration binds a client repository,\nplatform management binds a platform desired-state repository, and the two have\nnothing in common but the moment they happen.\n\nHolding a factory and a binding directly made that last step client-shaped all\nthe way down, so a second integration would have meant a second copy of the\ncallback correlation, the key handling and the record semantics -- the parts\nthat are genuinely the same and genuinely security-sensitive.\n\n`IntegrationTarget` is that last step, as a port. Two fields become one, and\n`ClientConfigurationTarget` is the existing behaviour with a name.\n\nNo behaviour changes. The client flow binds and unbinds exactly as it did, and\nits tests are unchanged apart from constructing the target the service now\ntakes.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"3a455eac141b6a10ee693b037367270eceaf7623","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/3a455eac141b6a10ee693b037367270eceaf7623"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/2a7e26a0a4667b151c3db0a98f3a68a003695548","verification":{"payload":"tree 3a455eac141b6a10ee693b037367270eceaf7623\nparent f58dcd9effcbbc6ddb4de017782c165b8f2d6968\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1788274079 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788274079 +1200\n\nMake the connection flow indifferent to what it connects (#52)\n\nThe connection flow -- create an application, install it, choose a repository,\nstore the record, restore it on restart -- is identical for both integrations.\nWhat differs is the last step: client configuration binds a client repository,\nplatform management binds a platform desired-state repository, and the two have\nnothing in common but the moment they happen.\n\nHolding a factory and a binding directly made that last step client-shaped all\nthe way down, so a second integration would have meant a second copy of the\ncallback correlation, the key handling and the record semantics -- the parts\nthat are genuinely the same and genuinely security-sensitive.\n\n`IntegrationTarget` is that last step, as a port. Two fields become one, and\n`ClientConfigurationTarget` is the existing behaviour with a name.\n\nNo behaviour changes. The client flow binds and unbinds exactly as it did, and\nits tests are unchanged apart from constructing the target the service now\ntakes.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqluWfCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAYQMQAETA3+IMrzPQHzNzg0z8XtYd\nAiJQUrc4ORUd86MEQknoTgwpzmC+nKJ/GYTjGqomuDzhOW7NKi7Vznjcznvqdq+u\nrZMtRmbUZgFQ+5ZJPaMOV381xqHUlAIqQDimPbZMcERZkDxl1OTRF7BUzsUGAroM\ng0lnDC/7h/wXwK/IkG02dibZ8+7nntu5+gIs3npc3a0fNuQvT/V8pLova/sE6Av/\n7vIBuWse4aHtnBr+YIcVY6tO0Qoa9brlqyeuEERxecUBuzO1/ZF5zPR46lDHRhEd\nnBssqpTnxGl3A7Cd6h6Zs8ClEj8KJzMsgyqpRwcqZm8jS447zxXu4xVn+7q6Aa1L\nnNbrbBZuneC0qzHSkAPHSiJBBO+5EUoXyKlwmGrnzEGo/SV4qoAewO25JEgbs9wW\n2BcyS0d8BXlgHQ1lZ0AVizW/rEjSeoBvs0voiiCTkIBT99tKs5MBaknTqDT0A4f0\nmh4cQmCZbFTCNHhcx7bJo37pG2aUXz5KejWel+NLBXuwpnzfw6RX9rgWhNpZ+Owf\nj5LF2wfpd6dfAWUsQ+S4XQes3YmyM0e2Ucp4CiSgs7+CVWG/PBXNfGfGa8b7jUVf\nqBtk0hoIey9IHHjb0Zv3NKCK8XQYXcVg4Lv8mtCAry8KnTSCFZPYqoHqMXzl/okq\nVv70qY/XpgupCyKXZav1\n=Q5H/\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-01T15:02:08Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/2a7e26a0a4667b151c3db0a98f3a68a003695548","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKDJhN2UyNmEwYTQ2NjdiMTUxYzNkYjBhOThmM2E2OGEwMDM2OTU1NDg","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/f58dcd9effcbbc6ddb4de017782c165b8f2d6968","sha":"f58dcd9effcbbc6ddb4de017782c165b8f2d6968","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/f58dcd9effcbbc6ddb4de017782c165b8f2d6968"}],"sha":"2a7e26a0a4667b151c3db0a98f3a68a003695548","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/2a7e26a0a4667b151c3db0a98f3a68a003695548"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/f58dcd9effcbbc6ddb4de017782c165b8f2d6968/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-01T14:22:44Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-01T14:22:44Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Key the integration store, and leave the live client path where it is (#51)\n\n`load()` / `save()` / `clear()` had become false as an abstraction. There are\ntwo Git integrations -- client configuration and platform management -- and the\nspecification requires them to be separately installable, configurable and\nremovable. A store that held exactly one could not do that.\n\nEvery operation now names its integration, and the kind is a closed enum rather\nthan a string. A string would let a caller invent one, and the first place that\nwould show is a record written to a path nobody chose. There are exactly two,\nthey are known at compile time, and a request reaches one by hitting its route\nrather than by naming it.\n\nThe kind is also not optional and has no default. A defaulted parameter would\nlet a caller written before the second integration existed keep working *and*\nkeep reaching the first one, which is the shape of bug that only shows up as\none integration overwriting another.\n\n# Nothing that exists moves\n\n    secret/platform/saas-fabric/instances/master/\n    ├── git/integration                                 unchanged\n    ├── git/app-private-key                             unchanged\n    └── integrations/platform-management/\n        ├── integration                                 new\n        └── app-private-key                             new\n\nThe asymmetry is deliberate. Relocating a live integration so that two paths\nlook alike is a migration whose upside is tidiness and whose downside is a\nplatform that has forgotten how to reach client configuration. The layout\nrecords which one came first, because that is what happened.\n\n# The acceptance criteria, as tests\n\n    the client record loads from git/integration          unchanged\n    saving client configuration writes there              unchanged\n    saving platform management writes elsewhere            new path\n    clearing one names only its own path                   isolation\n    saving one leaves the other absent                     isolation\n    clearing one leaves the other connected                isolation\n    nothing stored for a kind                              absence, not failure\n    an unknown kind                                        does not compile\n\nThree mutations, three named failures: both records sharing a path, both keys\nsharing a name, and the store ignoring the kind it was given.\n\n# A note on how those mutations were run\n\nThe first attempt timed out mid-run and was killed before it restored the file,\nleaving the repository with a deliberately broken path in it. The next run\nreported \"anchor absent\" rather than a passing suite, which is how it was\ncaught -- a mutation harness that cannot tell \"nothing failed\" from \"nothing was\napplied\" is worse than none, and this session had already produced two of those.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"b9b5a90a6299115bc594c607d2009e97076f665d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/b9b5a90a6299115bc594c607d2009e97076f665d"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/f58dcd9effcbbc6ddb4de017782c165b8f2d6968","verification":{"payload":"tree b9b5a90a6299115bc594c607d2009e97076f665d\nparent 800769cc6fa15e9140b9e23bc56d0d90c4078ce3\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1788272564 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788272564 +1200\n\nKey the integration store, and leave the live client path where it is (#51)\n\n`load()` / `save()` / `clear()` had become false as an abstraction. There are\ntwo Git integrations -- client configuration and platform management -- and the\nspecification requires them to be separately installable, configurable and\nremovable. A store that held exactly one could not do that.\n\nEvery operation now names its integration, and the kind is a closed enum rather\nthan a string. A string would let a caller invent one, and the first place that\nwould show is a record written to a path nobody chose. There are exactly two,\nthey are known at compile time, and a request reaches one by hitting its route\nrather than by naming it.\n\nThe kind is also not optional and has no default. A defaulted parameter would\nlet a caller written before the second integration existed keep working *and*\nkeep reaching the first one, which is the shape of bug that only shows up as\none integration overwriting another.\n\n# Nothing that exists moves\n\n    secret/platform/saas-fabric/instances/master/\n    ├── git/integration                                 unchanged\n    ├── git/app-private-key                             unchanged\n    └── integrations/platform-management/\n        ├── integration                                 new\n        └── app-private-key                             new\n\nThe asymmetry is deliberate. Relocating a live integration so that two paths\nlook alike is a migration whose upside is tidiness and whose downside is a\nplatform that has forgotten how to reach client configuration. The layout\nrecords which one came first, because that is what happened.\n\n# The acceptance criteria, as tests\n\n    the client record loads from git/integration          unchanged\n    saving client configuration writes there              unchanged\n    saving platform management writes elsewhere            new path\n    clearing one names only its own path                   isolation\n    saving one leaves the other absent                     isolation\n    clearing one leaves the other connected                isolation\n    nothing stored for a kind                              absence, not failure\n    an unknown kind                                        does not compile\n\nThree mutations, three named failures: both records sharing a path, both keys\nsharing a name, and the store ignoring the kind it was given.\n\n# A note on how those mutations were run\n\nThe first attempt timed out mid-run and was killed before it restored the file,\nleaving the repository with a deliberately broken path in it. The next run\nreported \"anchor absent\" rather than a passing suite, which is how it was\ncaught -- a mutation harness that cannot tell \"nothing failed\" from \"nothing was\napplied\" is worse than none, and this session had already produced two of those.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqlt+0CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA5VQQAJMK+L5TL49YCSvKghM9yOij\nE6/Mqdv5RrbKh26d3/AbR/TI8VwUe3omN0yw03M+z6be0rJifpEfOE2uSd32RrjD\nsjAkrCAzhS95Q7YU1RJ4Lg8AKuH6Ue8kDgkcEyUMzLZFXrM/NLeDMZ/hpTVMKZ+V\nvTT88xalw8L1048fahUYg+GYv/Ic3GTQkTNhEwIHZVNHr4tKvXWXbqEYwO9tvYr7\nGeUNZGb298OobYLysT6Rb9fj3i9zS3eEy+9ciYwSnGW76eYGENGR6qspsm/+I5U4\nGAa5t6eo6OcHIHaLRSlpumaYQdTyzFksRxnbfqNk7xV/GGI8pkiR9dgftlE0E9lP\nLqjyQ/IOr3ekqiNMVSC4ABwlcekAFof8Md8O4G4YYT8ZDw6M7L/5fk52+IOeeCqv\nDxX6WFojGb78IX4JPFshePAeG8q1SLFc/M3ZbFLNKdthU0Z3T0ijKPoT4inLEDYo\nVBQ5JlX5I4KQBhztz0pFTUXk/OZHJq5rz34D6pLLd8wCtb47aVIapKpHmqAN3TGm\nPpXJo/GfHP6YWUeXBPNZZ0zIWeN8pkJq7kjDLNB3QrlEIkNJ1MkfM+KPDgUeljK+\nSC/nC3MuQfx9pYQIHzFbM+gRGzBDyjPUO6NlCJjXof8zeDQZ9O+w6DQwRRPa3gI6\nnYAZM7e4cUbYBv1+uf33\n=DIdM\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-01T14:29:35Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/f58dcd9effcbbc6ddb4de017782c165b8f2d6968","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKGY1OGRjZDllZmZjYmJjNmRkYjRkZTAxNzc4MmMxNjViOGYyZDY5Njg","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/800769cc6fa15e9140b9e23bc56d0d90c4078ce3","sha":"800769cc6fa15e9140b9e23bc56d0d90c4078ce3","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/800769cc6fa15e9140b9e23bc56d0d90c4078ce3"}],"sha":"f58dcd9effcbbc6ddb4de017782c165b8f2d6968","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/f58dcd9effcbbc6ddb4de017782c165b8f2d6968"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/800769cc6fa15e9140b9e23bc56d0d90c4078ce3/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-01T09:37:18Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-01T09:37:18Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Make Platform Management late-bound, and stop asking a deployment for a repository (#50)\n\nThe repository and its credential are not configuration. An operator installs a\nGitHub App and picks a repository, and the platform stores what it learns doing\nso -- so there is nothing to build from at startup, and a control plane that\nrefused to start without one could not be used to connect one.\n\n`[platform_management]` shrinks to what a deployment genuinely knows:\n\n    environment                       which environment this control plane is\n    registry                          where artifacts are published\n    reconciliation_interval_seconds   how often to sweep\n\nNo repository. No credential. Both are operator-managed integration state, and\nthis deployment goes back to supplying no credential of any kind -- which is\nwhat the manifest and the README have said all along, and what the interim\nconfig field would have quietly made untrue.\n\n# Three states, not two\n\n    nothing connected      an operator has not connected a repository\n    connected and failing  it is connected and cannot be read\n    connected and working\n\n`DesiredStateError::NotConnected` is the first, and it is deliberately not a\nfailure. Collapsing it into one would tell an operator \"nothing is connected\"\nabout an integration they connected last week, and they would go and connect it\nagain rather than find out why it stopped working.\n\nThe console shows the first two identically as *not connected* only where they\nmean the same thing to it -- \"this deployment does no platform management\" and\n\"nobody has connected one\" are both \"there is nothing here yet\". A connected\nrepository that fails is a 503 and says so.\n\n# The sweep tolerates it\n\nNot connected is a no-op that records nothing. The record answers \"what did the\nlast attempt find\", and a tick that found nothing to look at was not an\nattempt -- a \"last check failed\" against an integration nobody has made would\nsend an operator looking for a fault instead of a connection.\n\nIt is also not logged. At a minute''s interval that would fill a log with the\nfact that nobody has done something yet, which the console already says.\n\n# What has not moved\n\n\"Configured and broken fails startup\" still holds. What changed is which things\nare configuration: an absent *integration* is legitimate runtime state, while a\ndeployment that states its own environment or registry wrongly still refuses to\nstart.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"a330b2c67d67a997fb89381bd61a4771490c6832","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/a330b2c67d67a997fb89381bd61a4771490c6832"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/800769cc6fa15e9140b9e23bc56d0d90c4078ce3","verification":{"payload":"tree a330b2c67d67a997fb89381bd61a4771490c6832\nparent d57319fc39997716867a6e1ee7a0c2d92fdf7d08\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1788255438 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788255438 +1200\n\nMake Platform Management late-bound, and stop asking a deployment for a repository (#50)\n\nThe repository and its credential are not configuration. An operator installs a\nGitHub App and picks a repository, and the platform stores what it learns doing\nso -- so there is nothing to build from at startup, and a control plane that\nrefused to start without one could not be used to connect one.\n\n`[platform_management]` shrinks to what a deployment genuinely knows:\n\n    environment                       which environment this control plane is\n    registry                          where artifacts are published\n    reconciliation_interval_seconds   how often to sweep\n\nNo repository. No credential. Both are operator-managed integration state, and\nthis deployment goes back to supplying no credential of any kind -- which is\nwhat the manifest and the README have said all along, and what the interim\nconfig field would have quietly made untrue.\n\n# Three states, not two\n\n    nothing connected      an operator has not connected a repository\n    connected and failing  it is connected and cannot be read\n    connected and working\n\n`DesiredStateError::NotConnected` is the first, and it is deliberately not a\nfailure. Collapsing it into one would tell an operator \"nothing is connected\"\nabout an integration they connected last week, and they would go and connect it\nagain rather than find out why it stopped working.\n\nThe console shows the first two identically as *not connected* only where they\nmean the same thing to it -- \"this deployment does no platform management\" and\n\"nobody has connected one\" are both \"there is nothing here yet\". A connected\nrepository that fails is a 503 and says so.\n\n# The sweep tolerates it\n\nNot connected is a no-op that records nothing. The record answers \"what did the\nlast attempt find\", and a tick that found nothing to look at was not an\nattempt -- a \"last check failed\" against an integration nobody has made would\nsend an operator looking for a fault instead of a connection.\n\nIt is also not logged. At a minute''s interval that would fill a log with the\nfact that nobody has done something yet, which the console already says.\n\n# What has not moved\n\n\"Configured and broken fails startup\" still holds. What changed is which things\nare configuration: an absent *integration* is legitimate runtime state, while a\ndeployment that states its own environment or registry wrongly still refuses to\nstart.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqlpzOCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA+q8QAB6WoDVBEsvKPpaBB+8GoorC\niBq1VjEHgOnUHfCeW5XOdP54n5nCDDvgJzTTxZ9ystsvuG1Z5/PbIHwq+oDMY577\ntlUJxfcUHRwiTD/P5yKN0R0UEfUz4B7gsramDjCG/LQBFIf79nRfAueRWxfK1h+v\nW2KaYpMNUxHyyPFb7I4gsQlQMwKYadQxpk/BKnzcYOa3YyxMrVzxMy1oPQqxpGP3\n+WAIEQS8TbTw6uaTz9gHxNbY8yurwXetzj4+IcfznEtFlzaNaOzuW1RONgOuvkPk\n//SRk7/jUlUCOpNImwwuyi8CFLzBwOpq8IFgIXx0lgnmJnZSSikEVPLsz7Q98rPY\nJ+lVEMDkHZxhkWaBX2ccltkLPkCz30BS1t9BSoM+7+I/cYuX7iao6eDLzqRpCF/S\nFfO+OQ8DX5I9NDBlosV3CmaMrpX0o5zOqGgnEUHvjxLhgwQyR47e27SLFpQSHc/5\nN9lHPNXMii8nL1/N4UttQEmcC3sLeaAGnE0Y81Wve4XZIll2GBA3MCEGI7fFQT/i\nXlDuwzn285OuQE5vZ4U6GdvSWM7iaDwpzbPxxUS52I07XdYvdGIXMHo/LnWjhw5i\n60oB4FFvcCtWfs2QXKi9iQUjeUXwkbUypuXy5SXv08YrcDyRrE54xSXDmlP2E6YF\nID1AfMwlACrB13VwYtR4\n=NaLH\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-01T09:37:19Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/800769cc6fa15e9140b9e23bc56d0d90c4078ce3","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKDgwMDc2OWNjNmZhMTVlOTE0MGI5ZTIzYmM1NmQwZDkwYzQwNzhjZTM","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/d57319fc39997716867a6e1ee7a0c2d92fdf7d08","sha":"d57319fc39997716867a6e1ee7a0c2d92fdf7d08","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/d57319fc39997716867a6e1ee7a0c2d92fdf7d08"}],"sha":"800769cc6fa15e9140b9e23bc56d0d90c4078ce3","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/800769cc6fa15e9140b9e23bc56d0d90c4078ce3"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/d57319fc39997716867a6e1ee7a0c2d92fdf7d08/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-01T06:43:50Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-01T06:43:50Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Show what an environment is asked to run (#49)\n\nSix lines, and no more:\n\n    Desired        v0.3.0-preview.2\n    Available      v0.3.0-preview.3\n    Running        Unknown\n    Policy         Automatic\n    Desired state  Update available\n    Last check     17:31:04 — success\n\nNo history, no release notes, no deployment status, no third-party components.\nEach of those is something the platform cannot yet answer honestly, and a\nconsole that showed them would be showing a guess.\n\n`Never` is the line that matters most. An operator whose published version has\nnot appeared has three possible explanations and they lead three different\nplaces: nothing has looked, something looked and found nothing, something\nlooked and failed. Without this line they are indistinguishable, which is the\nsituation this whole capability exists to remove.\n\n`Automatic — Paused` rather than a third policy value. The operator paused\nadvancement; they did not decide the environment should stop being automatic,\nand the console must not say they did.\n\nA deployment managing no platform repository gets a plain sentence, not an\nerror. It is a state an operator can act on -- and there is nothing here yet to\nact *with*, so it says what is true and stops rather than offering a settings\nflow that does not exist.\n\n# The route no longer takes an environment name\n\nBuilding the console is what exposed it. The name went into\n`environments/<name>/components.yaml`, so a caller who could name an\nenvironment could name a path -- which section 31.7 forbids in as many words.\n\nA deployment manages the environment it was deployed into, stated in its\nconfiguration. The cheapest way to satisfy that rule is to give a caller\nnowhere to say it, so `GET /api/platform` takes nothing and the environment\ntravels with the service in a `PlatformBinding`. One struct rather than two\noptional fields, because two that must agree is a shape that can disagree.\n\nThe console then has nothing to guess either, which is how the correction\nannounced itself.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"e9762dffb3769291c4450ed60ef5dc6cd335ee27","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/e9762dffb3769291c4450ed60ef5dc6cd335ee27"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/d57319fc39997716867a6e1ee7a0c2d92fdf7d08","verification":{"payload":"tree e9762dffb3769291c4450ed60ef5dc6cd335ee27\nparent 63997c9a92815035f308ee6a3e302889bdd262df\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1788245030 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788245030 +1200\n\nShow what an environment is asked to run (#49)\n\nSix lines, and no more:\n\n    Desired        v0.3.0-preview.2\n    Available      v0.3.0-preview.3\n    Running        Unknown\n    Policy         Automatic\n    Desired state  Update available\n    Last check     17:31:04 — success\n\nNo history, no release notes, no deployment status, no third-party components.\nEach of those is something the platform cannot yet answer honestly, and a\nconsole that showed them would be showing a guess.\n\n`Never` is the line that matters most. An operator whose published version has\nnot appeared has three possible explanations and they lead three different\nplaces: nothing has looked, something looked and found nothing, something\nlooked and failed. Without this line they are indistinguishable, which is the\nsituation this whole capability exists to remove.\n\n`Automatic — Paused` rather than a third policy value. The operator paused\nadvancement; they did not decide the environment should stop being automatic,\nand the console must not say they did.\n\nA deployment managing no platform repository gets a plain sentence, not an\nerror. It is a state an operator can act on -- and there is nothing here yet to\nact *with*, so it says what is true and stops rather than offering a settings\nflow that does not exist.\n\n# The route no longer takes an environment name\n\nBuilding the console is what exposed it. The name went into\n`environments/<name>/components.yaml`, so a caller who could name an\nenvironment could name a path -- which section 31.7 forbids in as many words.\n\nA deployment manages the environment it was deployed into, stated in its\nconfiguration. The cheapest way to satisfy that rule is to give a caller\nnowhere to say it, so `GET /api/platform` takes nothing and the environment\ntravels with the service in a `PlatformBinding`. One struct rather than two\noptional fields, because two that must agree is a shape that can disagree.\n\nThe console then has nothing to guess either, which is how the correction\nannounced itself.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqlnQmCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAyDoQAIMjaOJXBESUyZ6RlccYN7NH\nD80ysE2qOE9ePG3ymviHRQJg1V1f4moigR4FRTgRjNk1PlT2XeXWxktyk9BSb01/\nyMerYENwjHNWRxezoez8ld2yKvJFEk6FKkcpSBX1CTBPoAxGAY0CU4wi35WV3KXB\n725kJJ5xs6n1476duUyl/5k81CT99yxJXofeVBhZu3+d5e9uZHq5fEeRgMCc6Ose\nX36TKfyYowzmtmGprV8eynRglk70AlCw9Mp5eBBhow0xP/B3cA61hfFtcCR6b/rB\ntFMxZwPgYYjWQgj4DDDpXRA9foFzkDkzXi6J8Qxbw41snQuh/wHaMlQ3dHmmsWUW\nBsJo+Q2Vin1Xz4Vzk18bQuS8xAkuViYiLWLyJT3Yp78+vd1NZlC/zCn2YdH9Y9BK\nk21vykcJAi/+VIfz/NkHvMUzctVwdY/MLy3l21h3HPbGq9ce4Rwv1NYiFJSsMYax\nlg1S1kEDcwa9EsG00yM5x/k/5RBerUGBuiHpb13AtDqULH461frhDNhZOTVDp4Fq\nfUk+9C8EMecfUGeeyjeIj8ez/SnVwM6A36e5ktWAjtOdC/FqNYtYnaPI+hqZsDB5\nTO9kWcnBsJitGPc6QcSF+mxi3Y6dDIpR9su07hKtVBDYrspVw360Zqb6KuszaL9Y\n3fGxfAufMA2PLPECjoty\n=xdLG\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-01T06:43:51Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/d57319fc39997716867a6e1ee7a0c2d92fdf7d08","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKGQ1NzMxOWZjMzk5OTc3MTY4NjdhNmUxZWU3YTBjMmQ5MmZkZjdkMDg","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/63997c9a92815035f308ee6a3e302889bdd262df","sha":"63997c9a92815035f308ee6a3e302889bdd262df","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/63997c9a92815035f308ee6a3e302889bdd262df"}],"sha":"d57319fc39997716867a6e1ee7a0c2d92fdf7d08","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/d57319fc39997716867a6e1ee7a0c2d92fdf7d08"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/63997c9a92815035f308ee6a3e302889bdd262df/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-01T05:26:23Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-01T05:26:23Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Compose Platform Management, and start what advances an environment (#48)\n\nConfiguration, the two adapters, and the sweep the host runs.\n\n# Absent means unconfigured; present and broken is a startup failure\n\nA deployment that states this section and gets it wrong does not start. Falling\nback to unmanaged would present a misconfiguration as a deliberate choice: the\nconsole would say \"nothing is managed\" to an operator who had configured\nsomething, they would have no way to tell that from a platform never meant to\nmanage anything, and the symptom would be an environment that quietly never\nadvanced.\n\n# No environment''s name is in the binary\n\nThe cadence is configuration. LucentRoot''s deployment supplies sixty seconds\nbecause it is an integration environment; something slower elsewhere is a\nchange to a manifest rather than to a release. **Zero disables the sweep**, and\nso does omitting the section -- which is how a deployment observes an\nenvironment without advancing it.\n\n# The sweep is unattended, which is new here and deliberate\n\nThe client reconciliation loop was removed from this function when ADR 0012\ntook away the platform''s service-account credential: convergence happens when\nan operator asks, carrying their authority.\n\nThis one runs on its own, and the difference is what it touches. It changes no\nclient and holds no operator''s authority. It moves a version pin, in a\nrepository the deployment was given, under a policy that repository states --\nand a policy saying `automatic` that waited for a click would be a manual\npolicy with a longer name.\n\nIt starts after the router is built. A sweep that started earlier could advance\nan environment nobody could yet look at.\n\n# The credential is interim\n\nThe specification puts this behind a Platform Management GitHub App an operator\ninstalls, separate from the Client Configuration one. That flow does not exist\nyet, so the deployment states a secret reference -- the same shape every other\nplatform credential has. When the App flow lands, that field is what it\nreplaces, and the configuration says so where somebody will read it.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"d8d111f3be85155eba92f91cbc0c7c1df904707c","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/d8d111f3be85155eba92f91cbc0c7c1df904707c"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/63997c9a92815035f308ee6a3e302889bdd262df","verification":{"payload":"tree d8d111f3be85155eba92f91cbc0c7c1df904707c\nparent ff9e1ab38c02d465b0e6bd6968ad916119f96613\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1788240383 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788240383 +1200\n\nCompose Platform Management, and start what advances an environment (#48)\n\nConfiguration, the two adapters, and the sweep the host runs.\n\n# Absent means unconfigured; present and broken is a startup failure\n\nA deployment that states this section and gets it wrong does not start. Falling\nback to unmanaged would present a misconfiguration as a deliberate choice: the\nconsole would say \"nothing is managed\" to an operator who had configured\nsomething, they would have no way to tell that from a platform never meant to\nmanage anything, and the symptom would be an environment that quietly never\nadvanced.\n\n# No environment''s name is in the binary\n\nThe cadence is configuration. LucentRoot''s deployment supplies sixty seconds\nbecause it is an integration environment; something slower elsewhere is a\nchange to a manifest rather than to a release. **Zero disables the sweep**, and\nso does omitting the section -- which is how a deployment observes an\nenvironment without advancing it.\n\n# The sweep is unattended, which is new here and deliberate\n\nThe client reconciliation loop was removed from this function when ADR 0012\ntook away the platform''s service-account credential: convergence happens when\nan operator asks, carrying their authority.\n\nThis one runs on its own, and the difference is what it touches. It changes no\nclient and holds no operator''s authority. It moves a version pin, in a\nrepository the deployment was given, under a policy that repository states --\nand a policy saying `automatic` that waited for a click would be a manual\npolicy with a longer name.\n\nIt starts after the router is built. A sweep that started earlier could advance\nan environment nobody could yet look at.\n\n# The credential is interim\n\nThe specification puts this behind a Platform Management GitHub App an operator\ninstalls, separate from the Client Configuration one. That flow does not exist\nyet, so the deployment states a secret reference -- the same shape every other\nplatform credential has. When the App flow lands, that field is what it\nreplaces, and the configuration says so where somebody will read it.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqlmH/CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAATxEQAKwsYPZdkSVPTKoxBRLht3QN\nLRPb0Awjw5YJDbrwraHi6sPsBLC6nJfBM1C/bsubfy5dVuDC+ffZ8BBoLVD9b/U2\n4EpPGjsv5XBjYffoRtbsqQulS5vFbDzryNj49ZJu/Dhb0BTLuSPWFP1NHmUq4E3l\nf3ydNZDrHwW/TrPhn4Zp9gngiN/QPVbmGHTvPSvCZWGmgERn1VSK3TBAgtdSwIJD\nIWD00rLfmtRR8uG4tq2Ab293x6PzRzHTilTW+3qySH2rGrgBQ1hOqDXAVH3jLoEg\nkGRnldku57Zq9KbxTWJw17qIW4uvOOySxAiF+7r+SENm5z6KI77PcN01G/y1ZKIV\ntq8iNoTLf3e55A+UBGLW5iKYq6KboVfSibo+nCgaVuOAB7FtTC1u/o6cZ8ZcclfT\niG55SuD7aIRK/573tGCVYnwP24CHuJX5k7Z/dxe6lKJZV+Z8qX/uC00GRrn9c31q\nPjQmkCxBEEGwNDNf3feNkUlp4569NT6q3vLLw3nqFUrZ5M3O0jVOkPjpPhualrrC\nKsPr16c2IO+JhUCK/v5kN8j3KwR/zPHz0IUpWLMObIyKP/NWYOcV5KHWv5Mn5PcC\ntlPBeYqs42tLrjX2obPCg02z24zA5sOhbYQt7SQCYaOjwZODO68P3+DSeZ4F1tB3\nrlN9DDuQ3uRuY6DKTxXO\n=yWTM\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-01T05:26:24Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/63997c9a92815035f308ee6a3e302889bdd262df","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKDYzOTk3YzlhOTI4MTUwMzVmMzA4ZWU2YTNlMzAyODg5YmRkMjYyZGY","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/ff9e1ab38c02d465b0e6bd6968ad916119f96613","sha":"ff9e1ab38c02d465b0e6bd6968ad916119f96613","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/ff9e1ab38c02d465b0e6bd6968ad916119f96613"}],"sha":"63997c9a92815035f308ee6a3e302889bdd262df","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/63997c9a92815035f308ee6a3e302889bdd262df"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/ff9e1ab38c02d465b0e6bd6968ad916119f96613/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-01T05:02:08Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-01T05:02:08Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Give the console something to ask about an environment (#47)\n\n* Give the console something to ask about an environment\n\n`GET /api/platform/environments/{environment}` answers with one row per\ncomponent: what it is asked to run, what it could run, what is running, the\npolicy, whether an operator has paused it, and what the last sweep found.\n\n# Reading it cannot change anything\n\nIt calls `statuses`, which has no path to a write. Refreshing the page, a\nsecond operator opening it, or a browser prefetching it must not move an\nenvironment, and that is structural rather than a promise made in a handler.\nWhat advances an environment is the background sweep.\n\n# A deployment with no platform repository still mounts the route\n\nIt answers `platform_not_managed` with a 404, which is a truthful answer a\nconsole can render. A route that did not exist would be a 404 too, and the\nbrowser could not tell them apart -- one is a platform waiting to be connected\nand the other is a bug.\n\nThat is the same choice the client routes already make, for the same reason.\n\n# What the body says, and what it deliberately does not\n\n`running` is always `unknown`. There is no reconciliation integration, and Git\nhaving changed is not a rollout.\n\n`paused` is a field beside `policy`, not a third policy value. An operator who\nholds a component has not changed what the environment should do in general,\nand the console should not claim they did.\n\n`lastCheck` may be `null`, and that is a real answer: nothing has checked yet\nis different from checked and found nothing, and an operator wondering why a\npublished version has not appeared needs to know which.\n\nThe timestamp is unix seconds. A server formatting a time for somebody whose\ntimezone it does not know is a server guessing.\n\n# Not yet wired\n\n`platform` is `None` in the composition root: the adapters and the sweep''s\ncadence are the next step. The route is honest about that in the meantime,\nwhich is the point of mounting it now.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Drop the imports the split left behind\n\nThe registration split moved three types into their own module and left their\nimports where they were. `cargo check` is content with an unused import;\n`clippy -D warnings` is not, which is the point of running it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"153b49cadedefe6144167052c3d27ffb660eaa44","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/153b49cadedefe6144167052c3d27ffb660eaa44"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/ff9e1ab38c02d465b0e6bd6968ad916119f96613","verification":{"payload":"tree 153b49cadedefe6144167052c3d27ffb660eaa44\nparent 28aae4f31f9dfc2d3210b12ae602476c4861e658\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1788238928 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788238928 +1200\n\nGive the console something to ask about an environment (#47)\n\n* Give the console something to ask about an environment\n\n`GET /api/platform/environments/{environment}` answers with one row per\ncomponent: what it is asked to run, what it could run, what is running, the\npolicy, whether an operator has paused it, and what the last sweep found.\n\n# Reading it cannot change anything\n\nIt calls `statuses`, which has no path to a write. Refreshing the page, a\nsecond operator opening it, or a browser prefetching it must not move an\nenvironment, and that is structural rather than a promise made in a handler.\nWhat advances an environment is the background sweep.\n\n# A deployment with no platform repository still mounts the route\n\nIt answers `platform_not_managed` with a 404, which is a truthful answer a\nconsole can render. A route that did not exist would be a 404 too, and the\nbrowser could not tell them apart -- one is a platform waiting to be connected\nand the other is a bug.\n\nThat is the same choice the client routes already make, for the same reason.\n\n# What the body says, and what it deliberately does not\n\n`running` is always `unknown`. There is no reconciliation integration, and Git\nhaving changed is not a rollout.\n\n`paused` is a field beside `policy`, not a third policy value. An operator who\nholds a component has not changed what the environment should do in general,\nand the console should not claim they did.\n\n`lastCheck` may be `null`, and that is a real answer: nothing has checked yet\nis different from checked and found nothing, and an operator wondering why a\npublished version has not appeared needs to know which.\n\nThe timestamp is unix seconds. A server formatting a time for somebody whose\ntimezone it does not know is a server guessing.\n\n# Not yet wired\n\n`platform` is `None` in the composition root: the adapters and the sweep''s\ncadence are the next step. The route is honest about that in the meantime,\nwhich is the point of mounting it now.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Drop the imports the split left behind\n\nThe registration split moved three types into their own module and left their\nimports where they were. `cargo check` is content with an unused import;\n`clippy -D warnings` is not, which is the point of running it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqllxQCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAKz8QACOI2UvsFtXSLvsAVP0+ZmcL\nKMu7Hrbgwavvf117dERptluH1alNxqpPmkCOH1Ij/55aifDA0SBSt7zSUWeqEOC4\nvqtBtPqBX2Mxl9t/LkkiPRen80aNGwu8dbtwdVphZf/2E6mWp5KyYnQnTeVNvNSi\n9EDMDiwDrfJ5wNlYqkfcNkt2ZjtRNYAqnGovo0h9Db2OYzCJ6fdxr4iLuzXl1er7\niDwGVhThONQ0KbyIAYaH17H1EmPK9dZ20TK77L5MS8qO1HnKwEoEj7i/t7zUCI2K\ng6puYH+28bRLnu6vArUz31R0pNVK4Gd6kdQWnwRwtbfmusiiQNUzLrVr3HGBoksl\nohrBUx3+rGkW/cGPhLj+XBJuwCulw4Ufu6CUKpWjDjA2bK/ukS7lenUIC2ExMnol\nvnZ9s4zfk67oPDoB1ODYBBzK3rhsFikFnA/7FxFf/V+IQpUy1NqPG3cL1jQVhI9S\nT9CjVh0c5sIYCfhXafIbvz2iz4qRIfswLio4e+PY4fZqRKycIyMbqQKYzWa1MqCx\neQSs3H6TDrAPqAhk/xssG9nlw1vQa1VpSHl5Xg5P8YXWTTkGzN3ukSyyvHCiI0ES\nfTK9Zd45w9Py+k6NlOiaJedbs5zMehFS1VjlkZDr+qwcK19n1RTNuP0NAxn4/2/k\nAEKoKJuiyLz86O1HpvO3\n=EEcW\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-01T05:02:08Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/ff9e1ab38c02d465b0e6bd6968ad916119f96613","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKGZmOWUxYWIzOGMwMmQ0NjViMGU2YmQ2OTY4YWQ5MTYxMTlmOTY2MTM","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/28aae4f31f9dfc2d3210b12ae602476c4861e658","sha":"28aae4f31f9dfc2d3210b12ae602476c4861e658","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/28aae4f31f9dfc2d3210b12ae602476c4861e658"}],"sha":"ff9e1ab38c02d465b0e6bd6968ad916119f96613","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/ff9e1ab38c02d465b0e6bd6968ad916119f96613"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/28aae4f31f9dfc2d3210b12ae602476c4861e658/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-01T04:14:26Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-01T04:14:26Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Make it impossible to put a credential on the console (#46)\n\n* Make it impossible to put a credential on the console\n\n`CheckOutcome::Failed` carried a `String`, and every adapter is careful about\nwhat goes into one. A convention is only as good as the next person to add an\nerror variant -- and the failure it invites is somebody formatting an upstream\nerror with `Debug`: a response body, a transport error carrying a signed URL,\nan auth failure quoting the header it just sent.\n\nThe console is the worst place for that. It is where a leak is hardest to\nnotice and easiest to forward.\n\nSo `detail` is a `SafeDiagnostic`, and the only way to obtain one is through\nthe sanitiser. The record that reaches an operator cannot hold anything else,\nwhich is a property of the type rather than of anybody''s diligence.\n\n# What it removes\n\n    -----BEGIN …            the block, and everything after it\n    ghp_ gho_ ghu_ ghs_     GitHub tokens, by prefix\n    ghr_ github_pat_\n    hvs. hvb.               OpenBao service and batch tokens\n    Bearer <value>          the word after one that names a credential\n    token=<value>           and the inline form, which is one word\n    https://…?<query>       the query, keeping the path\n    anything over 200 chars truncated\n\nThe path of a URL survives, because which registry and which repository is\nexactly what an operator needs to read. The query does not, because it can\ncarry a signature.\n\nThe cap is the rule that survives a mistake nobody anticipated. Whatever\narrives, only the first sentence of it does -- and a truncated leak is still a\nleak and a much smaller one.\n\n# What it is not\n\nA proof. It is a tripwire, and the last line of a defence whose first line is\nadapters that classify their own failures rather than forwarding somebody\nelse''s words. The prefix list cannot be complete, which is why the structural\nrules -- what follows the word `token`, what follows `BEGIN`, how long a\nmessage may be -- do not depend on it.\n\nOne test asserts an ordinary diagnostic survives untouched, digest and all. A\nguard that mangles the message it is protecting would be quietly removed by\nwhoever next needed to read one.\n\nFive mutations, five named failures: the cap, the PEM rule, the prefix list,\nthe lookback, and the query redaction.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Say what SafeDiagnostic does not make safe\n\nIt makes text safe to show an operator. It says nothing about what is safe to\nput in a trace or a log line -- which go somewhere this type never sees, are\nkept far longer, and are forwarded to places nobody enumerated.\n\nAn upstream error chain carrying a credential is exactly as dangerous there as\nit would have been on the console, and the answer is still not to format one.\nWorth writing down before somebody reads this type as permission.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"2a169303f530fd0eec1c03c90c93067f138d0eb0","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/2a169303f530fd0eec1c03c90c93067f138d0eb0"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/28aae4f31f9dfc2d3210b12ae602476c4861e658","verification":{"payload":"tree 2a169303f530fd0eec1c03c90c93067f138d0eb0\nparent e6c6bb942d99a1242e9ffc27c861a0ad9269bc38\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1788236066 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788236066 +1200\n\nMake it impossible to put a credential on the console (#46)\n\n* Make it impossible to put a credential on the console\n\n`CheckOutcome::Failed` carried a `String`, and every adapter is careful about\nwhat goes into one. A convention is only as good as the next person to add an\nerror variant -- and the failure it invites is somebody formatting an upstream\nerror with `Debug`: a response body, a transport error carrying a signed URL,\nan auth failure quoting the header it just sent.\n\nThe console is the worst place for that. It is where a leak is hardest to\nnotice and easiest to forward.\n\nSo `detail` is a `SafeDiagnostic`, and the only way to obtain one is through\nthe sanitiser. The record that reaches an operator cannot hold anything else,\nwhich is a property of the type rather than of anybody''s diligence.\n\n# What it removes\n\n    -----BEGIN …            the block, and everything after it\n    ghp_ gho_ ghu_ ghs_     GitHub tokens, by prefix\n    ghr_ github_pat_\n    hvs. hvb.               OpenBao service and batch tokens\n    Bearer <value>          the word after one that names a credential\n    token=<value>           and the inline form, which is one word\n    https://…?<query>       the query, keeping the path\n    anything over 200 chars truncated\n\nThe path of a URL survives, because which registry and which repository is\nexactly what an operator needs to read. The query does not, because it can\ncarry a signature.\n\nThe cap is the rule that survives a mistake nobody anticipated. Whatever\narrives, only the first sentence of it does -- and a truncated leak is still a\nleak and a much smaller one.\n\n# What it is not\n\nA proof. It is a tripwire, and the last line of a defence whose first line is\nadapters that classify their own failures rather than forwarding somebody\nelse''s words. The prefix list cannot be complete, which is why the structural\nrules -- what follows the word `token`, what follows `BEGIN`, how long a\nmessage may be -- do not depend on it.\n\nOne test asserts an ordinary diagnostic survives untouched, digest and all. A\nguard that mangles the message it is protecting would be quietly removed by\nwhoever next needed to read one.\n\nFive mutations, five named failures: the cap, the PEM rule, the prefix list,\nthe lookback, and the query redaction.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Say what SafeDiagnostic does not make safe\n\nIt makes text safe to show an operator. It says nothing about what is safe to\nput in a trace or a log line -- which go somewhere this type never sees, are\nkept far longer, and are forwarded to places nobody enumerated.\n\nAn upstream error chain carrying a credential is exactly as dangerous there as\nit would have been on the console, and the answer is still not to format one.\nWorth writing down before somebody reads this type as permission.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqllEiCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAZFoQAB50WrrwjaYfjosbxu3YOiQP\n0+y+ngdKCmK1SscehfOOwgfEpwYbgyRAuMFtdpNNVzWWI4l3J+KsggNEVj5JbVgT\n1U6ZoF1a2KOvS7GQlazdLtFLauCJhosvJWgDu1GiqKzgsCXwFp5z7b708l4mXW0I\nBb+aJGrt6wCuxlsXkg6kT+HqR38HV8ZiQeE3Jtqnw5N0ZZ0nvoDinefFMwTMD7q4\nrbEATgd453ceK8u5bJdTn6ebGs3+U9x6qsUyG9+9UJsNMWzJJqznFqSfObev47i4\nuJDnqS39c4LzgmBhBsVk4To/tUfBeTeZRRj4YTmnZbJGxxHnVNB2VuYl2EZs9hTE\nANTUEKR1f/r1iXlNitUaeW+jL3Gl1MvXBX0rVVOmNTR2BYvpkll0z4MPEpu/BciZ\nq0Fp2w/YUH/oWLA9ebPdVvR/412pWqZDuP4sr1OftDq0EQxdRnaex4MEpx0wbm4L\nZNgFTFbiPN4ilGwuk07TyPo8s+VcJYvlR222B+cJ6HgM+wwBG2625uoAYJ6g4ncS\n1Kqxm9IFxGXyken8emQTvySajyGAxi6z/xmg3lS/fuTgGCouCGuDSDv+KOc1A/9k\nPbvfxakABlHttNRZFAYf0nl/xmwsJB+sYWMG3MEHNeUAAhl0gAJ1tGdeH/I0Qh3o\nhtoDOu+qLYBS0cOPdQfm\n=WbHv\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-01T04:14:27Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/28aae4f31f9dfc2d3210b12ae602476c4861e658","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKDI4YWFlNGYzMWY5ZGZjMmQzMjEwYjEyYWU2MDI0NzZjNDg2MWU2NTg","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/e6c6bb942d99a1242e9ffc27c861a0ad9269bc38","sha":"e6c6bb942d99a1242e9ffc27c861a0ad9269bc38","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/e6c6bb942d99a1242e9ffc27c861a0ad9269bc38"}],"sha":"28aae4f31f9dfc2d3210b12ae602476c4861e658","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/28aae4f31f9dfc2d3210b12ae602476c4861e658"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/e6c6bb942d99a1242e9ffc27c861a0ad9269bc38/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-01T03:24:28Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-01T03:24:28Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Implement the desired-state port, and record what the last sweep found (#45)\n\nTwo things, and the second is the one that will matter at three in the morning.\n\n# The platform repository is now a `DesiredState`\n\n`fabric-platform-git` implements the port the rules crate defines: list an\nenvironment''s components, read one, advance one. It is the translation and\nnothing more -- the version, the channel, the policy, the hold and the image\nrepositories come out of the manifest, and a release unit goes back in.\n\nA version the manifest carries that this cannot parse is a refusal rather than\na default. Guessing would mean deciding what to advance *from* on the strength\nof something nobody wrote deliberately.\n\nThe error mapping keeps `Conflict` distinct, which is the distinction that has\nto survive: it is not a failure of the component but an instruction to decide\nagain, and a caller that could not tell it from an outage would either retry\nforever or give up on a race it was always going to lose once.\n\n`Channel` and `Hold` join `UpdatePolicy` in the rules crate. Each is a concept\nbefore it is a field in a file, and two copies of a three-variant enum is two\ncopies to keep in step.\n\n# The last check is recorded\n\nWhen a published version does not appear there are three explanations, and they\nlead three different places: nothing has checked yet, something checked and\nthere was nothing to do, or something checked and failed. Without this they all\nlook identical -- which is exactly the situation that started this work.\n\n`SweepState` now carries the guard *and* the record, because they are the same\nconcern: what is happening, and what happened last. A console asking one\nwithout the other would be asking half a question.\n\nThe timestamp is unix seconds and is not formatted here. Recording a fact and\nrendering it for a human are different jobs, and only one of them belongs in a\ncrate with no transport.\n\nA skipped sweep deliberately does not touch the record. It found nothing\nbecause it did not look, and overwriting the last real answer with that would\nhide it.\n\nMutations: never recording, and always recording success. Both fail.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"ee9611adcbe24077dc2c0ce00d0fd29be3ddc30e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/ee9611adcbe24077dc2c0ce00d0fd29be3ddc30e"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/e6c6bb942d99a1242e9ffc27c861a0ad9269bc38","verification":{"payload":"tree ee9611adcbe24077dc2c0ce00d0fd29be3ddc30e\nparent 3a2ae537b68526dfd3bda905b353439007544877\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1788233068 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788233068 +1200\n\nImplement the desired-state port, and record what the last sweep found (#45)\n\nTwo things, and the second is the one that will matter at three in the morning.\n\n# The platform repository is now a `DesiredState`\n\n`fabric-platform-git` implements the port the rules crate defines: list an\nenvironment''s components, read one, advance one. It is the translation and\nnothing more -- the version, the channel, the policy, the hold and the image\nrepositories come out of the manifest, and a release unit goes back in.\n\nA version the manifest carries that this cannot parse is a refusal rather than\na default. Guessing would mean deciding what to advance *from* on the strength\nof something nobody wrote deliberately.\n\nThe error mapping keeps `Conflict` distinct, which is the distinction that has\nto survive: it is not a failure of the component but an instruction to decide\nagain, and a caller that could not tell it from an outage would either retry\nforever or give up on a race it was always going to lose once.\n\n`Channel` and `Hold` join `UpdatePolicy` in the rules crate. Each is a concept\nbefore it is a field in a file, and two copies of a three-variant enum is two\ncopies to keep in step.\n\n# The last check is recorded\n\nWhen a published version does not appear there are three explanations, and they\nlead three different places: nothing has checked yet, something checked and\nthere was nothing to do, or something checked and failed. Without this they all\nlook identical -- which is exactly the situation that started this work.\n\n`SweepState` now carries the guard *and* the record, because they are the same\nconcern: what is happening, and what happened last. A console asking one\nwithout the other would be asking half a question.\n\nThe timestamp is unix seconds and is not formatted here. Recording a fact and\nrendering it for a human are different jobs, and only one of them belongs in a\ncrate with no transport.\n\nA skipped sweep deliberately does not touch the record. It found nothing\nbecause it did not look, and overwriting the last real answer with that would\nhide it.\n\nMutations: never recording, and always recording success. Both fail.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqlkVsCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA+VgQAEJfrDKbChZq3C2WK7mYPXgG\ncPosdYBHKScn6KWi8J48Sg7ztG0uX/MAID6mYIF2OC23UKuiWyAf/PcheIh+pQ2M\n4I1Crpcu3IAfcZyPgadSwbZUVjpJQD2Ljky55MtW+arKtW+Ia32/307b89DE+Ko4\nl9J6cuqgcofXwjmrqJL8v7K4WJSQlgmVWXDJAnxl4u4tGibI8QiqD9iRydSJWsDq\nIQP6FH5w5e+22dlTQPZF0EROc0+7OcLJqgI8eyIXJkmaS3i5DzZZpWBUei+pn9R4\neS8LWt/7cXvsUZAxDHgn1raQCpaUFK1M9N4DYIEJFX21skKkfkf6EJ9Z+sFPwNxT\npYBkK2kRN9H1PdOX/lrkYE2v/hfzXIyMJsU1Lw32dHHvp+J/CWM8uRtG48UHbish\ndZxBywqBGHCUxUrzr2FQ/a8saUYbDZ2kONSan35BalBmvE8/RGkUZgbR9dQIwZf0\ngcFJPujcU5zHE+IpJr8tq4U0/itgZnz48bhpjeO4/g0kfdcoTapqkudHO0/9u2j7\nz2Rg6n+iPsgXZgSkKiPwFF0CQpUdwk1q/85IiX9X2vUsP0QN52bkfEtKiVVIPmRN\n98XwrIEh8vbP/d4E/jKWGtu+7r/VhmamucrAXk7ntgnM4XYVFen8w71hhaoQXfWE\nGVlvmhk8Ij1QVZUPQd0Z\n=onJg\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-01T03:24:28Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/e6c6bb942d99a1242e9ffc27c861a0ad9269bc38","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKGU2YzZiYjk0MmQ5OWExMjQyZTlmZmMyN2M4NjFhMGFkOTI2OWJjMzg","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/3a2ae537b68526dfd3bda905b353439007544877","sha":"3a2ae537b68526dfd3bda905b353439007544877","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/3a2ae537b68526dfd3bda905b353439007544877"}],"sha":"e6c6bb942d99a1242e9ffc27c861a0ad9269bc38","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/e6c6bb942d99a1242e9ffc27c861a0ad9269bc38"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/3a2ae537b68526dfd3bda905b353439007544877/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-01T02:15:02Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-01T02:15:02Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Compose reading, discovering, deciding and writing — and sweep on a cadence (#44)\n\n* Compose reading, discovering, deciding and writing\n\n`PlatformManagement` over two ports: a registry, and wherever desired state is\nkept. It adds no rules of its own -- discovery decides what is eligible, the\nselector decides whether to act, and the write decides whether it still can.\n\n# Reading never writes, and that is the contract\n\n`status` is what the console reads, and it has no path to a write at all. A\nread with mutation semantics is a read nobody can perform safely -- not a\nrefresh, not a health check, not a second operator looking at the same screen.\nAdvancement happens in `reconcile`, and only there. Making `status` reconcile\nfails a test that says so.\n\n# What `reconcile` reports is what it did\n\nThe status after a write is derived from the unit that was written, not from\nreading again. A second read would race the reconciliation this call just\nperformed, and answering \"what did I just do\" from a fresh query is how a\nconsole shows somebody else''s change as though it were yours.\n\n# Two shapes worth naming\n\n`Running` is an enum with one variant, `Unknown`. There is no reconciliation\nintegration, so nothing here can honestly say whether a deployment started --\nGit having changed is not a rollout. An enum rather than an `Option<Version>`\nso that gaining `Converged` and `Degraded` later is an addition rather than a\nreinterpretation of what `None` used to mean.\n\n`DesiredStateStatus` has two values, `Current` and `UpdateAvailable`. Paused is\nnot a third: it is a fact about the policy and the hold together, and folding\nit in would mix \"there is a newer version\" with \"anything may act on it\", which\nan operator needs answered separately.\n\n# The series is the desired version''s own line\n\nAn automatic policy walks forward within 0.3.0 and does not wander onto 0.4.0.\nMoving a line is a decision about what an environment is for, not something\ndiscovery does because a tag appeared.\n\nThat constraint had no test until a mutation found it: removing it changed\nnothing, because every fixture published one line. It has one now, and removing\nthe constraint fails it.\n\nFour other mutations, four named failures: `status` reconciling, the hold not\nconsulted, a conflict retried here rather than reported, and the series\nunconstrained.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Sweep every component, on a cadence somebody else keeps\n\n`automatic` has to mean automatic. A policy that required an operator to click\n\"check now\" would be a manual policy with a longer name, and driving\nreconciliation from the console''s read would undo the read/write separation\nthis crate just made structural.\n\nSo `sweep` reconciles every component an environment describes, and something\nelse decides when to call it. Not a scheduler: keeping the cadence out of here\nis what makes it configurable and what makes this testable without a clock.\n\n# One component failing does not stop the others\n\nA component that cannot be read or written is recorded as failed, logged, and\nthe sweep continues. A sweep that gave up on the first failure would leave\nevery component after it permanently unreconciled, in whatever order they\nhappened to be listed -- which is why the test''s broken component sorts first.\n\n# One sweep at a time, per process, and no leader election\n\nThe guard is an atomic flag, and a sweep already running is *skipped* rather\nthan queued. A sweep overrunning its interval means registries or Git are slow,\nand the answer to that is not to start a second one behind it.\n\nAcross replicas there is no lock, deliberately. Two control planes will both\ndecide to advance, and the second write is refused as stale -- because its\nprecondition is the desired state it was decided against. Duplicate sweeps are\nwasteful and not dangerous, so leader election would be machinery bought\nagainst a risk the Git model already carries.\n\nThe concurrency test parks the first sweep mid-read and attempts the second\nwhile it is genuinely in flight. Calling them one after another would have\nproved nothing: the first would have finished and released the guard before the\nsecond looked at it. Removing the guard fails it.\n\n# `reconcile` now says whether it moved\n\nIt returns where the component started as well as where it ended. \"Advanced\"\nand \"was already there\" produce the same status and are not the same event, and\nthe sweep was otherwise reading each component twice to tell them apart.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"ebd9e9f73c30d97f2a8b9da700a4e778d2490401","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/ebd9e9f73c30d97f2a8b9da700a4e778d2490401"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/3a2ae537b68526dfd3bda905b353439007544877","verification":{"payload":"tree ebd9e9f73c30d97f2a8b9da700a4e778d2490401\nparent e1ff089dc44accef532baa0943f74b5b034a62a0\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1788228902 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788228902 +1200\n\nCompose reading, discovering, deciding and writing — and sweep on a cadence (#44)\n\n* Compose reading, discovering, deciding and writing\n\n`PlatformManagement` over two ports: a registry, and wherever desired state is\nkept. It adds no rules of its own -- discovery decides what is eligible, the\nselector decides whether to act, and the write decides whether it still can.\n\n# Reading never writes, and that is the contract\n\n`status` is what the console reads, and it has no path to a write at all. A\nread with mutation semantics is a read nobody can perform safely -- not a\nrefresh, not a health check, not a second operator looking at the same screen.\nAdvancement happens in `reconcile`, and only there. Making `status` reconcile\nfails a test that says so.\n\n# What `reconcile` reports is what it did\n\nThe status after a write is derived from the unit that was written, not from\nreading again. A second read would race the reconciliation this call just\nperformed, and answering \"what did I just do\" from a fresh query is how a\nconsole shows somebody else''s change as though it were yours.\n\n# Two shapes worth naming\n\n`Running` is an enum with one variant, `Unknown`. There is no reconciliation\nintegration, so nothing here can honestly say whether a deployment started --\nGit having changed is not a rollout. An enum rather than an `Option<Version>`\nso that gaining `Converged` and `Degraded` later is an addition rather than a\nreinterpretation of what `None` used to mean.\n\n`DesiredStateStatus` has two values, `Current` and `UpdateAvailable`. Paused is\nnot a third: it is a fact about the policy and the hold together, and folding\nit in would mix \"there is a newer version\" with \"anything may act on it\", which\nan operator needs answered separately.\n\n# The series is the desired version''s own line\n\nAn automatic policy walks forward within 0.3.0 and does not wander onto 0.4.0.\nMoving a line is a decision about what an environment is for, not something\ndiscovery does because a tag appeared.\n\nThat constraint had no test until a mutation found it: removing it changed\nnothing, because every fixture published one line. It has one now, and removing\nthe constraint fails it.\n\nFour other mutations, four named failures: `status` reconciling, the hold not\nconsulted, a conflict retried here rather than reported, and the series\nunconstrained.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Sweep every component, on a cadence somebody else keeps\n\n`automatic` has to mean automatic. A policy that required an operator to click\n\"check now\" would be a manual policy with a longer name, and driving\nreconciliation from the console''s read would undo the read/write separation\nthis crate just made structural.\n\nSo `sweep` reconciles every component an environment describes, and something\nelse decides when to call it. Not a scheduler: keeping the cadence out of here\nis what makes it configurable and what makes this testable without a clock.\n\n# One component failing does not stop the others\n\nA component that cannot be read or written is recorded as failed, logged, and\nthe sweep continues. A sweep that gave up on the first failure would leave\nevery component after it permanently unreconciled, in whatever order they\nhappened to be listed -- which is why the test''s broken component sorts first.\n\n# One sweep at a time, per process, and no leader election\n\nThe guard is an atomic flag, and a sweep already running is *skipped* rather\nthan queued. A sweep overrunning its interval means registries or Git are slow,\nand the answer to that is not to start a second one behind it.\n\nAcross replicas there is no lock, deliberately. Two control planes will both\ndecide to advance, and the second write is refused as stale -- because its\nprecondition is the desired state it was decided against. Duplicate sweeps are\nwasteful and not dangerous, so leader election would be machinery bought\nagainst a risk the Git model already carries.\n\nThe concurrency test parks the first sweep mid-read and attempts the second\nwhile it is genuinely in flight. Calling them one after another would have\nproved nothing: the first would have finished and released the guard before the\nsecond looked at it. Removing the guard fails it.\n\n# `reconcile` now says whether it moved\n\nIt returns where the component started as well as where it ended. \"Advanced\"\nand \"was already there\" produce the same status and are not the same event, and\nthe sweep was otherwise reading each component twice to tell them apart.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqljUmCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAAlsQACX2AC3ZysYXv6K1AFiUXw/9\n5GJtt0ES5Ggyl1+LEJhd9E6E1QjCBYO4rHDGN3zaKkKJT4bUkrXiLT4PrGZRocLN\nVvgcGgm+ptbxPqacc5igTPchKo8abmbQ3TmyhAxc2KpFnbRy7P6chG4qKLq3mvRq\n+YvSkIi9evjTp3OHAHNhSmHzwDjOOCcBZtPkzGsCgPKsOkhETdPX+U4mZWqxXezL\nFtFt8M72i4gPr4kXc1zmMgBcCERNdfMEZzuo76BoxTEuK0CmVgHlPVrjAJbxtuSE\nfELg7TWb4P4WZi2nHHkbxy4bpSOB2/kuMII1vf3tPU6S8lW9NixjbSnUVdCbAD5N\nxpLeVRAK9gNOcQEakGAuk2+pLvMbM1l80HWrVxZclrMktokeJY3qf+kEemcMOQvD\nD/xbnblfPPFcgBYXXmcMXMZzoN1Eknt5lT9W6HMfUWABQ4ZTvY1pBkR63pZ3KUOt\n7jQAUGsYHTjmGhMLy1exhXATZbmgnoJJI41PI5N3lJFKrjleYSXycDjSFXGLU7Ez\nkxGq01Fvgea7HqGR8C5BwT1Ruul/B+5YL3FNol5deUcnRkDsyc3b9xXmzQOaoYk+\nLvQgwM4zNRFai73zHWpvIyeryJThiOujUOksPAA3MIXqg1Rmv7gP4dlOO81rI9e8\n/tatSs17nTA+XdCR/2Ak\n=e6MG\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-01T02:15:03Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/3a2ae537b68526dfd3bda905b353439007544877","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKDNhMmFlNTM3YjY4NTI2ZGZkM2JkYTkwNWIzNTM0MzkwMDc1NDQ4Nzc","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/e1ff089dc44accef532baa0943f74b5b034a62a0","sha":"e1ff089dc44accef532baa0943f74b5b034a62a0","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/e1ff089dc44accef532baa0943f74b5b034a62a0"}],"sha":"3a2ae537b68526dfd3bda905b353439007544877","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/3a2ae537b68526dfd3bda905b353439007544877"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/e1ff089dc44accef532baa0943f74b5b034a62a0/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-31T22:14:18Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-31T22:14:18Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Decide whether an environment advances, in five branches (#43)\n\nManual                          → stay\n    Locked                          → stay\n    Automatic + hold                → stay\n    Automatic + something available  → advance\n    Automatic + nothing available    → stay\n\nNo arithmetic, and nothing that needs a diagram. Everything that could have\nmade it complicated is already decided somewhere better, and re-checking any of\nit here would be a second copy of a rule that would drift from the first:\n\n- \"newer than what we run\" is discovery''s floor, so an `available` at all *is*\n  an available upgrade;\n- completeness and coherence are discovery''s too, so a version still\n  publishing or built from two commits never reaches this;\n- a concurrent change is the write''s precondition, which includes the manifest\n  this decision was read from.\n\n# It cannot modify what it read\n\n`decide` returns a decision and does not write, and an `Advance` carries a\n*version* -- so the write it feeds has nothing in it that could clear a hold or\nwiden a policy in order to succeed. The guarantee is structural rather than\nasserted, which is the strongest form available.\n\n# The reason is carried, not discarded\n\n\"Nothing is happening\" and \"nothing is happening because an operator paused it\"\nlook identical from outside and are not the same situation. Discovery also\nkeeps running while a hold stands, so the console can show `Automatic — Paused`\nbeside a newer available version rather than freezing its knowledge at the held\none.\n\n`UpdatePolicy` moves to this crate. A policy is a rule before it is a field in\na file: the rules own what it is, and `fabric-platform-git` serialises it.\n\nFour mutations, each failing the branch it belongs to and the test that asserts\nonly one of the five writes.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"145176826f46117bb83dfed3e34974ceb8525176","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/145176826f46117bb83dfed3e34974ceb8525176"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/e1ff089dc44accef532baa0943f74b5b034a62a0","verification":{"payload":"tree 145176826f46117bb83dfed3e34974ceb8525176\nparent 428f09ce03ffa4bc99968ed1e08129922f251cbe\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1788214458 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788214458 +1200\n\nDecide whether an environment advances, in five branches (#43)\n\nManual                          → stay\n    Locked                          → stay\n    Automatic + hold                → stay\n    Automatic + something available  → advance\n    Automatic + nothing available    → stay\n\nNo arithmetic, and nothing that needs a diagram. Everything that could have\nmade it complicated is already decided somewhere better, and re-checking any of\nit here would be a second copy of a rule that would drift from the first:\n\n- \"newer than what we run\" is discovery''s floor, so an `available` at all *is*\n  an available upgrade;\n- completeness and coherence are discovery''s too, so a version still\n  publishing or built from two commits never reaches this;\n- a concurrent change is the write''s precondition, which includes the manifest\n  this decision was read from.\n\n# It cannot modify what it read\n\n`decide` returns a decision and does not write, and an `Advance` carries a\n*version* -- so the write it feeds has nothing in it that could clear a hold or\nwiden a policy in order to succeed. The guarantee is structural rather than\nasserted, which is the strongest form available.\n\n# The reason is carried, not discarded\n\n\"Nothing is happening\" and \"nothing is happening because an operator paused it\"\nlook identical from outside and are not the same situation. Discovery also\nkeeps running while a hold stands, so the console can show `Automatic — Paused`\nbeside a newer available version rather than freezing its knowledge at the held\none.\n\n`UpdatePolicy` moves to this crate. A policy is a rule before it is a field in\na file: the rules own what it is, and `fabric-platform-git` serialises it.\n\nFour mutations, each failing the branch it belongs to and the test that asserts\nonly one of the five writes.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqlfy6CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAumUQADj//4+Cq0q3TVmsBRAr2pnl\n9+l0tJVyn6XpFMGRTBGM9Hz8QyWftMhEEDyD+JbphNGg0GmhPszYAcH3qSbhGhDn\nG1QVuNgYfkFKGUVvOO79dtfqQURdVsiHw1EnQLZbJJ2roFldOSuQgtx1FE3FB8Cb\nMH756RIyNDSWRXprEbTf4aAB5bNf+EFcu8W5V3j9uBzyi+LpJKPnIkcpPFD7I9Ye\ngD2qbg/QcdqcWBw7jUkLfP5dOHqCqqC1cT6JrPtkZzVK35k0kwHSCZynYTacUcA3\niFOA7ZCelDCqgRKAUHELdVz6JpdVh5Sr3u+LsIwpzQXLnLamB57Ub0Aso9lnO1/R\nEa5HW6i1wQrQQ/kaQEOdqVU0d8EivCra30vbnzjXB+TS0nFz2hlBJVVqKqTaXzZG\nRHw5bYvEfcwrpeqnfp6N/S8FFo18Dymb/FAcDmrz1cDeVtv5tGsKVdOk6D9iSI1g\nrfrPFcFQBjbx3it9WfHmRJnFW+2EEcSzzfLtZQehP55cp7hWx+fPaok7Ht5PHBlT\n9xOl+XuQik0pnSigeor6iErHqMgMVtRdggLVtdY4oK5sdAaYWtkNp2XhysQOlRwk\ng44goVuXTjkhSSXe63wwyN7lbYYHA5DMhSZJXXC90D/RsFvrRoTmE+5Y+cNMqjXd\n7M5dpr87T/f63uvG9vaA\n=n+/w\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-31T22:14:19Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/e1ff089dc44accef532baa0943f74b5b034a62a0","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKGUxZmYwODlkYzQ0YWNjZWY1MzJiYWEwOTQzZjc0YjViMDM0YTYyYTA","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/428f09ce03ffa4bc99968ed1e08129922f251cbe","sha":"428f09ce03ffa4bc99968ed1e08129922f251cbe","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/428f09ce03ffa4bc99968ed1e08129922f251cbe"}],"sha":"e1ff089dc44accef532baa0943f74b5b034a62a0","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/e1ff089dc44accef532baa0943f74b5b034a62a0"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/428f09ce03ffa4bc99968ed1e08129922f251cbe/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-31T21:19:31Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-31T21:19:31Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Read published versions from an OCI registry, anonymously (#42)\n\n* Move a component''s desired state without knowing where it is pinned\n\n`set_component_desired_state(environment, component, version)` is the operation\nPlatform Management will call. It reads the environment''s manifest, works out\nwhich files carry the pins from what that manifest declares, and writes the\nmanifest and those files in one commit.\n\nNothing in this crate knows the platform repository''s layout. `pinnedIn` is\nwhere the layout is stated, and this refuses to write anything the manifest\ndoes not name. When that repository eventually renders its overlays *from* the\nmanifest, the lists empty and this writes one file, unchanged.\n\n# What a caller may and may not say\n\nA caller moves a component to a new *version*. Two rules make that precise, and\nboth are enforced before anything is read for writing:\n\n- the roles must be exactly the ones the manifest declares. Promoting the\n  console without the control plane would put two thirds of a release on an\n  environment and call it integrated;\n- each role''s repository must match what the manifest already says. Where a\n  component is published is the platform repository''s statement, not a\n  caller''s -- specification section 31.8, made structural.\n\nThe channel, the update policy, the hold and every `pinnedIn` are carried\nthrough untouched. Advancing a *held* component is the selector''s decision, not\nthis one''s -- but silently dropping the hold while writing would make that\ndecision impossible to enforce afterwards, so there is a test that it survives.\n\n# Six rules on every path, and one of them is not obvious\n\n    repository-relative, never absolute\n    no traversal\n    under a managedRoot\n    a .yaml or .yml manifest\n    exists\n    and actually pins the image that declared it\n\nThe platform repository''s CI enforces the same six. That duplication is\ndeliberate: CI proves the manifest is coherent at the commit it ran on, and\nthis applies them to whatever was actually read, which may be a state no CI has\nseen.\n\nThe absolute-path rule looked redundant -- `/etc/hosts.yaml` is already refused\nfor being under no `applications/` root. It is not: a manifest declaring `/` as\na managed root would admit it, and then the path''s own shape is the only thing\nleft. There is a test for exactly that, and removing the rule fails it.\n\n# Files are edited as lines, and the header is not this crate''s to rewrite\n\nThe overlays carry comments that are the reason a pin is what it is, so the\n`images:` entry is replaced in place and everything around it -- including a\nblock-scalar patch -- is emitted unchanged.\n\nThe manifest is machine-managed and rewritten whole, but its *header* is where\nthe platform repository explains what the file is and who may write it. A\nFabric release replacing that with its own prose would be Fabric editorialising\nin somebody else''s repository, so the leading comment block is carried through\nverbatim.\n\n# Checked by breaking it\n\nEleven cases. Each guard was removed and the suite re-run: the managed-root\nrule, the registry equality, the hold, the absolute-path rule, and `repin`''s\ninsistence on finding exactly one entry each fail a test that names what they\nare for.\n\nOne mutation reported NOTHING FAILED and was wrong about itself --\n`false && a || b` short-circuits the wrong way. Re-running it properly is what\nturned up the `/`-as-a-root case above.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Find the newest preview a component may move to\n\nDiscovery, as rules with no transport. The crate defines a `Registry` port and\nis handed an implementation: which registry, and how it authenticates, is a\nseparate integration from the platform repository''s credential, and the GitHub\nApp that writes desired state must never become the registry credential.\n\n# An incomplete version is not a rejected one\n\nA component''s three images are published by parallel jobs, so a version\nexisting in two repositories and not the third is an ordinary window of a\nminute or two. Nothing is recorded about it: every pass recomputes from the\nregistry, and a version reported as still publishing is expected to become\navailable on a later one without a restart, a cache expiry, or a new object.\n\nThat is the property the design rests on, and it is the first test in the file:\n`.3` is missing its console image, `.2` is the answer, the console job\nfinishes, and the *same* registry asked again answers `.3`.\n\nImages that all exist and disagree about their source commit are the opposite\ncase, and are reported separately. That is one version built twice, and no\namount of waiting fixes it.\n\nAn image carrying no `org.opencontainers.image.revision` is treated as\nincomplete rather than incoherent: a missing label is indistinguishable from a\npush still in flight, and waiting is the cheaper mistake.\n\n# Never backwards, whatever a registry lists\n\nCandidates are drawn from every repository''s tags -- not one nominated as the\nleader, which would make the answer depend on which build job pushed first --\nfiltered to the channel and series, and required to sort strictly after the\nversion already desired. They are considered newest first, and the first\ncomplete one wins.\n\nOrdering is `SemVer` precedence, not string order. `preview.9` precedes\n`preview.10`, and a rule built on `<` over strings would wave through exactly\nthe rollback it exists to prevent, and only once a preview number reached two\ndigits. One test asserts that at least five of its own cases would be wrong\nunder string ordering, so that regression cannot pass.\n\n# Checked by breaking it\n\nSix mutations, each failing a test that names what it is for: string ordering\nin place of precedence, a missing image skipped rather than reported, images\nnot required to share a commit, the floor ignored, the series ignored, and\ncandidates considered oldest first.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Report the versions that were skipped, not just the one chosen\n\nDiscovery stopped at the first complete version, which is the right answer and\nan incomplete account of it. An environment moving from `preview.2` to\n`preview.4` had no way to say what happened to `preview.3` -- the version was\nnever examined, so the gap looked arbitrary.\n\nEvery candidate above the desired version is now examined. The newest complete\none is still the answer; the rest are reported as what they are, whether they\nsort above the selected version or below it.\n\nThe cost is proportional to how far behind the environment is, which is the\nright shape: one advancing normally examines one or two, and one that has been\nheld for a month examines a month''s worth -- which is exactly the case where\nthe diagnostics are worth having.\n\nOne bad build does not poison the channel. `preview.3` built from two different\ncommits is skipped and named, and `preview.4` is selected.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Read published versions from an OCI registry, anonymously\n\nThe adapter behind the `Registry` port. It holds no credential at all: the\nSaaS Fabric packages are public, so it exchanges an anonymous pull token per\nrepository and reads.\n\nThat is one fewer secret between a published preview and an environment, and it\nkeeps a boundary clean by construction -- the GitHub App that writes platform\ndesired state is not, and must never become, the registry credential. A\ncredential that does not exist cannot be conflated with another one. When a\npackage eventually needs authenticating to, that is a registry integration with\nits own configuration, not a wider scope on an existing App.\n\n# What is remembered, and what is not\n\nNothing about what was found. A component''s images are published by parallel\njobs, so a version present in two repositories and not the third is an ordinary\nminutes-long window; an adapter that cached \"not there\" would still believe it\nan hour later. Every pass asks again.\n\nThe one thing held between calls is the pull token, which is a credential and\nnot an answer. It carries no recorded expiry: ageing out arrives as a `401`,\nwhich is cheaper to notice than to predict and takes the same path as a token\nrevoked early, so there is one mechanism rather than two.\n\n# Three things that are easy to get subtly wrong\n\n**A `404` is an answer.** A tag that is not published yet is `Ok(None)`, not an\nerror -- otherwise every publishing window becomes a failed discovery pass.\n\n**The digest pinned is the tag''s own.** For a multi-architecture image that is\nthe index, not one platform''s manifest, so a node still picks its own\narchitecture. The labels are read from the `linux/amd64` child, and an index\nwith no such entry reports no revision rather than promoting something this\nplatform cannot run.\n\n**A truncated tag listing is indistinguishable from a component with no newer\nversions**, so pagination is followed through the `Link` header and a listing\nthat pages further than the bound is an error rather than a short answer. The\n`Link` target is resolved against the configured base URL, so a registry cannot\ntell this adapter to send its next request somewhere else.\n\n# Checked by breaking it\n\nEleven cases against a registry answering over a real socket, including the\nwhole discovery pass end to end: `.3` published to two of three repositories,\nthen completed, and seen without anything being restarted.\n\nSix mutations, six named failures: a `404` treated as an error, pagination not\nfollowed, a platform''s digest pinned instead of the index''s, a `401` not\nretried, a rate limit classified as a refusal, and any architecture accepted\nfrom an index.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Say whether an artifact''s parts agree about where it came from\n\n`Resolved` reported an optional revision, which forced two different situations\ninto one answer: an artifact still publishing, and an artifact whose own parts\nname different commits. The first resolves itself in a minute; the second never\ndoes, and reporting it as pending would retry a broken build forever.\n\n`Provenance` says which it is. `Absent` stays incomplete -- a push in flight\nlooks identical to a label that was never set, and waiting is the cheaper\nmistake. `Disagreed` is incoherent, which is the same judgement the check\nacross repositories makes, one level further down.\n\nThis also states what an adapter owes the port. Reading one platform''s label\nout of a multi-architecture index proves that platform''s provenance, not the\nartifact''s, and \"the architecture we happen to run today\" is not a fact about\nthe image. An adapter reporting `Agreed` for an index has to have satisfied\nitself that every manifest it inspected says the same thing.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Prove an index''s provenance from every child, not from the one we run\n\nFor a multi-architecture image the index digest is the deployable identity, and\nthat part was already right. Reading `org.opencontainers.image.revision` from\nthe `linux/amd64` child was not: it proves that child''s provenance, and \"the\narchitecture LucentRoot happens to run today\" is not a fact about the image.\n\nEvery supported child is now inspected and they must agree. A child carrying no\nrevision makes the artifact `Absent`; children naming different commits make it\n`Disagreed`, which discovery reports as incoherent rather than as still\npublishing -- an index built from two commits will not become one by waiting.\n\nA child is supported when it declares a real platform. Build systems put\nprovenance attestations in the same index under `unknown/unknown`; they carry\nno revision label, and inspecting them would report every multi-architecture\nimage as unprovenanced. The fake now publishes one alongside every index, so\nthat is a case the tests cross rather than a case the code merely handles.\n\nToday''s publishing produces one architecture, so this changes no behaviour. It\nstops \"amd64 is the truth\" from being baked into the registry abstraction,\nwhich is the part that would have been expensive to find later.\n\nThree mutations, three named failures: stopping at the first child that\nanswers, inspecting attestations, and inspecting only amd64.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* State the provenance rule in terms of deployable children\n\nThe filter was written as \"skip unknown/unknown\", which is a fact about one\nbuild system. What this actually cares about is whether a descriptor could be a\nworkload image: only deployable children participate in provenance agreement,\nand a deployable child declares a concrete runtime platform.\n\nIt still excludes Buildx''s attestations, and now for a reason that will keep\nbeing the right one when something else puts a non-runtime member in an index.\n\nAn index with no deployable child at all is `Absent`, not `Agreed`. Zero\nchildren cannot agree with each other, and there is no artifact here whose\nprovenance there would be to prove -- which makes it the answer that means \"not\nyet\" rather than the one that means \"built twice\". Already true by\nconstruction, and true by accident until something asserted it: making an empty\nchild set agree fails the new test.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"5577937e562f04341cdfecfb4ef46fb66dbf1bca","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/5577937e562f04341cdfecfb4ef46fb66dbf1bca"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/428f09ce03ffa4bc99968ed1e08129922f251cbe","verification":{"payload":"tree 5577937e562f04341cdfecfb4ef46fb66dbf1bca\nparent 7340246407d794f41eed22155158d442b9fc74b2\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1788211171 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788211171 +1200\n\nRead published versions from an OCI registry, anonymously (#42)\n\n* Move a component''s desired state without knowing where it is pinned\n\n`set_component_desired_state(environment, component, version)` is the operation\nPlatform Management will call. It reads the environment''s manifest, works out\nwhich files carry the pins from what that manifest declares, and writes the\nmanifest and those files in one commit.\n\nNothing in this crate knows the platform repository''s layout. `pinnedIn` is\nwhere the layout is stated, and this refuses to write anything the manifest\ndoes not name. When that repository eventually renders its overlays *from* the\nmanifest, the lists empty and this writes one file, unchanged.\n\n# What a caller may and may not say\n\nA caller moves a component to a new *version*. Two rules make that precise, and\nboth are enforced before anything is read for writing:\n\n- the roles must be exactly the ones the manifest declares. Promoting the\n  console without the control plane would put two thirds of a release on an\n  environment and call it integrated;\n- each role''s repository must match what the manifest already says. Where a\n  component is published is the platform repository''s statement, not a\n  caller''s -- specification section 31.8, made structural.\n\nThe channel, the update policy, the hold and every `pinnedIn` are carried\nthrough untouched. Advancing a *held* component is the selector''s decision, not\nthis one''s -- but silently dropping the hold while writing would make that\ndecision impossible to enforce afterwards, so there is a test that it survives.\n\n# Six rules on every path, and one of them is not obvious\n\n    repository-relative, never absolute\n    no traversal\n    under a managedRoot\n    a .yaml or .yml manifest\n    exists\n    and actually pins the image that declared it\n\nThe platform repository''s CI enforces the same six. That duplication is\ndeliberate: CI proves the manifest is coherent at the commit it ran on, and\nthis applies them to whatever was actually read, which may be a state no CI has\nseen.\n\nThe absolute-path rule looked redundant -- `/etc/hosts.yaml` is already refused\nfor being under no `applications/` root. It is not: a manifest declaring `/` as\na managed root would admit it, and then the path''s own shape is the only thing\nleft. There is a test for exactly that, and removing the rule fails it.\n\n# Files are edited as lines, and the header is not this crate''s to rewrite\n\nThe overlays carry comments that are the reason a pin is what it is, so the\n`images:` entry is replaced in place and everything around it -- including a\nblock-scalar patch -- is emitted unchanged.\n\nThe manifest is machine-managed and rewritten whole, but its *header* is where\nthe platform repository explains what the file is and who may write it. A\nFabric release replacing that with its own prose would be Fabric editorialising\nin somebody else''s repository, so the leading comment block is carried through\nverbatim.\n\n# Checked by breaking it\n\nEleven cases. Each guard was removed and the suite re-run: the managed-root\nrule, the registry equality, the hold, the absolute-path rule, and `repin`''s\ninsistence on finding exactly one entry each fail a test that names what they\nare for.\n\nOne mutation reported NOTHING FAILED and was wrong about itself --\n`false && a || b` short-circuits the wrong way. Re-running it properly is what\nturned up the `/`-as-a-root case above.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Find the newest preview a component may move to\n\nDiscovery, as rules with no transport. The crate defines a `Registry` port and\nis handed an implementation: which registry, and how it authenticates, is a\nseparate integration from the platform repository''s credential, and the GitHub\nApp that writes desired state must never become the registry credential.\n\n# An incomplete version is not a rejected one\n\nA component''s three images are published by parallel jobs, so a version\nexisting in two repositories and not the third is an ordinary window of a\nminute or two. Nothing is recorded about it: every pass recomputes from the\nregistry, and a version reported as still publishing is expected to become\navailable on a later one without a restart, a cache expiry, or a new object.\n\nThat is the property the design rests on, and it is the first test in the file:\n`.3` is missing its console image, `.2` is the answer, the console job\nfinishes, and the *same* registry asked again answers `.3`.\n\nImages that all exist and disagree about their source commit are the opposite\ncase, and are reported separately. That is one version built twice, and no\namount of waiting fixes it.\n\nAn image carrying no `org.opencontainers.image.revision` is treated as\nincomplete rather than incoherent: a missing label is indistinguishable from a\npush still in flight, and waiting is the cheaper mistake.\n\n# Never backwards, whatever a registry lists\n\nCandidates are drawn from every repository''s tags -- not one nominated as the\nleader, which would make the answer depend on which build job pushed first --\nfiltered to the channel and series, and required to sort strictly after the\nversion already desired. They are considered newest first, and the first\ncomplete one wins.\n\nOrdering is `SemVer` precedence, not string order. `preview.9` precedes\n`preview.10`, and a rule built on `<` over strings would wave through exactly\nthe rollback it exists to prevent, and only once a preview number reached two\ndigits. One test asserts that at least five of its own cases would be wrong\nunder string ordering, so that regression cannot pass.\n\n# Checked by breaking it\n\nSix mutations, each failing a test that names what it is for: string ordering\nin place of precedence, a missing image skipped rather than reported, images\nnot required to share a commit, the floor ignored, the series ignored, and\ncandidates considered oldest first.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Report the versions that were skipped, not just the one chosen\n\nDiscovery stopped at the first complete version, which is the right answer and\nan incomplete account of it. An environment moving from `preview.2` to\n`preview.4` had no way to say what happened to `preview.3` -- the version was\nnever examined, so the gap looked arbitrary.\n\nEvery candidate above the desired version is now examined. The newest complete\none is still the answer; the rest are reported as what they are, whether they\nsort above the selected version or below it.\n\nThe cost is proportional to how far behind the environment is, which is the\nright shape: one advancing normally examines one or two, and one that has been\nheld for a month examines a month''s worth -- which is exactly the case where\nthe diagnostics are worth having.\n\nOne bad build does not poison the channel. `preview.3` built from two different\ncommits is skipped and named, and `preview.4` is selected.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Read published versions from an OCI registry, anonymously\n\nThe adapter behind the `Registry` port. It holds no credential at all: the\nSaaS Fabric packages are public, so it exchanges an anonymous pull token per\nrepository and reads.\n\nThat is one fewer secret between a published pre