ommit/7340246407d794f41eed22155158d442b9fc74b2","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKDczNDAyNDY0MDdkNzk0ZjQxZWVkMjIxNTUxNThkNDQyYjlmYzc0YjI","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/041c6d6296380c274065661602b0b1371ce8f6df","sha":"041c6d6296380c274065661602b0b1371ce8f6df","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/041c6d6296380c274065661602b0b1371ce8f6df"}],"sha":"7340246407d794f41eed22155158d442b9fc74b2","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/7340246407d794f41eed22155158d442b9fc74b2"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/041c6d6296380c274065661602b0b1371ce8f6df/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-31T21:07:56Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-31T21:07:56Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Move a component''s desired state without knowing where it is pinned (#40)\n\n* Move a component''s desired state without knowing where it is pinned\n\n`set_component_desired_state(environment, component, version)` is the operation\nPlatform Management will call. It reads the environment''s manifest, works out\nwhich files carry the pins from what that manifest declares, and writes the\nmanifest and those files in one commit.\n\nNothing in this crate knows the platform repository''s layout. `pinnedIn` is\nwhere the layout is stated, and this refuses to write anything the manifest\ndoes not name. When that repository eventually renders its overlays *from* the\nmanifest, the lists empty and this writes one file, unchanged.\n\n# What a caller may and may not say\n\nA caller moves a component to a new *version*. Two rules make that precise, and\nboth are enforced before anything is read for writing:\n\n- the roles must be exactly the ones the manifest declares. Promoting the\n  console without the control plane would put two thirds of a release on an\n  environment and call it integrated;\n- each role''s repository must match what the manifest already says. Where a\n  component is published is the platform repository''s statement, not a\n  caller''s -- specification section 31.8, made structural.\n\nThe channel, the update policy, the hold and every `pinnedIn` are carried\nthrough untouched. Advancing a *held* component is the selector''s decision, not\nthis one''s -- but silently dropping the hold while writing would make that\ndecision impossible to enforce afterwards, so there is a test that it survives.\n\n# Six rules on every path, and one of them is not obvious\n\n    repository-relative, never absolute\n    no traversal\n    under a managedRoot\n    a .yaml or .yml manifest\n    exists\n    and actually pins the image that declared it\n\nThe platform repository''s CI enforces the same six. That duplication is\ndeliberate: CI proves the manifest is coherent at the commit it ran on, and\nthis applies them to whatever was actually read, which may be a state no CI has\nseen.\n\nThe absolute-path rule looked redundant -- `/etc/hosts.yaml` is already refused\nfor being under no `applications/` root. It is not: a manifest declaring `/` as\na managed root would admit it, and then the path''s own shape is the only thing\nleft. There is a test for exactly that, and removing the rule fails it.\n\n# Files are edited as lines, and the header is not this crate''s to rewrite\n\nThe overlays carry comments that are the reason a pin is what it is, so the\n`images:` entry is replaced in place and everything around it -- including a\nblock-scalar patch -- is emitted unchanged.\n\nThe manifest is machine-managed and rewritten whole, but its *header* is where\nthe platform repository explains what the file is and who may write it. A\nFabric release replacing that with its own prose would be Fabric editorialising\nin somebody else''s repository, so the leading comment block is carried through\nverbatim.\n\n# Checked by breaking it\n\nEleven cases. Each guard was removed and the suite re-run: the managed-root\nrule, the registry equality, the hold, the absolute-path rule, and `repin`''s\ninsistence on finding exactly one entry each fail a test that names what they\nare for.\n\nOne mutation reported NOTHING FAILED and was wrong about itself --\n`false && a || b` short-circuits the wrong way. Re-running it properly is what\nturned up the `/`-as-a-root case above.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Pin the rendered manifest layout to a fixture\n\nThe rendering of `components.yaml` is a cross-repository contract. That\nrepository''s manifest is written in exactly the layout this crate produces, so\na promotion changes the version, the source revision and three digests -- and\nnothing else.\n\nNothing enforced that. Reordering a field in `Manifest`, or adding one, would\nhave compiled, passed every test, and then produced a diff reformatting the\nwhole file, in a commit whose message said it changed a digest.\n\nThe fixture is that layout, and the test is a byte-for-byte round trip through\n`parse` and `render`. If the format changes deliberately, updating the fixture\nis part of changing the contract, and the platform repository''s manifest has to\nbe regenerated to match.\n\nIt covers both shapes of the one field that varies: a component with a `hold`,\nnote and all, and one without.\n\nChecked by reordering two fields in `Manifest` -- the test fails on the first\nline that moved.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Refuse a promotion decided against a manifest that has since changed\n\nOnce humans and automation both write this file there is a race worth naming: a\nselector reads `automatic, no hold, desired preview.1`, decides to advance, and\nan operator commits a hold before the write lands. That write must not apply on\nthe strength of a policy view that is no longer true.\n\nIt does not, and without a policy check. The manifest is one of the files being\nwritten, so its revision is part of the write''s own precondition, and a\ndecision made against an older manifest cannot be applied to a newer one. The\nsame mechanism that refuses a concurrent version edit covers this -- one rule\nrather than two that have to be kept in step.\n\nWhat was missing was the assertion. The test covers the whole outcome, not just\nthe error: the hold survives, desired state has not moved, and neither overlay\nhas either. Disabling the relevant-path check makes it fail, which is what ties\nthe property to the mechanism rather than to a coincidence.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"e5bd535070b9cdde9aa1ade93bc76fe151ac8a80","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/e5bd535070b9cdde9aa1ade93bc76fe151ac8a80"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/041c6d6296380c274065661602b0b1371ce8f6df","verification":{"payload":"tree e5bd535070b9cdde9aa1ade93bc76fe151ac8a80\nparent a95257045f9700a64c96de0e9450da209d6461e8\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1788210476 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788210476 +1200\n\nMove a component''s desired state without knowing where it is pinned (#40)\n\n* Move a component''s desired state without knowing where it is pinned\n\n`set_component_desired_state(environment, component, version)` is the operation\nPlatform Management will call. It reads the environment''s manifest, works out\nwhich files carry the pins from what that manifest declares, and writes the\nmanifest and those files in one commit.\n\nNothing in this crate knows the platform repository''s layout. `pinnedIn` is\nwhere the layout is stated, and this refuses to write anything the manifest\ndoes not name. When that repository eventually renders its overlays *from* the\nmanifest, the lists empty and this writes one file, unchanged.\n\n# What a caller may and may not say\n\nA caller moves a component to a new *version*. Two rules make that precise, and\nboth are enforced before anything is read for writing:\n\n- the roles must be exactly the ones the manifest declares. Promoting the\n  console without the control plane would put two thirds of a release on an\n  environment and call it integrated;\n- each role''s repository must match what the manifest already says. Where a\n  component is published is the platform repository''s statement, not a\n  caller''s -- specification section 31.8, made structural.\n\nThe channel, the update policy, the hold and every `pinnedIn` are carried\nthrough untouched. Advancing a *held* component is the selector''s decision, not\nthis one''s -- but silently dropping the hold while writing would make that\ndecision impossible to enforce afterwards, so there is a test that it survives.\n\n# Six rules on every path, and one of them is not obvious\n\n    repository-relative, never absolute\n    no traversal\n    under a managedRoot\n    a .yaml or .yml manifest\n    exists\n    and actually pins the image that declared it\n\nThe platform repository''s CI enforces the same six. That duplication is\ndeliberate: CI proves the manifest is coherent at the commit it ran on, and\nthis applies them to whatever was actually read, which may be a state no CI has\nseen.\n\nThe absolute-path rule looked redundant -- `/etc/hosts.yaml` is already refused\nfor being under no `applications/` root. It is not: a manifest declaring `/` as\na managed root would admit it, and then the path''s own shape is the only thing\nleft. There is a test for exactly that, and removing the rule fails it.\n\n# Files are edited as lines, and the header is not this crate''s to rewrite\n\nThe overlays carry comments that are the reason a pin is what it is, so the\n`images:` entry is replaced in place and everything around it -- including a\nblock-scalar patch -- is emitted unchanged.\n\nThe manifest is machine-managed and rewritten whole, but its *header* is where\nthe platform repository explains what the file is and who may write it. A\nFabric release replacing that with its own prose would be Fabric editorialising\nin somebody else''s repository, so the leading comment block is carried through\nverbatim.\n\n# Checked by breaking it\n\nEleven cases. Each guard was removed and the suite re-run: the managed-root\nrule, the registry equality, the hold, the absolute-path rule, and `repin`''s\ninsistence on finding exactly one entry each fail a test that names what they\nare for.\n\nOne mutation reported NOTHING FAILED and was wrong about itself --\n`false && a || b` short-circuits the wrong way. Re-running it properly is what\nturned up the `/`-as-a-root case above.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Pin the rendered manifest layout to a fixture\n\nThe rendering of `components.yaml` is a cross-repository contract. That\nrepository''s manifest is written in exactly the layout this crate produces, so\na promotion changes the version, the source revision and three digests -- and\nnothing else.\n\nNothing enforced that. Reordering a field in `Manifest`, or adding one, would\nhave compiled, passed every test, and then produced a diff reformatting the\nwhole file, in a commit whose message said it changed a digest.\n\nThe fixture is that layout, and the test is a byte-for-byte round trip through\n`parse` and `render`. If the format changes deliberately, updating the fixture\nis part of changing the contract, and the platform repository''s manifest has to\nbe regenerated to match.\n\nIt covers both shapes of the one field that varies: a component with a `hold`,\nnote and all, and one without.\n\nChecked by reordering two fields in `Manifest` -- the test fails on the first\nline that moved.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Refuse a promotion decided against a manifest that has since changed\n\nOnce humans and automation both write this file there is a race worth naming: a\nselector reads `automatic, no hold, desired preview.1`, decides to advance, and\nan operator commits a hold before the write lands. That write must not apply on\nthe strength of a policy view that is no longer true.\n\nIt does not, and without a policy check. The manifest is one of the files being\nwritten, so its revision is part of the write''s own precondition, and a\ndecision made against an older manifest cannot be applied to a newer one. The\nsame mechanism that refuses a concurrent version edit covers this -- one rule\nrather than two that have to be kept in step.\n\nWhat was missing was the assertion. The test covers the whole outcome, not just\nthe error: the hold survives, desired state has not moved, and neither overlay\nhas either. Disabling the relevant-path check makes it fail, which is what ties\nthe property to the mechanism rather than to a coincidence.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqle0sCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA6JEQAB/B7d6jWnl3Jb2LV86DDM9f\nNhLb+CLLtUDrw2DdPw89cOUYBN78HQTG+3YP1yEfaCOdw+dt3l+tyaiFaTq+PEZu\ngLHzS3rseOPg1lE+iUDISlER1fRArXko4pEXvYZdOgXnXxDPp0rFArILbvsngarS\nq06i5phC4ufhXaSKOnHL7hi1Lv9RQv+S1+4XqeHkGl9kamevk6Rho7B+SYEmtA+b\ni3pqu4CRuAfJch+afPogbTahhh6tmdNGsSLi79LH3BgSDPYIWt9Y4xU7IrU2ObVJ\n4v+T6uiYZjeJCvZW7mrDSTGmPVtf5OCOT7Lko3gxn9q78izy8IWv39tRxOyl3OFK\nilloS7UQC1DE8iQc3o6hXbYNiY1KM58hp86EaPLaadHqu5phb5rBuIJAn1Q7emD+\n0RwswLBPFE124Oot3zx5NLvWCpDX8oe9wGbKBruFTfnPM0Eob3VmHkX9apL4Z7vL\noTA5DhAcvRfjOv0fYchLrbi3MF6t6P+MU52e1GES2ENLbeLUwlM24llT8+XYnPm7\n/fmdtcTbBxj+bLZsYQ0gyejfRwKYylkBdIH+zDpuUjlImFKYCDXdB/X0pWLb6Wog\nNZf1a8e/YDYK2sd/7+XWTElLCSe9DFG+xi8+itzc9iqwvgLWjfKp3lYE5Xl3nJRN\n7/UxBVffkgeAW6aF/fBr\n=9mdF\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-31T21:07:56Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/041c6d6296380c274065661602b0b1371ce8f6df","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKDA0MWM2ZDYyOTYzODBjMjc0MDY1NjYxNjAyYjBiMTM3MWNlOGY2ZGY","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/a95257045f9700a64c96de0e9450da209d6461e8","sha":"a95257045f9700a64c96de0e9450da209d6461e8","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/a95257045f9700a64c96de0e9450da209d6461e8"}],"sha":"041c6d6296380c274065661602b0b1371ce8f6df","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/041c6d6296380c274065661602b0b1371ce8f6df"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/a95257045f9700a64c96de0e9450da209d6461e8/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-31T21:07:32Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-31T21:07:32Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Publish a preview from a prerelease tag (#36)\n\n* Publish a preview from a prerelease tag\n\n`v0.3.0-preview.42` now publishes the same three images as `v0.3.0`, from the\nsame gates. The version check compares the tag''s *version core* against\n`workspace.package.version` rather than the whole tag, so a prerelease of the\nversion under development is publishable and a prerelease of any other version\nis not.\n\nThat means you bump the workspace version when you start working towards a\nrelease, not when you cut it. Every preview on the way to 0.3.0 is a prerelease\nof 0.3.0, and the eventual `v0.3.0` matches with no second bump. The guarantee\nthe old rule gave — an image''s tag and its `--version` agree — is unchanged;\nthere is simply more than one tag that can satisfy it.\n\nBuild metadata is rejected outright. `+` is not a legal character in an OCI\ntag, so such a version could never name its own image, and finding that out at\n`docker push` is finding it out too late.\n\nPublishing is the whole of this repository''s part in it. Nothing here deploys a\npreview and nothing here announces one: the platform''s desired state names the\nversion an environment runs, and whatever maintains that desired state\ndiscovers a new preview by looking at the registry. So this repository holds no\ncredential for `saas-fabric-platform` and needs none — which is why\n`org.opencontainers.image.revision` matters more than it looks. It is what lets\nanything holding a digest prove which commit the artifact was built from\nwithout asking here.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Say where the artifact version actually lives\n\nTwo corrections, both to claims rather than behaviour.\n\nThe version step said a mismatched tag would publish \"an image whose name says\none thing and whose `--version` says another\". There is no `--version`. Nothing\nin the workspace reads `CARGO_PKG_VERSION` and no binary reports a version, so\nthat sentence described a guarantee that has never existed. What the check\nactually prevents is an image claiming a version its source was never on.\n\nThe second is the one that matters for what comes next. A preview has two\nversions and they are not the same number: the source says `0.3.0` and the\nartifact says `0.3.0-preview.7`. The artifact version is an *image* fact — the\ntag, and `org.opencontainers.image.version` — which is where anything reporting\n\"what is running\" reads it from, because the pod references an image and not a\nprocess it can ask.\n\nAlso stated outright, because it is the property that makes previews cheap:\ncutting `preview.2` after `preview.1` touches no source. The prerelease part is\nartifact metadata, not a property of the tree. Only moving to a new stable line\nis a commit.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"8af661d6c21f9213191ae140aa0ed1f8519f473a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/8af661d6c21f9213191ae140aa0ed1f8519f473a"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/a95257045f9700a64c96de0e9450da209d6461e8","verification":{"payload":"tree 8af661d6c21f9213191ae140aa0ed1f8519f473a\nparent d568e7c8976bac83bf88784ad1dd258f445efa84\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1788210452 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788210452 +1200\n\nPublish a preview from a prerelease tag (#36)\n\n* Publish a preview from a prerelease tag\n\n`v0.3.0-preview.42` now publishes the same three images as `v0.3.0`, from the\nsame gates. The version check compares the tag''s *version core* against\n`workspace.package.version` rather than the whole tag, so a prerelease of the\nversion under development is publishable and a prerelease of any other version\nis not.\n\nThat means you bump the workspace version when you start working towards a\nrelease, not when you cut it. Every preview on the way to 0.3.0 is a prerelease\nof 0.3.0, and the eventual `v0.3.0` matches with no second bump. The guarantee\nthe old rule gave — an image''s tag and its `--version` agree — is unchanged;\nthere is simply more than one tag that can satisfy it.\n\nBuild metadata is rejected outright. `+` is not a legal character in an OCI\ntag, so such a version could never name its own image, and finding that out at\n`docker push` is finding it out too late.\n\nPublishing is the whole of this repository''s part in it. Nothing here deploys a\npreview and nothing here announces one: the platform''s desired state names the\nversion an environment runs, and whatever maintains that desired state\ndiscovers a new preview by looking at the registry. So this repository holds no\ncredential for `saas-fabric-platform` and needs none — which is why\n`org.opencontainers.image.revision` matters more than it looks. It is what lets\nanything holding a digest prove which commit the artifact was built from\nwithout asking here.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Say where the artifact version actually lives\n\nTwo corrections, both to claims rather than behaviour.\n\nThe version step said a mismatched tag would publish \"an image whose name says\none thing and whose `--version` says another\". There is no `--version`. Nothing\nin the workspace reads `CARGO_PKG_VERSION` and no binary reports a version, so\nthat sentence described a guarantee that has never existed. What the check\nactually prevents is an image claiming a version its source was never on.\n\nThe second is the one that matters for what comes next. A preview has two\nversions and they are not the same number: the source says `0.3.0` and the\nartifact says `0.3.0-preview.7`. The artifact version is an *image* fact — the\ntag, and `org.opencontainers.image.version` — which is where anything reporting\n\"what is running\" reads it from, because the pod references an image and not a\nprocess it can ask.\n\nAlso stated outright, because it is the property that makes previews cheap:\ncutting `preview.2` after `preview.1` touches no source. The prerelease part is\nartifact metadata, not a property of the tree. Only moving to a new stable line\nis a commit.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqle0UCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAvlgQAHr57O4f8FeLa1n7mqf8D52p\nwUfFalH9yCwJklX9XQWdINNsXL+cAmP4bZJbXEI4OE94vZQmDSJPgDJpuf/T0THt\noAGqxXsVqVZYWsIz+C39hDXGvQV++cmMNhN3JEpIM4+a0UQV0sC5q70pLK0ItXpl\nj8Z6eYwdPxRvgNkkRpylGYfTFyQClWxk+1cEOcYyNZ91beBYuEDIYtyrXtzfOEX5\n7Bgy0Oi5WnzNkCzTEYg2JQdyo58ohmz6gpfSb82zR/dvM6wSuVhRa2Md4QdkaCyB\nqRI1559UqPUgjdlbGIQBJaJ6c+73xX+XqS2c/9lPmLzsJ8UAZNjpSCJF7FFzeqlc\nOIWiNPYD7lkCJDvOSnahe//sEHGzzvY91iDpSpQ7ejTRUwR/nZA3WGVvLXc+QBwc\nWYtfkogBPy7wpAhNGqMRxbKg+4K9BJudKqpyTa1U6ISJmAIH0ffhaZ1bpSnpPhuC\nYY0BZTVGPuF9dRtvg+x1qxju+/7qdb8Tl9NUl6WIc+fs3PBtwjOY+Mg8YSvydu2Q\nCZoU/Knn7GxtYa3wYdpQOpri/VtipKPKKhb8+n5IP4Dk9+mzvjPGFWBEYPTx7m0X\nW/iTSDJ13C7l3jG2kLeC1AymqFOyaB9aRdRm4nCAzgquaAuv6fQVOy5DmZIKiWNm\nx6rSuYeeH4nREn6o76aG\n=mh1o\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-31T21:07:33Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/a95257045f9700a64c96de0e9450da209d6461e8","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKGE5NTI1NzA0NWY5NzAwYTY0Yzk2ZGUwZTk0NTBkYTIwOWQ2NDYxZTg","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/d568e7c8976bac83bf88784ad1dd258f445efa84","sha":"d568e7c8976bac83bf88784ad1dd258f445efa84","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/d568e7c8976bac83bf88784ad1dd258f445efa84"}],"sha":"a95257045f9700a64c96de0e9450da209d6461e8","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/a95257045f9700a64c96de0e9450da209d6461e8"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/d568e7c8976bac83bf88784ad1dd258f445efa84/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-31T21:07:28Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-31T21:07:28Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Declare 0.3.0 as the next stable line (#37)\n\n`workspace.package.version` names the version under development, not the last\none released. Moving it to 0.3.0 declares the line the next release will be cut\nfrom; it does not release anything, and no tag is implied by it.\n\nIt is also what unblocks previews. The release workflow requires a tag''s\nversion *core* to equal this number, so `v0.3.0-preview.1` cannot be minted\nwhile the workspace says 0.2.2 — and once it says 0.3.0, every preview on the\nway to that release is a tag and nothing else. No source change stands between\n`preview.1` and `preview.2`.\n\nThe console moves with it. Nothing enforces that `apps/control-plane-ui`''s\npackage version tracks the workspace, but the release publishes all three\nimages under one version, and a console package claiming 0.2.2 inside an image\ntagged 0.3.0 is the same \"the name says one thing and the contents say another\"\nproblem the tag check exists to prevent. Worth a gate of its own at some point;\nit has been kept in step by hand so far.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"a4575f0d5232053f283dfaeec0e654188dc7bb7d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/a4575f0d5232053f283dfaeec0e654188dc7bb7d"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/d568e7c8976bac83bf88784ad1dd258f445efa84","verification":{"payload":"tree a4575f0d5232053f283dfaeec0e654188dc7bb7d\nparent d34a75c135763bb60acb8a428111e9767d40ee01\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1788210448 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788210448 +1200\n\nDeclare 0.3.0 as the next stable line (#37)\n\n`workspace.package.version` names the version under development, not the last\none released. Moving it to 0.3.0 declares the line the next release will be cut\nfrom; it does not release anything, and no tag is implied by it.\n\nIt is also what unblocks previews. The release workflow requires a tag''s\nversion *core* to equal this number, so `v0.3.0-preview.1` cannot be minted\nwhile the workspace says 0.2.2 — and once it says 0.3.0, every preview on the\nway to that release is a tag and nothing else. No source change stands between\n`preview.1` and `preview.2`.\n\nThe console moves with it. Nothing enforces that `apps/control-plane-ui`''s\npackage version tracks the workspace, but the release publishes all three\nimages under one version, and a console package claiming 0.2.2 inside an image\ntagged 0.3.0 is the same \"the name says one thing and the contents say another\"\nproblem the tag check exists to prevent. Worth a gate of its own at some point;\nit has been kept in step by hand so far.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqle0QCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAd6YQAKz/n1ng5vEU0TYPj5UMqRnI\nDsAypJHcsB28HMRBR0A86nVt3TEZfPW5uvbWSjQwm1WkQSI8Dj2JtqJcqex8e127\nm+wphClMrCObmOKwEX8Mx8XXTUuF4L5PqtLvLPkpU8W0IZsiix7rXQjlxJ/ddfDy\nCPRPTCh6QV3swck4Dcfz/UJcb1RHpk6oVrHv1NaxgRwfFpg8oKCRpmBsx3Ba/uCv\nmtFPwOqItVh06ylxDjs2Df2lQwRqYvathDltld5B1NAeXWPES6XnynujvUNlzezb\ndYF531HcNox0XHEr8Gkx32ub+kTldx7EcAWqs3rdOLTQUnNEU9sBbow/9xT8jwb5\n7VX0KlPn7pyAR+PJrXdaUOckcKEaQ3vrDqRlsjMpGT8WLeW2rK8RJGsvJG92wOcF\nXqZBn97EcUNSTa4WGycRY8OFq0bt8rVNnpIYqGsb+8UtxKgY9yTTQ1okOgSah7w1\nGpiHRtLVVzXGPPDJ84X/NTsyaMXymGnoB9rHX8Hz3Zr/em7k6D7ZROubKOLIDXFT\nKvf04g2hgO7DYsdhCgb1doEUqx6ef9z9ij12/HNza2dPPCL+mGemPerXZ3oQxW2S\npWQL5ooR/x5HlhmhUPiMPETEkkIA275YY8zu4S75jsz2X1uTEYx2ITD1hYbCOFkO\ny3mdBMcvEywcng1t8uFk\n=om9y\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-31T21:07:29Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/d568e7c8976bac83bf88784ad1dd258f445efa84","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKGQ1NjhlN2M4OTc2YmFjODNiZjg4Nzg0YWQxZGQyNThmNDQ1ZWZhODQ","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/d34a75c135763bb60acb8a428111e9767d40ee01","sha":"d34a75c135763bb60acb8a428111e9767d40ee01","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/d34a75c135763bb60acb8a428111e9767d40ee01"}],"sha":"d568e7c8976bac83bf88784ad1dd258f445efa84","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/d568e7c8976bac83bf88784ad1dd258f445efa84"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/d34a75c135763bb60acb8a428111e9767d40ee01/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-31T09:24:16Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-31T09:24:16Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Write platform desired state as one commit, or not at all (#39)\n\nMoving a component''s version touches more than one file: the version record,\nand every overlay that pins one of its images. Written as separate commits, the\nbranch passes through states nobody chose -- a record naming a version the\noverlays do not deploy, or two of three images moved -- and Argo CD is entitled\nto reconcile any of them, because a commit on the branch an environment follows\n*is* desired state.\n\n`fabric-platform-git` makes each change one tree, one commit, one ref update.\nThe contents API cannot do that; it writes one file per commit. So this speaks\nthe Git Data API: blobs, a tree layered on an explicit `base_tree`, a commit\nwith one parent, and a non-forced ref update.\n\n# The concurrency primitive is the parent commit\n\nGitHub''s update-a-reference endpoint takes no expected-old-SHA. It takes the new\nSHA and `force`, and with `force=false` it requires a fast-forward -- so a\ncommit whose parent is the head that was read cannot fast-forward a head that\nhas since moved, and the host answers `409`.\n\nThat is the only concurrency signal, and it is deliberately not the end of it:\n\n    409\n     ↓\n    re-read the head, and the revisions of the paths being written\n     ├─ unchanged → rebuild on the new head and retry\n     └─ changed   → Conflict, and nothing is written\n\nAn unrelated merge to the platform repository therefore costs a retry and never\nreaches an operator; a change to a file this write is editing is refused rather\nthan overwritten. That is the same distinction `fabric-client-git` makes with\nper-file blob revisions, reached differently because the unit of change here is\na *set* of files.\n\nRetries are bounded. An unbounded one is a reconciliation loop that never\nconverges and never says so, which is why `Contended` exists and is not the\nsame answer as `Conflict`: one means wait, the other means somebody has to\ndecide.\n\n# What the tests are for\n\nThe race is the feature, so the fake is a stateful Git host over a real socket\nthat applies a commit only when its parent is the head, keeps a snapshot per\ncommit so a pinned read answers honestly, and can be told to land somebody\nelse''s commit in the window between the head being read and the branch being\nasked to move. A stub returning a canned `409` would have proved the error\nmapping and nothing about the mechanism.\n\nEach guard was checked by breaking it:\n\n- forcing the ref             → 5 tests fail\n- disabling the path check    → 3 tests fail, and the write overwrites\n- unbounding the retries      → the bounded-retry test fails\n\n`force: true` is additionally scanned for across the crate''s source, because\nthe runtime assertion only covers paths a test exercises and the next one to be\nwritten is the one that matters.\n\n# Not yet wired to anything\n\nNo edge to `fabric-control-plane`: the Platform Management port does not exist\nyet. This stands alone until there is something to be the adapter *for*, which\nis better than inventing a port to satisfy a shape.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"ac04838f7a7cd46e107845537673f098f2013d4d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/ac04838f7a7cd46e107845537673f098f2013d4d"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/d34a75c135763bb60acb8a428111e9767d40ee01","verification":{"payload":"tree ac04838f7a7cd46e107845537673f098f2013d4d\nparent 89870d894e53cfbedb951071bf5b610ba3a3e7c6\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1788168256 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788168256 +1200\n\nWrite platform desired state as one commit, or not at all (#39)\n\nMoving a component''s version touches more than one file: the version record,\nand every overlay that pins one of its images. Written as separate commits, the\nbranch passes through states nobody chose -- a record naming a version the\noverlays do not deploy, or two of three images moved -- and Argo CD is entitled\nto reconcile any of them, because a commit on the branch an environment follows\n*is* desired state.\n\n`fabric-platform-git` makes each change one tree, one commit, one ref update.\nThe contents API cannot do that; it writes one file per commit. So this speaks\nthe Git Data API: blobs, a tree layered on an explicit `base_tree`, a commit\nwith one parent, and a non-forced ref update.\n\n# The concurrency primitive is the parent commit\n\nGitHub''s update-a-reference endpoint takes no expected-old-SHA. It takes the new\nSHA and `force`, and with `force=false` it requires a fast-forward -- so a\ncommit whose parent is the head that was read cannot fast-forward a head that\nhas since moved, and the host answers `409`.\n\nThat is the only concurrency signal, and it is deliberately not the end of it:\n\n    409\n     ↓\n    re-read the head, and the revisions of the paths being written\n     ├─ unchanged → rebuild on the new head and retry\n     └─ changed   → Conflict, and nothing is written\n\nAn unrelated merge to the platform repository therefore costs a retry and never\nreaches an operator; a change to a file this write is editing is refused rather\nthan overwritten. That is the same distinction `fabric-client-git` makes with\nper-file blob revisions, reached differently because the unit of change here is\na *set* of files.\n\nRetries are bounded. An unbounded one is a reconciliation loop that never\nconverges and never says so, which is why `Contended` exists and is not the\nsame answer as `Conflict`: one means wait, the other means somebody has to\ndecide.\n\n# What the tests are for\n\nThe race is the feature, so the fake is a stateful Git host over a real socket\nthat applies a commit only when its parent is the head, keeps a snapshot per\ncommit so a pinned read answers honestly, and can be told to land somebody\nelse''s commit in the window between the head being read and the branch being\nasked to move. A stub returning a canned `409` would have proved the error\nmapping and nothing about the mechanism.\n\nEach guard was checked by breaking it:\n\n- forcing the ref             → 5 tests fail\n- disabling the path check    → 3 tests fail, and the write overwrites\n- unbounding the retries      → the bounded-retry test fails\n\n`force: true` is additionally scanned for across the crate''s source, because\nthe runtime assertion only covers paths a test exercises and the next one to be\nwritten is the one that matters.\n\n# Not yet wired to anything\n\nNo edge to `fabric-control-plane`: the Platform Management port does not exist\nyet. This stands alone until there is something to be the adapter *for*, which\nis better than inventing a port to satisfy a shape.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqlUhACRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAYu4QAAm3uOnSmEyXh+gtyP16szrg\nx07aTVkaqhJUluM9gt8VQ0C07zZHZhu43oJdzSepYkr5yCh7Fg1G2jn8797mxrmL\nBY4/AMl6v7IgV+hEJJtadDQOq7C9ABdnBr+/MIcNAmQHi72CrFtUZ8Ixq75qpS0f\nYgMsMC5d2/EpSFfGGRgXr3vlFdJZI3Ejb86DfRgCDufpYzL6lioUua8KktgYFwuk\nASJFjKfaM+sMgay1ekPJYAHbz2zjw1q2YkT9mgC53hEyLjEq3/uvd9x5VmU/ildz\nwOAOrd1egVcTkPaSWb3ota9pLmScrPlKWHXOMtr2VAFSMkPo8oQSWM3wI2m8rHba\nSPeqUfDynfNX16vueEN7OoMrcUdSguugGs44EPQerOHUajSn4mb7Cv9eZnmEXNlB\nVve5N8IScSlmDTaTXdspfCLi/R1dmDQ5bspA2o3LXmPKZo+Yypcpvu+1f2+IFi2S\nRPIxINTYdDwNSNpRy7awpYZ9GhAhqrAqzRlZA8xL5gkEqNeRi2FBp/z++RHdOipP\n0iA8E42LJVZXs6Bod8Cn10MQhZqju1TE5ohQ4nP/8ZD6I6zgvNlz1gHJVn/OAjLk\nUPt5ewK1WI7boCExXvS2edKFoIuhehAmyPFN87ubr2rat2aTZ63Xi3DxsU9ZQqwd\nF/7LP4OFRKHYqvDsLjla\n=qfja\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-31T09:24:17Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/d34a75c135763bb60acb8a428111e9767d40ee01","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKGQzNGE3NWMxMzU3NjNiYjYwYWNiOGE0MjgxMTFlOTc2N2Q0MGVlMDE","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/89870d894e53cfbedb951071bf5b610ba3a3e7c6","sha":"89870d894e53cfbedb951071bf5b610ba3a3e7c6","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/89870d894e53cfbedb951071bf5b610ba3a3e7c6"}],"sha":"d34a75c135763bb60acb8a428111e9767d40ee01","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/d34a75c135763bb60acb8a428111e9767d40ee01"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/89870d894e53cfbedb951071bf5b610ba3a3e7c6/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-31T09:01:50Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-31T09:01:50Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Give the two Git integrations one credential and no shared adapter (#38)\n\nSaaS Fabric is about to connect to a Git host for a second, independent\nreason. Client Configuration writes client desired state; Platform Management\nwrites platform desired state. The specification requires them to be separate\nGitHub Apps, independently installable, configurable and removable — so two\nintegrations, two Apps, two repositories, two credentials, and no edge between\nthe adapters.\n\nWhat is not two things is how a private key becomes a bearer. That is one\nexchange with one endpoint, behind a cache whose correctness is subtle enough\nto have been got wrong once already: a stated expiry that must be read rather\nthan assumed, a wall-clock remaining lifetime measured against a monotonic\ndeadline, and an invalidation path for a token that stops working inside its\nstated life. Two copies of that would be two copies of the platform''s\ncredential-minting code, and a fix to one would silently miss the other.\n\n`fabric-git-host` now owns `GitCredential`, `BearerSource` and the assertion\nsigner. It depends on `fabric-core` and nothing else, and it knows nothing\nabout which repository it authenticates to or what is stored there.\n\nThe one thing that could not move as-is is the error vocabulary. `BearerSource`\nreturned `RepositoryError` — the *client* repository''s word for failure — which\nwould have made the shared crate depend on the port one of its two callers\nimplements. It now reports its own `TokenError`, and `fabric-client-git` maps\nthat into `RepositoryError` through a free function rather than a `From` impl:\nboth types are foreign to it, and more to the point the mapping is a decision\nthat adapter makes, which the platform integration is entitled to make\ndifferently.\n\nThe three variants survive the translation because each leads somewhere\ndifferent — look at the App, wait and retry, or fix the request. Collapsing\nthem would put a misconfigured App into a retry loop, or send an operator\nhunting a broken secret during a rate limit.\n\nNo behaviour changes. All eleven moved tests run in their new home, and were\nchecked against a broken margin to confirm they still fail when the thing they\nprotect does.\n\n`fabric-client-git` sheds `jsonwebtoken`, `time` and its `tokio` sync feature,\nwhich only the token path used.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"929a7ea98f65be41385b0060f5d215eb961b8613","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/929a7ea98f65be41385b0060f5d215eb961b8613"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/89870d894e53cfbedb951071bf5b610ba3a3e7c6","verification":{"payload":"tree 929a7ea98f65be41385b0060f5d215eb961b8613\nparent 3a1e7461b6aff10033384d16537081425eef6d67\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1788166910 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788166910 +1200\n\nGive the two Git integrations one credential and no shared adapter (#38)\n\nSaaS Fabric is about to connect to a Git host for a second, independent\nreason. Client Configuration writes client desired state; Platform Management\nwrites platform desired state. The specification requires them to be separate\nGitHub Apps, independently installable, configurable and removable — so two\nintegrations, two Apps, two repositories, two credentials, and no edge between\nthe adapters.\n\nWhat is not two things is how a private key becomes a bearer. That is one\nexchange with one endpoint, behind a cache whose correctness is subtle enough\nto have been got wrong once already: a stated expiry that must be read rather\nthan assumed, a wall-clock remaining lifetime measured against a monotonic\ndeadline, and an invalidation path for a token that stops working inside its\nstated life. Two copies of that would be two copies of the platform''s\ncredential-minting code, and a fix to one would silently miss the other.\n\n`fabric-git-host` now owns `GitCredential`, `BearerSource` and the assertion\nsigner. It depends on `fabric-core` and nothing else, and it knows nothing\nabout which repository it authenticates to or what is stored there.\n\nThe one thing that could not move as-is is the error vocabulary. `BearerSource`\nreturned `RepositoryError` — the *client* repository''s word for failure — which\nwould have made the shared crate depend on the port one of its two callers\nimplements. It now reports its own `TokenError`, and `fabric-client-git` maps\nthat into `RepositoryError` through a free function rather than a `From` impl:\nboth types are foreign to it, and more to the point the mapping is a decision\nthat adapter makes, which the platform integration is entitled to make\ndifferently.\n\nThe three variants survive the translation because each leads somewhere\ndifferent — look at the App, wait and retry, or fix the request. Collapsing\nthem would put a misconfigured App into a retry loop, or send an operator\nhunting a broken secret during a rate limit.\n\nNo behaviour changes. All eleven moved tests run in their new home, and were\nchecked against a broken margin to confirm they still fail when the thing they\nprotect does.\n\n`fabric-client-git` sheds `jsonwebtoken`, `time` and its `tokio` sync feature,\nwhich only the token path used.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqlUL+CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA2IAQAK6sQ1XKwAd9CzHsrXGNAmVK\ntoB8s/XjYbvp4ZDCoGkj0JrwqpEB9aIvN95Gx5R/Q5B+lLsWRFmloTOSKA4H4bJ0\nshHPgVH/O6sEYHGA0HXZfI2YIB2mN09x7+PNRqgOkEUZIl9d9aJhSOr21otNZbDu\n/Y/2hx/XEyg+ZTbiHzqRTzOsZzyMe/ORsFa2OUJrtUgTQ3tnAO38GtV/xRLIZ/s9\nXfboANiRlehio9P0zuhglgUW+AfNETLBtligfmtKtOOLlGV8NPxAm6UO7f8Nu73R\nQ41CKOU5mhyJwad1W2+JF3ybb+BH3IdMSCt+lcfJuYa2H8j+DQyFgiaTQzFgjROH\noQghtvy1erAbmCEV590bStN8TzHm8YJfWtAg4M3LEhAMB13Z6rCioFTuX/Waj9oX\n+BKKzxcX4ioPp4K3nV8FPVCX0dCxSTw8ioL+iz6u4vWOdL1iCigouQOcq+/grMyu\n9N1wlhAqTU9uV1jhbRL3p387BtvPosElX8Bo1BJFujs15+EB4wgrlVLE5Bvxwpef\nOAjLfoI5zWS7CE6cdnc+NGUXjMp1Av9ayBz+x/M9T8b4BqlvS4ha9ES9S4R2l7vF\n4ZLMxgsQXTgnMEY/BfUsalLjSwyLnNzc3kJV/BL2v8kBax4fdchbcc8lDWNOYVHK\nuOgYzH0jRS6J++Q5KIBa\n=a8Qd\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-31T09:01:51Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/89870d894e53cfbedb951071bf5b610ba3a3e7c6","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKDg5ODcwZDg5NGU1M2NmYmVkYjk1MTA3MWJmNWI2MTBiYTNhM2U3YzY","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/3a1e7461b6aff10033384d16537081425eef6d67","sha":"3a1e7461b6aff10033384d16537081425eef6d67","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/3a1e7461b6aff10033384d16537081425eef6d67"}],"sha":"89870d894e53cfbedb951071bf5b610ba3a3e7c6","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/89870d894e53cfbedb951071bf5b610ba3a3e7c6"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/3a1e7461b6aff10033384d16537081425eef6d67/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-31T02:36:29Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-31T02:36:29Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Write down what makes a slice done (#34)\n\nEmpirically justified rather than preferred. The Secrets slice had an\nadapter suite against a real OpenBao, unit tests over every type, and a\nconsole that built and passed its own tests -- and three of its five\noperations returned 400 to every call, because an extractor read its path\nparameter positionally and the routes had grown a second one.\n\nEvery layer was green over a feature that mostly did not work. Layer\ntests are designed not to cross the seams where composition defects live,\nwhich is why more of them would not have helped.\n\nThe rule asks for one test through the surface an operator uses, doing\nthe thing the slice exists to let them do. It does not ask for a browser\n-- driving the router in-process catches a route that does not match, an\nextractor that cannot read its parameter, a status that collapses two\nfailures, and a header nobody set.\n\nAlso records the related habit, since it has now cost us three times: a\ntest that cannot fail is worse than no test, because it reports success.\nMutate the thing, watch the test fail, then keep the test.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"7326d7363a3e1b7f3a9b7dfb20fb6047cbf899c7","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/7326d7363a3e1b7f3a9b7dfb20fb6047cbf899c7"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/3a1e7461b6aff10033384d16537081425eef6d67","verification":{"payload":"tree 7326d7363a3e1b7f3a9b7dfb20fb6047cbf899c7\nparent 8691737c41a5f52969b1a5de983c5a2e1f47c201\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1788143789 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788143789 +1200\n\nWrite down what makes a slice done (#34)\n\nEmpirically justified rather than preferred. The Secrets slice had an\nadapter suite against a real OpenBao, unit tests over every type, and a\nconsole that built and passed its own tests -- and three of its five\noperations returned 400 to every call, because an extractor read its path\nparameter positionally and the routes had grown a second one.\n\nEvery layer was green over a feature that mostly did not work. Layer\ntests are designed not to cross the seams where composition defects live,\nwhich is why more of them would not have helped.\n\nThe rule asks for one test through the surface an operator uses, doing\nthe thing the slice exists to let them do. It does not ask for a browser\n-- driving the router in-process catches a route that does not match, an\nextractor that cannot read its parameter, a status that collapses two\nfailures, and a header nobody set.\n\nAlso records the related habit, since it has now cost us three times: a\ntest that cannot fail is worse than no test, because it reports success.\nMutate the thing, watch the test fail, then keep the test.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqlOitCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAaFoQAATyHKOsvsDKDiKM2MjdNagT\n7LRkpJAnJ0SvevCe+m+qtqK/jtCyJH5wz4ZFaE6HYkdMUj5qneMr3fXvRY6bolbP\nGEih0CE67QnHy6BOgS5Tc1CbWRB15d9fG17pEZTWtnTGeQEOr0lcU76kf1wrTm2N\nmZuSy7N3j0SNvpsdIpsLmF8y+af7tI/pZhJhh1aR0FfODsLDID4Rq1jxJ3pvfB5W\nCuHk6+atduPHCX1lzS13o+AxDW0H+OFLb5JyMygd4UEe2aNE4XF4Wckbx7ZC44cw\n6mu17at1XXyTT5RmIV+6YR49VTrK/gyQRpvFwWi4Lf/seIz1aFdqxt79t1tsuEN2\ngFrGr/yduU+IGhJm+NnYFLBOQgVyFXQ/6pZy8tY4H2r1PTRIilIOPw7YBEt4vw3S\nAgfZdTXY25zi0qKqFSzHWzXaAWAgmU++qA/61TzqT4Kf7z8SD2nS/4qQrpr3gKcM\nzi0yirOpzKGQTJG6WXhjbXqFkWm7vxU7vxN8GN5MzmRYIbOwqQyEpCzPFMuW3sRh\nHc3Gm+KVFyaCOsaC9oM5y/NZBOM8KTi4U+fxKj0viExQv7Dxvgf+Mbu14w1rB/Jr\nf8xFu8PW9F1lFvd4e7ab+fXg4ZRl+kvAhlXDzZD+8EMiB2Rxpg0UOXRc0oCVk6Ev\nMQEVxyd7ttK1kWAfhCky\n=rMrN\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-31T02:36:30Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/3a1e7461b6aff10033384d16537081425eef6d67","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKDNhMWU3NDYxYjZhZmYxMDAzMzM4NGQxNjUzNzA4MTQyNWVlZjZkNjc","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/8691737c41a5f52969b1a5de983c5a2e1f47c201","sha":"8691737c41a5f52969b1a5de983c5a2e1f47c201","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/8691737c41a5f52969b1a5de983c5a2e1f47c201"}],"sha":"3a1e7461b6aff10033384d16537081425eef6d67","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/3a1e7461b6aff10033384d16537081425eef6d67"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/8691737c41a5f52969b1a5de983c5a2e1f47c201/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-31T01:56:16Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-31T01:56:16Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Give the release workflow the same permission floor CI has (#33)\n\nA job that declares no `permissions` inherits whatever the repository\ngrants the workflow token, which is usually more than reading a checkout.\n`release.yml` had no floor and its `verify` job had no block of its own,\nso it ran with the default while doing nothing but running gates.\n\n`ci.yml` already declares `contents: read` at the top, so this is\nrelease catching up rather than a new convention. `build` keeps its own\nblock and still states `packages: write`, because a job that publishes\nshould say so where somebody reading it will see it.\n\nNot introduced by any recent change -- the alert predates the secrets\nwork -- but pre-existing means not-introduced-here rather than\nsafe-to-leave.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"dec5d95b964dafe459e57c2bd29ea45c65643ee8","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/dec5d95b964dafe459e57c2bd29ea45c65643ee8"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/8691737c41a5f52969b1a5de983c5a2e1f47c201","verification":{"payload":"tree dec5d95b964dafe459e57c2bd29ea45c65643ee8\nparent 8c20615d94560f9ac6564b895bc1f96688f6235e\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1788141376 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788141376 +1200\n\nGive the release workflow the same permission floor CI has (#33)\n\nA job that declares no `permissions` inherits whatever the repository\ngrants the workflow token, which is usually more than reading a checkout.\n`release.yml` had no floor and its `verify` job had no block of its own,\nso it ran with the default while doing nothing but running gates.\n\n`ci.yml` already declares `contents: read` at the top, so this is\nrelease catching up rather than a new convention. `build` keeps its own\nblock and still states `packages: write`, because a job that publishes\nshould say so where somebody reading it will see it.\n\nNot introduced by any recent change -- the alert predates the secrets\nwork -- but pre-existing means not-introduced-here rather than\nsafe-to-leave.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqlN9ACRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAxtIQADrcyPUU20HQB0ElYIRtFHkC\nZ4N1Ykty9Op/UbqQrAGBAjm/hu0hAxKWewP5KN4p0ywibe9GfyopyEoaTZ9Vt5cK\nMfAz9+w85pyUxsE3HvtWph/nvY4KJTGG2OFrFLQgYMQQtb0kHkX2B0Xe1aI1nR5U\n1n31s1L8wqtr5JX1BMu69yFAe9czewt60AdYR/MAIC/UKAdf53K2RU6qFeQZGnxF\nUZi0znYOJ3453b0JZsozL1WEPDn/r17ab/YxteOXTNJ+nees80e8aL619V2/kpj3\nE3oCSaLXCgSJNvK2jdDr+e/Lxi16daKtn2K5TZbBbtnmDGJ0MENm1vRTS+iqN10c\nJmpo7qvXQlU/zvZ5lxKADDI8UK2IM4O+nH4baECyEz4z4zuUe3OXYyyJ1DTUf6Tk\nkoI7ZbVEo4g478VzFhR3m93xXdQx6SaysN3TIF3UHYEnaT/5C27cJWCa4rv1lPCL\nUYDbbP9eX+NSkQj5RngPDYz7C52L80iNS3pWZAR87AZXWlaJnWg5B/AijsDark+4\n0WOHBuy+WA4Wzov8izjrz5aFWOJ5h9ed9OEhtSnVH5vWO6ChOWeGh1qFusAxs/aN\nPEihWbloFtVm7esHDfNNNUt5GXbQJ6hs/A2Vwyg0g1Qw1qTH5OL00KbMgzed3kAz\nHfWUSGW7qIKAkBSc2iMp\n=mLqf\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-31T01:56:16Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/8691737c41a5f52969b1a5de983c5a2e1f47c201","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKDg2OTE3MzdjNDFhNWY1Mjk2OWIxYTVkZTk4M2M1YTJlMWY0N2MyMDE","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/8c20615d94560f9ac6564b895bc1f96688f6235e","sha":"8c20615d94560f9ac6564b895bc1f96688f6235e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/8c20615d94560f9ac6564b895bc1f96688f6235e"}],"sha":"8691737c41a5f52969b1a5de983c5a2e1f47c201","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/8691737c41a5f52969b1a5de983c5a2e1f47c201"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/8c20615d94560f9ac6564b895bc1f96688f6235e/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-31T01:45:04Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-31T01:45:04Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Manage Acme''s secrets, from the console to a real OpenBao (#32)\n\n* Reach one client''s secrets, inside that client''s namespace\n\nThe namespace travels as a header, so the boundary is the store''s to\nenforce rather than this adapter''s to remember. Measured against a real\nstore: the same path read without the header, and read in another\nclient''s namespace, both answer 404.\n\nKey-value version 2 is carried through rather than smoothed away. `cas`\nis always sent -- omitting it lets the store accept a write against a\nversion somebody has already moved past, which is the silent overwrite\nthe whole shape exists to prevent -- and a check-and-set mismatch becomes\na conflict rather than a generic failure.\n\nDelete goes through the metadata endpoint, which removes every version.\nThe data endpoint marks the newest deleted and leaves the older ones\nreadable, which is not what an operator pressing Delete means.\n\nMetadata carries no key names. The store does not return them, so\nincluding them would mean reading the secret to draw a list -- fetching\nevery value and discarding it, on the operation a console performs most\noften. Key names arrive with a deliberate reveal instead.\n\nListing a boundary with nothing in it answers 404 from the store, which\nis the ordinary state of a tab being opened for the first time and must\nnot reach an operator as an error.\n\nTwo things the real store taught the fixture. Policies are looked up in\nthe token''s namespace, so a bare `secret/*` grants the root namespace and\nno client''s -- every client operation answered permission denied. And a\nsingle-segment glob, `+/secret/*`, grants every client''s boundary and\nnothing deeper, so one policy covers all clients without being reconciled\nwhen one is added.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Resolve the boundary from the client, and record every act\n\nThe control-plane layer: an operator names a client and a path, the\nservice resolves that client''s declared boundary from desired state, and\nthe store is reached inside it. There is no field through which a\nnamespace, mount, address, token or policy could arrive, because there is\nno such field.\n\nEvery operation is recorded, including reading. Revealing a secret is an\nact rather than a side effect of looking at a page, and it is the act an\ninvestigation most wants to see. The record carries actor, client, path,\noperation, outcome and version -- and can carry no value, because nothing\nthat could contain one is passed to it.\n\nReveal answers `no-store`, set explicitly rather than left to a default:\nwithout it a proxy or a browser cache is free to keep the one response in\nthis API that carries a secret.\n\nFive failures, four statuses, and a machine code for each. A stale write\nand a client with no boundary are both 409 and are resolved very\ndifferently -- one reloads, the other provisions -- so a console that\ncould only see the status would have to guess.\n\nTwo things the router settled. A catch-all must be last, so every\npath-bearing route sits under `entry/`: otherwise a secret genuinely\nnamed `metadata/db` would be indistinguishable from an operation. And\nreveal takes its path in a body rather than a URL, which keeps it out of\nhistory, referrers and proxy logs -- the same reasoning as `no-store`.\n\nNo in-memory stand-in for a development store. One would let the console\ndemonstrate managing something that is not kept anywhere, which is a\nworse lie than the tab saying it is not configured.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Manage Acme''s secrets from the console\n\nThe tab: a table of paths, a value that appears only when somebody asks\nfor that one secret, a form that writes against the version they were\nlooking at, and a delete.\n\nRevealed values live in component state and nowhere else. They are never\nput in localStorage, sessionStorage or a URL, so hiding is just dropping\nthem and closing the tab is enough to be rid of them -- which is what\nmakes the response''s `no-store` mean something at the last step.\n\nThe listing shows paths and never values. Metadata carries no key names,\nso drawing this table reads no secrets at all.\n\nOne form creates and replaces, because the store has no separate create.\nWhat distinguishes them is the version the operator believes they are\nreplacing, and leaving it blank says \"I believe this does not exist\" --\nso an accidental overwrite is a refusal rather than a silent replacement.\n\nA stale write says somebody else changed it, not \"500\". That distinction\nwas carried from the store, through a machine code, to a sentence an\noperator can act on, and it is the reason versions were kept rather than\nflattened three commits ago.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Assert the invariants instead of asserting they hold\n\nThe four the slice was accepted on were not all covered: `no-store` and\nbrowser persistence existed only in comments. Writing the tests found a\nbug that neither the adapter tests nor the console noticed.\n\n`ClientPath` extracted its parameter positionally, so on a route with a\nsecond one it failed with \"the request path names no client\". Metadata,\nwrite and delete answered 400 to every call -- three of the five\noperations were entirely broken, and the adapter tests passed because\nthey bypass the routes. It reads the id by name now.\n\nWhat is asserted, at the wire: reveal carries the value and says\n`no-store`; the listing, metadata and a write response carry no value at\nall; a stale write is 409 with `secret_stale_version` rather than a 500;\na traversal is refused; and no secret route answers without an operator.\n\nAnd in the browser: a value appears only after somebody asks for that\nsecret, hiding forgets it, reveal goes by POST so the path never enters a\nURL, and nothing is left in local or session storage. Each shown to fail\n-- persisting the value, and removing the header, each kill exactly one.\n\nThe storage assertion reads key by key rather than spreading, because\n`Storage` is a class instance and spreading one loses its prototype --\nthe same trap that caught a test earlier in this repository.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Record who decides which secret boundary an operation reaches\n\nNot in ADR 0016: that one owns the OpenFGA runtime/control-plane\nboundary, and mixing OpenBao authority into it would make both harder to\nread. No existing ADR owned OpenBao tenancy -- 0008 and 0011 mention it\nonly in passing -- so this is its own.\n\nThe decision is the trust split. OpenBao enforces namespace isolation;\nFabric enforces which namespace a given operator operation may target.\nBoth halves are load-bearing and neither substitutes for the other:\nOpenBao guarantees a request carrying acme''s header cannot see contoso''s\nsecrets, and guarantees nothing about which header Fabric sends.\n\nSo the platform credential is deliberately broad -- `+/secret/*` reaches\nevery first-level client namespace and never needs reconciling when a\nclient is added -- and the client-to-namespace resolution and the\noperator authorisation are security boundaries rather than convenience\nlogic. A caller supplies a client and a path; a namespace, mount,\naddress, token or policy has no field to arrive in.\n\nAlso records why not per-client credentials: a real narrowing, at the\ncost of a credential lifecycle per client, for a component that already\nauthenticates every operator and reads every client''s desired state. Not\nruled out, just not now.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"a5fb4f342b1efb8c304d5b458e150fd2f9497aa3","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/a5fb4f342b1efb8c304d5b458e150fd2f9497aa3"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/8c20615d94560f9ac6564b895bc1f96688f6235e","verification":{"payload":"tree a5fb4f342b1efb8c304d5b458e150fd2f9497aa3\nparent 46f8a50e7bdefc782d1f1710f1639ecbde7c7195\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1788140704 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788140704 +1200\n\nManage Acme''s secrets, from the console to a real OpenBao (#32)\n\n* Reach one client''s secrets, inside that client''s namespace\n\nThe namespace travels as a header, so the boundary is the store''s to\nenforce rather than this adapter''s to remember. Measured against a real\nstore: the same path read without the header, and read in another\nclient''s namespace, both answer 404.\n\nKey-value version 2 is carried through rather than smoothed away. `cas`\nis always sent -- omitting it lets the store accept a write against a\nversion somebody has already moved past, which is the silent overwrite\nthe whole shape exists to prevent -- and a check-and-set mismatch becomes\na conflict rather than a generic failure.\n\nDelete goes through the metadata endpoint, which removes every version.\nThe data endpoint marks the newest deleted and leaves the older ones\nreadable, which is not what an operator pressing Delete means.\n\nMetadata carries no key names. The store does not return them, so\nincluding them would mean reading the secret to draw a list -- fetching\nevery value and discarding it, on the operation a console performs most\noften. Key names arrive with a deliberate reveal instead.\n\nListing a boundary with nothing in it answers 404 from the store, which\nis the ordinary state of a tab being opened for the first time and must\nnot reach an operator as an error.\n\nTwo things the real store taught the fixture. Policies are looked up in\nthe token''s namespace, so a bare `secret/*` grants the root namespace and\nno client''s -- every client operation answered permission denied. And a\nsingle-segment glob, `+/secret/*`, grants every client''s boundary and\nnothing deeper, so one policy covers all clients without being reconciled\nwhen one is added.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Resolve the boundary from the client, and record every act\n\nThe control-plane layer: an operator names a client and a path, the\nservice resolves that client''s declared boundary from desired state, and\nthe store is reached inside it. There is no field through which a\nnamespace, mount, address, token or policy could arrive, because there is\nno such field.\n\nEvery operation is recorded, including reading. Revealing a secret is an\nact rather than a side effect of looking at a page, and it is the act an\ninvestigation