mments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-30T23:31:04Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-30T23:31:04Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Say what a client''s secrets are, before touching any (#31)\n\nThe vocabulary and the port for \"manage Acme''s secrets\", with the\ndecisions that are expensive to change once an API exists.\n\n**The namespace is the boundary.** No second partition abstraction on top\nof the store. It is declared in the client''s desired state rather than\nderived from the client id, for the reason the realm is: a boundary that\nis only ever implied cannot be read out of the document, cannot differ\nfor the one client that needs it to, and cannot change without a\nmigration nobody can see. A client without one is refused rather than\nguessed at, because a guessed boundary is another client''s boundary\nsooner or later.\n\n**A caller supplies a client and a path. Nothing else.** No namespace,\nmount, address, token or policy -- those are trusted platform state, and\na request that could name its own boundary is a request that can read\nanother client''s secrets. `SecretNamespace` has no route from a request\nbody; its only serde path is desired state.\n\n**The path is the one thing a caller does supply**, so it is the one that\nis checked. Absolute paths, empty segments, `.` and `..` are refused, and\nso is `%`: an encoded separator is the same escape by a slower route,\ndecoded downstream where the check no longer applies. The boundary is\nenforced by prefixing a namespace, and a path that can climb out of its\nprefix makes that enforcement decorative.\n\n**`SecretValues` writes `Debug` by hand.** The derive would print every\nvalue the first time the type reached a log line, a panic, or an error\nrendered with `{:?}`. It names the keys and redacts the values, which is\nwhat somebody debugging actually needs. Tested.\n\n**The store''s versions are kept rather than flattened.** An operator\nopens version 7, somebody writes version 8, the first saves. Flattened,\nthat is a silent overwrite; kept, it is a refusal they can see and act\non, which is the only reason a console shows a version at all.\n\nReading a secret is a separate operation from listing them, so revealing\nis an act in the audit trail rather than a side effect of looking at a\npage.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"afe6b014792c69e1518818b3aab0d8d705586ea9","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/afe6b014792c69e1518818b3aab0d8d705586ea9"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/46f8a50e7bdefc782d1f1710f1639ecbde7c7195","verification":{"payload":"tree afe6b014792c69e1518818b3aab0d8d705586ea9\nparent aefd6d0c4f2f221e67c6681d978c595e3bcdc27a\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1788132664 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788132664 +1200\n\nSay what a client''s secrets are, before touching any (#31)\n\nThe vocabulary and the port for \"manage Acme''s secrets\", with the\ndecisions that are expensive to change once an API exists.\n\n**The namespace is the boundary.** No second partition abstraction on top\nof the store. It is declared in the client''s desired state rather than\nderived from the client id, for the reason the realm is: a boundary that\nis only ever implied cannot be read out of the document, cannot differ\nfor the one client that needs it to, and cannot change without a\nmigration nobody can see. A client without one is refused rather than\nguessed at, because a guessed boundary is another client''s boundary\nsooner or later.\n\n**A caller supplies a client and a path. Nothing else.** No namespace,\nmount, address, token or policy -- those are trusted platform state, and\na request that could name its own boundary is a request that can read\nanother client''s secrets. `SecretNamespace` has no route from a request\nbody; its only serde path is desired state.\n\n**The path is the one thing a caller does supply**, so it is the one that\nis checked. Absolute paths, empty segments, `.` and `..` are refused, and\nso is `%`: an encoded separator is the same escape by a slower route,\ndecoded downstream where the check no longer applies. The boundary is\nenforced by prefixing a namespace, and a path that can climb out of its\nprefix makes that enforcement decorative.\n\n**`SecretValues` writes `Debug` by hand.** The derive would print every\nvalue the first time the type reached a log line, a panic, or an error\nrendered with `{:?}`. It names the keys and redacts the values, which is\nwhat somebody debugging actually needs. Tested.\n\n**The store''s versions are kept rather than flattened.** An operator\nopens version 7, somebody writes version 8, the first saves. Flattened,\nthat is a silent overwrite; kept, it is a refusal they can see and act\non, which is the only reason a console shows a version at all.\n\nReading a secret is a separate operation from listing them, so revealing\nis an act in the audit trail rather than a side effect of looking at a\npage.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqlL04CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAfN0QADcsCwNmQ38hXdF3Z29MemV1\nQWxe32W1DAeUTebJKJjV89Vwfm/rTD5wQMpoFXb1Du9sAP3MvUkZWJ4M8r/DryW9\nyBF3GS0oCf4MdK4P54FQGx7rYperDIBXmkrb8sBWsJvo9SfoSkRU9ScUQIToee/+\nxfADO1N+605FMbry4kb9baR9CRXps1ez/UO+0xAthoZpdgbQ+kTgRWFReVTIwYKw\nhl8p/GlHF9HCobw6doIXjwwrN1H2n9f//hioVosjA8u2IjYosrCzwtA/ipC46P6/\nLGloIeMtb+K0urTQrtcD3y+76yODWSzDVRr15u+G4cAbaS867iAbHQjv/DCr7uZc\nMjb7r8HZAXA9f5YFSM0f2PyEwQkTLqv5gs/4NOUt8OCOVLL7APEkRq0l7El/Z8JL\nwP7gLdy20MdJfIStUkeidFDVgVfpji9zp4dr0tWyTcNbv6fJHrpFbzTbT0h952Zu\n0TtArgFfiznkOW92sGZJ5P66e112e3o0yBTbBf79F2zezZNtR7/3+P1vPdbJmHSo\nMVsess8ygD9NzeGZaRBkcZEfxmhjVNCUDjPaJHHM+3FH7QGuTd+d7VbWNYJgUk1w\nX/9qwixByNXDWVchR7re/LvQSoS54mAf7V7pv6xeVnsu3nQZ1oY16mln/PQ7lWDN\n3pmnAFt7D//ZMkKhaMm5\n=fDJk\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-30T23:31:04Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/46f8a50e7bdefc782d1f1710f1639ecbde7c7195","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKDQ2ZjhhNTBlN2JkZWZjNzgyZDFmMTcxMGYxNjM5ZWNiZGU3YzcxOTU","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/aefd6d0c4f2f221e67c6681d978c595e3bcdc27a","sha":"aefd6d0c4f2f221e67c6681d978c595e3bcdc27a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/aefd6d0c4f2f221e67c6681d978c595e3bcdc27a"}],"sha":"46f8a50e7bdefc782d1f1710f1639ecbde7c7195","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/46f8a50e7bdefc782d1f1710f1639ecbde7c7195"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/aefd6d0c4f2f221e67c6681d978c595e3bcdc27a/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-30T22:58:31Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-30T22:58:31Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Sketch the console''s surface, and make the gaps visible (#30)\n\nA screen contract rather than a design: what each screen shows, where\neach value comes from, and which values do not exist yet. The holes are\nthe point — they are the product requirements the UI is meant to reveal,\nin the order the UI needs them.\n\nThe console was less empty than I had assumed. A client list, a client\ndetail pane and an identity panel already work, so v0 is closer than\n\"start the console\" implied and the sketch''s real value is mapping what\nrenders today against what needs an API.\n\nClient detail gains the tab strip that states the product surface:\nOverview, Secrets, Authorization, Identity, Modules, Config, Health. Two\nof them have contents. The rest name what they will show and what it\nwould take, because a tab that quietly disappears until its API arrives\nleaves an operator unable to tell \"SaaS Fabric does not do this\" from\n\"SaaS Fabric does this and I cannot see it\", and those need very\ndifferent conversations.\n\nOverview renders its holes rather than omitting them, for the same\nreason: a screen that silently drops what it cannot show lies about how\nmuch of the platform is wired up. Issuer, secret partition, authorization\nstore and model, database endpoint, modules and health each say so in\nplace, with the reason.\n\nDeliberately no \"Add client\". Creating one is a workflow rather than a\nform (ADR 0008), and putting a disabled button there would design it by\naccident. Absent, so that its absence is the requirement.\n\nMuted rather than alarming, throughout. An unbuilt capability is not an\nerror, and colouring it like one trains operators to ignore real\nfailures.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"1df329d1cd7afad02833b394891699b44aad6faf","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/1df329d1cd7afad02833b394891699b44aad6faf"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/aefd6d0c4f2f221e67c6681d978c595e3bcdc27a","verification":{"payload":"tree 1df329d1cd7afad02833b394891699b44aad6faf\nparent fde892d95fa6b7bb266620c5a788306745fbadda\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1788130711 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788130711 +1200\n\nSketch the console''s surface, and make the gaps visible (#30)\n\nA screen contract rather than a design: what each screen shows, where\neach value comes from, and which values do not exist yet. The holes are\nthe point — they are the product requirements the UI is meant to reveal,\nin the order the UI needs them.\n\nThe console was less empty than I had assumed. A client list, a client\ndetail pane and an identity panel already work, so v0 is closer than\n\"start the console\" implied and the sketch''s real value is mapping what\nrenders today against what needs an API.\n\nClient detail gains the tab strip that states the product surface:\nOverview, Secrets, Authorization, Identity, Modules, Config, Health. Two\nof them have contents. The rest name what they will show and what it\nwould take, because a tab that quietly disappears until its API arrives\nleaves an operator unable to tell \"SaaS Fabric does not do this\" from\n\"SaaS Fabric does this and I cannot see it\", and those need very\ndifferent conversations.\n\nOverview renders its holes rather than omitting them, for the same\nreason: a screen that silently drops what it cannot show lies about how\nmuch of the platform is wired up. Issuer, secret partition, authorization\nstore and model, database endpoint, modules and health each say so in\nplace, with the reason.\n\nDeliberately no \"Add client\". Creating one is a workflow rather than a\nform (ADR 0008), and putting a disabled button there would design it by\naccident. Absent, so that its absence is the requirement.\n\nMuted rather than alarming, throughout. An unbuilt capability is not an\nerror, and colouring it like one trains operators to ignore real\nfailures.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqlLWXCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAKNQQACni54W/s10x4RR7Ee1rWvSF\nh0lckOHRnrJYQ75RLZJiJDKF68TpnxiPkWZx3tfiydzYvXzowysWVZ6XJHMKr8f/\nmEWO7+wY019fG6orFyZ4iLcKVJKNQP/4YDpxrug1JIWluRD+ldz8HUafD1AgThg6\nhkTCLKgJ2RLVmheqzpzz8YvFCcSq/0uZfmRe32PBNzOMXDoHf6yT+HgCgjNkAnyh\njco5z8X15DQN3YaaE72Vb/AFIhTKJrQnXZwfOKK/IPwLGYOwktLnplNaUUoqCerI\nzCH4XnOmsyFJnDXgh6UNY0J4mT15z+Q0ufk0415RBWTvicJmdCu1oDt3uHp7lJdq\nDDXIe1zX+w6vmsjM4Ge95LNf5SnykB+x3+xW4TZ/R9nhrLOgWVJskgzalgy7C0xc\n2iX61XZJ7eqE8IBcv2Qa4UMgxrVg/o9QSNlGknrPMtFxGtLuuNhq1ApFOvozyxuV\nXKbSrnrbkYdteFK66y/m9Az4nykO+QLrBZmg6FCmJC6jpCUuc3u6Czi9OInDMl+l\n6T3wH/22pBcnGvH0wnzWpPJZ9vizKZT70ydzpAxn5iwPANOWy3oTIXKV3g7HtBOu\nimppQandXqWLxLy4n44ZJfigPTExItICxdQz32+vIifzcTEpE+yfhhnb5KeZGuqu\n4qEIt8qc0mOFer43Xn4N\n=kCJO\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-30T22:58:31Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/aefd6d0c4f2f221e67c6681d978c595e3bcdc27a","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKGFlZmQ2ZDBjNGYyZjIyMWU2N2M2NjgxZDk3OGM1OTVlM2JjZGMyN2E","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/fde892d95fa6b7bb266620c5a788306745fbadda","sha":"fde892d95fa6b7bb266620c5a788306745fbadda","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/fde892d95fa6b7bb266620c5a788306745fbadda"}],"sha":"aefd6d0c4f2f221e67c6681d978c595e3bcdc27a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/aefd6d0c4f2f221e67c6681d978c595e3bcdc27a"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/fde892d95fa6b7bb266620c5a788306745fbadda/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-30T22:46:49Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-30T22:46:49Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Ship the front and the service it fronts as one unit (#29)\n\n`fabric-openfga`: the authorization front on the only published port, and\nan OpenFGA bound to 127.0.0.1 that nothing outside the container can\naddress. The invariant ADR 0016 could only assert is now testable, and\ntested -- with **both** ports published, the front answers and the\nservice refuses, so the refusal proves the binding rather than docker''s\nport mapping.\n\nThe front is PID 1 and owns the child. A shell that started both and\nwaited would leave the common failure looking healthy: the service exits,\nthe front keeps serving, every decision answers 503, and liveness passes\nbecause the process it asks about is still running.\n\nThree things this turned up.\n\n**A configuration struct leaked a credential.** `#[derive(Debug)]` on the\ndatastore printed the connection URI, password and all. That is how a\nconnection string reaches a log -- not by anybody deciding to print it.\n`Debug` is written by hand and redacts.\n\n**There was no datastore configuration at all**, so the service ran in\nmemory and lost every store, model and tuple on restart. Now stated\nrather than assumed, with a loud warning when absent, because the failure\nit causes is silent until the first restart.\n\n**A client''s first request cost fifteen seconds of blocking work inside a\npoll.** It stalled the task watching the child, so a service that died in\nthe first millisecond was noticed only when the stall ended. Measured:\nfirst request 15s, second 298µs, raw TCP connect 165µs -- the platform''s\ntrust store, loaded lazily. Fast on the deployment target, which is\nexactly why it would never have shown up there.\n\nTwo probes I ran proved nothing and were replaced: killing the child from\nthe host signalled into the wrong namespace, and `port = 1` bound\nsuccessfully rather than failing. The tests that stand assert the\nproperty rather than an arithmetic that a platform can change -- a start\nwindow of 300s and an exit noticed in a fraction of it, rather than a\nstopwatch measuring a trust store.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"54a6ab9582283ebe61e4de9567933941c80b9661","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/54a6ab9582283ebe61e4de9567933941c80b9661"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/fde892d95fa6b7bb266620c5a788306745fbadda","verification":{"payload":"tree 54a6ab9582283ebe61e4de9567933941c80b9661\nparent f80522f14549c38eaac089c7a152e3d5567fd1b8\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1788130009 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788130009 +1200\n\nShip the front and the service it fronts as one unit (#29)\n\n`fabric-openfga`: the authorization front on the only published port, and\nan OpenFGA bound to 127.0.0.1 that nothing outside the container can\naddress. The invariant ADR 0016 could only assert is now testable, and\ntested -- with **both** ports published, the front answers and the\nservice refuses, so the refusal proves the binding rather than docker''s\nport mapping.\n\nThe front is PID 1 and owns the child. A shell that started both and\nwaited would leave the common failure looking healthy: the service exits,\nthe front keeps serving, every decision answers 503, and liveness passes\nbecause the process it asks about is still running.\n\nThree things this turned up.\n\n**A configuration struct leaked a credential.** `#[derive(Debug)]` on the\ndatastore printed the connection URI, password and all. That is how a\nconnection string reaches a log -- not by anybody deciding to print it.\n`Debug` is written by hand and redacts.\n\n**There was no datastore configuration at all**, so the service ran in\nmemory and lost every store, model and tuple on restart. Now stated\nrather than assumed, with a loud warning when absent, because the failure\nit causes is silent until the first restart.\n\n**A client''s first request cost fifteen seconds of blocking work inside a\npoll.** It stalled the task watching the child, so a service that died in\nthe first millisecond was noticed only when the stall ended. Measured:\nfirst request 15s, second 298µs, raw TCP connect 165µs -- the platform''s\ntrust store, loaded lazily. Fast on the deployment target, which is\nexactly why it would never have shown up there.\n\nTwo probes I ran proved nothing and were replaced: killing the child from\nthe host signalled into the wrong namespace, and `port = 1` bound\nsuccessfully rather than failing. The tests that stand assert the\nproperty rather than an arithmetic that a platform can change -- a start\nwindow of 300s and an exit noticed in a fraction of it, rather than a\nstopwatch measuring a trust store.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqlLLZCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAGHAQAHpZ/RDf1ylHq3qtrInRyyMP\n4Q1sPoIYJ2ltqED/rKUI+wb5I9TRlBB0YYpjoCMF90+Dsat4/soZQ9cvJaxnZPzR\nVscc/1LozIZioC5qolol+bXHqvTXMRvnGWaM6vagHaMzQZ00+vx3dMmK+lVdNOyc\nWJllonavat9pOmaDhopNOteffL0tQabMFvfyexzN3iGlCAoCWgkUSbVOhQVTT8bP\nwXZbD70xrwfNnBSkqZ8Q1DivcZxCvGDuf0+FMnor3Js/jr2L9xjbs0N7kaYeiE2t\n2T8v7ijeovDtJ2C7PhJJEXKrTCaHobMVJ3QzTaALc8RUabWS9SALt/c8NvvQU5ZD\n4AnBRTTN1FsbH2TSPxKUG51YxqAH9V3TYa6AA+O/sewAzn154QJzuCWe2bZNhje4\n3Mbz+AxJHgS37FSKClaaxifGyltXGPvW3SFC7YebXF+ZHARwT8hmTagOWzzuvBih\nqqiymMQBk5nkk6tD4dEiXCzAeQyyhMWQ5c+uK0wWORKADucVs5I7Z1zRAgrtNUCm\nqV78WnN/XrEWH9qSgKFsAGhVnbcQKgneEQvUxzq14EgxMZcmIq1TiYHQFbjxkV3q\nTn3ARa36zigslj8F7IHFlh0BLGpPVAEOK+gs1ieJxbtGG1wpVj/bhrObqNPgDvVH\n8XP0n+733z3LPfRjE2P8\n=QhQ/\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-30T22:46:50Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/fde892d95fa6b7bb266620c5a788306745fbadda","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKGZkZTg5MmQ5NWZhNmI3YmIyNjY2MjBjNWE3ODgzMDY3NDVmYmFkZGE","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/f80522f14549c38eaac089c7a152e3d5567fd1b8","sha":"f80522f14549c38eaac089c7a152e3d5567fd1b8","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/f80522f14549c38eaac089c7a152e3d5567fd1b8"}],"sha":"fde892d95fa6b7bb266620c5a788306745fbadda","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/fde892d95fa6b7bb266620c5a788306745fbadda"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/f80522f14549c38eaac089c7a152e3d5567fd1b8/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-30T21:36:43Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-30T21:36:43Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Use the escape hatch the size policy already had (#28)\n\nThe check is not a hard gate: it refuses a file over the limit **unless\nsomebody records why**, and its own comment says the reason must be\ncohesion rather than inconvenience. Nobody had used it, so its first use\nlooked impossible and I split a module to satisfy arithmetic instead.\n\n`cache.rs` is one security-critical decision -- which key may verify a\ntoken, and when trust must be refreshed -- whose branches only make sense\nread together. Splitting it produced a per-issuer lock map in a file of\nits own, away from the rule it exists to protect. That is worse at 145\nlines than the whole thing is at 155, so the lock map is back and the\nfile is the first recorded exemption.\n\nThe genuinely separate part stayed separate: the two windows are policy\nan operator needs to reason about during an outage, and they remain in\n`windows.rs` as public constants.\n\nThe success line no longer overclaims. \"No production file exceeds 150\nlines\" stops being true the moment anything is exempted, and a success\nline nobody trusts is a success line nobody reads -- it now says how many\ncarry a recorded reason.\n\nVerified the exemption is load-bearing rather than decorative: removing\nit fails the check on this file.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"55218a68d670e0b9ab8216e11d115853f942e210","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/55218a68d670e0b9ab8216e11d115853f942e210"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/f80522f14549c38eaac089c7a152e3d5567fd1b8","verification":{"payload":"tree 55218a68d670e0b9ab8216e11d115853f942e210\nparent 1e64ced3a7d99a8c0b66702c2395ba127ada43e2\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1788125803 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788125803 +1200\n\nUse the escape hatch the size policy already had (#28)\n\nThe check is not a hard gate: it refuses a file over the limit **unless\nsomebody records why**, and its own comment says the reason must be\ncohesion rather than inconvenience. Nobody had used it, so its first use\nlooked impossible and I split a module to satisfy arithmetic instead.\n\n`cache.rs` is one security-critical decision -- which key may verify a\ntoken, and when trust must be refreshed -- whose branches only make sense\nread together. Splitting it produced a per-issuer lock map in a file of\nits own, away from the rule it exists to protect. That is worse at 145\nlines than the whole thing is at 155, so the lock map is back and the\nfile is the first recorded exemption.\n\nThe genuinely separate part stayed separate: the two windows are policy\nan operator needs to reason about during an outage, and they remain in\n`windows.rs` as public constants.\n\nThe success line no longer overclaims. \"No production file exceeds 150\nlines\" stops being true the moment anything is exempted, and a success\nline nobody trusts is a success line nobody reads -- it now says how many\ncarry a recorded reason.\n\nVerified the exemption is load-bearing rather than decorative: removing\nit fails the check on this file.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqlKJrCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAADygQACkNmEaAaqj94Ijqdp9qtxdE\nowDtkoT52zgrSmDxoOCdfZVKJmu/CIXPgcJhvm8Akh5q5RGpUW6T3/bPUqB6obfD\nKgmHwTastZV6rdall6NiSBiNzH5YdOsHWuCiTuG2QrgxZNCHlCyjm+8MF4MQtq6a\nJKe0Ug9s0gJhrqcQkX344hMqtPnc+cVSzjDvU+Wcc6gNF1ppiTaKl2+bmqXzcXlk\n4CC17ilyLqp3/FHmoqkMD4ViXeAMfb8p2JKmibRljqu0b4b7Hq3SObWwdZN91Gkr\nCDR/fnmaez5suVoAkybCfsBsoJawgIVQPflQf2mBuwx7fkEpqC8uDek0ru7oolYs\nGQ1aeV9OkMTyv0T02OsNrLL60IwPPm50GHo5onPJbr5kDJzV3K9Pdln4HP4V1aOF\nRfaOuNBSD5e6xrwwfmQcIXyhfYtRa8gfUSIlejvnfZt8kVVg0QyDCXnmjRjf0QpP\ngelLO/0ujOTTDvUDBw2JyYgAnGw7qh2zYDeQRlMbv6b1Ga4LqKyZLivnv1+hgPK+\nAcZmDb6I4i9q7phYaa8QpRm4co4b8XEB7aGJQYycrh9u/iwYoHJOpND6bmMQH+ro\nfbZhmjKQhyYwxm7E/MpjWLwtcALU67f6bgR0h0/wgegIaaHLY33VVfApgITqBqSz\nMh7487/ywzPRHQukRgw8\n=GqkF\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-30T21:36:44Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/f80522f14549c38eaac089c7a152e3d5567fd1b8","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKGY4MDUyMmYxNDU0OWMzOGVhYWMwODljN2ExNTJlM2Q1NTY3ZmQxYjg","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/1e64ced3a7d99a8c0b66702c2395ba127ada43e2","sha":"1e64ced3a7d99a8c0b66702c2395ba127ada43e2","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/1e64ced3a7d99a8c0b66702c2395ba127ada43e2"}],"sha":"f80522f14549c38eaac089c7a152e3d5567fd1b8","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/f80522f14549c38eaac089c7a152e3d5567fd1b8"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/1e64ced3a7d99a8c0b66702c2395ba127ada43e2/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-30T21:04:46Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-30T21:04:46Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Prove the assembled path, and find what only assembly could find (#27)\n\n* Prove the assembled path, and find what only assembly could find\n\nReal Keycloak, real OpenFGA, the real HTTP surface, two independently\ntrusted realms through one front.\n\nActivation is explicit and enabling it removes every escape: nothing set\nprints SKIPPED; `FABRIC_E2E=1` turns missing configuration, unreachable\nservices and setup failure into failures rather than skips. Verified all\nthree states -- unset is 6 passed with the notice, enabled-and-unconfigured\nis 0 passed and 6 failed, enabled-with-a-service-down likewise. Once\nsomebody has asked for the proof it must be impossible to get green by\nnot running it.\n\nBuilding it found three things no component test could.\n\n**There was no production `KeySource` at all.** Every implementation was\na test double, so the crate could not fetch JWKS from a real issuer. It\nwould have been discovered at deployment. `HttpKeySource` is the missing\npiece.\n\n**Everything discovery advertises is the address the provider was told to\nadvertise, not one you can reach.** The fixture pins Keycloak''s hostname\nto an issuer nothing resolves, and both the token endpoint and the\n`jwks_uri` come back pointing at it. Rewriting the base is what a\ndeployment does when it configures an internal address for a logical\nendpoint -- and doing it in the test is what proves the split works,\nwhich is the property Topaz''s filter made impossible.\n\n**The suite raced itself.** Tests sharing realm names against one\nKeycloak produced a token failure in one test reported as an assertion\nfailure in another. Realms are per test now.\n\nThe adapter-containment invariant then refused the fixture for naming\n`publicClient` and `openid-connect` -- correctly, since it scans tests\ndeliberately, because a test is where somebody first reaches for a\nrepresentation. Realm creation moved to `scripts/e2e-services.sh` and the\nsuite now finds its endpoints through discovery, so it names no\nprovider''s vocabulary and would work against any OIDC issuer.\n\nOne behaviour is worth review rather than acceptance: an unfamiliar `kid`\nanswers 401 inside the minimum refresh interval and 503 outside it. Both\nare truthful -- inside, the key set was confirmed seconds ago and does not\npublish it -- but the same token gets two answers ten seconds apart. The\ntest asserts both rather than hiding it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Let evidence decide the answer, not the rate limiter\n\nOne interval was doing two jobs, and the seam showed twice.\n\nIt let the amplification timer determine an authentication result, and\nthe ruling on that is right: an unknown `kid` is refused **only** when a\nsufficiently fresh, successfully fetched snapshot positively establishes\nthe key is absent. Otherwise the answer is that we cannot tell.\n\nIt also left a hole I had not seen. The single timer was reset by\n*success*, so during an outage nothing bounded retries at all -- a few\nthousand invented ids became a few thousand failing calls aimed at a\nprovider already unwell. The cooldown now advances on every attempt,\nwhich is exactly when the bound matters most.\n\nSo there are two windows. The cooldown is how often the issuer may be\ncalled, whatever the outcome. The freshness window is how long a\nsuccessful snapshot proves a key does not exist. A failed attempt updates\nthe first and never the second: a failure cannot create negative evidence\nabout a key, so it can never age into grounds for a refusal.\n\nThe same token may move from `401` to `503` as evidence ages. That is not\ninconsistency -- at the first moment we knew enough to reject it, at the\nsecond we did not, and the response follows the verifier''s evidence state\nrather than the token.\n\nThe tests say that rather than restating the arithmetic. They move the\nclock by the constants, so changing either window cannot quietly turn a\nsecurity property into a passing assertion about numbers, and both\nwindows are public because they are part of the contract rather than\nimplementation trivia.\n\nFour mutations kill tests: letting stale evidence refuse, letting a\nfailure overwrite the snapshot, removing the cooldown, and answering a\nsuppressed refresh as a refusal.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Give the two windows their own module, and the lock map its own file\n\n`cache.rs` went 5 lines over the limit when the public re-export landed\n-- caught by CI because I ran the size check before that edit and not\nafter, which is the same not-re-verifying mistake as the untracked\nrestore earlier.\n\nThe fix is placement rather than compression. The two windows are\ncrate-level policy, not cache internals: an operator reasoning about a\nprovider outage needs both numbers, so they live in `windows.rs` and are\npublic. The per-issuer lock map is bookkeeping and now sits beside the\ndecision it protects rather than inside it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"92d67888a8f1dc8d839995fbda50a93fd790fdcb","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/92d67888a8f1dc8d839995fbda50a93fd790fdcb"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/1e64ced3a7d99a8c0b66702c2395ba127ada43e2","verification":{"payload":"tree 92d67888a8f1dc8d839995fbda50a93fd790fdcb\nparent aa75265802c0d3ed21a45bc358535616a45b6852\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1788123886 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788123886 +1200\n\nProve the assembled path, and find what only assembly could find (#27)\n\n* Prove the assembled path, and find what only assembly could find\n\nReal Keycloak, real OpenFGA, the real HTTP surface, two independently\ntrusted realms through one front.\n\nActivation is explicit and enabling it removes every escape: nothing set\nprints SKIPPED; `FABRIC_E2E=1` turns missing configuration, unreachable\nservices and setup failure into failures rather than skips. Verified all\nthree states -- unset is 6 passed with the notice, enabled-and-unconfigured\nis 0 passed and 6 failed, enabled-with-a-service-down likewise. Once\nsomebody has asked for the proof it must be impossible to get green by\nnot running it.\n\nBuilding it found three things no component test could.\n\n**There was no production `KeySource` at all.** Every implementation was\na test double, so the crate could not fetch JWKS from a real issuer. It\nwould have been discovered at deployment. `HttpKeySource` is the missing\npiece.\n\n**Everything discovery advertises is the address the provider was told to\nadvertise, not one you can reach.** The fixture pins Keycloak''s hostname\nto an issuer nothing resolves, and both the token endpoint and the\n`jwks_uri` come back pointing at it. Rewriting the base is what a\ndeployment does when it configures an internal address for a logical\nendpoint -- and doing it in the test is what proves the split works,\nwhich is the property Topaz''s filter made impossible.\n\n**The suite raced itself.** Tests sharing realm names against one\nKeycloak produced a token failure in one test reported as an assertion\nfailure in another. Realms are per test now.\n\nThe adapter-containment invariant then refused the fixture for naming\n`publicClient` and `openid-connect` -- correctly, since it scans tests\ndeliberately, because a test is where somebody first reaches for a\nrepresentation. Realm creation moved to `scripts/e2e-services.sh` and the\nsuite now finds its endpoints through discovery, so it names no\nprovider''s vocabulary and would work against any OIDC issuer.\n\nOne behaviour is worth review rather than acceptance: an unfamiliar `kid`\nanswers 401 inside the minimum refresh interval and 503 outside it. Both\nare truthful -- inside, the key set was confirmed seconds ago and does not\npublish it -- but the same token gets two answers ten seconds apart. The\ntest asserts both rather than hiding it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Let evidence decide the answer, not the rate limiter\n\nOne interval was doing two jobs, and the seam showed twice.\n\nIt let the amplification timer determine an authentication result, and\nthe ruling on that is right: an unknown `kid` is refused **only** when a\nsufficiently fresh, successfully fetched snapshot positively establishes\nthe key is absent. Otherwise the answer is that we cannot tell.\n\nIt also left a hole I had not seen. The single timer was reset by\n*success*, so during an outage nothing bounded retries at all -- a few\nthousand invented ids became a few thousand failing calls aimed at a\nprovider already unwell. The cooldown now advances on every attempt,\nwhich is exactly when the bound matters most.\n\nSo there are two windows. The cooldown is how often the issuer may be\ncalled, whatever the outcome. The freshness window is how long a\nsuccessful snapshot proves a key does not exist. A failed attempt updates\nthe first and never the second: a failure cannot create negative evidence\nabout a key, so it can never age into grounds for a refusal.\n\nThe same token may move from `401` to `503` as evidence ages. That is not\ninconsistency -- at the first moment we knew enough to reject it, at the\nsecond we did not, and the response follows the verifier''s evidence state\nrather than the token.\n\nThe tests say that rather than restating the arithmetic. They move the\nclock by the constants, so changing either window cannot quietly turn a\nsecurity property into a passing assertion about numbers, and both\nwindows are public because they are part of the contract rather than\nimplementation trivia.\n\nFour mutations kill tests: letting stale evidence refuse, letting a\nfailure overwrite the snapshot, removing the cooldown, and answering a\nsuppressed refresh as a refusal.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Give the two windows their own module, and the lock map its own file\n\n`cache.rs` went 5 lines over the limit when the public re-export landed\n-- caught by CI because I ran the size check before that edit and not\nafter, which is the same not-re-verifying mistake as the untracked\nrestore earlier.\n\nThe fix is placement rather than compression. The two windows are\ncrate-level policy, not cache internals: an operator reasoning about a\nprovider outage needs both numbers, so they live in `windows.rs` and are\npublic. The per-issuer lock map is bookkeeping and now sits beside the\ndecision it protects rather than inside it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqlJruCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAvwIQAInndciBAPS3Ed+jDffbiE7o\nM4yYNVc31UqYr6M5VRsZMoliLZKOpDs0LIwTZpqwVwgwo9QNEgd8k74FhhSwLfHb\nbkE54Xy1o98Bgf1bWW1pD2fUTIClVs0tR7guLqo4xpNwy82UBhDyF6mPJF2VoUYE\nsyEfz15rbZzZI2HSCkOXLaHrWgMse0nBpEIfGxA5iCVeucIgwcGwPbdX0gNYA3yG\nYyq2CnLVUu+vq53zEy4Cx51zMOL67ISY0KEVsF7szRZmzqbDwsWuPNqnvXjF87Sl\nMgywid8VXRKvxzpLIcm6xPf30njNGAfytCPD7ukVv0WwTR1uNzcb5pMVguOCj7Ib\n+WHVuy4Z7HqRATVAuB7lZOcPNBgM/Bngxkfeq4ziDMVKsj/v2Ru5LXYoXBCWd4ga\nk+RJG6nKTzeYmYU6Bdtmb7evwerBccmApPKn9kbiSdcDi8cJciYxoG/WSYGvZx+Q\nbbwy/WR0I3cjaKvARjRZ+p0nBPS/R0Hn5rVSaABCMSxo57O9+lYrjpHuumneHpQ1\nJ2Hln3w7b535n97eP8qFLwjNQ40TdLtkBXhHmnr2Ji+jAOQBYDplYUww3LsNOkWZ\nvkCnLcSZyJxWHB9TftdZx8iT1295aJG/5OG/Qpy++WS/FvM+XZoybkhpjS5uyW29\nCEI3ti0zhlf2jR81tNvh\n=aM/w\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-30T21:04:47Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/1e64ced3a7d99a8c0b66702c2395ba127ada43e2","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKDFlNjRjZWQzYTdkOTlhOGMwYjY2NzAyYzIzOTViYTEyN2FkYTQzZTI","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/aa75265802c0d3ed21a45bc358535616a45b6852","sha":"aa75265802c0d3ed21a45bc358535616a45b6852","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/aa75265802c0d3ed21a45bc358535616a45b6852"}],"sha":"1e64ced3a7d99a8c0b66702c2395ba127ada43e2","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/1e64ced3a7d99a8c0b66702c2395ba127ada43e2"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/aa75265802c0d3ed21a45bc358535616a45b6852/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-30T10:18:04Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-30T10:18:04Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Put an HTTP surface on the runtime path, and keep the statuses apart (#26)\n\nEvery layer below this one keeps a credential problem, an outage, a\nplatform fault and a decision apart. A transport that mapped two of them\nto the same status would throw that away at the last step, and nothing\nbelow would notice -- so the mapping is its own module with the reasoning\nattached.\n\nThe one worth stating: **a denial is `200 {\"allowed\": false}`, never\n`403`.** `/v1/check` asks a question and a refusal is a successful answer\nto it. `403` would say the caller may not ask at all, which is a\ndifferent claim and sends an operator looking in the wrong place.\n\nThe surface is one decision route and two health routes. No catch-all, no\nproxy path, no query-string alternative for identity, tenant, store,\nrelation or object; only a bearer, read strictly. The response carries\n`allowed` and nothing else -- no identity echoed, no store, no model, no\nexplanation of why a check failed.\n\nLiveness answers for this process alone. Making it depend on Keycloak or\nthe authorization service would turn a neighbour''s outage into a restart\nloop, destroying the cached keys that were keeping this process useful.\nReadiness includes the authorization service, because a front that cannot\ndecide should leave rotation -- and deliberately does not require a fresh\nkey fetch, since surviving an identity-provider outage on cached keys is\na designed behaviour rather than a degraded one.\n\nTwo fixture faults found while probing, both the same shape as the\nmixed-case one: a test that could not fail.\n\nThe oversized-body test asserted only \"not 200\". Its body also named an\nunknown field, so removing the limit entirely still produced a refusal\nand the test still passed. It now asserts `413`, which meant teaching the\nhandler to answer `413` for a size rejection rather than folding it in\nwith parse failures -- a limit whose absence no test can detect is not a\nlimit anybody is maintaining.\n\nAnd a probe restoring `health.rs` with `git checkout` silently did\nnothing, because the file was untracked; the \"restored\" run was still\nmutated. Caught by the count not returning to green.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"89993afca41fc157d96054d3f9ea7d57ba23346b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/89993afca41fc157d96054d3f9ea7d57ba23346b"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/aa75265802c0d3ed21a45bc358535616a45b6852","verification":{"payload":"tree 89993afca41fc157d96054d3f9ea7d57ba23346b\nparent ed6bd23c7f05a68d3115763a5ef22d0fd968d6b1\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1788085084 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788085084 +1200\n\nPut an HTTP surface on the runtime path, and keep the statuses apart (#26)\n\nEvery layer below this one keeps a credential problem, an outage, a\nplatform fault and a decision apart. A transport that mapped two of them\nto the same status would throw that away at the last step, and nothing\nbelow would notice -- so the mapping is its own module with the reasoning\nattached.\n\nThe one worth stating: **a denial is `200 {\"allowed\": false}`, never\n`403`.** `/v1/check` asks a question and a refusal is a successful answer\nto it. `403` would say the caller may not ask at all, which is a\ndifferent claim and sends an operator looking in the wrong place.\n\nThe surface is one decision route and two health routes. No catch-all, no\nproxy path, no query-string alternative for identity, tenant, store,\nrelation or object; only a bearer, read strictly. The response carries\n`allowed` and nothing else -- no identity echoed, no store, no model, no\nexplanation of why a check failed.\n\nLiveness answers for this process alone. Making it depend on Keycloak or\nthe authorization service would turn a neighbour''s outage into a restart\nloop, destroying the cached keys that were keeping this process useful.\nReadiness includes the authorization service, because a front that cannot\ndecide should leave rotation -- and deliberately does not require a fresh\nkey fetch, since surviving an identity-provider outage on cached keys is\na designed behaviour rather than a degraded one.\n\nTwo fixture faults found while probing, both the same shape as the\nmixed-case one: a test that could not fail.\n\nThe oversized-body test asserted only \"not 200\". Its body also named an\nunknown field, so removing the limit entirely still produced a refusal\nand the test still passed. It now asserts `413`, which meant teaching the\nhandler to answer `413` for a size rejection rather than folding it in\nwith parse failures -- a limit whose absence no test can detect is not a\nlimit anybody is maintaining.\n\nAnd a probe restoring `health.rs` with `git checkout` silently did\nnothing, because the file was untracked; the \"restored\" run was still\nmutated. Caught by the count not returning to green.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqlANcCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA/doQACID3vYC2D8rrKT6blygxi/v\n2HK6WYUOlUWoTD/HBkBWhne9QEKulry9GuSQOwi1+yaCiPEDC6Vb8qmCSXDecieI\ntZ/Dtm4uhLzYPOmwiSEBNUqdNvbgr54E5jKVuv+dVjZI+mkAxJBi/P4H6bKhLyyC\nT47UEYuimf1HBsOkZw0yueITBMK07vEfZrr281yILxbPWkVtXMhXl3dO4cb2XdmP\nEZdxlyvT8ZFRaAs9VAKF3C3RABXaCOzMkrYO6/tIalqEYlptz6HpVGgANguJd9KY\nD2uPgNiE2vQkaVI6W/nNl3+D4GqAKgJ00CMslelMBpwc0813iDWhaAYRtEPwEXId\nvyJ0m3MiGeQoTzItftDRxU8tSB3IGEw2hUsH4U2O4MBowgd6Kp8FksjdIpRqwEgC\nDHSocMP8ukk/10S5dPC9AOW/m9D3a3unjI0jXur4p3DHMsmxH9QpvN+nHQCXZ386\nu5uLIPMm1kXEY5CSzgxoucBu2IoNm5R5u+oo/PlG2cjjT8sOCbWCfRc96UgltMDj\nkZuOPdH0bE1fj1xU7E4E89x4bhiDC6HtM2O/mRF4xOTBhDlNWG5WK4t+oXrQamtY\nEADGDXmspt72bQ13c2n/taZ1gHqvXwVVb3kDcPRf9K9beiiYnkaHGxl1QhewZ1v/\nZvsIh4VgAAnz8YGHlNlr\n=I8/d\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-30T10:18:04Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/aa75265802c0d3ed21a45bc358535616a45b6852","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKGFhNzUyNjU4MDJjMGQzZWQyMWE0NWJjMzU4NTM1NjE2YTQ1YjY4NTI","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/ed6bd23c7f05a68d3115763a5ef22d0fd968d6b1","sha":"ed6bd23c7f05a68d3115763a5ef22d0fd968d6b1","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/ed6bd23c7f05a68d3115763a5ef22d0fd968d6b1"}],"sha":"aa75265802c0d3ed21a45bc358535616a45b6852","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/aa75265802c0d3ed21a45bc358535616a45b6852"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/ed6bd23c7f05a68d3115763a5ef22d0fd968d6b1/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-30T09:55:39Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-30T09:55:39Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Speak to the OpenFGA beside us, and to nothing else (#25)\n\n`OpenFgaDecisions` is built from a **port**, never a URL. There is no\nper-request destination and none in the issuer registry, because having\nmade the store and the model trustworthy it would be careless to leave\nthe last hop pointable at anything. No argument exists that can send it\nto another host.\n\nThe failure mapping is the part worth reading twice. A `5xx`, a refused\nconnection or a timeout is `Unavailable`. A `4xx` is `Internal` -- never a\ndenial. Everything in that request except the relation and the object came\nfrom this platform, so an unknown store or a rejected body means *we* are\nwrong; answering \"denied\" would hide it behind a permissions message\nwhile the caller goes asking for access they already have. A response\nthat is not a decision is `Internal` for the same reason.\n\nTwo kinds of test, because either alone hides something. A socket-level\nfake asserts the exact outbound store, model, subject, relation and\nobject; a real OpenFGA proves it agrees -- that a model built from ADR\n0013''s vocabulary resolves, that a computed relation works, that a tuple\nwritten for `user:<realm>/<subject>` is found, and that the same subject\nin another realm is not.\n\nThe fixtures carry case, and that was earned. Every value in the first\ndraft was lowercase, so a mutation adding `.to_lowercase()` to the\nsubject passed both test kinds -- the exact property this was written to\nprotect, undetectable. With mixed-case fixtures all three normalisation\nmutations fail, on the socket and against the real engine.\n\nThe real-engine tests skip unless `FABRIC_TEST_OPENFGA_PORT` names a\nrunning service, and say so loudly rather than passing quietly.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"eb7b94151dfb80a44e37effe07236a4607fcb8dd","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/eb7b94151dfb80a44e37effe07236a4607fcb8dd"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/ed6bd23c7f05a68d3115763a5ef22d0fd968d6b1","verification":{"payload":"tree eb7b94151dfb80a44e37effe07236a4607fcb8dd\nparent 06cbc382f805447414ea9f99bca2cddca9562c0a\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1788083739 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788083739 +1200\n\nSpeak to the OpenFGA beside us, and to nothing else (#25)\n\n`OpenFgaDecisions` is built from a **port**, never a URL. There is no\nper-request destination and none in the issuer registry, because having\nmade the store and the model trustworthy it would be careless to leave\nthe last hop pointable at anything. No argument exists that can send it\nto another host.\n\nThe failure mapping is the part worth reading twice. A `5xx`, a refused\nconnection or a timeout is `Unavailable`. A `4xx` is `Internal` -- never a\ndenial. Everything in that request except the relation and the object came\nfrom this platform, so an unknown store or a rejected body means *we* are\nwrong; answering \"denied\" would hide it behind a permissions message\nwhile the caller goes asking for access they already have. A response\nthat is not a decision is `Internal` for the same reason.\n\nTwo kinds of test, because either alone hides something. A socket-level\nfake asserts the exact outbound store, model, subject, relation and\nobject; a real OpenFGA proves it agrees -- that a model built from ADR\n0013''s vocabulary resolves, that a computed relation works, that a tuple\nwritten for `user:<realm>/<subject>` is found, and that the same subject\nin another realm is not.\n\nThe fixtures carry case, and that was earned. Every value in the first\ndraft was lowercase, so a mutation adding `.to_lowercase()` to the\nsubject passed both test kinds -- the exact property this was written to\nprotect, undetectable. With mixed-case fixtures all three normalisation\nmutations fail, on the socket and against the real engine.\n\nThe real-engine tests skip unless `FABRIC_TEST_OPENFGA_PORT` names a\nrunning service, and say so loudly rather than passing quietly.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqk/4bCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA9dcQAJKLFpSaxEVpH7d/XIoHpmgN\ntt6kv3SIa6EWYuRra2iPbEmNVIv34kIWqlE2+pcH/m4S4H8beDvBk7I+zJzHjETi\nKw1F2CdRT46yttSIuVK7N6+Vx5Z7KuEnNLaD23P2igXHIl+MrbZ9LJU60zOmexCr\nnp4+PGZFrQIxtI8xBrVeKFntG75IvaWoalDFz6VUtednrE6+T3qr6v6ufKDxrg5n\nED+LAgZkJpWbb8SN3F/UOK7hvAfGeOGV/9JL4k9zKIPH0W/2PlZxUDkm+xxeNgAz\nZKKiJQ4p8uOFOJ2aqawTlNvjwawU1C00GmHqrhJCWViLHfU+8dxyMtQKr3TIcNN+\nrKzGlMq3fgWmumICWl+tjeD3GiasimQ4c7YsnhCp8ic5ypgFP5IQFMwwq/FUeZHU\nv++SlLlRgb3bWa9AazPy9ehoVQ8LJLC1rpYAas/kbdf+WdTfwcruwKXvkOKKRLgv\nDb13zDVuNcDm28lo7w98s1Z6z0HWHe1KZlNYMFaPP2HhXRjqRU+oUDotbRNJxeZd\n2wjRfsDOXFOp7nvq9Ch3s2cRfQZXxQG+S/LlJp6xGdF4otTBgpf9zeUyHcU3XbNI\nRLs851R/usaaQ6JalEJckIn+4bh3wTMBQM41MkWEzwa8Z8weL4N00V/kNE6MZXNB\n8XKmOacgNXUNlqO2XQGZ\n=ecuZ\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-30T09:55:40Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/ed6bd23c7f05a68d3115763a5ef22d0fd968d6b1","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKGVkNmJkMjNjN2YwNWE2OGQzMTE1NzYzYTVlZjIyZDBmZDk2OGQ2YjE","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/06cbc382f805447414ea9f99bca2cddca9562c0a","sha":"06cbc382f805447414ea9f99bca2cddca9562c0a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/06cbc382f805447414ea9f99bca2cddca9562c0a"}],"sha":"ed6bd23c7f05a68d3115763a5ef22d0fd968d6b1","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/ed6bd23c7f05a68d3115763a5ef22d0fd968d6b1"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/06cbc382f805447414ea9f99bca2cddca9562c0a/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-30T09:24:07Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-30T09:24:07Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Pin the authorization model, and stop calling a shared name the Data API''s (#24)\n\nTwo things settled before the adapter, because both are cheaper to fix\nnow than after one exists.\n\n**The model is pinned.** The authorization service uses its most recent\nmodel when a request names none, which would mean writing a model deploys\nit -- a new one changing runtime decisions before Fabric had\nintentionally switched. It is now a required field on the registration,\nrefused at startup when absent, and a **parameter on the `Decisions`\nport** rather than something an adapter may look up. A port that cannot\nomit it is a port no adapter can get wrong.\n\nThat also gives deployment a shape: write the immutable model, validate\nit, then change the configured id. Writing is no longer deploying.\n\nStore and model are both authorization *routing* rather than identity,\nand both come from the registration the verified issuer selected. They\nride on `VerifiedIdentity` because one trusted lookup produces all of it;\nif the routing grows it deserves its own type rather than more fields\nhere, and the doc says so.\n\n**`LogicalResourceName` is the platform''s vocabulary, not the Data API''s.**\nIt already lived in `fabric-core` and is named by six crates across both\nplanes -- desired state declares relations on it, the Data API resolves\nit through its catalogue, authorization uses it as the type half of an\nobject. Its documentation described only the middle one, which would have\nmade authorization read as subordinate to the Data API for no better\nreason than the order the two were built in. Only the framing changed.\n\nBoth new rules are shown to fail on their own mutation: removing the\nstartup check for a pinned model, and swapping the model at the call\nsite, each kill exactly one test.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"35fcba777636e11c1c3be56f63a987fce979b99b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/35fcba777636e11c1c3be56f63a987fce979b99b"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/06cbc382f805447414ea9f99bca2cddca9562c0a","verification":{"payload":"tree 35fcba777636e11c1c3be56f63a987fce979b99b\nparent fb2b96b013a3a36c28b0c77711b186f4f92d3ac8\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1788081847 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788081847 +1200\n\nPin the authorization model, and stop calling a shared name the Data API''s (#24)\n\nTwo things settled before the adapter, because both are cheaper to fix\nnow than after one exists.\n\n**The model is pinned.** The authorization service uses its most recent\nmodel when a request names none, which would mean writing a model deploys\nit -- a new one changing runtime decisions before Fabric had\nintentionally switched. It is now a required field on the registration,\nrefused at startup when absent, and a **parameter on the `Decisions`\nport** rather than something an adapter may look up. A port that cannot\nomit it is a port no adapter can get wrong.\n\nThat also gives deployment a shape: write the immutable model, validate\nit, then change the configured id. Writing is no longer deploying.\n\nStore and model are both authorization *routing* rather than identity,\nand both come from the registration the verified issuer selected. They\nride on `VerifiedIdentity` because one trusted lookup produces all of it;\nif the routing grows it deserves its own type rather than more fields\nhere, and the doc says so.\n\n**`LogicalResourceName` is the platform''s vocabulary, not the Data API''s.**\nIt already lived in `fabric-core` and is named by six crates across both\nplanes -- desired state declares relations on it, the Data API resolves\nit through its catalogue, authorization uses it as the type half of an\nobject. Its documentation described only the middle one, which would have\nmade authorization read as subordinate to the Data API for no better\nreason than the order the two were built in. Only the framing changed.\n\nBoth new rules are shown to fail on their own mutation: removing the\nstartup check for a pinned model, and swapping the model at the call\nsite, each kill exactly one test.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqk/a3CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAjXQQABPj7LFSLctFK7RtBdy/qEnE\nNk0Pg3ALZMytZ7A7zPweSDh8cPcP1wso1Fjaub7UseVlxBAL0xI+qBZ6kdGar9p6\nkEq4ko/wrc9OUZUxQzaIeL3+tjJsDIEb+OYBT6FYFjlBeoGCztiQj3VPxiXgTox4\nhwQCwBFixg+xL6F7uwovX5Kob9Z/3yXAs8AVyF8Z7XJfeoyTQO7sSaSgmjEhUAX0\nK37waM7/poW8rC9rOjkM8adhdhDrgh+fgP+UsniwHpRXZacpWwrjt5I7JgXv8BYk\n5dM1kDtX/Zv/l4R/1j6r9p4B048j/nwftCgw8A84dUsBZns8p4DhI5d55VJxdiwK\nFsHNGq1GUronxNDQBM+YM132e++tHMDPqVAPG7a4zCP6boIfGgCCRi/XiOrEtndI\nxPWbp0V32ZyFU8muuTlIFKOUXjlkKKxqAIqTcRh8EcDum1tp8WA9G8hTZbJVmUkl\nPzJZGa0QAP151No9ThJj4StEPlvLgKdVJeYaNBUGSfBrzC8RvlT5MZNTlctZ/eXn\nwwRplPilpia+B3ul3UF04++GHhJp92s8WzE9+0GrRNoQhHz0+to9mRFGH6qik68f\nJiuE6yMnmpi1QH2GnsIdPxUg35BHw7nM1IQsZIbxVM2RHOeFv9YhSgXKnLoo1W+s\nj1497X+XbcW4veXh86mF\n=S2U8\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-30T09:24:08Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/06cbc382f805447414ea9f99bca2cddca9562c0a","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKDA2Y2JjMzgyZjgwNTQ0NzQxNGVhOWY5OWJjYTJjZGRjYTk1NjJjMGE","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/fb2b96b013a3a36c28b0c77711b186f4f92d3ac8","sha":"fb2b96b013a3a36c28b0c77711b186f4f92d3ac8","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/fb2b96b013a3a36c28b0c77711b186f4f92d3ac8"}],"sha":"06cbc382f805447414ea9f99bca2cddca9562c0a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/06cbc382f805447414ea9f99bca2cddca9562c0a"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/fb2b96b013a3a36c28b0c77711b186f4f92d3ac8/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-30T09:06:55Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-30T09:06:55Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Ask Check about the caller, and give it nowhere to say otherwise (#23)\n\nThe first operation on the runtime surface, in Fabric''s own request shape\nrather than the authorization service''s:\n\n    { \"relation\": \"viewer\", \"object\": \"document:123\" }\n\nThere is no `user` field to overwrite, no `store_id` to ignore, and no\ntenant or realm to strip, because none of them is in the schema. An\noperation that accepted a `CheckRequest` and sanitised it would be one\nforgotten field away from letting a caller ask about somebody else; this\none has nowhere for them to try.\n\n`deny_unknown_fields` finishes the argument. A request naming `user` is\n*refused* rather than quietly ignored, so a caller attempting to name\ntheir own principal gets an error instead of a decision that silently\ndisregarded them.\n\nThree inputs, and the caller supplies one and a half: relation and object\ncome from the request, principal and store come from the verified\nidentity, which came from the registry.\n\nA denial is `Ok(false)` and an unreachable service is an error. Telling a\ncaller \"denied\" when the authorization service is down sends them to ask\nan administrator for access they already have, and hides an incident\nbehind a message about permissions.\n\n`RelationName` moves to `fabric-core` for the reason `OperationKind` and\n`SubjectId` did: the control plane writes relations into desired state\nand the runtime names one on every check, and two definitions of `viewer`\nwould drift without failing a build. `fabric-client-model` re-exports it,\nso nothing else moves.\n\nEach binding rule is shown to fail on its own mutation: dropping\n`deny_unknown_fields`, swapping the subject for another principal,\nswapping the store for another tenant''s, and turning an outage into a\ndenial each kill exactly the tests that exist to catch them.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"1fc87d85befeda44e0153460d5a5c0e171f9d814","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/1fc87d85befeda44e0153460d5a5c0e171f9d814"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/fb2b96b013a3a36c28b0c77711b186f4f92d3ac8","verification":{"payload":"tree 1fc87d85befeda44e0153460d5a5c0e171f9d814\nparent 01b0ad1197f95ed0aa57e0de69e739df3dc03d63\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1788080815 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788080815 +1200\n\nAsk Check about the caller, and give it nowhere to say otherwise (#23)\n\nThe first operation on the runtime surface, in Fabric''s own request shape\nrather than the authorization service''s:\n\n    { \"relation\": \"viewer\", \"object\": \"document:123\" }\n\nThere is no `user` field to overwrite, no `store_id` to ignore, and no\ntenant or realm to strip, because none of them is in the schema. An\noperation that accepted a `CheckRequest` and sanitised it would be one\nforgotten field away from letting a caller ask about somebody else; this\none has nowhere for them to try.\n\n`deny_unknown_fields` finishes the argument. A request naming `user` is\n*refused* rather than quietly ignored, so a caller attempting to name\ntheir own principal gets an error instead of a decision that silently\ndisregarded them.\n\nThree inputs, and the caller supplies one and a half: relation and object\ncome from the request, principal and store come from the verified\nidentity, which came from the registry.\n\nA denial is `Ok(false)` and an unreachable service is an error. Telling a\ncaller \"denied\" when the authorization service is down sends them to ask\nan administrator for access they already have, and hides an incident\nbehind a message about permissions.\n\n`RelationName` moves to `fabric-core` for the reason `OperationKind` and\n`SubjectId` did: the control plane writes relations into desired state\nand the runtime names one on every check, and two definitions of `viewer`\nwould drift without failing a build. `fabric-client-model` re-exports it,\nso nothing else moves.\n\nEach binding rule is shown to fail on its own mutation: dropping\n`deny_unknown_fields`, swapping the subject for another principal,\nswapping the store for another tenant''s, and turning an outage into a\ndenial each kill exactly the tests that exist to catch them.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqk/KvCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAzEUQALGTz/dBozcV0p4/ERUXNjwK\nYwg0fhmwokU68aj7eFy6urZhGw56d9Lb4kcQ0sN9Ly5z4sAJlen5fWFGLS/MwJsa\nLUHtWbvoXysEceI22mqcSXVEKXcx7QGfitQg9vWEBPHPvYOwNquUUI1fyc7t94Jc\nNP5XU4uzQfSV+PoUoWn8dWK5rE7x5kpcoQgUp2lKCd+FJMmh7bg7cLthUISO5vsU\ns2KSp3QmKytk2KIz4NjN8+SOcTnT+sg7WHGS3am2m3sioaMfR8lBWoj5wqGAdEHM\nY6gpLWhr34qzGfroe6opfj4CzWDxACKly6WrI6qR7Pqo8angjsh4PAN+ipYnYYLa\nUEJb0FcML69nzMOp9S4NxVsSufjh/KQfcygIqBw92OPMpsHpnvSP6bSMHMEhvIuw\n0F1dk/W3kb0XDsbjPqAip1Y7mHiltxOCXb/mzN4e+m0xszq+1k6jyAnM6m7HWR6B\ndUo0cWu4WXpOtX/xW8KQKlcNmkxwDru3UgkZ6WGy/Vo3yPx30e+r4YtendbTwkGi\nQoomq/6TbBA7vB0vo4jB4Qdj8e/XMk7F8WWO/IaawhqiG3v5Tgeu1sKpmVHsfrfE\nFGfWGi9iekLldEJjnTRFKYvs7TcNEV2T7dfxGR1AhHbtATeC1aMt6onD+QUE6OvJ\nG+ekFJvlX6m8YV1VMp8p\n=Vrc6\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-30T09:06:55Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/fb2b96b013a3a36c28b0c77711b186f4f92d3ac8","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKGZiMmI5NmIwMTNhM2EzNmMyOGIwYzc3NzExYjE4NmY0ZjkyZDNhYzg","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/01b0ad1197f95ed0aa57e0de69e739df3dc03d63","sha":"01b0ad1197f95ed0aa57e0de69e739df3dc03d63","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/01b0ad1197f95ed0aa57e0de69e739df3dc03d63"}],"sha":"fb2b96b013a3a36c28b0c77711b186f4f92d3ac8","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/fb2b96b013a3a36c28b0c77711b186f4f92d3ac8"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/01b0ad1197f95ed0aa57e0de69e739df3dc03d63/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-30T08:46:16Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-30T08:46:16Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Give OpenFGA one door and Fabric two surfaces (#22)\n\nAn earlier draft of ADR 0016 held two things that cannot both be true:\nOpenFGA binds loopback with `authn=none`, and store administration stays\non ADR 0014''s path. A control plane in another pod cannot reach a\nloopback listener, so one of them had to give.\n\nNot the loopback invariant. Adding an administrative listener to OpenFGA\nwould trade away the property the whole design just established, and for\nthe convenience of the one caller least in need of a shortcut. OpenFGA\nkeeps exactly one door; the front exposes two surfaces behind it, and the\ncontrol plane goes through Fabric-owned code like everything else.\n\nSeparate listeners rather than path prefixes on one port, so Kubernetes\ncan enforce the distinction too: `:8080` is published, `:8081` is\ninternal and can be NetworkPolicy-restricted to the controller that needs\nit. Defence in depth and nothing more -- both surfaces authenticate\nunconditionally, and network isolation is not the authorization\nmechanism.\n\nThe contracts differ on purpose. On the runtime surface the subject *is*\nthe authenticated principal and no workload identity may be substituted\nfor a user''s. On the control-plane surface the subject is defined per\noperation and need not be the caller, because an operator administering\naccess is legitimately acting about somebody else -- the Alice-grants-Bob\ncase, now with somewhere to live.\n\nTwo invariants follow: everything reaching the embedded OpenFGA\ntraverses an explicitly supported Fabric operation, and the two surfaces\ncarry independent contracts with no generic passthrough on either.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"f533adc58db6a70677efc74f479639ef660a2a59","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/f533adc58db6a70677efc74f479639ef660a2a59"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/01b0ad1197f95ed0aa57e0de69e739df3dc03d63","verification":{"payload":"tree f533adc58db6a70677efc74f479639ef660a2a59\nparent bda31e4231a11c621d304f2b9b9f5376743865d7\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1788079576 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788079576 +1200\n\nGive OpenFGA one door and Fabric two surfaces (#22)\n\nAn earlier draft of ADR 0016 held two things that cannot both be true:\nOpenFGA binds loopback with `authn=none`, and store administration stays\non ADR 0014''s path. A control plane in another pod cannot reach a\nloopback listener, so one of them had to give.\n\nNot the loopback invariant. Adding an administrative listener to OpenFGA\nwould trade away the property the whole design just established, and for\nthe convenience of the one caller least in need of a shortcut. OpenFGA\nkeeps exactly one door; the front exposes two surfaces behind it, and the\ncontrol plane goes through Fabric-owned code like everything else.\n\nSeparate listeners rather than path prefixes on one port, so Kubernetes\ncan enforce the distinction too: `:8080` is published, `:8081` is\ninternal and can be NetworkPolicy-restricted to the controller that needs\nit. Defence in depth and nothing more -- both surfaces authenticate\nunconditionally, and network isolation is not the authorization\nmechanism.\n\nThe contracts differ on purpose. On the runtime surface the subject *is*\nthe authenticated principal and no workload identity may be substituted\nfor a user''s. On the control-plane surface the subject is defined per\noperation and need not be the caller, because an operator administering\naccess is legitimately acting about somebody else -- the Alice-grants-Bob\ncase, now with somewhere to live.\n\nTwo invariants follow: everything reaching the embedded OpenFGA\ntraverses an explicitly supported Fabric operation, and the two surfaces\ncarry independent contracts with no generic passthrough on either.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqk+3YCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA/nkQADEJSgP0GcDY1zicFc7s1ffP\nvvRthpbsKfCyKKBiSYhI/HO8HfU8cnU6U+oOBzSE8gGjjBD3dtRz0YKZucfgGGns\nDgEmnihLKzlhQ0nH0nnBiEpHmIgsYYZ3VQVvmW29UCm70VbVl/4eL5V695pupi9e\n20sia0i7eHBhzu9DWwYw8X/IFwgliD8d1VlATz9+bzjEE0nx7KJ8i1XujzUIMaS+\nMhaqtZoqdh8a+95RACwUuHxrHJBQQZDjzuKyDvp6wYXatk0FGYcE6zKxkwgdq4pN\naFaSEYawZlZSmNsxIREM0C6ZWh3geUdvfa1h55nEl2m0pMEtGlpSpNIWM0/TLgGb\nANqrscVkSy2QkViv8iL18poyDWHRDWU0cigfLAQvEj3eC0dT01Jtx36J4jqP/Keo\nhfa+qZVyO63nPU/5r4Cpks02GswONXFJO/OhxJzumu3RiHsc/Qj/wzXyy9GL4AzX\nIa2Gd4zkQZvUHDSKR/WHUjL20LC9eed/FFgpa0d3YVXd/vM2n5Je+fwTURCBct6p\n4bDEK5YE2rWSeCqEaO5Jdk0ZW9JQ7PiXeyYs/CYekPV/bRLJHER/MZrm73HwWV3h\nDIjUIvN9nqD52bapsGsQf+LjUVpV7FkW35Ql35iYNHS5q0/DMCRvzOh/v2153i+h\nitv0IcPZiSRepRyeuGF4\n=6I5d\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-30T08:46:16Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/01b0ad1197f95ed0aa57e0de69e739df3dc03d63","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKDAxYjBhZDExOTdmOTVlZDBhYTU3ZTBkZTY5ZTczOWRmM2RjMDNkNjM","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/bda31e4231a11c621d304f2b9b9f5376743865d7","sha":"bda31e4231a11c621d304f2b9b9f5376743865d7","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/bda31e4231a11c621d304f2b9b9f5376743865d7"}],"sha":"01b0ad1197f95ed0aa57e0de69e739df3dc03d63","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/01b0ad1197f95ed0aa57e0de69e739df3dc03d63"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/bda31e4231a11c621d304f2b9b9f5376743865d7/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-30T08:35:34Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-30T08:35:34Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Establish the one thing the authorization front may trust (#21)\n\n`fabric-fga-auth` answers one question -- given a token, who is this and\nwhich store answers for them -- and does nothing else. No OpenFGA\ndependency, no proxying, no policy. Everything after it is allowed to\ntrust its answer, which is why it is built and attacked on its own first\n(ADR 0016).\n\nThe registry is checked once, at startup, and every problem it can find\nis fatal there: an empty registry, a duplicated issuer, a tenant that\ncannot be a realm, a missing audience or algorithm list. None of those is\na state worth discovering from a request -- and an empty issuer list is\nexactly the shape in which one real authorization service silently stops\nverifying signatures.\n\nOnly `subject` comes from the token. Tenant, realm and store come from\nthe registration the *verified* issuer selected, so a token carrying\n`tenant`, `store_id` or `principal` claims is not wrong to carry them and\nis simply never read. There is a test that sets all four to an attacker''s\nvalues and asserts the identity is unchanged.\n\nThree failure classes, kept apart because collapsing them is the bug: a\ncredential is refused, a configuration is fatal, and an unreachable\nprovider is `Unavailable` -- never a refusal. A Keycloak outage must not\ntell a legitimate user their token is bad.\n\nTwo things the tests found rather than confirmed.\n\n`jsonwebtoken` applies a **60-second leeway by default**, so a token\nexpired by one second still verified. That is a security property nobody\nchose; the tolerance is now an explicit 30 seconds with a test at 45.\n\nAnd a per-issuer lock serialises refreshes without *bounding* them: 500\nrequests carrying random `kid` values produced 500 fetches, one at a\ntime, aimed at the identity provider. A minimum refresh interval means an\nunfamiliar `kid` inside the window is refused from a key set confirmed\nseconds ago. The test asserts 500 attempts cost exactly one fetch.\n\nSigned with a symmetric key in tests rather than an embedded RSA private\nkey, following the control plane''s OIDC tests.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"a17522836608646853feb82f4a780ffdbc079609","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/a17522836608646853feb82f4a780ffdbc079609"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/bda31e4231a11c621d304f2b9b9f5376743865d7","verification":{"payload":"tree a17522836608646853feb82f4a780ffdbc079609\nparent a0ad35f04a556db867b3241b02b9ca2d7c422a9b\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1788078934 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788078934 +1200\n\nEstablish the one thing the authorization front may trust (#21)\n\n`fabric-fga-auth` answers one question -- given a token, who is this and\nwhich store answers for them -- and does nothing else. No OpenFGA\ndependency, no proxying, no policy. Everything after it is allowed to\ntrust its answer, which is why it is built and attacked on its own first\n(ADR 0016).\n\nThe registry is checked once, at startup, and every problem it can find\nis fatal there: an empty registry, a duplicated issuer, a tenant that\ncannot be a realm, a missing audience or algorithm list. None of those is\na state worth discovering from a request -- and an empty issuer list is\nexactly the shape in which one real authorization service silently stops\nverifying signatures.\n\nOnly `subject` comes from the token. Tenant, realm and store come from\nthe registration the *verified* issuer selected, so a token carrying\n`tenant`, `store_id` or `principal` claims is not wrong to carry them and\nis simply never read. There is a test that sets all four to an attacker''s\nvalues and asserts the identity is unchanged.\n\nThree failure classes, kept apart because collapsing them is the bug: a\ncredential is refused, a configuration is fatal, and an unreachable\nprovider is `Unavailable` -- never a refusal. A Keycloak outage must not\ntell a legitimate user their token is bad.\n\nTwo things the tests found rather than confirmed.\n\n`jsonwebtoken` applies a **60-second leeway by default**, so a token\nexpired by one second still verified. That is a security property nobody\nchose; the tolerance is now an explicit 30 seconds with a test at 45.\n\nAnd a per-issuer lock serialises refreshes without *bounding* them: 500\nrequests carrying random `kid` values produced 500 fetches, one at a\ntime, aimed at the identity provider. A minimum refresh interval means an\nunfamiliar `kid` inside the window is refused from a key set confirmed\nseconds ago. The test asserts 500 attempts cost exactly one fetch.\n\nSigned with a symmetric key in tests rather than an embedded RSA private\nkey, following the control plane''s OIDC tests.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqk+tWCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA978QAIsVZkoN1coQ6K1qenLqzL8J\nDb/r04+2cm+HAU7YUXTbQwPtHULOsOYSe9AFz0dBZBiEhbpY3FERTVf3HTBmFdzP\nRt5QpuOZLpj++EPGiv3GXy3eLMjcJ6dIkU+vrQn9gwEBDEUiEdAs4gtZNZhwxGnL\nmMYBvkYH3j/MWIJO7AWuCym3F1r1gnk+HBtvs15T2WQNe5rRF5AkG796/yO31Ow2\nLs88UT4wa52xqbYC0f4s3oOHf/Q90wxZaPnAx9W2H4oy8A53l5GUyRbu3RK8JAx8\nVhFRclq6b722xWgQp0UX10zd4bpZFDXVSCY67b2vUH9bvY8gW7k6MgITuAK8t67l\nn/ZroD1yowW4h+8K3i56SRRLR0aWXm7GRAlYSHcUiS7iGXkx2dRClGL78jYnk3a9\nJzeuG8Ven846iN71sv1EAPJ9N4nca+uTZRn6iF6cpnve8Qi1vypgBlYjomMMGqeS\ne5RCJyoM+AoIn5coF3thlSfT6S5ckJtnRxjHGD/TfRyQ61QIMAMXMTnlC8k0Dp0c\n4UYJt90p+li2sTd+BubyV4RB2jw4x5uAmG0FfertcKCMNatE+z1njoVRjjbMZZga\nmcVYSN9M3zDbqp1mGuw5u5ZcXjul1PejM/lbpx4yhrkAOykAlrCra01BH/dTNXDX\no3JVZNw9oWQAmRihf1f1\n=Jt6u\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-30T08:35:35Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/bda31e4231a11c621d304f2b9b9f5376743865d7","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKGJkYTMxZTQyMzFhMTFjNjIxZDMwNGYyYjliOWY1Mzc2NzQzODY1ZDc","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/a0ad35f04a556db867b3241b02b9ca2d7c422a9b","sha":"a0ad35f04a556db867b3241b02b9ca2d7c422a9b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/a0ad35f04a556db867b3241b02b9ca2d7c422a9b"}],"sha":"bda31e4231a11c621d304f2b9b9f5376743865d7","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/bda31e4231a11c621d304f2b9b9f5376743865d7"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/a0ad35f04a556db867b3241b02b9ca2d7c422a9b/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-30T07:50:29Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-30T07:50:29Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Own the authorization front door instead of adopting one (#20)\n\nThe spike closed the Topaz branch. Its multi-issuer design is exactly the\nshape wanted -- `allowed_issuers` as a list, an issuer-to-JWKS map, each\ntoken validated against its own issuer''s keys -- and its OIDC client\nrefuses to fetch from any private IP. Every Kubernetes ClusterIP is\nprivate, so adopting it would mean designing Fabric''s networking around an\nauthorization engine''s defensive assumption: publishing Keycloak, or\nleaning on 100.64/10 happening not to satisfy Go''s `IsPrivate()`. That is\nbackwards, so we build the missing piece instead.\n\n`fabric-fga-auth` sits in front of an OpenFGA nothing else can reach, and\nships with it as one image. It does what a proxy that merely validated a\nbearer would not: it *binds* identity. Alice''s valid token asking a\ndecision question about Bob is the failure being designed out.\n\nBut it is not an OpenFGA proxy and must not promise API compatibility,\nbecause a single binding rule is wrong the moment relationships are\nadministered. Alice may legitimately be a realm administrator granting Bob\naccess, and rewriting Bob to Alice there would be a bug rather than a\nsafeguard. Three surfaces, three rules: on a decision the subject *is* the\ncaller; relationship mutation is an explicit operation that authorizes\nAlice to administer access and leaves Bob as the subject; administration\nis not on this path at all.\n\nHence the contract: the front exposes only explicitly supported\noperations, each defining how principal, tenant/store and any\nrequest-supplied subject are interpreted, and everything else fails\nclosed. \"Wrap OpenFGA''s API\" is explicitly not the goal, which is what\nkeeps a few hundred lines believable.\n\nThe registry separates the logical issuer from where keys are fetched --\nthe browser sees the public issuer, the verifier reads JWKS in-cluster.\nThat is why it does not reintroduce the risk Topaz''s filter defends\nagainst: `jwks_uri` comes from Fabric''s configuration and never from a\nclaim in an incoming token.\n\nRecorded as invariants rather than advice, including the two the spike\nearned: zero configured issuers must refuse to start (Topaz validates\nexpiry but not signature in that state -- measured, with a signature that\nwas the literal bytes `not-a-real-signature`), and OpenFGA binds\n127.0.0.1 so `authn=none` is never network-reachable.\n\nThis answers what ADR 0014 left open for the runtime by removing the\nquestion. No workload identity exists anywhere in the authorization path.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"f79664ef75e7d5554d93d15ebe1f9adebf137125","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/f79664ef75e7d5554d93d15ebe1f9adebf137125"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/a0ad35f04a556db867b3241b02b9ca2d7c422a9b","verification":{"payload":"tree f79664ef75e7d5554d93d15ebe1f9adebf137125\nparent 85a9a154ca3d7ead8592e3dc7ad28d590200e026\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1788076229 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788076229 +1200\n\nOwn the authorization front door instead of adopting one (#20)\n\nThe spike closed the Topaz branch. Its multi-issuer design is exactly the\nshape wanted -- `allowed_issuers` as a list, an issuer-to-JWKS map, each\ntoken validated against its own issuer''s keys -- and its OIDC client\nrefuses to fetch from any private IP. Every Kubernetes ClusterIP is\nprivate, so adopting it would mean designing Fabric''s networking around an\nauthorization engine''s defensive assumption: publishing Keycloak, or\nleaning on 100.64/10 happening not to satisfy Go''s `IsPrivate()`. That is\nbackwards, so we build the missing piece instead.\n\n`fabric-fga-auth` sits in front of an OpenFGA nothing else can reach, and\nships with it as one image. It does what a proxy that merely validated a\nbearer would not: it *binds* identity. Alice''s valid token asking a\ndecision question about Bob is the failure being designed out.\n\nBut it is not an OpenFGA proxy and must not promise API compatibility,\nbecause a single binding rule is wrong the moment relationships are\nadministered. Alice may legitimately be a realm administrator granting Bob\naccess, and rewriting Bob to Alice there would be a bug rather than a\nsafeguard. Three surfaces, three rules: on a decision the subject *is* the\ncaller; relationship mutation is an explicit operation that authorizes\nAlice to administer access and leaves Bob as the subject; administration\nis not on this path at all.\n\nHence the contract: the front exposes only explicitly supported\noperations, each defining how principal, tenant/store and any\nrequest-supplied subject are interpreted, and everything else fails\nclosed. \"Wrap OpenFGA''s API\" is explicitly not the goal, which is what\nkeeps a few hundred lines believable.\n\nThe registry separates the logical issuer from where keys are fetched --\nthe browser sees the public issuer, the verifier reads JWKS in-cluster.\nThat is why it does not reintroduce the risk Topaz''s filter defends\nagainst: `jwks_uri` comes from Fabric''s configuration and never from a\nclaim in an incoming token.\n\nRecorded as invariants rather than advice, including the two the spike\nearned: zero configured issuers must refuse to start (Topaz validates\nexpiry but not signature in that state -- measured, with a signature that\nwas the literal bytes `not-a-real-signature`), and OpenFGA binds\n127.0.0.1 so `authn=none` is never network-reachable.\n\nThis answers what ADR 0014 left open for the runtime by removing the\nquestion. No workload identity exists anywhere in the authorization path.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqk+DFCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAr70QAAjuUQezBntoNExNGixdRVVk\nv2hIj+hirdalPxUDTfdZ3LhCQp/2WHnIdLsaMC4PVcgsCD7xa9w2OM+/jU/WgC77\nYeKojkyTpmMSHMe2cvcWCoYFK8JC2zCufyNOhX+ZXhvq00BKAvjqp0AZ2pEVhSr5\nG/crYKPLFyUuvb58w8sj2tsHSAH/qa5TViqJFL00w8a8LqTLT+yDssgsZ4ICo1wm\nNB0jf7TNOtdoww9iK7gHBq1+DOqXdHGSBk1Or1Y9H69AFjwJJ7OitVekX0xxhd+q\nVMWYM19wIulcZjOwZRnJg1DP1kUYWm0qC9dZUNqyS2LsFpFUGL6OjF7mGtnAv9eO\nYNxIHhuFZ3V18nOGk22IY6m1Yd7cdnxVK3naOzx4i4q28HBxy+FMQlUv0FIHGIts\nStbzrMqWJUlIWXYpnbdbInLGxcRM1RdbRh6v4dxsKdAKAAmO2MgbpKk0K2euMjXc\nQgH631W+TUyELQH9XtTm7v4p27jmyR2lc+YOJQeKnDmQA651QJ2/DxdI24irgq1+\nS+syUGj35ujCpTqg/N4jfFxpqauRhFN0m0kXE/dk9b6TYsfkxFKRmRTPQPy+WEXt\ntACVMLUTj3GcwTBYV+yG0XPIXUAJO0JeJpKDY+gOhBpvwCK8e7POO7YuVJLUOKOP\nuzPzS/BmrHh3/pUqnIIV\n=QZEV\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-30T07:50:29Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/a0ad35f04a556db867b3241b02b9ca2d7c422a9b","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKGEwYWQzNWYwNGE1NTZkYjg2N2IzMjQxYjAyYjljYTJkN2M0MjJhOWI","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/85a9a154ca3d7ead8592e3dc7ad28d590200e026","sha":"85a9a154ca3d7ead8592e3dc7ad28d590200e026","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/85a9a154ca3d7ead8592e3dc7ad28d590200e026"}],"sha":"a0ad35f04a556db867b3241b02b9ca2d7c422a9b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/a0ad35f04a556db867b3241b02b9ca2d7c422a9b"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/85a9a154ca3d7ead8592e3dc7ad28d590200e026/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-30T07:33:01Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-30T07:33:01Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Name a subject by its realm, and narrow the runtime''s options (#18)\n\n* Name a subject by its realm, and narrow the runtime''s options\n\nTwo things ADR 0014 left open. One is now decided; the other is narrower\nthan \"a stored secret\", which was worth measuring before conceding.\n\nA subject is `<realm>/<subject>`, carried by a validated `SubjectId` in\n`fabric-core`. The realm is part of the name because a provider''s subject\nis unique within its realm and nowhere else -- but the operational reason\nis better than the semantic one. Store-per-client means a tuple written\ninto the wrong client''s store with a bare subject is a grant that\nsilently applies to whoever holds that subject there; the same tuple\ncarrying its realm matches nothing. Qualification turns a misrouted grant\nfrom a security failure into an inert row.\n\nThe validation is here because the authorization service has none.\nMeasured against OpenFGA v1.19: `acme/`, `/subject` and `acme//doubled`\nare all accepted as ordinary identifiers, each a distinct subject that\nwill never match the one intended -- so a malformed value fails as a\nsilently denied request rather than as an error anybody sees. `:`, `#`\nand `*` are refused because they are structural to that service''s syntax,\nand `/` because it is ours.\n\nOn the runtime''s credential, two measurements change what is possible.\nThe one-issuer limit is per deployment, not per datastore: two OpenFGA\ndeployments over one Postgres hold the same stores and tuples while\nauthenticating callers independently, so the control plane can keep\npresenting operator tokens while the runtime uses something else\nentirely. And Kubernetes is itself an OIDC issuer whose workload tokens\nare secretless -- audience-bound, durably subjected, kubelet-rotated,\ncarrying every claim OpenFGA validates.\n\nTwo obstacles there, one solved and one not: OpenFGA does not trust the\ncluster CA (`SSL_CERT_FILE` fixes it) and then gets 401, because\ndiscovery is bound to authenticated service accounts. Opening that is a\ndeliberate cluster change and belongs to whoever runs the cluster.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Supersede the runtime decision formally, and name the precondition\n\nTwo corrections before this becomes the baseline.\n\nADR 0014 said \"superseded\" only in prose while its status still read as\naccepted, which left two ADRs that both looked authoritative and\ndisagreed about the trust boundary. Its status now says the runtime half\nis superseded by ADR 0016, and the section itself opens with a block\nsaying nothing in it is normative. The measurements stay: they are why\nthe route was chosen, and deleting them would leave the choice looking\narbitrary.\n\nThe realm in a `SubjectId` must come from the trusted issuer registry,\nlooked up by the verified `iss` -- never parsed from an issuer URL, taken\nfrom a claim, or accepted from a request. A subject qualified by a realm\nthe caller influenced is worse than an unqualified one, because it looks\nlike a tenant boundary and is not.\n\nThe constructor is therefore `from_verified` rather than the crate''s\nusual `try_new`. The convention hides the precondition, and this is the\none identifier where getting the precondition wrong is a security failure\nrather than a malformed string. The type cannot enforce it while the\nrealm is a `&str`; when the registry lands the signature takes the\nidentity the registry issues, and the comment becomes a type.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"f0c64080d6873cc761b91846edc885d469169cc7","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/f0c64080d6873cc761b91846edc885d469169cc7"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/85a9a154ca3d7ead8592e3dc7ad28d590200e026","verification":{"payload":"tree f0c64080d6873cc761b91846edc885d469169cc7\nparent 457216145a308f739af4ab46dda0f14b8dee8de7\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1788075181 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788075181 +1200\n\nName a subject by its realm, and narrow the runtime''s options (#18)\n\n* Name a subject by its realm, and narrow the runtime''s options\n\nTwo things ADR 0014 left open. One is now decided; the other is narrower\nthan \"a stored secret\", which was worth measuring before conceding.\n\nA subject is `<realm>/<subject>`, carried by a validated `SubjectId` in\n`fabric-core`. The realm is part of the name because a provider''s subject\nis unique within its realm and nowhere else -- but the operational reason\nis better than the semantic one. Store-per-client means a tuple written\ninto the wrong client''s store with a bare subject is a grant that\nsilently applies to whoever holds that subject there; the same tuple\ncarrying its realm matches nothing. Qualification turns a misrouted grant\nfrom a security failure into an inert row.\n\nThe validation is here because the authorization service has none.\nMeasured against OpenFGA v1.19: `acme/`, `/subject` and `acme//doubled`\nare all accepted as ordinary identifiers, each a distinct subject that\nwill never match the one intended -- so a malformed value fails as a\nsilently denied request rather than as an error anybody sees. `:`, `#`\nand `*` are refused because they are structural to that service''s syntax,\nand `/` because it is ours.\n\nOn the runtime''s credential, two measurements change what is possible.\nThe one-issuer limit is per deployment, not per datastore: two OpenFGA\ndeployments over one Postgres hold the same stores and tuples while\nauthenticating callers independently, so the control plane can keep\npresenting operator tokens while the runtime uses something else\nentirely. And Kubernetes is itself an OIDC issuer whose workload tokens\nare secretless -- audience-bound, durably subjected, kubelet-rotated,\ncarrying every claim OpenFGA validates.\n\nTwo obstacles there, one solved and one not: OpenFGA does not trust the\ncluster CA (`SSL_CERT_FILE` fixes it) and then gets 401, because\ndiscovery is bound to authenticated service accounts. Opening that is a\ndeliberate cluster change and belongs to whoever runs the cluster.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Supersede the runtime decision formally, and name the precondition\n\nTwo corrections before this becomes the baseline.\n\nADR 0014 said \"superseded\" only in prose while its status still read as\naccepted, which left two ADRs that both looked authoritative and\ndisagreed about the trust boundary. Its status now says the runtime half\nis superseded by ADR 0016, and the section itself opens with a block\nsaying nothing in it is normative. The measurements stay: they are why\nthe route was chosen, and deleting them would leave the choice looking\narbitrary.\n\nThe realm in a `SubjectId` must come from the trusted issuer registry,\nlooked up by the verified `iss` -- never parsed from an issuer URL, taken\nfrom a claim, or accepted from a request. A subject qualified by a realm\nthe caller influenced is worse than an unqualified one, because it looks\nlike a tenant boundary and is not.\n\nThe constructor is therefore `from_verified` rather than the crate''s\nusual `try_new`. The convention hides the precondition, and this is the\none identifier where getting the precondition wrong is a security failure\nrather than a malformed string. The type cannot enforce it while the\nrealm is a `&str`; when the registry lands the signature takes the\nidentity the registry issues, and the comment becomes a type.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqk9ytCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAbcUQAJ2cgD+NI1new1EYERFlZIb2\nocMfUVyvAuemu5o1qn+iMS0RG3cxfYTYSgqOTIjOmSzO2mdCjyT5Prjgjnw8LHZb\nrXe9RbO/9apkFRFXtKmiveoSSBLuzVE46FnTYV7uqXyZY2ag7X0KyGzLuRMmqzOw\nakg+/BP/wSDeFdKK6Yq7hSgtsWP6hgz3FfLF3zCxjwscIpkui/FUIfCrsaLFBWMS\nusgSSBsj5fpv6LJJlcuZvDxGeedgJHdYE0S/kOr+5IS2KwdOd481Q+3pvYP6vPc8\nuaaUwgnx99td/FX5eLO+moErQdF8bvqBXaQXTZPWejbuKXkTNGu1vYVHGuD0Emka\nZoH+EOLxX9lvu6u09oZ/OHn2CIS+gXjQStQQ2goV1JnlDQlXLqF847plk8oZ7Jme\nrlH5cWmf7y+DdSpX2atilTG7bL6jA9vxf5DUjX1p3bH+g92xJHB7CGMl854b7tYh\nShUM34we/FeDf8LHmBCPhb3rNYNNZ8ndT9ELta0bwiKXSi9MEpI3uSezbp5xKr4V\n0HLixoZ2jE5zvINp7kw8/FEQkjuW3Oz4TeGoT+92sOOeWs3GldgS3p+BhRroUvJe\nA++x0veGHj4Eb3x7b/tRoPE2xDcH+N1GJfGm0NIvU8B6ZKKVjSPKYyraqdD2PhVm\n1JDyKtNv3KSGE7oWymjT\n=2GYH\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-30T07:33:01Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/85a9a154ca3d7ead8592e3dc7ad28d590200e026","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKDg1YTlhMTU0Y2EzZDdlYWQ4NTkyZTNkYzdhZDI4ZDU5MDIwMGUwMjY","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/457216145a308f739af4ab46dda0f14b8dee8de7","sha":"457216145a308f739af4ab46dda0f14b8dee8de7","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/457216145a308f739af4ab46dda0f14b8dee8de7"}],"sha":"85a9a154ca3d7ead8592e3dc7ad28d590200e026","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/85a9a154ca3d7ead8592e3dc7ad28d590200e026"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/457216145a308f739af4ab46dda0f14b8dee8de7/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-30T04:14:07Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-30T04:14:07Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Record what OpenFGA''s authentication actually allows (#17)\n\nADR 0013 left two questions open and one of them is now measured, so\nleaving it open would make the docs wrong rather than incomplete.\n\nThe trap it clears up first: authenticating to the OpenFGA API and being\nthe subject of an authorization check are two different identities. A\ntoken accepted at the door says nothing about whom the decision inside\nconcerns. Proven directly -- a caller authenticated from one realm ran a\ncheck about a subject from another, and it answered `allowed: true`.\n\nFor the control plane the answer is ADR 0012''s. OpenFGA validates `iss`,\n`aud`, signature and expiry, and its `subjects` list defaults to empty --\nits own help says every `sub` is then allowed -- so a person''s token is\nas acceptable as a machine''s. Fabric presents the operator''s own token\nand holds no standing credential. An audience mapper adds `openfga` to\n`aud`, which says explicitly that this credential may be presented there;\na shared service key would say nothing.\n\nThe constraint that stops this generalising is the part worth writing\ndown. One OpenFGA cannot trust more than one Keycloak realm: the issuer\nis a single value and is where keys are fetched from, and aliases accept\nother `iss` strings against that same key set. A token from a second\nrealm is refused even with its issuer listed as an alias.\n\nSo the runtime cannot forward a client''s realm token to a shared OpenFGA\n-- and does not need to, because the caller and the subject are\nindependent. Its own credential is left deliberately undecided: a tenant\nrequest arrives with no operator present, so there is no authority to\nborrow and whatever answers will be standing by necessity. That should be\nchosen on purpose rather than reached by assuming the control plane''s\nanswer carries over.\n\nAlso recorded: OpenFGA panics rather than starting when it cannot reach\nits issuer, which makes Keycloak a startup dependency; and it must never\nbe user-facing, because with an empty `subjects` list any correctly\nissued token is an accepted caller.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"006f62dbb212e961ab5d798609a98eb6c85492a9","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/006f62dbb212e961ab5d798609a98eb6c85492a9"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/457216145a308f739af4ab46dda0f14b8dee8de7","verification":{"payload":"tree 006f62dbb212e961ab5d798609a98eb6c85492a9\nparent e30e5e535a1486ca2b6a21088cad772eb5af6c2a\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1788063247 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788063247 +1200\n\nRecord what OpenFGA''s authentication actually allows (#17)\n\nADR 0013 left two questions open and one of them is now measured, so\nleaving it open would make the docs wrong rather than incomplete.\n\nThe trap it clears up first: authenticating to the OpenFGA API and being\nthe subject of an authorization check are two different identities. A\ntoken accepted at the door says nothing about whom the decision inside\nconcerns. Proven directly -- a caller authenticated from one realm ran a\ncheck about a subject from another, and it answered `allowed: true`.\n\nFor the control plane the answer is ADR 0012''s. OpenFGA validates `iss`,\n`aud`, signature and expiry, and its `subjects` list defaults to empty --\nits own help says every `sub` is then allowed -- so a person''s token is\nas acceptable as a machine''s. Fabric presents the operator''s own token\nand holds no standing credential. An audience mapper adds `openfga` to\n`aud`, which says explicitly that this credential may be presented there;\na shared service key would say nothing.\n\nThe constraint that stops this generalising is the part worth writing\ndown. One OpenFGA cannot trust more than one Keycloak realm: the issuer\nis a single value and is where keys are fetched from, and aliases accept\nother `iss` strings against that same key set. A token from a second\nrealm is refused even with its issuer listed as an alias.\n\nSo the runtime cannot forward a client''s realm token to a shared OpenFGA\n-- and does not need to, because the caller and the subject are\nindependent. Its own credential is left deliberately undecided: a tenant\nrequest arrives with no operator present, so there is no authority to\nborrow and whatever answers will be standing by necessity. That should be\nchosen on purpose rather than reached by assuming the control plane''s\nanswer carries over.\n\nAlso recorded: OpenFGA panics rather than starting when it cannot reach\nits issuer, which makes Keycloak a startup dependency; and it must never\nbe user-facing, because with an empty `subjects` list any correctly\nissued token is an accepted caller.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqk64PCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAALmkQAF9l6WgvvrbBUj9Vvs6DaGm3\nMXvu8VoRJ1OE6rGn20YyrMRD1C9tumtTp6JortX2+AYfIU1U98kqzlCh732cISEB\nb7Adymst/Du9WrWfwlFD+MEpmVl0uJYAkjUvRBrISSCv9o/3N27bNLdRbPi/q8gf\nYd8SsDcyEq2peXOIFVaRKilsE+Dbq1Fwl8fmtPjMh+TDGMRhUszoBPv3bFXQEUzV\n1nCucMdxH/EhqB0vvS3x8qDtq953uY2kozLopfo9NS3iY/Ia4tkm6LT9aH348mWu\nmijD85OeJCioglaHgOCzLj7NWnublanqgXXao60ETQX+4ci5YeSo++L1OUwfEOqq\nIrTCtT5Hds65mh71AlcwKcG6Q0kbMOnCwf+iHDyezS64nsixLEY6BiYnVoee6gFe\nx2QEO5NS39KABXCLTcUmwE9LTyXUTdOI387J+toWB1opSnqKqVPQWvcAo7BcrOSr\nZOh7bT8z62lyxqIaL++LhSlV0tgfaArPrHhjMO3c+MbJzU7HeOkNHT0O+L2MEV6Q\n1DqxAiGyGjkagcCtnujatMsA14L+lxsV1JvXdGeaDsrVxdsHzIR9wcQ9jv6EW9RA\nWj/cYeitTqfk80/d8h3OBjDidFjbIJqve6YAPShkgkJCy9QQnWZ1ppcRnJKxE5TV\nXXFcFLMWf3JF9LOum9Ez\n=T719\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-30T04:14:07Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/457216145a308f739af4ab46dda0f14b8dee8de7","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKDQ1NzIxNjE0NWEzMDhmNzM5YWY0YWI0NmRkYTBmMTRiOGRlZThkZTc","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/e30e5e535a1486ca2b6a21088cad772eb5af6c2a","sha":"e30e5e535a1486ca2b6a21088cad772eb5af6c2a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/e30e5e535a1486ca2b6a21088cad772eb5af6c2a"}],"sha":"457216145a308f739af4ab46dda0f14b8dee8de7","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/457216145a308f739af4ab46dda0f14b8dee8de7"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/e30e5e535a1486ca2b6a21088cad772eb5af6c2a/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-30T02:44:32Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-30T02:44:32Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Declare authorization in the platform''s words (#16)\n\nThe architecture has always named OpenFGA as the Authorization capability\nbeside Keycloak''s Identity, and always marked it not built. This settles\nthe part that is expensive to change later: the vocabulary a client\ndocument uses.\n\nA client declares resources, the relations that can be held on them, and\nthe operations each relation permits. It does not carry OpenFGA''s model\nDSL or its type-definition JSON. That shortcut would work immediately and\nwould make the document a thin wrapper over somebody else''s schema, which\nis the failure ADR 0008 and the NDC containment rule both exist to\nprevent -- `IdentityConfiguration` says realm and role, not\n`RealmRepresentation`, and this follows it.\n\nThree things fall out, and each is the point.\n\nThe model is desired state; the memberships are not. The document says a\n`customers` resource has an `editor` relation and what an editor may do.\nIt never says Alice is an editor: that is tenant data, it changes\nconstantly, and routing it through a Git commit and a reconciliation pass\nwould be both slow and wrong.\n\n`OperationKind` moves to `fabric-core`. The control plane now writes\ndocuments naming operations the runtime plane enforces, and the two may\nnot depend on each other. A vocabulary duplicated across that boundary\ndrifts silently -- one side writes `modify`, the other looks for\n`update`, and nothing fails until a caller is refused something an\noperator granted. `fabric-data-api` re-exports it, so no call site moves;\n`required_scope` becomes a free function there, because a scope name is\nthat API''s convention and not shared vocabulary.\n\nRelations are nouns, permissions are stated beside them. Widening what an\neditor may do should not require inventing a new word for an editor.\n\nNothing converges this yet, deliberately -- same order identity was built\nin. An absent or empty section reads as \"not managed here\" rather than\n\"deny everything\", so every document already in a repository still parses.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"5aa65e4166223330ec31eb063a2b1182d8494b3d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/5aa65e4166223330ec31eb063a2b1182d8494b3d"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/e30e5e535a1486ca2b6a21088cad772eb5af6c2a","verification":{"payload":"tree 5aa65e4166223330ec31eb063a2b1182d8494b3d\nparent 5707f5e725eb2179a255428bc8c08e22d0845f50\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1788057872 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788057872 +1200\n\nDeclare authorization in the platform''s words (#16)\n\nThe architecture has always named OpenFGA as the Authorization capability\nbeside Keycloak''s Identity, and always marked it not built. This settles\nthe part that is expensive to change later: the vocabulary a client\ndocument uses.\n\nA client declares resources, the relations that can be held on them, and\nthe operations each relation permits. It does not carry OpenFGA''s model\nDSL or its type-definition JSON. That shortcut would work immediately and\nwould make the document a thin wrapper over somebody else''s schema, which\nis the failure ADR 0008 and the NDC containment rule both exist to\nprevent -- `IdentityConfiguration` says realm and role, not\n`RealmRepresentation`, and this follows it.\n\nThree things fall out, and each is the point.\n\nThe model is desired state; the memberships are not. The document says a\n`customers` resource has an `editor` relation and what an editor may do.\nIt never says Alice is an editor: that is tenant data, it changes\nconstantly, and routing it through a Git commit and a reconciliation pass\nwould be both slow and wrong.\n\n`OperationKind` moves to `fabric-core`. The control plane now writes\ndocuments naming operations the runtime plane enforces, and the two may\nnot depend on each other. A vocabulary duplicated across that boundary\ndrifts silently -- one side writes `modify`, the other looks for\n`update`, and nothing fails until a caller is refused something an\noperator granted. `fabric-data-api` re-exports it, so no call site moves;\n`required_scope` becomes a free function there, because a scope name is\nthat API''s convention and not shared vocabulary.\n\nRelations are nouns, permissions are stated beside them. Widening what an\neditor may do should not require inventing a new word for an editor.\n\nNothing converges this yet, deliberately -- same order identity was built\nin. An absent or empty section reads as \"not managed here\" rather than\n\"deny everything\", so every document already in a repository still parses.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqk5kQCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAssEQAGDknMRcKXeGWQO8oLaQ+Yc7\n28Hc2OQ5qdbhbnx+68RF1y1p/2QhK8k5rxB3MgD5NSFRdwr+xMWvLzWFYF6V9CL2\ncRb8yIuAzVG3iILomzH9Z3+tUxcasztFPBF8T6uKhPqmpoRi4BMz6kuofBUNt0P+\nqwiHRxpcEALZjRD5qnV8YeOFGcPUXV6hdJYIzq+UMlrL6waP5AksvnkSusgnSp/+\nOzEq+t5jOPybtJzRtvGC5Or61aKyF/tot9p7o2L1FuCSWbPyafLXpXNo41R6ei0l\n/q3U8y1INfHin+wqmkCgNSQt9RkPyK/uuwoNXpoO4DWzMfWjsApXbXGxw6iDztkR\nm6TLI6kLva45PAqDgpqL99GbguMzUTdt53mLsXomFqTSBnzqhDUOhNC9MxYt5fKh\nJfgC6mrHWBdj1lsmAw9UivAqUJpORqTh2KOZGKboun5HnI3C9lE5ug9EeZv43JDb\nXBSU5H1K8EV9UhkxAi3jy+P2DIGBkJK29V0Zmkshu39Rk+bzOI/cAZe6NuPhH/y1\n/JBDkk3e8fW1Y225OHIp4gXF5ReQpOa0ECj1iaG9sRYn8/nwErxKmRufMS3iPUCO\n+OMUXaQuc+Vr/D7LEuHPNkbggoCLqXg6mmcaaAEI3LxICiaEUuf9jDTvaTc0P9h3\ne+WQmJiYEh4t9aKje4Qd\n=cyYr\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-30T02:44:33Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/e30e5e535a1486ca2b6a21088cad772eb5af6c2a","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKGUzMGU1ZTUzNWExNDg2Y2EyYjZhMjEwODhjYWQ3NzJlYjVhZjZjMmE","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/5707f5e725eb2179a255428bc8c08e22d0845f50","sha":"5707f5e725eb2179a255428bc8c08e22d0845f50","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/5707f5e725eb2179a255428bc8c08e22d0845f50"}],"sha":"e30e5e535a1486ca2b6a21088cad772eb5af6c2a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/e30e5e535a1486ca2b6a21088cad772eb5af6c2a"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/5707f5e725eb2179a255428bc8c08e22d0845f50/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-29T23:22:46Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-29T23:22:46Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Cut 0.2.2 (#15)\n\n0.2.1''s console starts every page load signed out and asks the operator\nto click through it -- a refresh, a new tab, and both round trips through\nGitHub. #14 makes the console ask the provider first; the fix is entirely\nin the bundle, so it changes nothing running until an image carries it.\n\nA patch release: no configuration schema change, no API change, and a\nlock diff that is version lines only.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"fb5178f936dbfbf734f4355722d6a9da7ca88647","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/fb5178f936dbfbf734f4355722d6a9da7ca88647"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/5707f5e725eb2179a255428bc8c08e22d0845f50","verification":{"payload":"tree fb5178f936dbfbf734f4355722d6a9da7ca88647\nparent b747aea10a060170c0bd7bed29930ade557ea79c\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1788045766 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788045766 +1200\n\nCut 0.2.2 (#15)\n\n0.2.1''s console starts every page load signed out and asks the operator\nto click through it -- a refresh, a new tab, and both round trips through\nGitHub. #14 makes the console ask the provider first; the fix is entirely\nin the bundle, so it changes nothing running until an image carries it.\n\nA patch release: no configuration schema change, no API change, and a\nlock diff that is version lines only.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqk2nGCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAT/4QABfDvBeTqwlH6e18weCSIIoK\n/t2baJLrN5Qob/wwaFpQeI7dyCOjy2F1rJy6bHERA7lvENvysyDaWPdKxPCgCwK4\nn3wYDFzko5Ps9XonRvQNFR+f8WkocGIbL8cp0KpRidS/6aW72wh6jbsr6wD/WLVy\nCP0hGmvOFi9ULbaX758OWmJncyWEqASXCF6DmTf5yDbpUyBcv8bZoEGb8NBRC2bW\nSJXCPR91NY3qU8md+mdEHk2z8ZAIorLjGtFyjbW301ywlQ8MO3PFCwG1ZHJjtD9c\nb9nCBANbumDJTPOkkk90aozvA7Ms5bTyoJDoC2fyRLkXMRB1v/j70VzRceY6mb0+\nlaQVaRyDvPcaVDt9h/EcsHWZUqSOdjRCihZVsVuUOEUnthfmIcmsV3gKm4GE5DAm\nYiAvrsl4VkuVQ6rF3BXjfOId435mCmZOma9W8hx8cM5y/Z8AhKThG6r+L6KLJ9a0\nsYvATr2I+KFc9mctQUtnOioPq+Zr2OxrC+3wcBwPSu1m47NgwrlIQPqRsx0YpvO0\nmO5xynr41543Vw41lGMBM4nZ2zTnGE7YqwlAEN+AR9YK34Aa8nMwpL5gjo5lkWT9\nO37JqoxMXDEucMnbuQtzUPy9bIGxM5z1zdt75DsUfvaRKPMFu8xWNuEe8MxlkvCq\nuxvGLtQEn475Gw6Z4VOi\n=/rkq\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-29T23:22:47Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/5707f5e725eb2179a255428bc8c08e22d0845f50","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKDU3MDdmNWU3MjVlYjIxNzlhMjU1NDI4YmM4YzA4ZTIyZDA4NDVmNTA","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/b747aea10a060170c0bd7bed29930ade557ea79c","sha":"b747aea10a060170c0bd7bed29930ade557ea79c","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/b747aea10a060170c0bd7bed29930ade557ea79c"}],"sha":"5707f5e725eb2179a255428bc8c08e22d0845f50","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/5707f5e725eb2179a255428bc8c08e22d0845f50"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/b747aea10a060170c0bd7bed29930ade557ea79c/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-29T23:18:51Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-29T23:18:51Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Sign the operator back in instead of asking them to click (#14)\n\n* Sign the operator back in instead of asking them to click\n\nThe token lives in memory, so every page load starts signed out: a\nrefresh, a new tab, and both round trips through GitHub that creating and\ninstalling the application require. `session.ts` said a reload \"costs one\nredirect that the provider''s own session makes invisible\" -- true of the\nredirect, false of the console, which rendered a button and waited for a\nclick that told the provider nothing it did not already know.\n\nSo the console now asks the provider before it asks the operator, with\n`prompt=none`: answer from the session you hold, and answer with an error\nrather than a login page if you cannot. Measured against the deployed\nrealm -- with no session it returns 302 to `?error=login_required`, never\na form -- which is what makes trying always safe.\n\nThe token stays in memory. The alternative was `sessionStorage`, which\nbuys the same result by putting a bearer where every script on the page\ncan read it, and a UX papercut is not worth that.\n\nIt cannot loop. An attempt is recorded before the browser leaves and\ncleared the moment an outcome is observed -- a code, an error, or a\nreturn carrying neither -- so at most one is ever in flight and the worst\ncase is one wasted redirect and the button. `login_required` and its\nsiblings show that button with no error text, because a provider with no\nsession is doing as it was told; anything else is surfaced.\n\nFour tests over the state machine, each shown to fail on its own\nmutation: dropping `prompt=none`, dropping the loop guard, and treating\nevery refusal as alarming each kill exactly one.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Stub location field by field, not by spreading a class instance\n\neslint''s no-misused-spread caught what running only build and tests did\nnot: `location` is a class instance, so spreading it copies the fields\nand loses the prototype. Spying on `assign` instead is not open either\n-- jsdom makes it non-configurable -- so the stub lists the three fields\nthe code under test actually reads.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"864b342c559a1fcb74da58fcf5e61fe3f1f8b350","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/864b342c559a1fcb74da58fcf5e61fe3f1f8b350"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/b747aea10a060170c0bd7bed29930ade557ea79c","verification":{"payload":"tree 864b342c559a1fcb74da58fcf5e61fe3f1f8b350\nparent 262877b796545d161f8065431e61aa58b1c2eb31\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1788045531 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788045531 +1200\n\nSign the operator back in instead of asking them to click (#14)\n\n* Sign the operator back in instead of asking them to click\n\nThe token lives in memory, so every page load starts signed out: a\nrefresh, a new tab, and both round trips through GitHub that creating and\ninstalling the application require. `session.ts` said a reload \"costs one\nredirect that the provider''s own session makes invisible\" -- true of the\nredirect, false of the console, which rendered a button and waited for a\nclick that told the provider nothing it did not already know.\n\nSo the console now asks the provider before it asks the operator, with\n`prompt=none`: answer from the session you hold, and answer with an error\nrather than a login page if you cannot. Measured against the deployed\nrealm -- with no session it returns 302 to `?error=login_required`, never\na form -- which is what makes trying always safe.\n\nThe token stays in memory. The alternative was `sessionStorage`, which\nbuys the same result by putting a bearer where every script on the page\ncan read it, and a UX papercut is not worth that.\n\nIt cannot loop. An attempt is recorded before the browser leaves and\ncleared the moment an outcome is observed -- a code, an error, or a\nreturn carrying neither -- so at most one is ever in flight and the worst\ncase is one wasted redirect and the button. `login_required` and its\nsiblings show that button with no error text, because a provider with no\nsession is doing as it was told; anything else is surfaced.\n\nFour tests over the state machine, each shown to fail on its own\nmutation: dropping `prompt=none`, dropping the loop guard, and treating\nevery refusal as alarming each kill exactly one.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Stub location field by field, not by spreading a class instance\n\neslint''s no-misused-spread caught what running only build and tests did\nnot: `location` is a class instance, so spreading it copies the fields\nand loses the prototype. Spying on `assign` instead is not open either\n-- jsdom makes it non-configurable -- so the stub lists the three fields\nthe code under test actually reads.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqk2jbCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA0sMQAIu9mJKLRnsWiVf3uDb+XzLA\nuUpg59+aJOAIOqNj4he5DuXJG+klCwfoz3YVqDZwzyx8sUU1NcyvB57V4tUD6PfC\nh0vjIhSxaUoAZI5VZWIkc68QAfsu2/Guovl28OIiFppxhRNIyzWgjXl4LWH/0r2r\nfiloge/oiq1/Nh/boCeImzk338UOKZ3DQsZfzalVjGwYt2chiNMNlanTG5K74jo/\nmeIo8sevWO5mSG+SLB5kNFA/HkKKP5IBEPIdVNR85xvkGj8NtsAq0HRSZ8tFvAvD\nU9VVEGy3Eu/PRb10gsoVWLwCdHdqfDzI3n0Jz1JZYReqxP3spGCBT5R2r+uSA0Q7\nJYvN4vF1/D1Zhkzx/ehhkFmBcuy84ZzrLNUCLv+lqRIWb5BiGtl9DnPbaqtdZyUX\nnfiMbPCcH3wJCJW340coIJeDExZ0BBMJAr2LxbbTrD40Uz4MU+nu4zLoFC6eb6Xe\nkadqQv0P7INoz5PHL55XZHCTnT8p2Tbi+8qtEp8BZNpp7CT9fbo8l2OxHJ5/inU2\n6RrBflh0OrRrImp3+g+m1C0qC9hoYIUHY91vKX+D6DHrlYbAlKA7v6RI9MhiFar5\nA7BJFv368smmTq4EVzYaDWEJrZjVw+biWgHQHN0zh/I5rObjgogr3ombQ/f5X+FM\n65istjhn2NdLXYpwXRQr\n=tiZK\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-29T23:18:52Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/b747aea10a060170c0bd7bed29930ade557ea79c","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKGI3NDdhZWExMGEwNjAxNzBjMGJkN2JlZDI5OTMwYWRlNTU3ZWE3OWM","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/262877b796545d161f8065431e61aa58b1c2eb31","sha":"262877b796545d161f8065431e61aa58b1c2eb31","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/262877b796545d161f8065431e61aa58b1c2eb31"}],"sha":"b747aea10a060170c0bd7bed29930ade557ea79c","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/b747aea10a060170c0bd7bed29930ade557ea79c"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/262877b796545d161f8065431e61aa58b1c2eb31/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-29T22:43:48Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-29T22:43:48Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Cut 0.2.1 (#13)\n\n0.2.0 serves a console whose Content-Security-Policy blocks the first\nstep of in-product installation. The policy is baked into the console\nimage at build time, so the fix in #12 changes nothing running until\nthere is an image carrying it.\n\nA patch release: no configuration schema change, no API change, and a\nlock diff that is version lines only.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"0fb4a45532735965b0a3be8477e583ef516ab37b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/0fb4a45532735965b0a3be8477e583ef516ab37b"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/262877b796545d161f8065431e61aa58b1c2eb31","verification":{"payload":"tree 0fb4a45532735965b0a3be8477e583ef516ab37b\nparent 6b909bba9661cb673c527ed635413a6c9c5eabc4\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1788043428 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788043428 +1200\n\nCut 0.2.1 (#13)\n\n0.2.0 serves a console whose Content-Security-Policy blocks the first\nstep of in-product installation. The policy is baked into the console\nimage at build time, so the fix in #12 changes nothing running until\nthere is an image carrying it.\n\nA patch release: no configuration schema change, no API change, and a\nlock diff that is version lines only.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqk2CkCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAG20QAIihSqmyyal8DivhR7FLh3in\nLBYKigfXya/HFvylhfahYX+CQAe2lkOcAiVZhiFWuKD61B/MObxH7UmkDFQpGho/\n9UVKqG3fx/cqa71gLdkyWfzJ2ZCXfaTHpV6Lf3GFTkSF170JtaMs0LInpZHMF17m\nKbb/+18NtVtRGSMI2FPiUDsSYK8Rg1e/N+Fgyq/g9XfNhffDgnPjVLvYPIWrVfDo\n+T0Us5ivoAxX3UCRD4Yp/C848H9YVIVB9pXIRyeV5rnOGsROMv8RQSL/jQuIFXbU\nSvxaHEp39Glf0B7513xN8Gu4tpiOlgPzRJCye0Zl0XuKvn1QjAsAw3kjAbpzUh4C\n86CcaW2GEuq66qHZJuWSCDGgU8b1cadxuKoQjr0l3HNsd++37fi9yaBbH+NyQOse\nQEHwj5phnmGbglTLy6jxY4OKYvwVX72n49I0rCTB5mZEka1nlbWzeCufhv4BHxSi\npV1TBBwG5wfF3NWlfLNm+yPLfuznuVesFUbTxt6YwJO2BZVt1Rhy8Pr3FAp2omNj\nIWO2752Gtj5vcWKSaGXmAhKgQAwq/AJhBs25NLDU2bIBoSs5aEFT9KE7AABj/XJI\njmfY1LQ5PL4TFHuz4g4T5RBGkxzNzRqqG/80/HeL2WRnBNuqtLeLYtT0bXNUpef1\ng4jzZKph2ianDGLZfycZ\n=MJk9\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-29T22:43:49Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/262877b796545d161f8065431e61aa58b1c2eb31","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKDI2Mjg3N2I3OTY1NDVkMTYxZjgwNjU0MzFlNjFhYTU4YjFjMmViMzE","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/6b909bba9661cb673c527ed635413a6c9c5eabc4","sha":"6b909bba9661cb673c527ed635413a6c9c5eabc4","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/6b909bba9661cb673c527ed635413a6c9c5eabc4"}],"sha":"262877b796545d161f8065431e61aa58b1c2eb31","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/262877b796545d161f8065431e61aa58b1c2eb31"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/6b909bba9661cb673c527ed635413a6c9c5eabc4/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-29T22:38:52Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-29T22:38:52Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Let the console post the App manifest to GitHub (#12)\n\nSigning in works, and the first thing an operator does next fails:\n\n    Sending form data to ''https://github.com/organizations/.../apps/new''\n    violates the following Content Security Policy directive:\n    \"form-action ''self''\". The request has been blocked.\n\nCreating the GitHub App is a cross-origin form POST, not a fetch. GitHub''s\nApp Manifest flow takes the manifest as POST data and navigates the\noperator to an approval screen, which is why `postManifest` builds a real\nform. `form-action ''self''` blocks that, so in-product installation stops\nat its first step -- and stops silently, with nothing but a console\nmessage the operator never sees.\n\nSo `form-action` gains one origin. It is the narrowest widening that\nmakes the flow work: one host, and only for submitting a form. Script,\nstyle, image and connect sources still fall through `default-src ''self''`,\nso the console still cannot load from GitHub or call it -- which is the\nsame rule `check_the_browser_gets_no_platform_credentials` already holds\nabout the UI''s source. The flow''s two other outbound steps are top-level\nnavigations, which `form-action` does not govern and which needed\nnothing.\n\nThe header had no test of any kind, which is how it survived the whole\nintegration being built against it. `check_the_console_widens_its_policy_\nonly_for_the_manifest_post` now holds both directions: github.com must be\npresent in `form-action`, because losing it breaks installation silently;\nand it must appear in no other directive, because permission to post a\nform is not permission to fetch from the Git host.\n\nVerified against the pinned nginx image (`nginx -t` passes), and the\ninvariant proved to fail three ways: form-action tightened back to\n''self'', github.com added to default-src, and the header removed.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"ef7c2dbdff2727fbbb5ff3d8929a70cf8e34a60e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/ef7c2dbdff2727fbbb5ff3d8929a70cf8e34a60e"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/6b909bba9661cb673c527ed635413a6c9c5eabc4","verification":{"payload":"tree ef7c2dbdff2727fbbb5ff3d8929a70cf8e34a60e\nparent 5383e4a22cc95a25392e508ae0629da7cad4b8c4\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1788043132 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788043132 +1200\n\nLet the console post the App manifest to GitHub (#12)\n\nSigning in works, and the first thing an operator does next fails:\n\n    Sending form data to ''https://github.com/organizations/.../apps/new''\n    violates the following Content Security Policy directive:\n    \"form-action ''self''\". The request has been blocked.\n\nCreating the GitHub App is a cross-origin form POST, not a fetch. GitHub''s\nApp Manifest flow takes the manifest as POST data and navigates the\noperator to an approval screen, which is why `postManifest` builds a real\nform. `form-action ''self''` blocks that, so in-product installation stops\nat its first step -- and stops silently, with nothing but a console\nmessage the operator never sees.\n\nSo `form-action` gains one origin. It is the narrowest widening that\nmakes the flow work: one host, and only for submitting a form. Script,\nstyle, image and connect sources still fall through `default-src ''self''`,\nso the console still cannot load from GitHub or call it -- which is the\nsame rule `check_the_browser_gets_no_platform_credentials` already holds\nabout the UI''s source. The flow''s two other outbound steps are top-level\nnavigations, which `form-action` does not govern and which needed\nnothing.\n\nThe header had no test of any kind, which is how it survived the whole\nintegration being built against it. `check_the_console_widens_its_policy_\nonly_for_the_manifest_post` now holds both directions: github.com must be\npresent in `form-action`, because losing it breaks installation silently;\nand it must appear in no other directive, because permission to post a\nform is not permission to fetch from the Git host.\n\nVerified against the pinned nginx image (`nginx -t` passes), and the\ninvariant proved to fail three ways: form-action tightened back to\n''self'', github.com added to default-src, and the header removed.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqk198CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAGk8QADVz427/mKEC2isKKqzcU8cv\nXPuqtW1AtoTTDmF3Cl7tIXBhtm1/kN9BgCVRCCqO17cpTi9XZzK7xWjEdqOhqVb8\n9dRsfVCoAP0RnkgJnfXioGKBZ/lKfrNBDoyZyMzD1YGJvhEvhlMbVgK5/d2eYcJn\nMNUUmVBE3Wtd6bkFJTIA7rAV4GB/9e+HvHwAdpGzE/ZpqLy3I3F7OW8of4LjL0tV\na1uVdVJVK4M+To5H0/xPqzpPPU+y8/rXLHD+1W/HVpquv+W0v3UIPfli5VUFAxxu\nbiYi8ihaknaIOsvctAi9URceLJBQN8NdEl/2NArp4wY8nuVUn2IkCGnJDlVrGKuc\nVjtprj+0EPOIfyZU+UJDC/5PF3OUgGTB/VdgbXpho0Y03LzhvAxP4vTBE1rkenxx\n2PyFa46ScvMSvp64gOXM8Qpsxuutg4UR+zBI2xahvSYVRIJ06WGNA+SlgY6zrvIo\n6bznRgN8J952Qu13HLwTPabGVyXg+9MLG+CY8/dMyfzG9GCgH3OeBhGyTDyFadcR\nJ6q0rYjHAWArG9YaYhWAX9EwpqcaHSrdV3UnwCpfZyZMuF5lQp3JF8tZg+nuhGaS\nVRFZOtXdHv9jsOFcNcMQPxX/1fBfMZR91/VIPyO4TY9BBdr5LNAMLr1acLEs8WeM\nvZd+0Tu1GSk6aefCno1Q\n=9w2h\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-29T22:38:52Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/6b909bba9661cb673c527ed635413a6c9c5eabc4","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKDZiOTA5YmJhOTY2MWNiNjczYzUyN2VkNjM1NDEzYTZjOWM1ZWFiYzQ","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/5383e4a22cc95a25392e508ae0629da7cad4b8c4","sha":"5383e4a22cc95a25392e508ae0629da7cad4b8c4","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/5383e4a22cc95a25392e508ae0629da7cad4b8c4"}],"sha":"6b909bba9661cb673c527ed635413a6c9c5eabc4","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/6b909bba9661cb673c527ed635413a6c9c5eabc4"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/5383e4a22cc95a25392e508ae0629da7cad4b8c4/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-29T07:12:46Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-29T07:12:46Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Cut 0.2.0 (#11)\n\n0.1.0 predates the whole credential-ownership change. Its configuration\nschema still has `client_secret_ref`, a trusted-header operator posture,\nand a stated Git repository -- so it cannot parse the configuration the\nplatform now ships, and `deny_unknown_fields` turns that into a refusal\nto start rather than a quiet default.\n\nCaught before Argo created the Application: the deployment pins an image\ntag, and the tag it pinned was five merges behind the config beside it.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"04bb609a2f1ff411ea48f7bf6212923945726eb6","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/04bb609a2f1ff411ea48f7bf6212923945726eb6"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/5383e4a22cc95a25392e508ae0629da7cad4b8c4","verification":{"payload":"tree 04bb609a2f1ff411ea48f7bf6212923945726eb6\nparent 1860e4d4a7142cbcc77b4f5e19ca2f122619c910\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1787987566 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1787987566 +1200\n\nCut 0.2.0 (#11)\n\n0.1.0 predates the whole credential-ownership change. Its configuration\nschema still has `client_secret_ref`, a trusted-header operator posture,\nand a stated Git repository -- so it cannot parse the configuration the\nplatform now ships, and `deny_unknown_fields` turns that into a refusal\nto start rather than a quiet default.\n\nCaught before Argo created the Application: the deployment pins an image\ntag, and the tag it pinned was five merges behind the config beside it.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqkoZuCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA64IQACAmmsjIsiPgcl9lDJB+99Tq\nd+lzrQZ8SMxWdDHWz4p2KZhyxKWBdcQwukVavPfFFYKCwnTWCjjVzJSqObKHmOzZ\ny3/U24HYimib8/GpD6d6D0sbbbj9msQ0c4tQEhXPZapiX1pIfqo1naJ7wTPlWmdD\n+NznZUvVMw3sDpBxRYxl/GgdiCuc/5m+m/s/AP52AQ9arBTT7BWQDeJZ4iDqdmF1\nmmoEM0MGUocIAEy1Y5v2gk9hB2GNgUluBBZrwJ5n7gA0uxSRdzQ3k14BJPE4Bp+k\n3lOu1d8mhK4cKiyJFAmPs0T5A4NVFYHg0X3VrK80ewJzILkEB4GtGHbL3+Yth2j2\nY/i31Eyjsf2WvPLoEB/vfjPdq5jkj969bQnV24gKnxDGYLwiOEo9RPMaOSL3vXT9\nF+pZYDwOQz6KUpd/6wmwT+T+BNxHxcnlznbcXzMTPESD9C7KIP/jkU7Hq+7Sj19Y\nbX73yVGneoP8gkQJ64PTWcSYoZKda8kj6AOfyqSTkFLZbKoZuDWSwUHEcyBtVoeq\nDtBiZWhpCKn0ch7piVLW+DreC6eEaErRlUtc5U8ZJWF2dR69K2Xk5ZmgZKBKt5m7\n0niCKAE/hkx460Qfn+kOCV+JiXgQnmiDjv0KZagO0t5T4UiyTDALHRt9dFrUGaLG\ntYusgZBg3/x9B9Ru3nEh\n=9z1b\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-29T07:12:46Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/5383e4a22cc95a25392e508ae0629da7cad4b8c4","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKDUzODNlNGEyMmNjOTVhMjUzOTJlNTA4YWUwNjI5ZGE3Y2FkNGI4YzQ","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/1860e4d4a7142cbcc77b4f5e19ca2f122619c910","sha":"1860e4d4a7142cbcc77b4f5e19ca2f122619c910","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/1860e4d4a7142cbcc77b4f5e19ca2f122619c910"}],"sha":"5383e4a22cc95a25392e508ae0629da7cad4b8c4","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/5383e4a22cc95a25392e508ae0629da7cad4b8c4"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/1860e4d4a7142cbcc77b4f5e19ca2f122619c910/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-29T07:03:50Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-29T07:03:50Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Tell a realm''s issuer apart from the address this pod can reach (#10)\n\nA realm''s issuer is what appears in its tokens and where a browser is\nsent. Where *this process* reaches the same realm can be a different\naddress entirely, and on a cluster it usually is: the issuer is a public\nhostname resolved outside, and the pod has a service address resolved\ninside.\n\nFound while preparing the LucentRoot deployment, which is exactly the\ncase: Keycloak''s public hostname is auth.lucentroot.internal, nothing in\nthe cluster resolves it, and the signing keys were about to be fetched\nfrom there.\n\nCollapsing the two fails in a way that reads as something else. Use the\nissuer for everything and the pod fetches no keys, so every operator is\nrefused and the log says only that no key set arrived. Use the internal\naddress for everything and the `iss` in a real token never matches, so\nevery operator is refused and the log says the token is not from this\nrealm. Both are hours spent looking in the wrong place.\n\n`reachable_at` defaults to the issuer, because most deployments do have\none URL. Each is still derived into its endpoints rather than stated, so\na deployment cannot supply URLs that disagree with each other.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"eac5e298b2424ae84e99c520d7a55a98ee586923","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/eac5e298b2424ae84e99c520d7a55a98ee586923"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/1860e4d4a7142cbcc77b4f5e19ca2f122619c910","verification":{"payload":"tree eac5e298b2424ae84e99c520d7a55a98ee586923\nparent 712a3966ce4670df1b780b7980fb584c34c66bf2\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1787987030 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1787987030 +1200\n\nTell a realm''s issuer apart from the address this pod can reach (#10)\n\nA realm''s issuer is what appears in its tokens and where a browser is\nsent. Where *this process* reaches the same realm can be a different\naddress entirely, and on a cluster it usually is: the issuer is a public\nhostname resolved outside, and the pod has a service address resolved\ninside.\n\nFound while preparing the LucentRoot deployment, which is exactly the\ncase: Keycloak''s public hostname is auth.lucentroot.internal, nothing in\nthe cluster resolves it, and the signing keys were about to be fetched\nfrom there.\n\nCollapsing the two fails in a way that reads as something else. Use the\nissuer for everything and the pod fetches no keys, so every operator is\nrefused and the log says only that no key set arrived. Use the internal\naddress for everything and the `iss` in a real token never matches, so\nevery operator is refused and the log says the token is not from this\nrealm. Both are hours spent looking in the wrong place.\n\n`reachable_at` defaults to the issuer, because most deployments do have\none URL. Each is still derived into its endpoints rather than stated, so\na deployment cannot supply URLs that disagree with each other.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqkoRWCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAYRIQAK11Uw5D2V4L7hb7VHuThA6C\nzmdZW7Fahjgu6FsRU1sKo47I193FcrlIDCmCAcu6hgcULDzWswH3SbGIiC1r12ud\nH1NOXdzOz/H6vS8BveKH0pWogTrR4uhC/4eOFETgb2jF3k8SWXUFox84BRHEUPmq\n1yp9EBXekF3AgNhpL8kvOU1xcX6RW393rRwyIWCKGDkuQq+++/HbePk34S5YaBt9\n/b/WMoZuu5bJqTTzrqTlHD0BkAsgCaGD1Unz0zoVWjTqMFevcnlh43m8xQwzaxnx\ngp+/1bZHSH05uKRxdnigF6tXkAUpMFGkxI5COnKDkB0LJwZ/yac6Fn/TCId9Fnav\nSexqNHaBDWGJ98+UpXllObcEBzD8NlW+j5mCXe6x95pIF33atLaYyjhr9KrV3X7Z\nxasJK3yRsI/+PsUo6WHc5RB8BcNQ5PewPWcRf2V2YHYaO2251iF9b6Kijxxry2X3\nXtmbv5WiLKd3k6oFEuke76nkr4IGFKMRZDnM1d2auN+rkB0IQhR0LHxxJAYlunDU\nkmvKh7vAgMVfJkfTYk+/yNDUNHWwj3JdpjEAoiaCOQRyV3+uknTyYdbIHZTtlEKX\ndmwcTC1IdmTTQV9HQ9OEIZbtmtyOyzTHOnjwZ5MxF7XTrAa4oL7Vxdk9ToigAnKx\nWCCmcoII6qHXRzd31iLR\n=fUT4\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-29T07:03:51Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/1860e4d4a7142cbcc77b4f5e19ca2f122619c910","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKDE4NjBlNGQ0YTcxNDJjYmNjNzdiNGY1ZTE5Y2EyZjEyMjYxOWM5MTA","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/712a3966ce4670df1b780b7980fb584c34c66bf2","sha":"712a3966ce4670df1b780b7980fb584c34c66bf2","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/712a3966ce4670df1b780b7980fb584c34c66bf2"}],"sha":"1860e4d4a7142cbcc77b4f5e19ca2f122619c910","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/1860e4d4a7142cbcc77b4f5e19ca2f122619c910"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/712a3966ce4670df1b780b7980fb584c34c66bf2/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-29T05:33:01Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-29T05:33:01Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Act on Keycloak as the operator, not as a service account (#9)\n\nTwo things point at Keycloak and conflating them is easy. Operator\nsign-in is Keycloak guarding access *to* Fabric, and ADR 0010 settled\nit. This is the other direction: Fabric''s authority *over* Keycloak,\nwhich was still a standing machine credential -- a confidential client\nwhose service account held create-realm, delivered by External Secrets\nand exchanged for an admin token with client_credentials.\n\nThat credential could create a realm at three in the morning with\nnobody having asked. Its authority was the platform''s own and\nindependent of any human''s, which made \"who decided this?\" a question\nthe platform could not answer about its most consequential action.\n\nThe platform now holds no authority over Keycloak at all. A provider is\nbuilt per operator from the bearer they presented, so permission to\ncreate a realm belongs to a person in the master realm where Keycloak''s\nown RBAC already governs it. AdminCredential, the token cache and the\nclient_credentials exchange are deleted, and KeycloakConfig has no\ncredential field.\n\nThe trusted-header posture is removed rather than kept for development.\nIt was safe only because of where the service sat, with nothing in the\napplication enforcing that -- and it asserted a name while lending\nnothing, so an operator established by a proxy could not authorise a\nrealm and half the control plane would not work. A development posture\nthat cannot do what production does hides the failures worth finding\nearly. Local development needs a Keycloak now, and the example says so.\n\nTwo consequences worth reading rather than skimming.\n\nThere is no unattended convergence. The interval loop is gone, because\nthere is no authority available when nobody is there; convergence\nhappens on a write and on demand through POST /api/reconciliation, and\nthe console grows a \"check for drift\" button. That is a real loss. It\nis acceptable because Keycloak''s console is published on no plane, so\nchanges outside SaaS Fabric are largely prevented rather than merely\nnoticed -- and because drift found when somebody is present is drift\nsomebody can act on.\n\nAn operator needs more than create-realm. Creating a realm causes\nKeycloak to grant the creator that realm''s admin roles into tokens\nminted *afterwards*. A service account minted a fresh one, which is why\nthe adapter''s invalidate-and-retry existed. A borrowed token cannot be\nre-minted, so an operator holding create-realm alone will create a\nrealm and then be refused on the first role inside it, with a token\nthat is valid and simply too old. Their authority has to already cover\nrealms that do not exist yet; master-realm admin does. The retry is\ndeleted rather than adapted, because there is nothing to retry with.\n\nThe workspace test count falls from 1262 to 1252, which is the change\nreporting itself: tests for a mechanism that no longer exists are not\ncoverage. Four were rewritten rather than dropped, because what they\nprotected still holds -- every request carries the operator''s own\nbearer, nothing is ever exchanged for a token, a refusal is reported in\nexactly one attempt, and a request establishing no operator is refused.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"18c7f083f3c1d8670ce98d564310756f30d9d9bb","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/18c7f083f3c1d8670ce98d564310756f30d9d9bb"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/712a3966ce4670df1b780b7980fb584c34c66bf2","verification":{"payload":"tree 18c7f083f3c1d8670ce98d564310756f30d9d9bb\nparent 7ba00d999cc7e7e950665461910a28fab7422a23\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1787981581 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1787981581 +1200\n\nAct on Keycloak as the operator, not as a service account (#9)\n\nTwo things point at Keycloak and conflating them is easy. Operator\nsign-in is Keycloak guarding access *to* Fabric, and ADR 0010 settled\nit. This is the other direction: Fabric''s authority *over* Keycloak,\nwhich was still a standing machine credential -- a confidential client\nwhose service account held create-realm, delivered by External Secrets\nand exchanged for an admin token with client_credentials.\n\nThat credential could create a realm at three in the morning with\nnobody having asked. Its authority was the platform''s own and\nindependent of any human''s, which made \"who decided this?\" a question\nthe platform could not answer about its most consequential action.\n\nThe platform now holds no authority over Keycloak at all. A provider is\nbuilt per operator from the bearer they presented, so permission to\ncreate a realm belongs to a person in the master realm where Keycloak''s\nown RBAC already governs it. AdminCredential, the token cache and the\nclient_credentials exchange are deleted, and KeycloakConfig has no\ncredential field.\n\nThe trusted-header posture is removed rather than kept for development.\nIt was safe only because of where the service sat, with nothing in the\napplication enforcing that -- and it asserted a name while lending\nnothing, so an operator established by a proxy could not authorise a\nrealm and half the control plane would not work. A development posture\nthat cannot do what production does hides the failures worth finding\nearly. Local development needs a Keycloak now, and the example says so.\n\nTwo consequences worth reading rather than skimming.\n\nThere is no unattended convergence. The interval loop is gone, because\nthere is no authority available when nobody is there; convergence\nhappens on a write and on demand through POST /api/reconciliation, and\nthe console grows a \"check for drift\" button. That is a real loss. It\nis acceptable because Keycloak''s console is published on no plane, so\nchanges outside SaaS Fabric are largely prevented rather than merely\nnoticed -- and because drift found when somebody is present is drift\nsomebody can act on.\n\nAn operator needs more than create-realm. Creating a realm causes\nKeycloak to grant the creator that realm''s admin roles into tokens\nminted *afterwards*. A service account minted a fresh one, which is why\nthe adapter''s invalidate-and-retry existed. A borrowed token cannot be\nre-minted, so an operator holding create-realm alone will create a\nrealm and then be refused on the first role inside it, with a token\nthat is valid and simply too old. Their authority has to already cover\nrealms that do not exist yet; master-realm admin does. The retry is\ndeleted rather than adapted, because there is nothing to retry with.\n\nThe workspace test count falls from 1262 to 1252, which is the change\nreporting itself: tests for a mechanism that no longer exists are not\ncoverage. Four were rewritten rather than dropped, because what they\nprotected still holds -- every request carries the operator''s own\nbearer, nothing is ever exchanged for a token, a refusal is reported in\nexactly one attempt, and a request establishing no operator is refused.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqkm8NCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAc5cQAFCOYjFHkUotomxGnV8EltDH\n4qQAQRDT3BW4RO/XlWLYFL27+0BzbxcZOF/nYNNkDr/yRAx8dATACUrl4bedNVdG\nATX+PBUhmEumifyQrk5gSeMHRTA/nolICTIgw16+W2pAyfdxOHVaGr+8avd2XrK2\n/lELb4puf699GV+rRPP9wxIyblT0TAYqqvp/tmCGkdDsjsrjf8xdG7sln2bF8NcK\nHsmLpl0vgnmhlO0iO6WH6BWIEtgJ9auAGuByBopcVOj+FwsIBealA8ZYrD28cFgN\nP51MkXjxFY5OZwueeH3PTBG/1N58yA9D9LHgHEeSJBiYWFA+dJ9ksC+0wj+iCgFz\nUW7u7o5BB92/EBMGseBXRS+KaI9ect1uRSkN6TPYsqAExKPwM7sGCGzw+36rnEgU\n6ihoh34W6nb9452dRcC0APSPREz2133RwtdJ4V9J1d8YQ7IMxK4k90lI7mjQOVyu\n3YRt2FHE6LtvDOyhXx7KP/pDABQwStA0K244Ew98+V8nH6yrzdLjsYa/CjjLtpRQ\n0NnWx9W/x74yORm0HDaKaZSAbGPog2q/PTOYCKZrYZmG4djHYrGl0yoGzzVmOCNL\n6muYKIQlrSOm2CIAx5yBnoUOlSrC8NhqjfySV/I0++UcYMOO24Gsv0VxGTFCmu2e\nWZJUYWGrXvOSb5hBapWS\n=EuRi\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-29T05:33:02Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/712a3966ce4670df1b780b7980fb584c34c66bf2","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKDcxMmEzOTY2Y2U0NjcwZGYxYjc4MGI3OTgwZmI1ODRjMzRjNjZiZjI","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/7ba00d999cc7e7e950665461910a28fab7422a23","sha":"7ba00d999cc7e7e950665461910a28fab7422a23","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/7ba00d999cc7e7e950665461910a28fab7422a23"}],"sha":"712a3966ce4670df1b780b7980fb584c34c66bf2","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/712a3966ce4670df1b780b7980fb584c34c66bf2"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/7ba00d999cc7e7e950665461910a28fab7422a23/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-29T02:38:49Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-29T02:38:49Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Create the platform''s Git application from inside the product (#8)\n\nReaching client desired state needed a GitHub App, and getting one meant\na human creating it by hand, copying its private key into a secret\nstore, and writing two identifiers into a deployment file before the\nprocess would start. That made onboarding somebody else''s job, made a\nprivate key a thing people handled, and left the console -- the one tool\nfor fixing a broken integration -- unable to start for exactly the\nreason it was needed.\n\nAn authorised operator now establishes the integration from inside the\nproduct. GitHub''s App Manifest flow is what makes it possible: the\nplatform describes the application it wants, the operator approves it in\ntheir organisation, and the host hands back the identity including a\nprivate key that arrives exactly once.\n\nThree things are taken from Workspec deliberately -- the application is\ncreated rather than configured, state is derived from ground truth\nrather than advanced by a stored flag, and nothing is recorded before it\nis proven. The last one is the ordering this change turns on:\n\n  - the private key is stored *before* the record, because a record\n    without its key describes an application this platform can never\n    authenticate as, and no retry could fix it since the host will not\n    hand the key over twice;\n  - an installation is recorded only after a token has been minted for\n    it, so a recorded installation always means a working one and no\n    separate verified flag can drift.\n\nFour places diverge from Workspec, each forced by something real:\n\n  - No webhooks. The control plane is on the operator plane and on no\n    public one, so GitHub cannot reach it. The platform is therefore not\n    told when an installation is revoked and finds out on its next sweep\n    -- which is why `invalid` is a status separate from `error`.\n  - Correlation is server-side and genuinely single-use. Workspec signs\n    a stateless blob, which is right for many processes and has a cost\n    it does not hide: nothing is consumed, so a captured state is\n    replayable while valid. One control-plane process can do better. The\n    trade is that an in-flight flow does not survive a restart, which\n    means \"start again\" and cannot produce a wrong outcome.\n  - Credentials go to the instance''s secret partition rather than an\n    encrypted column, because this platform has no database. Two ports,\n    implemented by fabric-openbao -- the only crate that knows OpenBao\n    exists. It authenticates with the pod''s own Kubernetes identity, so\n    there is still no credential for anybody to create or transport.\n  - No connect-existing path. It reintroduces pasting a private key into\n    a browser form, which is the posture this removes.\n\nThe platform declines to guess a repository. One reachable repository is\nadopted; several are reported as undecided and the operator chooses,\nbecause guessing would write client configuration somewhere nobody\nexpects and it would look like it worked.\n\nDisconnecting removes the record, the key and the binding. It does not\nuninstall or delete the application: doing that from a console button\nwould be considerably more than the button said.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"9e363d7754fcba0b16a473237031ebaa56fc0058","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/9e363d7754fcba0b16a473237031ebaa56fc0058"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/7ba00d999cc7e7e950665461910a28fab7422a23","verification":{"payload":"tree 9e363d7754fcba0b16a473237031ebaa56fc0058\nparent 193a35291d7688df3eb4fc939b01dc4bd9e798bc\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1787971129 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1787971129 +1200\n\nCreate the platform''s Git application from inside the product (#8)\n\nReaching client desired state needed a GitHub App, and getting one meant\na human creating it by hand, copying its private key into a secret\nstore, and writing two identifiers into a deployment file before the\nprocess would start. That made onboarding somebody else''s job, made a\nprivate key a thing people handled, and left the console -- the one tool\nfor fixing a broken integration -- unable to start for exactly the\nreason it was needed.\n\nAn authorised operator now establishes the integration from inside the\nproduct. GitHub''s App Manifest flow is what makes it possible: the\nplatform describes the application it wants, the operator approves it in\ntheir organisation, and the host hands back the identity including a\nprivate key that arrives exactly once.\n\nThree things are taken from Workspec deliberately -- the application is\ncreated rather than configured, state is derived from ground truth\nrather than advanced by a stored flag, and nothing is recorded before it\nis proven. The last one is the ordering this change turns on:\n\n  - the private key is stored *before* the record, because a record\n    without its key describes an application this platform can never\n    authenticate as, and no retry could fix it since the host will not\n    hand the key over twice;\n  - an installation is recorded only after a token has been minted for\n    it, so a recorded installation always means a working one and no\n    separate verified flag can drift.\n\nFour places diverge from Workspec, each forced by something real:\n\n  - No webhooks. The control plane is on the operator plane and on no\n    public one, so GitHub cannot reach it. The platform is therefore not\n    told when an installation is revoked and finds out on its next sweep\n    -- which is why `invalid` is a status separate from `error`.\n  - Correlation is server-side and genuinely single-use. Workspec signs\n    a stateless blob, which is right for many processes and has a cost\n    it does not hide: nothing is consumed, so a captured state is\n    replayable while valid. One control-plane process can do better. The\n    trade is that an in-flight flow does not survive a restart, which\n    means \"start again\" and cannot produce a wrong outcome.\n  - Credentials go to the instance''s secret partition rather than an\n    encrypted column, because this platform has no database. Two ports,\n    implemented by fabric-openbao -- the only crate that knows OpenBao\n    exists. It authenticates with the pod''s own Kubernetes identity, so\n    there is still no credential for anybody to create or transport.\n  - No connect-existing path. It reintroduces pasting a private key into\n    a browser form, which is the posture this removes.\n\nThe platform declines to guess a repository. One reachable repository is\nadopted; several are reported as undecided and the operator chooses,\nbecause guessing would write client configuration somewhere nobody\nexpects and it would look like it worked.\n\nDisconnecting removes the record, the key and the binding. It does not\nuninstall or delete the application: doing that from a console button\nwould be considerably more than the button said.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqkkY5CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAO7QQAIY/YKfEK+8nSwmaFfb44K5o\nlWAJ52Niw+zGOS9izV1Q4LljxgmH0TLJj6vpV740ycYD4ZE23mLBNuXUQILPYIvk\nH1WZCfN3hwm6d1zha9GeeX01Ir9CG1saYozUUTaZzOffiAiYvyGy0Ktg3e3vyJnE\nXsPum5+iVgFF7fmsQGjyTyUr26BjWWa8vqK/dRn+tBh0qpdf0JnnAimPkyqcrkvL\nQCESe4UN1mxq1OMAWNTGtjZs3rSFANgn4BwdINMiV6jIXsr8rz8O6b1RbQzQErvY\nPCwONFSAc7HvnZ672vdVaFEg2DVWzusz8lfKfnmBThzNz20+M4+s7wOtwaoZSSCH\nvxRrtVIY5IKBeEA5IRnoe1dWc4z61NKGfJf+3h4cHj6lc9M9t6LBxdc3uwyWgBQn\nwpEm36GJaZ2+SOIcMqPAoTI7HpH9xC67xj3x3ypqFj5ADF24DQ8M18DLHi4yKdAT\nKoX1XTVPDUwVnEWRQFkVcTTsFk0cq7/+tqfAMPb3Xz6IR6AM90W3Onw30iXPstkw\n6POzZuGbxW1imIFa2x3Q2CAu5MWg8KjnS3l15H30iqFgzq6O/tkvWO3XIWBdM/2b\nnqPwcovTngg2sEAtp4ZZdpFOa+StIdg0jaouQgx11UO+LgkYHTjUL8Si+qJf2TqX\n/F1F5C0a5nwLIBDuy4yz\n=91Iu\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-29T02:38:49Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/7ba00d999cc7e7e950665461910a28fab7422a23","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKDdiYTAwZDk5OWNjN2U3ZTk1MDY2NTQ2MTkxMGEyOGZhYjc0MjJhMjM","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/193a35291d7688df3eb4fc939b01dc4bd9e798bc","sha":"193a35291d7688df3eb4fc939b01dc4bd9e798bc","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/193a35291d7688df3eb4fc939b01dc4bd9e798bc"}],"sha":"7ba00d999cc7e7e950665461910a28fab7422a23","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/7ba00d999cc7e7e950665461910a28fab7422a23"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/193a35291d7688df3eb4fc939b01dc4bd9e798bc/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-29T01:23:05Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-29T01:23:05Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Let the control plane start with nothing connected (#7)\n\nThe control plane built its desired-state repository at startup and\ntreated every failure as fatal. That made the platform''s own onboarding\nsomebody else''s problem: a deployment had to hold a Git host''s\nidentifiers and a credential a human created by hand before the process\nwould start at all -- and the console, the one tool for fixing that, was\nthe thing that could not start.\n\nDesired state is now late-bound. `mode = \"managed\"` is the production\nmode and states nothing else: the platform runs, reports itself\nunconfigured, and an operator connects a repository through the console.\n`mode = \"git\"` remains for a deployment that genuinely wants to state\nwhere desired state lives, and stating it wrongly is still fatal --\nopting out of the managed path is opting into being checked.\n\n\"Not configured\" is a ClientRepository implementation rather than an\nOption threaded through the service, the loop and every handler. Every\ncaller keeps one code path and the state arrives as an ordinary\nRepositoryError, so it is impossible to forget to handle. The Option\nlives inside the binding alone, where `is_configured` can still answer\nthe one question `current` cannot -- and that question matters, because\n\"nobody has connected this\" and \"the repository is refusing us\" send an\noperator somewhere completely different.\n\nListing clients on an unconnected platform therefore answers 503\n`integration_not_configured` rather than a 500 or an empty list. It\ncarries no Retry-After: retrying will not connect it. The two failures\nthat share 503 do carry one, so that header is now decided by the error\nrather than by the status.\n\nGET /api/integrations/git reports the connection, derived from ground\ntruth rather than advanced by a stored flag -- whether anything is bound,\nand what the last sweep observed. `invalid` is separated from `error`\nbecause only one is fixed by reconnecting, and a revoked installation is\nexactly the case this platform cannot be told about: the operator plane\nis a tailnet with no inbound path from a Git host, so there is no webhook\nand it has to notice for itself. The response carries status, a\ndescription and a last-success time -- no credential, no reference to\none, no path.\n\nA sweep with nothing configured logs at debug, not error. Filling the\nlog of a healthy platform that is simply waiting for an operator would\ntrain whoever reads it to ignore the level that matters.\n\nThe console asks about the integration before the client list, because\nit decides what to show. \"Nobody has connected this yet\" is not rendered\nas \"could not load clients\", and the list''s own failure is suppressed\nwhen it would be the same fact reported twice.\n\nScope note: the write-capable secret capability and the integration\nstate store were in this PR''s plan and have moved to the flow that\npopulates them. Landing them here would add two ports and an adapter\nthat nothing calls, and this codebase is explicit that an unused\ncapability suggests the control plane can already do something it\ncannot. The record shape is also much easier to get right with the\nmanifest conversion in hand than guessed ahead of it.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"ff6c1a72752515e545864277428af647051e8349","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/ff6c1a72752515e545864277428af647051e8349"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/193a35291d7688df3eb4fc939b01dc4bd9e798bc","verification":{"payload":"tree ff6c1a72752515e545864277428af647051e8349\nparent dd18a8cceffea50cbe6f466a19a49c5e5a373a92\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1787966585 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1787966585 +1200\n\nLet the control plane start with nothing connected (#7)\n\nThe control plane built its desired-state repository at startup and\ntreated every failure as fatal. That made the platform''s own onboarding\nsomebody else''s problem: a deployment had to hold a Git host''s\nidentifiers and a credential a human created by hand before the process\nwould start at all -- and the console, the one tool for fixing that, was\nthe thing that could not start.\n\nDesired state is now late-bound. `mode = \"managed\"` is the production\nmode and states nothing else: the platform runs, reports itself\nunconfigured, and an operator connects a repository through the console.\n`mode = \"git\"` remains for a deployment that genuinely wants to state\nwhere desired state lives, and stating it wrongly is still fatal --\nopting out of the managed path is opting into being checked.\n\n\"Not configured\" is a ClientRepository implementation rather than an\nOption threaded through the service, the loop and every handler. Every\ncaller keeps one code path and the state arrives as an ordinary\nRepositoryError, so it is impossible to forget to handle. The Option\nlives inside the binding alone, where `is_configured` can still answer\nthe one question `current` cannot -- and that question matters, because\n\"nobody has connected this\" and \"the repository is refusing us\" send an\noperator somewhere completely different.\n\nListing clients on an unconnected platform therefore answers 503\n`integration_not_configured` rather than a 500 or an empty list. It\ncarries no Retry-After: retrying will not connect it. The two failures\nthat share 503 do carry one, so that header is now decided by the error\nrather than by the status.\n\nGET /api/integrations/git reports the connection, derived from ground\ntruth rather than advanced by a stored flag -- whether anything is bound,\nand what the last sweep observed. `invalid` is separated from `error`\nbecause only one is fixed by reconnecting, and a revoked installation is\nexactly the case this platform cannot be told about: the operator plane\nis a tailnet with no inbound path from a Git host, so there is no webhook\nand it has to notice for itself. The response carries status, a\ndescription and a last-success time -- no credential, no reference to\none, no path.\n\nA sweep with nothing configured logs at debug, not error. Filling the\nlog of a healthy platform that is simply waiting for an operator would\ntrain whoever reads it to ignore the level that matters.\n\nThe console asks about the integration before the client list, because\nit decides what to show. \"Nobody has connected this yet\" is not rendered\nas \"could not load clients\", and the list''s own failure is suppressed\nwhen it would be the same fact reported twice.\n\nScope note: the write-capable secret capability and the integration\nstate store were in this PR''s plan and have moved to the flow that\npopulates them. Landing them here would add two ports and an adapter\nthat nothing calls, and this codebase is explicit that an unused\ncapability suggests the control plane can already do something it\ncannot. The record shape is also much easier to get right with the\nmanifest conversion in hand than guessed ahead of it.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqkjR5CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA0JIQACk1k2L4VfcbsusdGaxOVYdJ\ngSZ4b35OhKx+UAPWqe+o+BWhm44jyTHvAhEW1QuzbwNaTvpMryFTokKkFCow2FlU\n847lP/t3VSduZwHijKC7W1t0uiVs8QQqgDkYkDYeljL0LrwVzn/umFQu4kDfoEIo\nBPmqFbtuoqd36JJpTLIS9vSXQfpFgLoFWTEIhGWk28xsV3R3UQ5OppuTAkVa/GGK\nQjpNnRH59Y3s130BCnFhoeUc+gD0BbKgOCXBCD9vd1KjZsjwU5CLiYYZOshJrnkq\nQ8zY8iVsj4nk/EcJvdmh9AW0KHkGlDlqyGUHuHvtPyie/pYzGZJ8mCcbsZdfnFmw\n2L+zUhVa0TkbgJtT4ccH2NUX+dZM8jxneDbh8b1fLyGKfoLzfBpPUK6SCetVH0uB\nEwLfz4AuWfSYEoB1aosoUS1xZCnNc1JunGrE/upr9phrTkQgcTI6w2iYGViX8MdO\nDe89XUwJxMrLb2ei1p5fwNcNEgewPfYqK926xraUVXl9c80YMCVOLNimaAw08H1n\nEtayHVAU3xTbp2o+Ah3LZUl3CWqLmHqueskZTK0sxW+vPGiVIXu/5hS4p6AX85m/\ny454lQSggaPksfII6TTOZ6kR7Rq5Zgg7MhfKH9uVaYyX3q6AM0zaGmIosZQCQh0Q\ngjlcP/0UCFVb1OUDLf/v\n=PAPL\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-29T01:23:05Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/193a35291d7688df3eb4fc939b01dc4bd9e798bc","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKDE5M2EzNTI5MWQ3Njg4ZGYzZWI0ZmM5MzliMDFkYzRiZDllNzk4YmM","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/dd18a8cceffea50cbe6f466a19a49c5e5a373a92","sha":"dd18a8cceffea50cbe6f466a19a49c5e5a373a92","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/dd18a8cceffea50cbe6f466a19a49c5e5a373a92"}],"sha":"193a35291d7688df3eb4fc939b01dc4bd9e798bc","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/193a35291d7688df3eb4fc939b01dc4bd9e798bc"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/dd18a8cceffea50cbe6f466a19a49c5e5a373a92/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-29T01:16:37Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-29T01:16:37Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Authenticate operators against the platform''s own realm (#6)\n\nThe control plane took an operator''s identity from a header the\noperator-plane proxy set, and decided who administers the platform from\nan allowlist in a config file. Both were the right first move and both\nhave a cost worth naming.\n\nThe header is trustworthy only because of where the service sits.\nNothing in the application enforces that: publish the same container on\nanother network and every request can claim to be anybody, with no code\nchange and no failing test. And authority living in a deployment means\nadding or removing an operator is a pipeline change, handled somewhere\nother than where joiners and leavers already are.\n\nSo an operator is now somebody the platform''s own realm authenticated,\nholding a realm role. OidcOperators verifies the signature against the\nrealm''s published keys, requires the issuer to match exactly, requires\nthe token to have been issued to the console''s client, and requires the\nrole. Keys are held in memory and refreshed on a task, so verification\ndoes no I/O and the extractor stays synchronous -- the runtime plane\nmade the same call for the same reason, and ADR 0010 records that the\nagreement is deliberate.\n\nThe trusted-header posture stays, as the development posture. The\nshipped example uses it because the example has to run without a\ncluster and OIDC cannot.\n\nThe console never meets the identity provider. Its policy is\ndefault-src ''self'', so it may navigate to another origin but not fetch\none -- it asks the API where to sign in, sends the browser there with a\nPKCE challenge, and posts the code back to /api/session, which redeems\nit server-side. The console holds no client secret because it is a\npublic client, and the token lives in a module variable for the life of\nthe tab: not localStorage, not a cookie, no refresh token.\n\nTwo things are worth knowing about the verification:\n\n  - `azp`, not `aud`. A realm mints access tokens whose audience is the\n    resource server the caller asked for -- commonly `account` -- and\n    names the obtaining client in `azp`. Requiring `aud` to equal the\n    console''s client id refuses every genuine token until somebody adds\n    an audience mapper, and it presents as a signature problem rather\n    than a missing mapper.\n  - The algorithm is pinned at construction and never configurable. An\n    algorithm a deployment can choose is one an attacker can choose.\n    Tests pin a symmetric algorithm so they can sign without a private\n    key in this repository; nothing else differs.\n\nfabric-keycloak now implements two ports from two crates, which the\ndependency graph did not allow and now does. That is not a smell:\nKeycloak is two things to this platform -- the provider reconciliation\ndrives, and the realm operators sign in against -- and one interface\ncovering both would couple them for no reason beyond the vendor being\nthe same.\n\nThe realm needs a public console client and an operator role before the\nposture works, and nothing creates them yet. Automating that needs a\nbroader grant on the master realm than the service account holds, and\nthat grant deserves its own decision rather than arriving as a side\neffect of this one. Documented in ADR 0010 and the architecture note.\n\nAlso lands the Workspec Enterprise analysis the GitHub App work asked\nfor first, including where SaaS Fabric must diverge -- no webhooks on a\ntailnet, no reachability self-check, credentials to the secret\ncapability rather than an encrypted column, and single-use callback\nstate.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"ddd108c3a9d8d2a3aad45c250be7b6e8b1b28945","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/ddd108c3a9d8d2a3aad45c250be7b6e8b1b28945"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/dd18a8cceffea50cbe6f466a19a49c5e5a373a92","verification":{"payload":"tree ddd108c3a9d8d2a3aad45c250be7b6e8b1b28945\nparent 1d90f38455e6056e33d11e24a41bb5c49459bdb9\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1787966197 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1787966197 +1200\n\nAuthenticate operators against the platform''s own realm (#6)\n\nThe control plane took an operator''s identity from a header the\noperator-plane proxy set, and decided who administers the platform from\nan allowlist in a config file. Both were the right first move and both\nhave a cost worth naming.\n\nThe header is trustworthy only because of where the service sits.\nNothing in the application enforces that: publish the same container on\nanother network and every request can claim to be anybody, with no code\nchange and no failing test. And authority living in a deployment means\nadding or removing an operator is a pipeline change, handled somewhere\nother than where joiners and leavers already are.\n\nSo an operator is now somebody the platform''s own realm authenticated,\nholding a realm role. OidcOperators verifies the signature against the\nrealm''s published keys, requires the issuer to match exactly, requires\nthe token to have been issued to the console''s client, and requires the\nrole. Keys are held in memory and refreshed on a task, so verification\ndoes no I/O and the extractor stays synchronous -- the runtime plane\nmade the same call for the same reason, and ADR 0010 records that the\nagreement is deliberate.\n\nThe trusted-header posture stays, as the development posture. The\nshipped example uses it because the example has to run without a\ncluster and OIDC cannot.\n\nThe console never meets the identity provider. Its policy is\ndefault-src ''self'', so it may navigate to another origin but not fetch\none -- it asks the API where to sign in, sends the browser there with a\nPKCE challenge, and posts the code back to /api/session, which redeems\nit server-side. The console holds no client secret because it is a\npublic client, and the token lives in a module variable for the life of\nthe tab: not localStorage, not a cookie, no refresh token.\n\nTwo things are worth knowing about the verification:\n\n  - `azp`, not `aud`. A realm mints access tokens whose audience is the\n    resource server the caller asked for -- commonly `account` -- and\n    names the obtaining client in `azp`. Requiring `aud` to equal the\n    console''s client id refuses every genuine token until somebody adds\n    an audience mapper, and it presents as a signature problem rather\n    than a missing mapper.\n  - The algorithm is pinned at construction and never configurable. An\n    algorithm a deployment can choose is one an attacker can choose.\n    Tests pin a symmetric algorithm so they can sign without a private\n    key in this repository; nothing else differs.\n\nfabric-keycloak now implements two ports from two crates, which the\ndependency graph did not allow and now does. That is not a smell:\nKeycloak is two things to this platform -- the provider reconciliation\ndrives, and the realm operators sign in against -- and one interface\ncovering both would couple them for no reason beyond the vendor being\nthe same.\n\nThe realm needs a public console client and an operator role before the\nposture works, and nothing creates them yet. Automating that needs a\nbroader grant on the master realm than the service account holds, and\nthat grant deserves its own decision rather than arriving as a side\neffect of this one. Documented in ADR 0010 and the architecture note.\n\nAlso lands the Workspec Enterprise analysis the GitHub App work asked\nfor first, including where SaaS Fabric must diverge -- no webhooks on a\ntailnet, no reachability self-check, credentials to the secret\ncapability rather than an encrypted column, and single-use callback\nstate.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqkjL1CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAyVUQABkIv1p7mW07bjwEnLA7+n2S\ncf6J92K/luNFeoqAGWFlvq8GaYgAL5c+frgVpAlGae5ThAEDRLc8TTaF53bP71sS\nNBpaGxz0QvHswbN4tLrvit06ssT2U9Ii6im+8ukFkRJ4H9VG5e+U2PXA4qWebJpx\nr4tf343j0dBCDuefU65CIhe+5GNAvhNyOKh4rOv6vNOCkbVo7MGjDRHs1If6lvOT\n1yjh/x/3tmYsAdO9fWHSl9t8iKcc0nhz1octrxxDxZMGi/t3rRuS6wO36m/JxHww\nHP0sfi6nOC4X+J5y9xEpu80fI4RpEaz4BfPPUXTsTCFUZWMG9bylOTDK7TJpYAmS\nkGPEGvfFvHj9X4231jNA7gMA2llciKfj5zNRRY+vEhob4qGV0BlQXbcjWLqDAIBO\nzk+4S14Q0jnZKLG69+eSh7OZc7gn8JddYPbjhG/iS2ayG1nhQqdLsjNAQKKAJYAX\nVV6nGU+FQT6gx7Tk1RYbjp27kTG4jc8lDZOCr5VmtHTjMR58TBp93ySJyGOV66/d\nCdN6c7xtB6QQECwiD8G8LQfpNZ7zOA1+wd1l4wsG2OaK4RfJeL28lNqAJAAsytsV\n0/bGT3sfIw2pVkByVZW/mQaZ6sc4CFrr5bNOODLJXlF4NLXhlbqt3z0QMXi3avt8\nFLdfgUF/vAhRbS0dNtO8\n=Q6GS\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-29T01:16:37Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/dd18a8cceffea50cbe6f466a19a49c5e5a373a92","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKGRkMThhOGNjZWZmZWE1MGNiZTZmNDY2YTE5YTQ5YzVlNWEzNzNhOTI","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/1d90f38455e6056e33d11e24a41bb5c49459bdb9","sha":"1d90f38455e6056e33d11e24a41bb5c49459bdb9","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/1d90f38455e6056e33d11e24a41bb5c49459bdb9"}],"sha":"dd18a8cceffea50cbe6f466a19a49c5e5a373a92","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/dd18a8cceffea50cbe6f466a19a49c5e5a373a92"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/1d90f38455e6056e33d11e24a41bb5c49459bdb9/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-28T19:16:46Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-28T19:16:46Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Merge pull request #5 from FieldstateNZ/claude/package-saas-fabric\n\nPackage SaaS Fabric as three images, published by a tag","tree":{"sha":"f594b1372e4992822d31dc61376b217d8da18e22","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/f594b1372e4992822d31dc61376b217d8da18e22"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/1d90f38455e6056e33d11e24a41bb5c49459bdb9","verification":{"payload":"tree f594b1372e4992822d31dc61376b217d8da18e22\nparent 12a75b3987f2faa5e0f718db5e3171e70e4fa66e\nparent 1cc3e865717f9bff4e5d224a2de263aacb0cf58d\nauthor Brett Smith <brett@pukekos.co.nz> 1787944606 +1200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1787944606 +1200\n\nMerge pull request #5 from FieldstateNZ/claude/package-saas-fabric\n\nPackage SaaS Fabric as three images, published by a tag","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqkd6eCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA9tgQAHo1OJzaj3s+gT3C04FWrCNW\naZ3VDsVZzbl71eFgvFX9HvZQwuHBebLnE0qFnifyyo4BxV4LVC1zQ55xiUOOdt3L\n+Vl1idWc7x2dYv3hFeaxGdznqZaLiEKmY8cdI2KN9TouwvDC81hBlXJdmx8/D2N5\nzpIwQbL6AX7ipg4hirnTOedmTbry3SmhuyOGyYp4iJL0As3k5zv8pCBUQuLyF4DM\n2sOCsj89VedggTLKApktDe2DtknCO7ZksGtUp6JuFpby+che8F0VDOdirS7Gl5nE\nqEemfx4F/hB0pmXoLOpF1eO/rrwDSvh6whibejt9nIeuKYGTmghuIbZW2Aywc5ee\nwfG730OOF0eBMcQl4ewJg2uoFkNvPZu2SNJ1SXH5kzBpfKDTp/K0zaeYn28MwL8O\newdmnskSVpVAeCnkeq1JYak9DY+09w16c4XqEeP/9+ksHH0N6HETk77f/QaIOz8L\nG2C4XPdoiCdtXYWIiqdEND/NPdErhINumx/rXieHuyQI77a3KTNGkKm1gSXTjFdc\nXVzwLroafVB5+I82hvjAy4niamD0+obm5jNqzyRzxdj4JAxkm3PCW4ojeoB0xtR4\nh1GMUAudPm0yuBgkP/IRshJy2BV2YQT6biKO456OAQ+x/Mmfrcn9pPuvCwLas04E\nIWeV+ZBgzBboF/8iNxWM\n=Rb4V\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-28T19:16:47Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/1d90f38455e6056e33d11e24a41bb5c49459bdb9","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKDFkOTBmMzg0NTVlNjA1NmUzM2QxMWUyNGE0MWJiNWM0OTQ1OWJkYjk","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/12a75b3987f2faa5e0f718db5e3171e70e4fa66e","sha":"12a75b3987f2faa5e0f718db5e3171e70e4fa66e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/12a75b3987f2faa5e0f718db5e3171e70e4fa66e"},{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/1cc3e865717f9bff4e5d224a2de263aacb0cf58d","sha":"1cc3e865717f9bff4e5d224a2de263aacb0cf58d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/1cc3e865717f9bff4e5d224a2de263aacb0cf58d"}],"sha":"1d90f38455e6056e33d11e24a41bb5c49459bdb9","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/1d90f38455e6056e33d11e24a41bb5c49459bdb9"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/1cc3e865717f9bff4e5d224a2de263aacb0cf58d/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-28T09:52:16Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-28T09:52:16Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"message":"Require the release gates to pass, not merely to avoid failing\n\nReview of #5 caught a fail-open, and it was in the one job whose entire\npurpose is to fail closed.\n\nThe build job''s condition was:\n\n    always() && needs.verify.result != ''failure''\n\nwhich reads as \"only when the gates passed\" and is not. A GitHub job\nresult is one of four values, and `cancelled` is not `failure` — so a\nrelease whose gates were killed, or whose runner was lost, would have\nbuilt and published. That is exactly the artifact-from-an-unchecked-tree\nthe job exists to prevent, arriving through the condition meant to\nprevent it.\n\nExcluding the result you fear is not the same as requiring the one you\nwant. The condition now requires `success` outright for a tag, and\npermits the deliberate skip only when the ref is not one:\n\n    tag     success     builds, publishes\n    tag     failure     no\n    tag     cancelled   no\n    tag     skipped     no\n    branch  skipped     builds, publishes nothing\n\nPublishing remains gated a second time inside the job on the ref being a\ntag, so a pull-request build cannot push even if this condition were\nwrong again.\n\nThe truth table is in the workflow beside the condition and in\ndocs/architecture/packaging.md, because the reason it is not the shorter\nexpression is not visible from the shorter expression.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"f594b1372e4992822d31dc61376b217d8da18e22","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/f594b1372e4992822d31dc61376b217d8da18e22"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/1cc3e865717f9bff4e5d224a2de263aacb0cf58d","verification":{"payload":null,"reason":"unsigned","signature":null,"verified":false,"verified_at":null}},"committer":null,"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/1cc3e865717f9bff4e5d224a2de263aacb0cf58d","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKDFjYzNlODY1NzE3ZjliZmY0ZTVkMjI0YTJkZTI2M2FhY2IwY2Y1OGQ","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/e30b70eb9c0aa415d8b5182169052be0e3b90218","sha":"e30b70eb9c0aa415d8b5182169052be0e3b90218","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/e30b70eb9c0aa415d8b5182169052be0e3b90218"}],"sha":"1cc3e865717f9bff4e5d224a2de263aacb0cf58d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/1cc3e865717f9bff4e5d224a2de263aacb0cf58d"},{"author":null,"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/e30b70eb9c0aa415d8b5182169052be0e3b90218/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-28T09:42:36Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-28T09:42:36Z","email":"brett@pukekos.co.nz","name":"Brett Smith"},"message":"Package SaaS Fabric as three images, published by a tag\n\nThe first half of the deployment work. This repository has never produced\na deployable artifact — `ghcr.io/fieldstatenz/saas-fabric` has no tag,\nwhich is why the platform ships that Deployment scaled to zero. No\ncluster is touched here; that is the next piece.\n\nThree images, because they are three deployments on two networks with\ndifferent availability requirements. The runtime plane must keep serving\ntenants while the control plane is down, and that is not a property one\nimage can have.\n\n  saas-fabric                    the runtime plane, on the product edge\n  saas-fabric-control-plane      the control-plane API, operator plane\n  saas-fabric-control-plane-ui   the console, operator plane\n\nThe runtime plane keeps its name because `saas-fabric-platform` already\nexpects it.\n\n# What is in them\n\nThe two Rust services come from one builder stage selected with\n`--target`: compiling a thirteen-crate workspace twice would double the\nslowest part of a release for nothing. Both are distroless and run as\n`nonroot` — no shell, no package manager, nothing to exec into, which\nmatters most for the runtime API because every tenant''s application can\nreach it. About 62 MB each.\n\nThe console is built with node and served without it, from\n`nginx-unprivileged` rather than `nginx`: the stock image starts as root\nto bind port 80, and a static file server on the operator plane has no\nreason to begin life as root.\n\nAll three were built and run before this was committed. The Rust images\nstart and refuse a missing configuration; the console serves, renders,\nand 404s an unknown path.\n\n# What running it found\n\nThe console''s nginx configuration silently dropped every security\nheader from the document. `add_header` is inherited from an outer block\nonly if the inner block declares no `add_header` of its own — so a\nserver-level `X-Frame-Options` beside a location-level `Cache-Control`\ndisappears from exactly the response that needs it. Caching is now\nvaried by content type through a `map`, leaving no `add_header` in any\nlocation to suppress the rest. Assets were also carrying two\n`Cache-Control` headers.\n\nThe console now sets a strict `Content-Security-Policy` — `default-src\n''self''`, no framing, no inline anything — verified by loading the\ncontainerised console in a browser: it renders, styles apply, and there\nare no violations.\n\n# What publishes\n\nA tag, and nothing else. Each image gets the version and `sha-<commit>`,\nand never `latest`: the platform pins an explicit version, and a\nfloating tag is how a cluster runs something nobody chose. A tag that\ndisagrees with `workspace.package.version` fails the release.\n\nThe gates re-run before a release builds. That duplicates `ci.yml`\ndeliberately — CI has almost certainly checked a tagged commit, and\n\"almost certainly\" admits an artifact built from a red tree.\n\nA pull request touching packaging builds all three images and pushes\nnothing, on the architecture the cluster runs rather than a developer''s.\nPackaging that only runs at release time breaks at release time.\n\n# One version, one place, one layer down\n\n`scripts/check_toolchain_pin.py` fails the build if a Dockerfile''s\n`RUST_VERSION` disagrees with `rust-toolchain.toml`. Without it, CI\nwould check the workspace with the pinned compiler and ship an artifact\nbuilt by another — the failure #3 fixed, reintroduced where nothing was\nlooking. Base images are pinned by digest as well as tag.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"ce8fadad75093c2344be438f32f517d440c7c3f4","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/trees/ce8fadad75093c2344be438f32f517d440c7c3f4"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/git/commits/e30b70eb9c0aa415d8b5182169052be0e3b90218","verification":{"payload":null,"reason":"unsigned","signature":null,"verified":false,"verified_at":null}},"committer":null,"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/e30b70eb9c0aa415d8b5182169052be0e3b90218","node_id":"C_kwDOT19v0doAKGUzMGI3MGViOWMwYWE0MTVkOGI1MTgyMTY5MDUyYmUwZTNiOTAyMTg","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commit/12a75b3987f2faa5e0f718db5e3171e70e4fa66e","sha":"12a75b3987f2faa5e0f718db5e3171e70e4fa66e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/12a75b3987f2faa5e0f718db5e3171e70e4fa66e"}],"sha":"e30b70eb9c0aa415d8b5182169052be0e3b90218","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits/e30b70eb9c0aa415d8b5182169052be0e3b90218"}],"requestUrl":"https://api.github.com/repos/FieldstateNZ/saas-fabric/commits?per_page=100&page=1&sha=eed365b93249720cc4163cc3d5b99349e950a688&since=2025-03-31T23%3A59%3A59.000Z&until=2026-10-08T16%3A26%3A07.968Z"}');
